const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Troy. Before Priam's palace.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Prologue.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"PROLOGUE","lines":["In Troy, there lies the scene. From isles of Greece","The princes orgulous, their high blood chaf'd,","Have to the port of Athens sent their ships","Fraught with the ministers and instruments","Of cruel war. Sixty and nine that wore","Their crownets regal from the Athenian bay","Put forth toward Phrygia; and their vow is made","To ransack Troy, within whose strong immures","The ravish'd Helen, Menelaus' queen,","With wanton Paris sleeps—and that's the quarrel.","To Tenedos they come,","And the deep-drawing barks do there disgorge","Their war-like fraughtage. Now on Dardan plains","The fresh and yet unbruised Greeks do pitch","Their brave pavilions: Priam's six-gated city,","Dardan, and Tymbria, Ilias, Chetas, Troien,","And Antenorides, with massy staples","And corresponsive and fulfilling bolts,","Stir up the sons of Troy.","Now expectation, tickling skittish spirits","On one and other side, Trojan and Greek,","Sets all on hazard. And hither am I come","A prologue arm'd, but not in confidence","Of author's pen or actor's voice, but suited","In like conditions as our argument,","To tell you, fair beholders, that our play","Leaps o'er the vaunt and firstlings of those broils,","Beginning in the middle; starting thence away,","To what may be digested in a play.","Like or find fault; do as your pleasures are;","Now good or bad, 'tis but the chance of war."],"id":2,"m":"Our scene is Troy. Proud princes from the islands of Greece, their noble blood inflamed, have sent their ships to the port of Athens, loaded with the men and weapons of cruel war. Sixty-nine kings wearing royal crowns set sail from the bay of Athens towards Phrygia, sworn to sack Troy, where, behind strong walls, Helen, Menelaus' stolen queen, sleeps with the lustful Paris. That is the quarrel. They land at Tenedos, and the heavy ships unload their warlike cargo there. Now the fresh Greeks, not yet bruised by fighting, pitch their splendid tents on the plains of Troy, while Priam's city, with its six gates (Dardan, Tymbria, Ilias, Chetas, Troien and Antenorides), barred with massive staples and close-fitting bolts, shuts in the sons of Troy. Now anticipation, teasing the nervous spirits of both sides, Trojan and Greek, puts everything at risk. And I have come here as a prologue in armour, not because I am confident in the author's pen or the actors' voices, but dressed to suit our subject, to tell you, fair audience, that our play skips the boasts and opening skirmishes of this war and begins in the middle, going on from there to whatever can be fitted into a play. Like it or find fault; do as you please. Good or bad, it is only the luck of war."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Troilus armed, and Pandarus.","id":3},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Call here my varlet; I'll unarm again.","Why should I war without the walls of Troy","That find such cruel battle here within?","Each Trojan that is master of his heart,","Let him to field; Troilus, alas! hath none."],"id":4,"m":"Call my servant here; I'm taking my armour off again. Why should I fight outside the walls of Troy when I am suffering such a cruel battle in here, inside myself? Let every Trojan who is master of his own heart go to the battlefield. Troilus, alas, has no heart left."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Will this gear ne'er be mended?"],"id":5,"m":"Will this business never be put right?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["The Greeks are strong, and skilful to their strength,","Fierce to their skill, and to their fierceness valiant;","But I am weaker than a woman's tear,","Tamer than sleep, fonder than ignorance,","Less valiant than the virgin in the night,","And skilless as unpractis'd infancy."],"id":6,"m":"The Greeks are strong, and skilful as well as strong, fierce as well as skilful, and brave as well as fierce. But I am weaker than a woman's tear, tamer than sleep, more foolish than ignorance, less brave than a virgin at night, and as unskilled as an inexperienced child."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Well, I have told you enough of this; for my part, I'll not meddle nor make no farther. He that will have a cake out of the wheat must tarry the grinding."],"id":7,"m":"Well, I have told you enough about this. For my part, I won't interfere or have anything more to do with it. Whoever wants a cake made from the wheat must wait for the grinding."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Have I not tarried?"],"id":8,"m":"Haven't I waited?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Ay, the grinding; but you must tarry the bolting."],"id":9,"m":"Yes, for the grinding, but you must also wait for the sifting."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Have I not tarried?"],"id":10,"m":"Haven't I waited?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Ay, the bolting; but you must tarry the leavening."],"id":11,"m":"Yes, for the sifting, but you must wait for the rising."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Still have I tarried."],"id":12,"m":"I have still waited."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Ay, to the leavening; but here's yet in the word 'hereafter' the kneading, the making of the cake, the heating of the oven, and the baking; nay, you must stay the cooling too, or you may chance burn your lips."],"id":13,"m":"Yes, for the rising, but in the word \"afterwards\" there is still the kneading, the shaping of the cake, the heating of the oven and the baking. And you must wait for it to cool down too, or you might burn your lips."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Patience herself, what goddess e'er she be,","Doth lesser blench at suff'rance than I do.","At Priam's royal table do I sit;","And when fair Cressid comes into my thoughts,","So, traitor! 'when she comes'! when she is thence?"],"id":14,"m":"Patience herself, whatever goddess she is, flinches less at suffering than I do. I sit at Priam's royal table, and when lovely Cressida comes into my thoughts... Traitor that I am! \"When she comes\"! When is she ever out of them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Well, she look'd yesternight fairer than ever I saw her look, or any woman else."],"id":15,"m":"Well, she looked lovelier last night than I have ever seen her look, or any other woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["I was about to tell thee: when my heart,","As wedged with a sigh, would rive in twain,","Lest Hector or my father should perceive me,","I have, as when the sun doth light a storm,","Buried this sigh in wrinkle of a smile.","But sorrow that is couch'd in seeming gladness","Is like that mirth fate turns to sudden sadness."],"id":16,"m":"I was going to tell you: when my heart felt as if a sigh had been wedged into it and would split it in two, I buried that sigh in a wrinkled smile, like the sun shining through a storm, so that Hector or my father would not notice. But sorrow hidden under a show of happiness is like the cheerfulness that fate suddenly turns to sadness."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["An her hair were not somewhat darker than Helen's, well, go to, there were no more comparison between the women. But, for my part, she is my kinswoman; I would not, as they term it, praise her, but I would somebody had heard her talk yesterday, as I did. I will not dispraise your sister Cassandra's wit; but—"],"id":17,"m":"If her hair weren't a bit darker than Helen's, well, there'd be no comparing the two women. But, for my part, she's my relative, so I wouldn't, as they say, praise her. But I wish someone had heard her talking yesterday, as I did. I won't run down your sister Cassandra's intelligence, but..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O Pandarus! I tell thee, Pandarus,","When I do tell thee there my hopes lie drown'd,","Reply not in how many fathoms deep","They lie indrench'd. I tell thee I am mad","In Cressid's love. Thou answer'st 'She is fair';","Pour'st in the open ulcer of my heart","Her eyes, her hair, her cheek, her gait, her voice,","Handlest in thy discourse. O! that her hand,","In whose comparison all whites are ink","Writing their own reproach; to whose soft seizure","The cygnet's down is harsh, and spirit of sense","Hard as the palm of ploughman! This thou tell'st me,","As true thou tell'st me, when I say I love her;","But, saying thus, instead of oil and balm,","Thou lay'st in every gash that love hath given me","The knife that made it."],"id":18,"m":"Oh Pandarus! I tell you, Pandarus, when I tell you that my hopes are drowned there, don't answer by telling me how many fathoms deep they lie. I tell you I am mad with love for Cressida. You answer, \"She's beautiful,\" and pour into the open wound of my heart her eyes, her hair, her cheek, her walk, her voice. You go on and on about them. Oh, her hand, next to which every white thing looks like ink writing its own disgrace, and compared to whose soft touch a young swan's down is rough and the most delicate sense of touch is as hard as a ploughman's palm! You tell me this, and it is true, when I say I love her. But by saying it, instead of oil and soothing ointment, you lay in every wound that love has given me the knife that made it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I speak no more than truth."],"id":19,"m":"I speak nothing but the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Thou dost not speak so much."],"id":20,"m":"You don't speak even as much as the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Faith, I'll not meddle in't. Let her be as she is: if she be fair, 'tis the better for her; and she be not, she has the mends in her own hands."],"id":21,"m":"Honestly, I won't interfere. Let her be as she is. If she's beautiful, so much the better for her; if she isn't, she can do something about it herself."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Good Pandarus! How now, Pandarus!"],"id":22,"m":"Good Pandarus! What's the matter, Pandarus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I have had my labour for my travail, ill thought on of her and ill thought on of you; gone between and between, but small thanks for my labour."],"id":23,"m":"I've had nothing but trouble for my efforts, been badly thought of by her and badly thought of by you. I've gone back and forth between you, and got little thanks for my work."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["What! art thou angry, Pandarus? What! with me?"],"id":24,"m":"What! Are you angry, Pandarus? What! With me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Because she's kin to me, therefore she's not so fair as Helen. And she were not kin to me, she would be as fair on Friday as Helen is on Sunday. But what care I? I care not and she were a blackamoor; 'tis all one to me."],"id":25,"m":"Because she's related to me, she's not as beautiful as Helen. If she weren't related to me, she'd look as good in her weekday clothes as Helen does in her Sunday best. But what do I care? I wouldn't care if she were black as a Moor; it's all the same to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Say I she is not fair?"],"id":26,"m":"Did I say she isn't beautiful?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I do not care whether you do or no. She's a fool to stay behind her father. Let her to the Greeks; and so I'll tell her the next time I see her. For my part, I'll meddle nor make no more i' the matter."],"id":27,"m":"I don't care whether you do or not. She's a fool to stay behind when her father has gone. Let her go to the Greeks, and I'll tell her so next time I see her. For my part, I won't interfere or meddle any more in the matter."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Pandarus—"],"id":28,"m":"Pandarus..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Not I."],"id":29,"m":"Not me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Sweet Pandarus—"],"id":30,"m":"Sweet Pandarus..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Pray you, speak no more to me: I will leave all as I found it, and there an end."],"id":31,"m":"Please, don't say any more to me. I'll leave everything as I found it, and that's the end of it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Pandarus. An alarum.","id":32},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Peace, you ungracious clamours! Peace, rude sounds!","Fools on both sides! Helen must needs be fair,","When with your blood you daily paint her thus.","I cannot fight upon this argument;","It is too starv'd a subject for my sword.","But Pandarus, O gods! how do you plague me!","I cannot come to Cressid but by Pandar;","And he's as tetchy to be woo'd to woo","As she is stubborn-chaste against all suit.","Tell me, Apollo, for thy Daphne's love,","What Cressid is, what Pandar, and what we?","Her bed is India; there she lies, a pearl;","Between our Ilium and where she resides","Let it be call'd the wild and wandering flood;","Ourself the merchant, and this sailing Pandar","Our doubtful hope, our convoy, and our bark."],"id":33,"m":"Quiet, you ugly noises! Quiet, you rough sounds! Fools on both sides! Helen must be beautiful when you paint her red with your blood every day. I can't fight for this cause; it's too thin a subject for my sword. But Pandarus! Oh gods, how you torment me! I can't get to Cressida except through Pandarus, and he is as touchy about being persuaded to do the wooing as she is stubbornly chaste against every suitor. Tell me, Apollo, for the love of your Daphne, what Cressida is, what Pandarus is, and what we are. Her bed is India; she lies there like a pearl. Let the space between our palace in Troy and where she lives be called the wild and stormy sea. I am the merchant, and this drifting Pandarus is my uncertain hope, my escort and my ship."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter Aeneas.","id":34},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["How now, Prince Troilus! Wherefore not afield?"],"id":35,"m":"Hello, Prince Troilus! Why aren't you on the battlefield?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Because not there. This woman's answer sorts,","For womanish it is to be from thence.","What news, Aeneas, from the field today?"],"id":36,"m":"Because I'm not there. That is a woman's answer, and it fits, because it is womanish to stay away from there. What news from the battlefield today, Aeneas?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["That Paris is returned home, and hurt."],"id":37,"m":"That Paris has come home wounded."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["By whom, Aeneas?"],"id":38,"m":"Wounded by whom, Aeneas?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Troilus, by Menelaus."],"id":39,"m":"By Menelaus, Troilus."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Let Paris bleed: 'tis but a scar to scorn;","Paris is gor'd with Menelaus' horn."],"id":40,"m":"Let Paris bleed. It's only a scratch, nothing to worry about. Paris has been gored by Menelaus' horn. (Menelaus is the cuckold, with a cuckold's horns.)"},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum.","id":41},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Hark what good sport is out of town today!"],"id":42,"m":"Listen to the fine sport going on outside the town today!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Better at home, if 'would I might' were 'may.'","But to the sport abroad. Are you bound thither?"],"id":43,"m":"There would be better sport at home, if \"I wish I might\" could become \"I may.\" But let's go to the sport out there. Are you heading there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["In all swift haste."],"id":44,"m":"As fast as I can."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Come, go we then together."],"id":45,"m":"Come on, then, let's go together."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":46},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"Troy. A street.","id":47},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cressida and her man Alexander.","id":48},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Who were those went by?"],"id":49,"m":"Who were those people who went by?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALEXANDER","lines":["Queen Hecuba and Helen."],"id":50,"m":"Queen Hecuba and Helen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["And whither go they?"],"id":51,"m":"And where are they going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALEXANDER","lines":["Up to the eastern tower,","Whose height commands as subject all the vale,","To see the battle. Hector, whose patience","Is as a virtue fix'd, today was mov'd.","He chid Andromache, and struck his armourer;","And, like as there were husbandry in war,","Before the sun rose he was harness'd light,","And to the field goes he; where every flower","Did as a prophet weep what it foresaw","In Hector's wrath."],"id":52,"m":"Up to the eastern tower, which is high enough to look down over the whole valley, to watch the battle. Hector, whose patience is as steady as a virtue, was angry today. He scolded Andromache and hit his armourer, and, as if war were a matter of good housekeeping, he put on his light armour before sunrise and went out to the battlefield, where every flower wept with dew, like a prophet foreseeing what Hector's anger would do."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["What was his cause of anger?"],"id":53,"m":"Why was he angry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALEXANDER","lines":["The noise goes, this: there is among the Greeks","A lord of Trojan blood, nephew to Hector;","They call him Ajax."],"id":54,"m":"The rumour goes like this: there is a lord among the Greeks who has Trojan blood, Hector's nephew. They call him Ajax."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Good; and what of him?"],"id":55,"m":"Good. And what about him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALEXANDER","lines":["They say he is a very man per se","And stands alone."],"id":56,"m":"They say he is a man unique in himself, and stands alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["So do all men, unless they are drunk, sick, or have no legs."],"id":57,"m":"So do all men, unless they are drunk, sick, or have no legs."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALEXANDER","lines":["This man, lady, hath robb'd many beasts of their particular additions: he is as valiant as the lion, churlish as the bear, slow as the elephant—a man into whom nature hath so crowded humours that his valour is crush'd into folly, his folly sauced with discretion. There is no man hath a virtue that he hath not a glimpse of, nor any man an attaint but he carries some stain of it; he is melancholy without cause and merry against the hair; he hath the joints of everything; but everything so out of joint that he is a gouty Briareus, many hands and no use, or purblind Argus, all eyes and no sight."],"id":58,"m":"This man, lady, has stolen from many animals their special qualities. He is as brave as the lion, as surly as the bear, as slow as the elephant. He is a man into whom nature has crammed so many moods that his courage is crushed into foolishness, and his foolishness is seasoned with a little sense. There is no man with a virtue that he doesn't have a glimpse of, and no man with a fault that he doesn't share a trace of. He is gloomy for no reason and cheerful at the wrong time. He has the parts of everything, but everything is so out of joint that he is like a gouty Briareus, with many hands and no use of them, or a half-blind Argus, all eyes and no sight."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["But how should this man, that makes me smile, make Hector angry?"],"id":59,"m":"But how could this man, who makes me smile, make Hector angry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALEXANDER","lines":["They say he yesterday cop'd Hector in the battle and struck him down, the disdain and shame whereof hath ever since kept Hector fasting and waking."],"id":60,"m":"They say that yesterday he met Hector in the battle and knocked him down, and the disgust and shame of it have kept Hector from eating or sleeping ever since."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pandarus.","id":61},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Who comes here?"],"id":62,"m":"Who's coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALEXANDER","lines":["Madam, your uncle Pandarus."],"id":63,"m":"Madam, your uncle Pandarus."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Hector's a gallant man."],"id":64,"m":"Hector's a fine man."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALEXANDER","lines":["As may be in the world, lady."],"id":65,"m":"As fine as any in the world, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["What's that? What's that?"],"id":66,"m":"What's that? What's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Good morrow, uncle Pandarus."],"id":67,"m":"Good morning, uncle Pandarus."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Good morrow, cousin Cressid. What do you talk of?—Good morrow, Alexander.—How do you, cousin? When were you at Ilium?"],"id":68,"m":"Good morning, niece Cressida. What are you talking about? Good morning, Alexander. How are you, niece? When were you at the palace?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["This morning, uncle."],"id":69,"m":"This morning, uncle."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["What were you talking of when I came? Was Hector arm'd and gone ere you came to Ilium? Helen was not up, was she?"],"id":70,"m":"What were you talking about when I arrived? Had Hector armed and gone before you got to the palace? Helen wasn't up, was she?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Hector was gone; but Helen was not up."],"id":71,"m":"Hector had gone, but Helen wasn't up."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["E'en so. Hector was stirring early."],"id":72,"m":"Just so. Hector was up early."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["That were we talking of, and of his anger."],"id":73,"m":"That's what we were talking about, and about his anger."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Was he angry?"],"id":74,"m":"Was he angry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["So he says here."],"id":75,"m":"So he says here."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["True, he was so; I know the cause too; he'll lay about him today, I can tell them that. And there's Troilus will not come far behind him; let them take heed of Troilus, I can tell them that too."],"id":76,"m":"That's true, he was. I know the reason too. He'll lay into them today, I can tell them that. And Troilus won't be far behind him. Let them watch out for Troilus, I can tell them that too."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["What, is he angry too?"],"id":77,"m":"What, is he angry too?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Who, Troilus? Troilus is the better man of the two."],"id":78,"m":"Who, Troilus? Troilus is the better man of the two."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["O Jupiter! there's no comparison."],"id":79,"m":"Oh Jupiter! There's no comparison."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["What, not between Troilus and Hector? Do you know a man if you see him?"],"id":80,"m":"What, not between Troilus and Hector? Do you know a real man when you see one?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Ay, if I ever saw him before and knew him."],"id":81,"m":"Yes, if I've seen him before and know who he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Well, I say Troilus is Troilus."],"id":82,"m":"Well, I say Troilus is Troilus."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Then you say as I say, for I am sure he is not Hector."],"id":83,"m":"Then you're saying what I'm saying, because I'm sure he isn't Hector."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["No, nor Hector is not Troilus in some degrees."],"id":84,"m":"No, and Hector isn't Troilus in some ways."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["'Tis just to each of them: he is himself."],"id":85,"m":"That's fair to both of them: each is himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Himself! Alas, poor Troilus! I would he were!"],"id":86,"m":"Himself! Alas, poor Troilus! I wish he were!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["So he is."],"id":87,"m":"He is."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Condition I had gone barefoot to India."],"id":88,"m":"And if he is, I'll walk barefoot to India."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["He is not Hector."],"id":89,"m":"He isn't Hector."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Himself! no, he's not himself. Would a' were himself! Well, the gods are above; time must friend or end. Well, Troilus, well! I would my heart were in her body! No, Hector is not a better man than Troilus."],"id":90,"m":"Himself! No, he's not himself. I wish he were himself! Well, the gods are above us; time will either help or finish things. Well, Troilus, well! I wish my heart were in her body! No, Hector is not a better man than Troilus."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Excuse me."],"id":91,"m":"Excuse me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["He is elder."],"id":92,"m":"He's older."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Pardon me, pardon me."],"id":93,"m":"Pardon me, pardon me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Th'other's not come to't; you shall tell me another tale when th'other's come to't. Hector shall not have his wit this year."],"id":94,"m":"The other one hasn't reached his age yet. You'll tell a different story when the other one has. Hector won't have his brains this year."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["He shall not need it if he have his own."],"id":95,"m":"He won't need them if he has his own."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Nor his qualities."],"id":96,"m":"Nor his qualities."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["No matter."],"id":97,"m":"It doesn't matter."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Nor his beauty."],"id":98,"m":"Nor his good looks."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["'Twould not become him: his own's better."],"id":99,"m":"They wouldn't suit him: his own are better."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["You have no judgement, niece. Helen herself swore th'other day that Troilus, for a brown favour, for so 'tis, I must confess—not brown neither—"],"id":100,"m":"You have no judgement, niece. Helen herself swore the other day that Troilus, for a dark complexion (for it is dark, I have to admit; well, not dark exactly)..."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["No, but brown."],"id":101,"m":"No, just dark."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Faith, to say truth, brown and not brown."],"id":102,"m":"Honestly, to tell the truth, dark and not dark."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["To say the truth, true and not true."],"id":103,"m":"To tell the truth, true and not true."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["She prais'd his complexion above Paris."],"id":104,"m":"She praised his complexion above Paris's."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Why, Paris hath colour enough."],"id":105,"m":"Why, Paris has enough colour."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["So he has."],"id":106,"m":"So he has."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Then Troilus should have too much. If she prais'd him above, his complexion is higher than his; he having colour enough, and the other higher, is too flaming a praise for a good complexion. I had as lief Helen's golden tongue had commended Troilus for a copper nose."],"id":107,"m":"Then Troilus must have too much. If she praised his colour above Paris's, his colour is higher than Paris's. If Paris has enough colour and Troilus has more, that's too fiery a compliment for a good complexion. I'd rather Helen's golden tongue had praised Troilus for having a red nose."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I swear to you I think Helen loves him better than Paris."],"id":108,"m":"I swear to you, I think Helen loves him more than Paris."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Then she's a merry Greek indeed."],"id":109,"m":"Then she's a merry Greek indeed (a loose woman)."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Nay, I am sure she does. She came to him th'other day into the compass'd window—and you know he has not past three or four hairs on his chin—"],"id":110,"m":"No, I'm sure she does. She came up to him the other day in the bay window, and you know he hasn't more than three or four hairs on his chin..."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Indeed a tapster's arithmetic may soon bring his particulars therein to a total."],"id":111,"m":"Indeed, a barman's arithmetic could soon add up his hairs."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Why, he is very young, and yet will he within three pound lift as much as his brother Hector."],"id":112,"m":"Why, he's very young, and yet he can lift almost as much as his brother Hector, within three pounds."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Is he so young a man and so old a lifter?"],"id":113,"m":"Is he such a young man and such an old lifter (thief)?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["But to prove to you that Helen loves him: she came and puts me her white hand to his cloven chin—"],"id":114,"m":"But to prove to you that Helen loves him: she came and put her white hand to his cleft chin..."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Juno have mercy! How came it cloven?"],"id":115,"m":"Juno have mercy! How did it get split?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Why, you know, 'tis dimpled. I think his smiling becomes him better than any man in all Phrygia."],"id":116,"m":"Why, you know, it's dimpled. I think smiling suits him better than any man in all Troy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["O, he smiles valiantly!"],"id":117,"m":"Oh, he smiles bravely!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Does he not?"],"id":118,"m":"Doesn't he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["O yes, an 'twere a cloud in autumn!"],"id":119,"m":"Oh yes, like a cloud in autumn!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Why, go to, then! But to prove to you that Helen loves Troilus—"],"id":120,"m":"Oh, come on! But to prove to you that Helen loves Troilus..."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Troilus will stand to the proof, if you'll prove it so."],"id":121,"m":"Troilus will stand up to the test, if you can prove it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Troilus! Why, he esteems her no more than I esteem an addle egg."],"id":122,"m":"Troilus! Why, he thinks no more of her than I think of a rotten egg."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["If you love an addle egg as well as you love an idle head, you would eat chickens i' th' shell."],"id":123,"m":"If you loved a rotten egg as much as you love an empty head, you'd eat chickens still in the shell."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I cannot choose but laugh to think how she tickled his chin. Indeed, she has a marvell's white hand, I must needs confess."],"id":124,"m":"I can't help laughing to think how she tickled his chin. She really does have a marvellously white hand, I have to admit."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Without the rack."],"id":125,"m":"Without being forced on the rack to admit it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["And she takes upon her to spy a white hair on his chin."],"id":126,"m":"And she claimed to spot a white hair on his chin."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Alas, poor chin! Many a wart is richer."],"id":127,"m":"Alas, poor chin! Plenty of warts have more hair."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["But there was such laughing! Queen Hecuba laugh'd that her eyes ran o'er."],"id":128,"m":"But there was such laughing! Queen Hecuba laughed until her eyes ran over."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["With millstones."],"id":129,"m":"With millstones (she'd be as likely to cry millstones)."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["And Cassandra laugh'd."],"id":130,"m":"And Cassandra laughed."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["But there was a more temperate fire under the pot of her eyes. Did her eyes run o'er too?"],"id":131,"m":"But there was a cooler fire under the pot of her eyes. Did her eyes run over too?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["And Hector laugh'd."],"id":132,"m":"And Hector laughed."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["At what was all this laughing?"],"id":133,"m":"What was everyone laughing at?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Marry, at the white hair that Helen spied on Troilus' chin."],"id":134,"m":"Why, at the white hair Helen spotted on Troilus' chin."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["And't had been a green hair I should have laugh'd too."],"id":135,"m":"If it had been a green hair, I'd have laughed too."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["They laugh'd not so much at the hair as at his pretty answer."],"id":136,"m":"They didn't laugh so much at the hair as at his clever answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["What was his answer?"],"id":137,"m":"What was his answer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Quoth she 'Here's but two and fifty hairs on your chin, and one of them is white.'"],"id":138,"m":"She said, \"There are only fifty-two hairs on your chin, and one of them is white.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["This is her question."],"id":139,"m":"That's what she said."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["That's true; make no question of that. 'Two and fifty hairs,' quoth he 'and one white. That white hair is my father, and all the rest are his sons.' 'Jupiter!' quoth she 'which of these hairs is Paris my husband?' 'The forked one,' quoth he, 'pluck't out and give it him.' But there was such laughing! and Helen so blush'd, and Paris so chaf'd; and all the rest so laugh'd that it pass'd."],"id":140,"m":"That's true, no question about it. \"Fifty-two hairs,\" he said, \"and one white one. That white hair is my father, and all the rest are his sons.\" \"Jupiter!\" she said, \"which of these hairs is my husband Paris?\" \"The forked one,\" he said. \"Pluck it out and give it to him.\" But there was such laughing! And Helen blushed so much, and Paris got so annoyed, and everyone else laughed so much, it was beyond anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["So let it now; for it has been a great while going by."],"id":141,"m":"Then let it pass now, because it's been passing for a long time."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Well, cousin, I told you a thing yesterday; think on't."],"id":142,"m":"Well, niece, I told you something yesterday. Think about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["So I do."],"id":143,"m":"I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I'll be sworn 'tis true; he will weep you, and 'twere a man born in April."],"id":144,"m":"I swear it's true. He'll weep for you like a man born in rainy April."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["And I'll spring up in his tears, an 'twere a nettle against May."],"id":145,"m":"And I'll spring up in his tears like a nettle before May."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound a retreat.","id":146},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Hark! they are coming from the field. Shall we stand up here and see them as they pass toward Ilium? Good niece, do, sweet niece Cressida."],"id":147,"m":"Listen! They're coming back from the battlefield. Shall we stand up here and watch them as they pass towards the palace? Do, dear niece, sweet niece Cressida."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["At your pleasure."],"id":148,"m":"As you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Here, here, here's an excellent place; here we may see most bravely. I'll tell you them all by their names as they pass by; but mark Troilus above the rest."],"id":149,"m":"Here, here, here's an excellent spot. We can see beautifully from here. I'll tell you all their names as they go past, but watch out for Troilus above all."},{"t":"sd","text":"Aeneas passes.","id":150},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Speak not so loud."],"id":151,"m":"Don't talk so loud."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["That's Aeneas. Is not that a brave man? He's one of the flowers of Troy, I can tell you. But mark Troilus; you shall see anon."],"id":152,"m":"That's Aeneas. Isn't he a fine man? He's one of the flowers of Troy, I can tell you. But watch for Troilus; you'll see him soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Antenor passes.","id":153},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Who's that?"],"id":154,"m":"Who's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["That's Antenor. He has a shrewd wit, I can tell you; and he's a man good enough; he's one o' th' soundest judgements in Troy, whosoever, and a proper man of person. When comes Troilus? I'll show you Troilus anon. If he see me, you shall see him nod at me."],"id":155,"m":"That's Antenor. He's very shrewd, I can tell you, and he's a good enough man. He has one of the soundest judgements in Troy, whoever you compare him with, and he's a handsome man. When is Troilus coming? I'll show you Troilus soon. If he sees me, you'll see him nod at me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Will he give you the nod?"],"id":156,"m":"Will he give you the nod (call you a fool)?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["You shall see."],"id":157,"m":"You'll see."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["If he do, the rich shall have more."],"id":158,"m":"If he does, the rich will have more (you have plenty of foolishness already)."},{"t":"sd","text":"Hector passes.","id":159},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["That's Hector, that, that, look you, that; there's a fellow! Go thy way, Hector! There's a brave man, niece. O brave Hector! Look how he looks. There's a countenance! Is't not a brave man?"],"id":160,"m":"That's Hector, that one, that one, look, that one. There's a man for you! Go on, Hector! There's a brave man, niece. Oh brave Hector! Look how he looks. What a face! Isn't he a brave man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["O, a brave man!"],"id":161,"m":"Oh, a brave man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Is a' not? It does a man's heart good. Look you what hacks are on his helmet! Look you yonder, do you see? Look you there. There's no jesting; there's laying on; take't off who will, as they say. There be hacks."],"id":162,"m":"Isn't he? It does your heart good. Look at the dents on his helmet! Look over there, do you see? Look there. That's no joke; that's hard fighting. Let anyone take that off who can, as they say. Those are real dents."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Be those with swords?"],"id":163,"m":"Were those made with swords?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Swords! anything, he cares not; and the devil come to him, it's all one. By God's lid, it does one's heart good. Yonder comes Paris, yonder comes Paris."],"id":164,"m":"Swords! Anything, he doesn't care. If the devil himself came at him, it would be all the same. By God's eyelid, it does your heart good. Here comes Paris, here comes Paris."},{"t":"sd","text":"Paris passes.","id":165},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","cont":1,"lines":["Look ye yonder, niece; is't not a gallant man too, is't not? Why, this is brave now. Who said he came hurt home today? He's not hurt. Why, this will do Helen's heart good now, ha! Would I could see Troilus now! You shall see Troilus anon."],"id":166,"m":"Look over there, niece. Isn't he a fine man too, isn't he? Why, this is splendid. Who said he came home wounded today? He's not hurt. Why, this will do Helen's heart good, ha! I wish I could see Troilus now! You'll see Troilus soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Helenus passes.","id":167},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Who's that?"],"id":168,"m":"Who's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["That's Helenus. I marvel where Troilus is. That's","Helenus. I think he went not forth today. That's Helenus."],"id":169,"m":"That's Helenus. I wonder where Troilus is. That's Helenus. I don't think he went out today. That's Helenus."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Can Helenus fight, uncle?"],"id":170,"m":"Can Helenus fight, uncle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Helenus! no. Yes, he'll fight indifferent well. I marvel where Troilus is. Hark! do you not hear the people cry 'Troilus'?—Helenus is a priest."],"id":171,"m":"Helenus! No. Yes, he fights reasonably well. I wonder where Troilus is. Listen! Can't you hear the people shouting \"Troilus\"? Helenus is a priest."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["What sneaking fellow comes yonder?"],"id":172,"m":"Who's that sneaky-looking fellow over there?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Troilus passes.","id":173},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Where? yonder? That's Deiphobus. 'Tis Troilus. There's a man, niece. Hem! Brave Troilus, the prince of chivalry!"],"id":174,"m":"Where? Over there? That's Deiphobus. No, it's Troilus! There's a man, niece. Ahem! Brave Troilus, the prince of knighthood!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Peace, for shame, peace!"],"id":175,"m":"Quiet, for shame, quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Mark him; note him. O brave Troilus! Look well upon him, niece; look you how his sword is bloodied, and his helm more hack'd than Hector's; and how he looks, and how he goes! O admirable youth! he never saw three and twenty. Go thy way, Troilus, go thy way. Had I a sister were a grace or a daughter a goddess, he should take his choice. O admirable man! Paris? Paris is dirt to him; and, I warrant, Helen, to change, would give an eye to boot."],"id":176,"m":"Watch him, take note of him. Oh brave Troilus! Look at him carefully, niece. Look how bloody his sword is, and his helmet more dented than Hector's. And look how he looks, and how he walks! Oh admirable young man! He isn't even twenty-three yet. Go on, Troilus, go on. If I had a sister who was one of the Graces, or a daughter who was a goddess, he could take his pick. Oh admirable man! Paris? Paris is dirt next to him, and I bet Helen would give an eye to make the exchange."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Here comes more."],"id":177,"m":"Here come more."},{"t":"sd","text":"Common soldiers pass.","id":178},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Asses, fools, dolts! chaff and bran, chaff and bran! porridge after meat! I could live and die in the eyes of Troilus. Ne'er look, ne'er look; the eagles are gone. Crows and daws, crows and daws! I had rather be such a man as Troilus than Agamemnon and all Greece."],"id":179,"m":"Donkeys, fools, idiots! Chaff and bran, chaff and bran! Porridge after the meat! I could live and die gazing into Troilus' eyes. Don't bother looking, don't bother looking; the eagles have gone. Crows and jackdaws, crows and jackdaws! I'd rather be a man like Troilus than Agamemnon and all of Greece."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["There is amongst the Greeks Achilles, a better man than Troilus."],"id":180,"m":"Among the Greeks there's Achilles, a better man than Troilus."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Achilles? A drayman, a porter, a very camel!"],"id":181,"m":"Achilles? A carthorse driver, a porter, a real camel!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Well, well."],"id":182,"m":"Well, well."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Well, well! Why, have you any discretion? Have you any eyes? Do you know what a man is? Is not birth, beauty, good shape, discourse, manhood, learning, gentleness, virtue, youth, liberality, and such like, the spice and salt that season a man?"],"id":183,"m":"Well, well! Why, have you no judgement? Have you no eyes? Do you know what a man is? Aren't good birth, beauty, a good figure, conversation, manliness, learning, gentleness, virtue, youth, generosity and so on the spice and salt that season a man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Ay, a minc'd man; and then to be bak'd with no date in the pie, for then the man's date is out."],"id":184,"m":"Yes, a minced man, then baked in a pie with no dates in it, because then the man's date has run out (he is past it)."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["You are such a woman! A man knows not at what ward you lie."],"id":185,"m":"You're such a woman! A man never knows what guard you're keeping."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Upon my back, to defend my belly; upon my wit, to defend my wiles; upon my secrecy, to defend mine honesty; my mask, to defend my beauty; and you, to defend all these; and at all these wards I lie, at a thousand watches."],"id":186,"m":"On my back, to defend my belly; on my wit, to defend my tricks; on my secrecy, to defend my chastity; my mask, to defend my looks; and you, to defend all of these. I keep all these guards, and a thousand watches."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Say one of your watches."],"id":187,"m":"Tell me one of your watches."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Nay, I'll watch you for that; and that's one of the chiefest of them too. If I cannot ward what I would not have hit, I can watch you for telling how I took the blow; unless it swell past hiding, and then it's past watching."],"id":188,"m":"No, I'll watch you for that, and that's one of the most important ones. If I can't guard what I don't want hit, I can watch to stop you telling how I took the blow, unless it swells too big to hide, and then it's past watching (unless I get pregnant)."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["You are such another!"],"id":189,"m":"You're one of a kind!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Troilus' Boy.","id":190},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["Sir, my lord would instantly speak with you."],"id":191,"m":"Sir, my lord wants to speak to you at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Where?"],"id":192,"m":"Where?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["At your own house; there he unarms him."],"id":193,"m":"At your house. He's taking his armour off there."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Good boy, tell him I come. [Exit Boy] I doubt he be hurt. Fare ye well, good niece."],"id":194,"m":"Good boy, tell him I'm coming. (The Boy leaves.) I'm afraid he's been wounded. Goodbye, good niece."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Adieu, uncle."],"id":195,"m":"Goodbye, uncle."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I will be with you, niece, by and by."],"id":196,"m":"I'll be with you soon, niece."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["To bring, uncle."],"id":197,"m":"To bring me something, uncle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Ay, a token from Troilus."],"id":198,"m":"Yes, a love token from Troilus."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Pandarus.","id":199},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["By the same token, you are a bawd.","Words, vows, gifts, tears, and love's full sacrifice,","He offers in another's enterprise;","But more in Troilus thousand-fold I see","Than in the glass of Pandar's praise may be,","Yet hold I off. Women are angels, wooing:","Things won are done; joy's soul lies in the doing.","That she belov'd knows naught that knows not this:","Men prize the thing ungain'd more than it is.","That she was never yet that ever knew","Love got so sweet as when desire did sue;","Therefore this maxim out of love I teach:","'Achievement is command; ungain'd, beseech.'","Then though my heart's content firm love doth bear,","Nothing of that shall from mine eyes appear."],"id":200,"m":"By that same token, you are a pimp. He offers words, vows, gifts, tears, and the whole sacrifice of love on another man's behalf. But I see a thousand times more in Troilus than the mirror of Pandarus' praise can show. Still I hold back. Women are angels while they are being wooed. Once a thing is won, it is finished; the soul of joy is in the chase. A woman who is loved knows nothing if she doesn't know this: men value what they haven't won more than it is worth. No woman has ever known love to be as sweet once it is gained as when desire was still pleading. So love teaches me this rule: \"Once won, men command; while unwon, they beg.\" So although my heart is full of steady love, none of it will show in my eyes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":201},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"The Grecian camp. Before Agamemnon's tent.","id":202},{"t":"sd","text":"Sennet. Enter Agamemnon, Nestor, Ulysses, Diomedes, Menelaus and others.","id":203},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Princes,","What grief hath set these jaundies o'er your cheeks?","The ample proposition that hope makes","In all designs begun on earth below","Fails in the promis'd largeness; checks and disasters","Grow in the veins of actions highest rear'd,","As knots, by the conflux of meeting sap,","Infects the sound pine, and diverts his grain","Tortive and errant from his course of growth.","Nor, princes, is it matter new to us","That we come short of our suppose so far","That after seven years' siege yet Troy walls stand;","Sith every action that hath gone before,","Whereof we have record, trial did draw","Bias and thwart, not answering the aim,","And that unbodied figure of the thought","That gave't surmised shape. Why then, you princes,","Do you with cheeks abash'd behold our works","And call them shames, which are, indeed, naught else","But the protractive trials of great Jove","To find persistive constancy in men;","The fineness of which metal is not found","In fortune's love? For then the bold and coward,","The wise and fool, the artist and unread,","The hard and soft, seem all affin'd and kin.","But in the wind and tempest of her frown","Distinction, with a broad and powerful fan,","Puffing at all, winnows the light away;","And what hath mass or matter by itself","Lies rich in virtue and unmingled."],"id":204,"m":"Princes, what trouble has spread this yellow sickness over your faces? The great promises that hope makes at the start of every plan on earth fall short of what was promised. Setbacks and disasters grow inside the highest undertakings, just as knots, caused by the sap meeting, infect the healthy pine and twist its grain off its straight course of growth. And it is nothing new to us, princes, that we have fallen so far short of our expectations that after seven years of siege the walls of Troy still stand. Every action in the past that we have records of went crooked and wrong when it was put to the test, and did not match the aim or the imagined shape that the thought first gave it. So why, princes, do you look at our efforts with embarrassed faces and call them shameful? They are really nothing but the drawn-out tests of great Jove, to find out which men have lasting endurance. The quality of that metal is not found when fortune is kind, because then the brave and the cowardly, the wise and the foolish, the learned and the ignorant, the hard and the soft, all seem alike and related. But in the wind and storm of fortune's frown, the difference between them, like a broad and powerful fan, blows on everything and winnows the light chaff away, and what has real weight and substance lies there rich in virtue and pure."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["With due observance of thy godlike seat,","Great Agamemnon, Nestor shall apply","Thy latest words. In the reproof of chance","Lies the true proof of men. The sea being smooth,","How many shallow bauble boats dare sail","Upon her patient breast, making their way","With those of nobler bulk!","But let the ruffian Boreas once enrage","The gentle Thetis, and anon behold","The strong-ribb'd bark through liquid mountains cut,","Bounding between the two moist elements","Like Perseus' horse. Where's then the saucy boat,","Whose weak untimber'd sides but even now","Co-rivall'd greatness? Either to harbour fled","Or made a toast for Neptune. Even so","Doth valour's show and valour's worth divide","In storms of fortune; for in her ray and brightness","The herd hath more annoyance by the breeze","Than by the tiger; but when the splitting wind","Makes flexible the knees of knotted oaks,","And flies fled under shade—why, then the thing of courage,","As rous'd with rage, with rage doth sympathise,","And with an accent tun'd in self-same key","Retorts to chiding fortune."],"id":205,"m":"With due respect to your godlike throne, great Agamemnon, Nestor will apply your last words. The true test of men lies in how they stand up to bad luck. When the sea is calm, how many flimsy little boats dare to sail on her patient surface, keeping pace with much larger ships! But let the rough north wind once stir up the gentle sea, and soon you see the strong-ribbed ship cutting through mountains of water, leaping between the sea and the sky like Perseus' winged horse. Where is the cheeky little boat then, whose weak, flimsy sides a moment ago rivalled greatness? Either it has fled to harbour or it has been swallowed by Neptune like toast in wine. In the same way, the appearance of courage and real courage separate in the storms of fortune. In fortune's bright sunshine the herd is more troubled by the gadfly than by the tiger, but when the splitting wind bends the knees of knotted oaks, and the flies have run for shelter, then the courageous creature, roused to anger, matches the storm's rage and answers back to scolding fortune in the same key."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Agamemnon,","Thou great commander, nerve and bone of Greece,","Heart of our numbers, soul and only spirit","In whom the tempers and the minds of all","Should be shut up—hear what Ulysses speaks.","Besides th'applause and approbation","The which, [To Agamemnon] most mighty, for thy place and sway,","[To Nestor] And, thou most reverend, for thy stretch'd-out life,","I give to both your speeches—which were such","As Agamemnon and the hand of Greece","Should hold up high in brass; and such again","As venerable Nestor, hatch'd in silver,","Should with a bond of air, strong as the axle-tree","On which heaven rides, knit all the Greekish ears","To his experienc'd tongue—yet let it please both,","Thou great, and wise, to hear Ulysses speak."],"id":206,"m":"Agamemnon, great commander, nerve and bone of Greece, heart of our army, soul and single spirit in whom the temperaments and minds of all of us should be contained: listen to what Ulysses says. Besides the applause and approval that I give to both your speeches (to you, most mighty, for your rank and power, and to you, most reverend, for your long life), speeches which Agamemnon and the hand of Greece should engrave in brass, and which venerable Nestor, silver-haired, should use to bind all Greek ears to his experienced tongue with a bond of breath as strong as the axle on which heaven turns: still, may it please you both, great and wise, to hear Ulysses speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Speak, Prince of Ithaca; and be't of less expect","That matter needless, of importless burden,","Divide thy lips than we are confident,","When rank Thersites opes his mastic jaws,","We shall hear music, wit, and oracle."],"id":207,"m":"Speak, Prince of Ithaca. We are less likely to hear useless, unimportant things from your lips than we are to hear music, wit and prophecy when foul Thersites opens his jaws."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Troy, yet upon his basis, had been down,","And the great Hector's sword had lack'd a master,","But for these instances:","The specialty of rule hath been neglected;","And look how many Grecian tents do stand","Hollow upon this plain, so many hollow factions.","When that the general is not like the hive,","To whom the foragers shall all repair,","What honey is expected? Degree being vizarded,","Th'unworthiest shows as fairly in the mask.","The heavens themselves, the planets, and this centre,","Observe degree, priority, and place,","Insisture, course, proportion, season, form,","Office, and custom, in all line of order;","And therefore is the glorious planet Sol","In noble eminence enthron'd and spher'd","Amidst the other, whose med'cinable eye","Corrects the influence of evil planets,","And posts, like the commandment of a king,","Sans check, to good and bad. But when the planets","In evil mixture to disorder wander,","What plagues and what portents, what mutiny,","What raging of the sea, shaking of earth,","Commotion in the winds! Frights, changes, horrors,","Divert and crack, rend and deracinate,","The unity and married calm of states","Quite from their fixture! O, when degree is shak'd,","Which is the ladder of all high designs,","The enterprise is sick! How could communities,","Degrees in schools, and brotherhoods in cities,","Peaceful commerce from dividable shores,","The primogenity and due of birth,","Prerogative of age, crowns, sceptres, laurels,","But by degree stand in authentic place?","Take but degree away, untune that string,","And hark what discord follows! Each thing melts","In mere oppugnancy: the bounded waters","Should lift their bosoms higher than the shores,","And make a sop of all this solid globe;","Strength should be lord of imbecility,","And the rude son should strike his father dead;","Force should be right; or, rather, right and wrong—","Between whose endless jar justice resides—","Should lose their names, and so should justice too.","Then everything includes itself in power,","Power into will, will into appetite;","And appetite, an universal wolf,","So doubly seconded with will and power,","Must make perforce an universal prey,","And last eat up himself. Great Agamemnon,","This chaos, when degree is suffocate,","Follows the choking.","And this neglection of degree it is","That by a pace goes backward, with a purpose","It hath to climb. The general's disdain'd","By him one step below, he by the next,","That next by him beneath; so every step,","Exampl'd by the first pace that is sick","Of his superior, grows to an envious fever","Of pale and bloodless emulation.","And 'tis this fever that keeps Troy on foot,","Not her own sinews. To end a tale of length,","Troy in our weakness stands, not in her strength."],"id":208,"m":"Troy would have fallen by now, still on its foundations, and great Hector's sword would have lost its master, if not for these reasons. The proper order of command has been neglected. Look how many Greek tents stand empty on this plain: there are just as many empty factions. When the general is not like the hive that all the foraging bees return to, what honey can be expected? When rank is disguised, the most unworthy man looks as good as anyone behind the mask. The heavens themselves, the planets, and the earth at the centre keep to rank, priority and place, regularity, course, proportion, season, form, function and custom, all in order. That is why the glorious sun is enthroned in noble majesty among the other planets, and its healing eye corrects the influence of evil planets and moves without hindrance, like a king's command, to good and bad alike. But when the planets wander into disorder in a bad combination, what plagues and omens, what rebellion, what raging of the sea, earthquakes, storms of wind! Terrors, upheavals and horrors twist and crack, tear and uproot the unity and wedded calm of states right off their foundations! Oh, when rank is shaken, which is the ladder to all high undertakings, the whole enterprise falls sick! How could communities, ranks in universities, guilds in cities, peaceful trade between distant shores, the rights of the eldest son, the privileges of age, crowns, sceptres and laurels keep their rightful place except through rank? Just take away rank, put that string out of tune, and listen to the discord that follows! Everything would clash in pure conflict. The seas would rise higher than the shores and turn the whole solid globe into a soggy mess. The strong would rule the weak, and the brutal son would strike his father dead. Might would be right; or rather, right and wrong, between whose endless quarrel justice lives, would lose their names, and so would justice. Then everything would be reduced to power, power to will, and will to appetite. And appetite, a universal wolf, backed up by both will and power, would have to make the whole world its prey, and finally eat itself. Great Agamemnon, this chaos follows the choking of rank. It is this neglect of rank that makes us go backwards when we mean to climb. The general is scorned by the man one step below him, that man by the next, and that one by the man below him. So every step, following the example of the first one that grew sick of its superior, grows into a jealous fever of pale, bloodless rivalry. It is this fever that keeps Troy standing, not her own strength. To finish a long story: Troy stands because of our weakness, not her strength."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Most wisely hath Ulysses here discover'd","The fever whereof all our power is sick."],"id":209,"m":"Ulysses has very wisely uncovered the fever that our whole army is sick with."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["The nature of the sickness found, Ulysses,","What is the remedy?"],"id":210,"m":"Now that you have found what the sickness is, Ulysses, what is the cure?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["The great Achilles, whom opinion crowns","The sinew and the forehand of our host,","Having his ear full of his airy fame,","Grows dainty of his worth, and in his tent","Lies mocking our designs; with him Patroclus","Upon a lazy bed the livelong day","Breaks scurril jests;","And with ridiculous and awkward action—","Which, slanderer, he imitation calls—","He pageants us. Sometime, great Agamemnon,","Thy topless deputation he puts on;","And like a strutting player whose conceit","Lies in his hamstring, and doth think it rich","To hear the wooden dialogue and sound","'Twixt his stretch'd footing and the scaffoldage—","Such to-be-pitied and o'er-wrested seeming","He acts thy greatness in; and when he speaks","'Tis like a chime a-mending; with terms unsquar'd,","Which, from the tongue of roaring Typhon dropp'd,","Would seem hyperboles. At this fusty stuff","The large Achilles, on his press'd bed lolling,","From his deep chest laughs out a loud applause;","Cries 'Excellent! 'Tis Agamemnon right!","Now play me Nestor; hem, and stroke thy beard,","As he being drest to some oration.'","That's done—as near as the extremest ends","Of parallels, as like as Vulcan and his wife;","Yet god Achilles still cries 'Excellent!","'Tis Nestor right. Now play him me, Patroclus,","Arming to answer in a night alarm.'","And then, forsooth, the faint defects of age","Must be the scene of mirth: to cough and spit","And, with a palsy fumbling on his gorget,","Shake in and out the rivet. And at this sport","Sir Valour dies; cries 'O, enough, Patroclus;","Or give me ribs of steel! I shall split all","In pleasure of my spleen.' And in this fashion","All our abilities, gifts, natures, shapes,","Severals and generals of grace exact,","Achievements, plots, orders, preventions,","Excitements to the field or speech for truce,","Success or loss, what is or is not, serves","As stuff for these two to make paradoxes."],"id":211,"m":"The great Achilles, whom public opinion crowns as the muscle and front line of our army, has had his ears filled with his windy fame and has become fussy about his own worth. He lies in his tent mocking our plans. Patroclus lies with him on a lazy bed all day long cracking crude jokes, and with ridiculous, clumsy gestures, which the slanderer calls imitation, he acts us out. Sometimes, great Agamemnon, he puts on your supreme authority, and like a strutting actor whose talent is all in his legs, who thinks it wonderful to hear the wooden echo of his stamping on the stage, he acts your greatness in an over-strained performance that deserves pity. When he speaks, it sounds like a chime being repaired, with crooked words that would seem exaggerated even from the mouth of the roaring giant Typhon. At this stale stuff, the large Achilles, lolling on his crushed bed, lets out loud applause from his deep chest, crying, \"Excellent! That's Agamemnon exactly! Now play Nestor for me. Clear your throat and stroke your beard, like him getting ready to make a speech.\" That is done, as close to the real thing as two parallel lines are to each other, as alike as Vulcan and his wife Venus. Yet god Achilles still cries, \"Excellent! That's Nestor exactly! Now play him for me, Patroclus, arming himself to answer a night attack.\" And then, if you please, the weaknesses of old age become the comic scene: coughing and spitting, and shaking the rivets in and out of his neck armour with trembling fingers. At this game Sir Courage dies laughing and cries, \"Oh, enough, Patroclus, or give me ribs of steel! I'll split my sides laughing.\" And in this way all our abilities, gifts, characters, appearances, our individual and shared qualities, our achievements, plans, orders, precautions, calls to battle or speeches for truce, success or loss, what is and what is not, all become material for these two to make jokes about."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["And in the imitation of these twain—","Who, as Ulysses says, opinion crowns","With an imperial voice—many are infect.","Ajax is grown self-will'd and bears his head","In such a rein, in full as proud a place","As broad Achilles; keeps his tent like him;","Makes factious feasts; rails on our state of war","Bold as an oracle, and sets Thersites,","A slave whose gall coins slanders like a mint,","To match us in comparisons with dirt,","To weaken and discredit our exposure,","How rank soever rounded in with danger."],"id":212,"m":"And many have been infected by copying these two, whom, as Ulysses says, opinion crowns with an emperor's authority. Ajax has become self-willed and holds his head as high and proudly as big Achilles. He keeps to his tent like him, holds rebellious feasts, and criticises our conduct of the war as boldly as an oracle. And he sets Thersites, a slave whose bile turns out slanders like a mint turns out coins, to compare us to dirt, to weaken and discredit our position, however badly we are surrounded by danger."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["They tax our policy and call it cowardice,","Count wisdom as no member of the war,","Forestall prescience, and esteem no act","But that of hand. The still and mental parts","That do contrive how many hands shall strike","When fitness calls them on, and know, by measure","Of their observant toil, the enemies' weight—","Why, this hath not a finger's dignity:","They call this bed-work, mapp'ry, closet-war;","So that the ram that batters down the wall,","For the great swinge and rudeness of his poise,","They place before his hand that made the engine,","Or those that with the fineness of their souls","By reason guide his execution."],"id":213,"m":"They criticise our strategy and call it cowardice. They don't count wisdom as part of the war. They reject foresight and value no action except physical fighting. The quiet thinking that plans how many soldiers should strike when the moment is right, and that works out the enemy's strength through careful observation, isn't worth a finger to them. They call it bed-work, map-making, war in a study. So they rate the battering ram that knocks down the wall, because of the great swing and force of its weight, above the man who made the machine, or above those who guide its use by reason and subtle intelligence."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Let this be granted, and Achilles' horse","Makes many Thetis' sons."],"id":214,"m":"Once we grant this, Achilles' horse is worth many Achilles (sons of Thetis)."},{"t":"sd","text":"Tucket.","id":215},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["What trumpet? Look, Menelaus."],"id":216,"m":"What trumpet is that? Look, Menelaus."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["From Troy."],"id":217,"m":"From Troy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aeneas.","id":218},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["What would you fore our tent?"],"id":219,"m":"What do you want in front of our tent?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Is this great Agamemnon's tent, I pray you?"],"id":220,"m":"Is this great Agamemnon's tent, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Even this."],"id":221,"m":"This very one."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["May one that is a herald and a prince","Do a fair message to his kingly eyes?"],"id":222,"m":"May someone who is both a herald and a prince deliver an honourable message to his royal eyes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["With surety stronger than Achilles' arm","Fore all the Greekish heads, which with one voice","Call Agamemnon head and general."],"id":223,"m":"Yes, with a guarantee of safety stronger than Achilles' arm, in front of all the Greek leaders, who all agree in calling Agamemnon their head and general."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Fair leave and large security. How may","A stranger to those most imperial looks","Know them from eyes of other mortals?"],"id":224,"m":"Generous permission and great safety. How can a stranger to those most imperial looks tell them apart from the eyes of other mortals?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["How?"],"id":225,"m":"What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Ay;","I ask, that I might waken reverence,","And bid the cheek be ready with a blush","Modest as morning when she coldly eyes","The youthful Phoebus.","Which is that god in office, guiding men?","Which is the high and mighty Agamemnon?"],"id":226,"m":"Yes. I ask so that I can stir up my respect and tell my cheeks to be ready with a blush as modest as the dawn when she looks shyly at the young sun god. Which one is that god in power, guiding men? Which one is the high and mighty Agamemnon?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["This Trojan scorns us, or the men of Troy","Are ceremonious courtiers."],"id":227,"m":"This Trojan is mocking us, or else the men of Troy are very formal courtiers."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Courtiers as free, as debonair, unarm'd,","As bending angels; that's their fame in peace.","But when they would seem soldiers, they have galls,","Good arms, strong joints, true swords; and, Jove's accord,","Nothing so full of heart. But peace, Aeneas,","Peace, Trojan; lay thy finger on thy lips.","The worthiness of praise distains his worth,","If that the prais'd himself bring the praise forth;","But what the repining enemy commends,","That breath fame blows; that praise, sole pure, transcends."],"id":228,"m":"Courtiers as open and gracious, when unarmed, as bowing angels; that is their reputation in peace. But when they want to be soldiers, they have spirit, good weapons, strong limbs, true swords, and, with Jove's help, nothing is so full of courage. But quiet, Aeneas, quiet, Trojan; put your finger to your lips. Praise loses its worth if the man praised praises himself. But what an unwilling enemy praises, that is what fame spreads; that praise alone is pure and goes beyond everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Sir, you of Troy, call you yourself Aeneas?"],"id":229,"m":"Sir, from Troy, do you call yourself Aeneas?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Ay, Greek, that is my name."],"id":230,"m":"Yes, Greek, that is my name."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["What's your affairs, I pray you?"],"id":231,"m":"What is your business, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Sir, pardon; 'tis for Agamemnon's ears."],"id":232,"m":"Excuse me, sir, it is for Agamemnon's ears."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["He hears naught privately that comes from Troy."],"id":233,"m":"He hears nothing in private that comes from Troy."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Nor I from Troy come not to whisper with him;","I bring a trumpet to awake his ear,","To set his sense on the attentive bent,","And then to speak."],"id":234,"m":"And I haven't come from Troy to whisper with him. I bring a trumpet to wake his ear and make him pay attention, and then I will speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Speak frankly as the wind;","It is not Agamemnon's sleeping hour.","That thou shalt know, Trojan, he is awake,","He tells thee so himself."],"id":235,"m":"Speak as freely as the wind. It is not Agamemnon's bedtime. So that you know he is awake, Trojan, he tells you so himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Trumpet, blow loud,","Send thy brass voice through all these lazy tents;","And every Greek of mettle, let him know","What Troy means fairly shall be spoke aloud."],"id":236,"m":"Trumpet, blow loud. Send your brass voice through all these lazy tents, and let every Greek with any spirit know that what Troy honourably intends will be spoken aloud."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound trumpet.","id":237},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","cont":1,"lines":["We have, great Agamemnon, here in Troy","A prince called Hector—Priam is his father—","Who in this dull and long-continued truce","Is resty grown; he bade me take a trumpet","And to this purpose speak: Kings, princes, lords!","If there be one among the fair'st of Greece","That holds his honour higher than his ease,","That feeds his praise more than he fears his peril,","That knows his valour and knows not his fear,","That loves his mistress more than in confession","With truant vows to her own lips he loves,","And dare avow her beauty and her worth","In other arms than hers—to him this challenge.","Hector, in view of Trojans and of Greeks,","Shall make it good or do his best to do it:","He hath a lady wiser, fairer, truer,","Than ever Greek did couple in his arms;","And will tomorrow with his trumpet call","Mid-way between your tents and walls of Troy","To rouse a Grecian that is true in love.","If any come, Hector shall honour him;","If none, he'll say in Troy, when he retires,","The Grecian dames are sunburnt and not worth","The splinter of a lance. Even so much."],"id":238,"m":"Great Agamemnon, we have here in Troy a prince called Hector, Priam's son, who has grown restless in this dull, long truce. He told me to bring a trumpeter and say this: \"Kings, princes, lords! If there is one among the best of Greece who values his honour more than his comfort, who seeks praise more than he fears danger, who knows his courage and does not know fear, who loves his lady more than in idle vows spoken to her own lips, and dares to defend her beauty and worth in other arms than hers, to him this challenge. Hector, in sight of Trojans and Greeks, will prove, or do his best to prove, that he has a lady wiser, more beautiful and more faithful than any Greek ever held in his arms. Tomorrow he will call with his trumpet, halfway between your tents and the walls of Troy, to rouse a Greek who is true in love. If anyone comes, Hector will honour him. If no one comes, he will say in Troy, when he goes back, that the Greek ladies are sunburnt and not worth a splinter of a lance.\" That is all."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["This shall be told our lovers, Lord Aeneas.","If none of them have soul in such a kind,","We left them all at home. But we are soldiers;","And may that soldier a mere recreant prove","That means not, hath not, or is not in love.","If then one is, or hath, or means to be,","That one meets Hector; if none else, I am he."],"id":239,"m":"This will be told to our lovers, Lord Aeneas. If none of them has that kind of spirit, we left them all at home. But we are soldiers, and may any soldier prove a total coward who doesn't intend to be, hasn't been, or isn't in love. So if anyone is, or has been, or intends to be, that one will meet Hector. If no one else will, I'll be the one."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Tell him of Nestor, one that was a man","When Hector's grandsire suck'd. He is old now;","But if there be not in our Grecian host","A noble man that hath one spark of fire","To answer for his love, tell him from me","I'll hide my silver beard in a gold beaver,","And in my vambrace put this wither'd brawns,","And meeting him, will tell him that my lady","Was fairer than his grandam, and as chaste","As may be in the world. His youth in flood,","I'll prove this troth with my three drops of blood."],"id":240,"m":"Tell him about Nestor, a man who was grown up when Hector's grandfather was still a baby. He is old now, but if there isn't a single nobleman in our Greek army with one spark of fire to defend his love, tell him from me that I'll hide my silver beard in a gold visor, put these withered arms into armour, and when I meet him tell him that my lady was more beautiful than his grandmother, and as chaste as anyone in the world. Against his youth in full flood I'll prove this truth with my three drops of blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Now heavens forfend such scarcity of youth!"],"id":241,"m":"Heaven forbid there should be such a shortage of young men!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Amen."],"id":242,"m":"Amen."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Fair Lord Aeneas, let me touch your hand;","To our pavilion shall I lead you, sir.","Achilles shall have word of this intent;","So shall each lord of Greece, from tent to tent.","Yourself shall feast with us before you go,","And find the welcome of a noble foe."],"id":243,"m":"Fair Lord Aeneas, let me take your hand. I will lead you to our tent, sir. Achilles will be told of this plan, and so will every lord of Greece, from tent to tent. You will feast with us before you go, and find the welcome a noble enemy deserves."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Ulysses and Nestor.","id":244},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Nestor!"],"id":245,"m":"Nestor!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["What says Ulysses?"],"id":246,"m":"What is it, Ulysses?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["I have a young conception in my brain;","Be you my time to bring it to some shape."],"id":247,"m":"I have a new idea in my brain. Be the time that helps it grow into some shape."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["What is't?"],"id":248,"m":"What is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["This 'tis:","Blunt wedges rive hard knots. The seeded pride","That hath to this maturity blown up","In rank Achilles must or now be cropp'd","Or, shedding, breed a nursery of like evil","To overbulk us all."],"id":249,"m":"This is it: blunt wedges split hard knots. The pride that has been sown and has grown to full ripeness in swollen Achilles must either be cut down now or, scattering its seeds, breed a crop of similar evil that will overgrow us all."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Well, and how?"],"id":250,"m":"Well, and how?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["This challenge that the gallant Hector sends,","However it is spread in general name,","Relates in purpose only to Achilles."],"id":251,"m":"This challenge that brave Hector sends, however widely it is addressed, is really aimed only at Achilles."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["True. The purpose is perspicuous even as substance","Whose grossness little characters sum up;","And, in the publication, make no strain","But that Achilles, were his brain as barren","As banks of Libya—though, Apollo knows,","'Tis dry enough—will with great speed of judgement,","Ay, with celerity, find Hector's purpose","Pointing on him."],"id":252,"m":"True. The aim is as clear as a total made up of small numbers. When it is announced, there is no doubt that Achilles, even if his brain were as barren as the shores of Libya (and Apollo knows it's dry enough), will very quickly, yes, at once, see that Hector's challenge is aimed at him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["And wake him to the answer, think you?"],"id":253,"m":"And you think it will rouse him to answer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Why, 'tis most meet. Who may you else oppose","That can from Hector bring those honours off,","If not Achilles? Though 't be a sportful combat,","Yet in this trial much opinion dwells","For here the Trojans taste our dear'st repute","With their fin'st palate; and trust to me, Ulysses,","Our imputation shall be oddly pois'd","In this vile action; for the success,","Although particular, shall give a scantling","Of good or bad unto the general;","And in such indexes, although small pricks","To their subsequent volumes, there is seen","The baby figure of the giant mass","Of things to come at large. It is suppos'd","He that meets Hector issues from our choice;","And choice, being mutual act of all our souls,","Makes merit her election, and doth boil,","As 'twere from forth us all, a man distill'd","Out of our virtues; who miscarrying,","What heart receives from hence a conquering part,","To steel a strong opinion to themselves?","Which entertain'd, limbs are his instruments,","In no less working than are swords and bows","Directive by the limbs."],"id":254,"m":"Why, that would be most fitting. Who else could you put up against him who could come away from Hector with honour, if not Achilles? Although it is a friendly fight, a lot of reputation depends on this trial. Here the Trojans will taste our dearest reputation with their finest palate, and believe me, Ulysses, our standing will be strangely balanced on this petty action, because its outcome, though only a single fight, will give a sample of good or bad for the whole army. And in such indexes, though they are small marks compared with the volumes that follow, you can see a baby version of the giant mass of things to come. People will assume that the man who meets Hector is our chosen one, and choice, being the agreed act of all our souls, picks by merit and boils out of us all, as it were, one man distilled from our virtues. If he fails, what encouragement will the winning side take from that to strengthen their opinion of themselves? Once they believe that, their limbs become its tools, just as swords and bows are directed by limbs."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Give pardon to my speech. Therefore 'tis meet","Achilles meet not Hector. Let us, like merchants,","First show foul wares, and think perchance they'll sell;","If not, the lustre of the better shall exceed","By showing the worse first. Do not consent","That ever Hector and Achilles meet;","For both our honour and our shame in this","Are dogg'd with two strange followers."],"id":255,"m":"Excuse me for saying so, but that is why Achilles must not meet Hector. Let's be like merchants: show the shoddy goods first and see if they might sell. If not, the shine of the better goods will look even brighter after showing the worse. Don't agree to Hector and Achilles ever meeting, because both our honour and our shame in this are followed by two strange companions."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["I see them not with my old eyes. What are they?"],"id":256,"m":"I can't see them with my old eyes. What are they?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["What glory our Achilles shares from Hector,","Were he not proud, we all should share with him;","But he already is too insolent;","And it were better parch in Afric sun","Than in the pride and salt scorn of his eyes,","Should he scape Hector fair. If he were foil'd,","Why, then we do our main opinion crush","In taint of our best man. No, make a lott'ry;","And, by device, let blockish Ajax draw","The sort to fight with Hector. Among ourselves","Give him allowance for the better man;","For that will physic the great Myrmidon,","Who broils in loud applause, and make him fall","His crest, that prouder than blue Iris bends.","If the dull brainless Ajax come safe off,","We'll dress him up in voices; if he fail,","Yet go we under our opinion still","That we have better men. But, hit or miss,","Our project's life this shape of sense assumes—","Ajax employ'd plucks down Achilles' plumes."],"id":257,"m":"Whatever glory our Achilles wins from Hector, we would all share with him if he weren't proud. But he is already too arrogant, and it would be better to be scorched in the African sun than in the pride and bitter scorn of his eyes if he came away from Hector well. If he were beaten, then we would crush our main reputation by disgracing our best man. No, let's hold a lottery, and rig it so that thick-headed Ajax draws the lot to fight Hector. Among ourselves, let's treat him as the better man, because that will be good medicine for the great leader of the Myrmidons, who basks in loud applause, and make him lower his crest, which curves more proudly than the rainbow. If the dull, brainless Ajax comes off safely, we'll dress him up in praise. If he fails, we can still keep our reputation that we have better men. But hit or miss, the life of our plan comes down to this: using Ajax will pluck Achilles' feathers."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Now, Ulysses, I begin to relish thy advice;","And I will give a taste thereof forthwith","To Agamemnon. Go we to him straight.","Two curs shall tame each other: pride alone","Must tarre the mastiffs on, as 'twere their bone."],"id":258,"m":"Now, Ulysses, I begin to like the taste of your advice, and I'll give Agamemnon a taste of it at once. Let's go to him straight away. Two dogs will tame each other. Pride alone must set the mastiffs fighting, as if it were their bone."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":259},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"The Grecian camp.","id":260},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ajax and Thersites.","id":261},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Thersites!"],"id":262,"m":"Thersites!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Agamemnon—how if he had boils, full, all over, generally?"],"id":263,"m":"Agamemnon: what if he had boils, full ones, all over him, everywhere?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Thersites!"],"id":264,"m":"Thersites!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["And those boils did run—say so. Did not the general run then? Were not that a botchy core?"],"id":265,"m":"And what if those boils ran? Suppose they did. Wouldn't the general be running then? Wouldn't that be a pus-filled sore?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Dog!"],"id":266,"m":"Dog!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Then there would come some matter from him;","I see none now."],"id":267,"m":"Then some matter (pus, or sense) would come out of him. I see none now."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Thou bitch-wolf's son, canst thou not hear? Feel, then."],"id":268,"m":"You son of a she-wolf, can't you hear? Then feel."},{"t":"sd","text":"Strikes him.","id":269},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["The plague of Greece upon thee, thou mongrel beef-witted lord!"],"id":270,"m":"May the plague of Greece fall on you, you mongrel lord with a brain of beef!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Speak, then, thou unsalted leaven, speak. I will beat thee into handsomeness."],"id":271,"m":"Speak, then, you stale lump of dough, speak. I'll beat you into good looks."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["I shall sooner rail thee into wit and holiness; but I think thy horse will sooner con an oration than thou learn a prayer without book. Thou canst strike, canst thou? A red murrain o' thy jade's tricks!"],"id":272,"m":"I'll sooner nag you into wisdom and holiness. But I think your horse will learn a speech by heart sooner than you will learn a prayer without reading it. You can hit, can you? A red plague on your nag's tricks!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Toadstool, learn me the proclamation."],"id":273,"m":"Toadstool, find out the proclamation for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Dost thou think I have no sense, thou strikest me thus?"],"id":274,"m":"Do you think I have no feeling, that you hit me like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["The proclamation!"],"id":275,"m":"The proclamation!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Thou art proclaim'd fool, I think."],"id":276,"m":"You've been proclaimed a fool, I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Do not, porpentine, do not; my fingers itch."],"id":277,"m":"Don't, porcupine, don't. My fingers are itching."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["I would thou didst itch from head to foot and I had the scratching of thee; I would make thee the loathsomest scab in Greece. When thou art forth in the incursions, thou strikest as slow as another."],"id":278,"m":"I wish you itched from head to foot and I had the job of scratching you. I'd make you the most disgusting scab in Greece. When you're out on raids, you strike as slowly as anyone else."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["I say, the proclamation."],"id":279,"m":"I said, the proclamation."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Thou grumblest and railest every hour on Achilles; and thou art as full of envy at his greatness as Cerberus is at Proserpina's beauty—ay, that thou bark'st at him."],"id":280,"m":"You grumble and rant about Achilles every hour, and you are as full of envy of his greatness as Cerberus is of Proserpina's beauty. Yes, that's why you bark at him."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Mistress Thersites!"],"id":281,"m":"Mistress Thersites!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Thou shouldst strike him."],"id":282,"m":"You should hit him."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Cobloaf!"],"id":283,"m":"Bread-loaf!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["He would pun thee into shivers with his fist, as a sailor breaks a biscuit."],"id":284,"m":"He'd pound you to bits with his fist, the way a sailor breaks a biscuit."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["You whoreson cur!"],"id":285,"m":"You bastard dog!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Strikes him.","id":286},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Do, do."],"id":287,"m":"Go on, go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Thou stool for a witch!"],"id":288,"m":"You seat for a witch!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Ay, do, do; thou sodden-witted lord! Thou hast no more brain than I have in mine elbows; an asinico may tutor thee. You scurvy valiant ass! Thou art here but to thrash Trojans, and thou art bought and sold among those of any wit like a barbarian slave. If thou use to beat me, I will begin at thy heel and tell what thou art by inches, thou thing of no bowels, thou!"],"id":289,"m":"Yes, go on, go on, you soggy-brained lord! You have no more brain than I have in my elbows; a little donkey could teach you. You scabby, brave donkey! You're only here to beat Trojans, and anyone with any sense buys and sells you like a foreign slave. If you make a habit of beating me, I'll start at your heel and say what you are inch by inch, you heartless thing, you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["You dog!"],"id":290,"m":"You dog!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["You scurvy lord!"],"id":291,"m":"You scabby lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["You cur!"],"id":292,"m":"You cur!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Strikes him.","id":293},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Mars his idiot! Do, rudeness; do, camel; do, do."],"id":294,"m":"Mars's idiot! Go on, brute; go on, camel; go on, go on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Achilles and Patroclus.","id":295},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Why, how now, Ajax! Wherefore do ye thus?","How now, Thersites! What's the matter, man?"],"id":296,"m":"Why, what's this, Ajax? Why are you doing this? What's going on, Thersites? What's the matter, man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["You see him there, do you?"],"id":297,"m":"You see him there, do you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Ay; what's the matter?"],"id":298,"m":"Yes. What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Nay, look upon him."],"id":299,"m":"No, look at him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["So I do. What's the matter?"],"id":300,"m":"I am. What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Nay, but regard him well."],"id":301,"m":"No, but look at him carefully."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Well! why, so I do."],"id":302,"m":"Carefully! Why, I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["But yet you look not well upon him; for whosomever you take him to be, he is Ajax."],"id":303,"m":"But you're still not looking at him properly, because whatever you take him for, he is Ajax (a jakes, a toilet)."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["I know that, fool."],"id":304,"m":"I know that, fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Ay, but that fool knows not himself."],"id":305,"m":"Yes, but that fool doesn't know himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Therefore I beat thee."],"id":306,"m":"That's why I'm beating you."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Lo, lo, lo, lo, what modicums of wit he utters! His evasions have ears thus long. I have bobb'd his brain more than he has beat my bones. I will buy nine sparrows for a penny, and his pia mater is not worth the ninth part of a sparrow. This lord, Achilles—Ajax, who wears his wit in his belly and his guts in his head—I'll tell you what I say of him."],"id":307,"m":"Look, look, look, look, what tiny scraps of wit he comes out with! His answers have donkey's ears this long. I've hit his brain more than he's beaten my bones. I can buy nine sparrows for a penny, and his brain isn't worth a ninth of a sparrow. This lord, Achilles, Ajax, who wears his brains in his belly and his guts in his head, I'll tell you what I say about him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["What?"],"id":308,"m":"What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["I say this Ajax—"],"id":309,"m":"I say this Ajax..."},{"t":"sd","text":"Ajax offers to strike him.","id":310},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Nay, good Ajax."],"id":311,"m":"No, good Ajax."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Has not so much wit—"],"id":312,"m":"...hasn't enough brains..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Nay, I must hold you."],"id":313,"m":"No, I have to hold you back."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["As will stop the eye of Helen's needle, for whom he comes to fight."],"id":314,"m":"...to fill the eye of Helen's needle, and she's the one he's come to fight for."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Peace, fool."],"id":315,"m":"Quiet, fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["I would have peace and quietness, but the fool will not— he there; that he; look you there."],"id":316,"m":"I'd like peace and quiet, but the fool won't let me: him there, that one, look there."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["O thou damned cur! I shall—"],"id":317,"m":"Oh you damned dog! I'll..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Will you set your wit to a fool's?"],"id":318,"m":"Will you match your wits against a fool's?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["No, I warrant you, the fool's will shame it."],"id":319,"m":"No, I promise you, the fool's will put his to shame."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Good words, Thersites."],"id":320,"m":"Watch your language, Thersites."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["What's the quarrel?"],"id":321,"m":"What's the quarrel about?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["I bade the vile owl go learn me the tenour of the proclamation, and he rails upon me."],"id":322,"m":"I told the nasty owl to go and find out what the proclamation said, and he insults me."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["I serve thee not."],"id":323,"m":"I'm not your servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Well, go to, go to."],"id":324,"m":"Well, come on, come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["I serve here voluntary."],"id":325,"m":"I serve here as a volunteer."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Your last service was suff'rance; 'twas not voluntary. No man is beaten voluntary. Ajax was here the voluntary, and you as under an impress."],"id":326,"m":"Your last service was putting up with a beating; that wasn't voluntary. No one gets beaten voluntarily. Ajax was the volunteer here, and you were the one press-ganged."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["E'en so; a great deal of your wit too lies in your sinews, or else there be liars. Hector shall have a great catch and knock out either of your brains: a' were as good crack a fusty nut with no kernel."],"id":327,"m":"Just so. A lot of your brains are in your muscles too, or else people are liars. Hector will have a great catch if he knocks out either of your brains: he might as well crack a mouldy nut with no kernel."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["What, with me too, Thersites?"],"id":328,"m":"What, me too, Thersites?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["There's Ulysses and old Nestor—whose wit was mouldy ere your grandsires had nails on their toes—yoke you like draught oxen, and make you plough up the wars."],"id":329,"m":"There's Ulysses and old Nestor, whose wits were mouldy before your grandfathers had toenails, yoking you like oxen and making you plough the wars."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["What, what?"],"id":330,"m":"What, what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Yes, good sooth. To Achilles, to Ajax, to—"],"id":331,"m":"Yes, truly. To Achilles, to Ajax, to..."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["I shall cut out your tongue."],"id":332,"m":"I'll cut your tongue out."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["'Tis no matter; I shall speak as much as thou afterwards."],"id":333,"m":"It doesn't matter. I'll speak as much as you do afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["No more words, Thersites; peace!"],"id":334,"m":"No more words, Thersites. Quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["I will hold my peace when Achilles' brach bids me, shall I?"],"id":335,"m":"I'm supposed to keep quiet when Achilles' bitch-hound tells me to, am I?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["There's for you, Patroclus."],"id":336,"m":"That's one for you, Patroclus."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["I will see you hang'd like clotpoles ere I come any more to your tents. I will keep where there is wit stirring, and leave the faction of fools."],"id":337,"m":"I'll see you hanged like blockheads before I come to your tents again. I'll stay where there's some wit stirring and leave the party of fools."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":338},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["A good riddance."],"id":339,"m":"Good riddance."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Marry, this, sir, is proclaim'd through all our host,","That Hector, by the fifth hour of the sun,","Will with a trumpet 'twixt our tents and Troy,","Tomorrow morning, call some knight to arms","That hath a stomach; and such a one that dare","Maintain I know not what; 'tis trash. Farewell."],"id":340,"m":"Well, sir, this is what has been proclaimed through our whole army: that Hector, at the fifth hour after sunrise tomorrow morning, will call out with a trumpet, between our tents and Troy, for some knight with the appetite for it to fight him, someone who dares to maintain I don't know what. It's rubbish. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Farewell. Who shall answer him?"],"id":341,"m":"Goodbye. Who will answer him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["I know not; 'tis put to lott'ry, otherwise,","He knew his man."],"id":342,"m":"I don't know. It's to be decided by lottery. Otherwise he would know his man."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["O, meaning you? I will go learn more of it."],"id":343,"m":"Oh, meaning you? I'll go and find out more about it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":344},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Troy. Priam's palace.","id":345},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Priam, Hector, Troilus, Paris and Helenus.","id":346},{"t":"sp","who":"PRIAM","lines":["After so many hours, lives, speeches spent,","Thus once again says Nestor from the Greeks:","'Deliver Helen, and all damage else—","As honour, loss of time, travail, expense,","Wounds, friends, and what else dear that is consum'd","In hot digestion of this cormorant war—","Shall be struck off.' Hector, what say you to't?"],"id":347,"m":"After so many hours, lives and speeches spent, Nestor sends this message once again from the Greeks: \"Hand over Helen, and all other losses, such as honour, lost time, effort, expense, wounds, friends, and everything else precious that has been consumed in the hot stomach of this greedy war, will be cancelled.\" Hector, what do you say to that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Though no man lesser fears the Greeks than I,","As far as toucheth my particular,","Yet, dread Priam,","There is no lady of more softer bowels,","More spongy to suck in the sense of fear,","More ready to cry out 'Who knows what follows?'","Than Hector is. The wound of peace is surety,","Surety secure; but modest doubt is call'd","The beacon of the wise, the tent that searches","To th' bottom of the worst. Let Helen go.","Since the first sword was drawn about this question,","Every tithe soul 'mongst many thousand dismes","Hath been as dear as Helen—I mean, of ours.","If we have lost so many tenths of ours","To guard a thing not ours, nor worth to us,","Had it our name, the value of one ten,","What merit's in that reason which denies","The yielding of her up?"],"id":348,"m":"Although no man fears the Greeks less than I do, as far as I personally am concerned, still, revered Priam, there is no lady with a softer heart, more ready to soak up fear, more ready to cry out \"Who knows what will happen next?\" than Hector. The danger of peace is overconfidence, careless overconfidence. But reasonable doubt is called the beacon of the wise, the probe that searches the worst of a wound to the bottom. Let Helen go. Since the first sword was drawn over this question, every tenth soul among many thousands of tenths has been as precious as Helen; I mean, of our people. If we have lost so many tenths of ours to keep something that is not ours, and that would not be worth one of those tenths even if it were ours, what sense is there in the argument that refuses to give her up?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Fie, fie, my brother!","Weigh you the worth and honour of a king,","So great as our dread father's, in a scale","Of common ounces? Will you with counters sum","The past-proportion of his infinite,","And buckle in a waist most fathomless","With spans and inches so diminutive","As fears and reasons? Fie, for godly shame!"],"id":349,"m":"Shame, shame, my brother! Do you weigh the worth and honour of a king as great as our revered father on scales meant for ordinary ounces? Will you add up his immeasurable greatness with counting tokens, and fasten a belt around a waist beyond measuring with spans and inches as tiny as fears and reasons? Shame on you, for God's sake!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELENUS","lines":["No marvel though you bite so sharp of reasons,","You are so empty of them. Should not our father","Bear the great sway of his affairs with reason,","Because your speech hath none that tells him so?"],"id":350,"m":"No wonder you snap so sharply at reasons, you're so short of them. Should our father not govern his affairs with reason, just because your speech has no reason to tell him so?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["You are for dreams and slumbers, brother priest;","You fur your gloves with reason. Here are your reasons:","You know an enemy intends you harm;","You know a sword employ'd is perilous,","And reason flies the object of all harm.","Who marvels, then, when Helenus beholds","A Grecian and his sword, if he do set","The very wings of reason to his heels","And fly like chidden Mercury from Jove,","Or like a star disorb'd? Nay, if we talk of reason,","Let's shut our gates and sleep. Manhood and honour","Should have hare hearts, would they but fat their thoughts","With this cramm'd reason. Reason and respect","Make livers pale and lustihood deject."],"id":351,"m":"You are for dreams and sleeping, brother priest. You line your gloves with reason. Here are your reasons: you know an enemy means to harm you; you know a sword in use is dangerous; and reason runs from anything that can harm. So who is surprised when Helenus, seeing a Greek with his sword, fixes the wings of reason to his heels and flies off like Mercury scolded by Jove, or like a star knocked out of its orbit? No, if we're going to talk about reason, let's shut our gates and go to sleep. Manhood and honour would have the hearts of hares if they fed their thoughts on this stuffed reason. Reason and caution turn the liver pale and make vigour lose heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Brother, she is not worth what she doth cost the keeping."],"id":352,"m":"Brother, she isn't worth what it costs to keep her."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["What's aught but as 'tis valued?"],"id":353,"m":"What is anything worth, except what it is valued at?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["But value dwells not in particular will:","It holds his estimate and dignity","As well wherein 'tis precious of itself","As in the prizer. 'Tis mad idolatry","To make the service greater than the god,","And the will dotes that is attributive","To what infectiously itself affects,","Without some image of th'affected merit."],"id":354,"m":"But value doesn't depend on one person's will. A thing has its worth and dignity as much in being precious in itself as in the person who prizes it. It is mad idolatry to make the worship greater than the god, and a will is foolish if it gives value to whatever it is attracted to, without any sign of real merit in the thing it desires."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["I take today a wife, and my election","Is led on in the conduct of my will;","My will enkindled by mine eyes and ears,","Two traded pilots 'twixt the dangerous shores","Of will and judgement: how may I avoid,","Although my will distaste what it elected,","The wife I chose? There can be no evasion","To blench from this and to stand firm by honour.","We turn not back the silks upon the merchant","When we have soil'd them; nor the remainder viands","We do not throw in unrespective sieve,","Because we now are full. It was thought meet","Paris should do some vengeance on the Greeks;","Your breath with full consent bellied his sails;","The seas and winds, old wranglers, took a truce,","And did him service. He touch'd the ports desir'd;","And for an old aunt whom the Greeks held captive","He brought a Grecian queen, whose youth and freshness","Wrinkles Apollo's, and makes stale the morning.","Why keep we her? The Grecians keep our aunt.","Is she worth keeping? Why, she is a pearl","Whose price hath launch'd above a thousand ships,","And turn'd crown'd kings to merchants.","If you'll avouch 'twas wisdom Paris went—","As you must needs, for you all cried 'Go, go'—","If you'll confess he brought home worthy prize—","As you must needs, for you all clapp'd your hands,","And cried 'Inestimable!'—why do you now","The issue of your proper wisdoms rate,","And do a deed that never Fortune did—","Beggar the estimation which you priz'd","Richer than sea and land? O theft most base,","That we have stol'n what we do fear to keep!","But thieves unworthy of a thing so stol'n","That in their country did them that disgrace","We fear to warrant in our native place!"],"id":355,"m":"Suppose I take a wife today, and my choice is guided by my desire; my desire set on fire by my eyes and ears, two experienced pilots steering between the dangerous shores of desire and judgement. How could I get rid of the wife I chose, even if my desire came to dislike what it had chosen? There is no way to back out of it and still stand firm in honour. We don't give silks back to the merchant once we've dirtied them, and we don't throw leftover food into the slop bucket just because we're now full. It was thought right that Paris should take some revenge on the Greeks. Your breath, all in agreement, filled his sails. The seas and winds, old quarrellers, made a truce and helped him on his way. He reached the ports he wanted, and in exchange for an old aunt whom the Greeks held captive, he brought back a Greek queen whose youth and freshness make Apollo look wrinkled and the morning look stale. Why do we keep her? The Greeks keep our aunt. Is she worth keeping? Why, she is a pearl whose price has launched more than a thousand ships and turned crowned kings into merchants. If you agree it was wise for Paris to go (as you must, because you all cried \"Go, go\"), if you admit he brought home a worthy prize (as you must, because you all clapped your hands and cried \"Priceless!\"), why do you now criticise the result of your own wisdom, and do something Fortune never did, reduce to nothing the value of what you rated richer than sea and land? Oh, what a shameful theft, to have stolen something we are afraid to keep! We would be thieves unworthy of what we stole, if we disgraced the Greeks in their own country and are now afraid to defend it in our own."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["[Within] Cry, Trojans, cry."],"id":356,"m":"(Offstage) Weep, Trojans, weep."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRIAM","lines":["What noise, what shriek is this?"],"id":357,"m":"What noise, what scream is that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["'Tis our mad sister; I do know her voice."],"id":358,"m":"It's our mad sister. I know her voice."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["[Within] Cry, Trojans."],"id":359,"m":"(Offstage) Weep, Trojans."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["It is Cassandra."],"id":360,"m":"It's Cassandra."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cassandra, raving.","id":361},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["Cry, Trojans, cry. Lend me ten thousand eyes,","And I will fill them with prophetic tears."],"id":362,"m":"Weep, Trojans, weep. Lend me ten thousand eyes, and I will fill them with prophetic tears."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Peace, sister, peace."],"id":363,"m":"Quiet, sister, quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["Virgins and boys, mid-age and wrinkled eld,","Soft infancy, that nothing canst but cry,","Add to my clamours. Let us pay betimes","A moiety of that mass of moan to come.","Cry, Trojans, cry. Practise your eyes with tears.","Troy must not be, nor goodly Ilion stand;","Our firebrand brother, Paris, burns us all.","Cry, Trojans, cry, A Helen and a woe!","Cry, cry. Troy burns, or else let Helen go."],"id":364,"m":"Virgins and boys, the middle-aged and the wrinkled old, tender infants who can do nothing but cry, add to my wailing. Let's pay in advance part of the huge debt of grief to come. Weep, Trojans, weep. Train your eyes to cry. Troy will not survive, and splendid Ilium will not stand. Our brother Paris, the firebrand, is burning us all. Weep, Trojans, weep: Helen, and grief! Weep, weep. Troy burns, or else let Helen go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":365},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Now, youthful Troilus, do not these high strains","Of divination in our sister work","Some touches of remorse? Or is your blood","So madly hot, that no discourse of reason,","Nor fear of bad success in a bad cause,","Can qualify the same?"],"id":366,"m":"Now, young Troilus, don't these lofty prophecies from our sister stir some feeling of remorse in you? Or is your blood so madly hot that no reasoned argument, and no fear of a bad outcome in a bad cause, can cool it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Why, brother Hector,","We may not think the justness of each act","Such and no other than event doth form it;","Nor once deject the courage of our minds","Because Cassandra's mad. Her brain-sick raptures","Cannot distaste the goodness of a quarrel","Which hath our several honours all engag'd","To make it gracious. For my private part,","I am no more touch'd than all Priam's sons;","And Jove forbid there should be done amongst us","Such things as might offend the weakest spleen","To fight for and maintain."],"id":367,"m":"Why, brother Hector, we shouldn't judge whether an action is right only by how it turns out, nor should we lose heart because Cassandra is mad. Her brain-sick fits can't spoil the goodness of a quarrel that all our honours are committed to making honourable. For my own part, I am no more involved than any of Priam's sons, and Jove forbid that we should do anything that even the weakest temper would find offensive to fight for and defend."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["Else might the world convince of levity","As well my undertakings as your counsels;","But I attest the gods, your full consent","Gave wings to my propension, and cut off","All fears attending on so dire a project.","For what, alas, can these my single arms?","What propugnation is in one man's valour","To stand the push and enmity of those","This quarrel would excite? Yet I protest,","Were I alone to pass the difficulties,","And had as ample power as I have will,","Paris should ne'er retract what he hath done,","Nor faint in the pursuit."],"id":368,"m":"Otherwise the world might accuse both my actions and your advice of being frivolous. But I call the gods to witness, your full agreement gave wings to my inclination and cut off all the fears that came with such a dangerous project. For what, alas, can my two arms do on their own? What defence is there in one man's courage to withstand the attack and hostility that this quarrel would stir up? Yet I swear, if I had to face the difficulties alone, and had as much power as I have will, Paris would never take back what he has done, or lose heart in following it through."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRIAM","lines":["Paris, you speak","Like one besotted on your sweet delights.","You have the honey still, but these the gall;","So to be valiant is no praise at all."],"id":369,"m":"Paris, you speak like someone infatuated with your own sweet pleasures. You still have the honey, but these others have the bitterness, so there's no credit at all in being brave like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["Sir, I propose not merely to myself","The pleasures such a beauty brings with it;","But I would have the soil of her fair rape","Wip'd off in honourable keeping her.","What treason were it to the ransack'd queen,","Disgrace to your great worths, and shame to me,","Now to deliver her possession up","On terms of base compulsion! Can it be,","That so degenerate a strain as this","Should once set footing in your generous bosoms?","There's not the meanest spirit on our party","Without a heart to dare or sword to draw","When Helen is defended; nor none so noble","Whose life were ill bestow'd or death unfam'd,","Where Helen is the subject. Then, I say,","Well may we fight for her whom we know well","The world's large spaces cannot parallel."],"id":370,"m":"Sir, I am not thinking only of the pleasures such a beauty brings with her. I want the stain of her abduction wiped away by keeping her honourably. What treason it would be to the stolen queen, what a disgrace to your great honour and what a shame to me, to hand her over now on terms forced on us so humiliatingly! Can such a degenerate idea ever find a foothold in your noble hearts? There isn't the lowest spirit on our side who lacks the heart to dare or the sword to draw when Helen is being defended, and no one so noble that his life would be wasted or his death unfamous when Helen is the cause. So I say we may well fight for her, since we know the whole wide world has no equal to her."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Paris and Troilus, you have both said well;","And on the cause and question now in hand","Have gloz'd, but superficially; not much","Unlike young men, whom Aristotle thought","Unfit to hear moral philosophy.","The reasons you allege do more conduce","To the hot passion of distemp'red blood","Than to make up a free determination","'Twixt right and wrong; for pleasure and revenge","Have ears more deaf than adders to the voice","Of any true decision. Nature craves","All dues be rend'red to their owners. Now,","What nearer debt in all humanity","Than wife is to the husband? If this law","Of nature be corrupted through affection;","And that great minds, of partial indulgence","To their benumbed wills, resist the same;","There is a law in each well-order'd nation","To curb those raging appetites that are","Most disobedient and refractory.","If Helen, then, be wife to Sparta's king—","As it is known she is—these moral laws","Of nature and of nations speak aloud","To have her back return'd. Thus to persist","In doing wrong extenuates not wrong,","But makes it much more heavy. Hector's opinion","Is this, in way of truth. Yet, ne'ertheless,","My spritely brethren, I propend to you","In resolution to keep Helen still;","For 'tis a cause that hath no mean dependence","Upon our joint and several dignities."],"id":371,"m":"Paris and Troilus, you have both spoken well, and you have commented on the cause and question we are discussing, but only superficially, not unlike the young men whom Aristotle thought unfit to hear moral philosophy. The reasons you give have more to do with the hot passion of disordered blood than with reaching a free judgement between right and wrong, because pleasure and revenge have ears deafer than adders to the voice of any true decision. Nature demands that everything owed be given back to its owner. Now, what closer debt is there in all humanity than a wife to her husband? If this law of nature is corrupted by passion, and great men resist it out of indulgence to their own numbed wills, there is a law in every well-ordered nation to curb those raging appetites that are most disobedient and stubborn. So if Helen is the wife of the king of Sparta, as everyone knows she is, these moral laws of nature and of nations loudly demand that she be returned. To keep on doing wrong does not lessen the wrong, but makes it much heavier. That is Hector's opinion, as a matter of truth. But nevertheless, my spirited brothers, I lean towards your decision to keep Helen, because it is a cause on which our honour, together and individually, depends heavily."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Why, there you touch'd the life of our design.","Were it not glory that we more affected","Than the performance of our heaving spleens,","I would not wish a drop of Trojan blood","Spent more in her defence. But, worthy Hector,","She is a theme of honour and renown,","A spur to valiant and magnanimous deeds,","Whose present courage may beat down our foes,","And fame in time to come canonize us;","For I presume brave Hector would not lose","So rich advantage of a promis'd glory","As smiles upon the forehead of this action","For the wide world's revenue."],"id":372,"m":"Why, now you've touched the heart of our purpose. If it weren't glory that we cared about more than giving vent to our angry tempers, I wouldn't want a single drop more Trojan blood spent defending her. But, worthy Hector, she is a cause of honour and fame, a spur to brave and noble deeds, whose courage now may beat down our enemies and whose fame in times to come may make saints of us. For I assume brave Hector would not give up such a rich chance of promised glory as smiles on the face of this enterprise, not for all the wealth of the wide world."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["I am yours,","You valiant offspring of great Priamus.","I have a roisting challenge sent amongst","The dull and factious nobles of the Greeks","Will strike amazement to their drowsy spirits.","I was advertis'd their great general slept,","Whilst emulation in the army crept.","This, I presume, will wake him."],"id":373,"m":"I'm with you, you brave sons of great Priam. I have sent a rousing challenge among the dull, quarrelling Greek nobles that will shock their drowsy spirits. I was told their great general was asleep while rivalry crept through the army. This, I think, will wake him up."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":374},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"The Grecian camp. Before the tent of Achilles.","id":375},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Thersites, solus.","id":376},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["How now, Thersites! What, lost in the labyrinth of thy fury? Shall the elephant Ajax carry it thus? He beats me, and I rail at him. O worthy satisfaction! Would it were otherwise: that I could beat him, whilst he rail'd at me! 'Sfoot, I'll learn to conjure and raise devils, but I'll see some issue of my spiteful execrations. Then there's Achilles, a rare engineer! If Troy be not taken till these two undermine it, the walls will stand till they fall of themselves. O thou great thunder-darter of Olympus, forget that thou art Jove, the king of gods, and, Mercury, lose all the serpentine craft of thy caduceus, if ye take not that little little less than little wit from them that they have! which short-arm'd ignorance itself knows is so abundant scarce, it will not in circumvention deliver a fly from a spider without drawing their massy irons and cutting the web. After this, the vengeance on the whole camp! or, rather, the Neapolitan bone-ache! for that, methinks, is the curse depending on those that war for a placket. I have said my prayers; and devil Envy say 'Amen.' What ho! my Lord Achilles!"],"id":377,"m":"Well, Thersites! What, lost in the maze of your fury? Is the elephant Ajax going to get away with it like this? He beats me, and I insult him. Oh, what fine satisfaction! I wish it were the other way round: that I could beat him while he insulted me. By God's foot, I'll learn to do magic and raise devils, but I'll see some result from my spiteful curses. Then there's Achilles, a fine engineer! If Troy isn't taken until these two undermine it, the walls will stand until they fall down by themselves. Oh, you great thunderbolt-thrower of Olympus, forget that you are Jove, the king of gods, and Mercury, lose all the snaky cunning of your staff, if you don't take away the little, little, less than little wit that they have! Even short-armed ignorance knows it is so scarce that it couldn't rescue a fly from a spider without drawing their heavy swords and cutting the web. After that, vengeance on the whole camp! Or rather, syphilis! Because that, I think, is the curse hanging over those who fight a war for a petticoat. I've said my prayers, and let the devil Envy say \"Amen.\" Hey there! My Lord Achilles!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Patroclus.","id":378},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Who's there? Thersites! Good Thersites, come in and rail."],"id":379,"m":"Who's there? Thersites! Good Thersites, come in and rant."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["If I could a' rememb'red a gilt counterfeit, thou wouldst not have slipp'd out of my contemplation; but it is no matter; thyself upon thyself! The common curse of mankind, folly and ignorance, be thine in great revenue! Heaven bless thee from a tutor, and discipline come not near thee! Let thy blood be thy direction till thy death. Then if she that lays thee out says thou art a fair corse, I'll be sworn and sworn upon't she never shrouded any but lazars. Amen. Where's Achilles?"],"id":380,"m":"If I had remembered a gilded fake coin, you wouldn't have slipped out of my thoughts. But it doesn't matter: may you get what you are! The common curse of mankind, folly and ignorance, be yours in great quantity! Heaven keep you from a teacher, and may education never come near you! Let your passions guide you until you die. Then, if the woman who lays you out says you're a good-looking corpse, I'll swear and swear again that she has never wrapped any bodies but lepers'. Amen. Where's Achilles?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["What, art thou devout? Wast thou in prayer?"],"id":381,"m":"What, are you religious? Were you praying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Ay, the heavens hear me!"],"id":382,"m":"Yes, may the heavens hear me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Amen."],"id":383,"m":"Amen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Achilles.","id":384},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Who's there?"],"id":385,"m":"Who's there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Thersites, my lord."],"id":386,"m":"Thersites, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Where, where? O, where? Art thou come? Why, my cheese, my digestion, why hast thou not served thyself in to my table so many meals? Come, what's Agamemnon?"],"id":387,"m":"Where, where? Oh, where? Have you come? Why, my cheese, my digestive, why haven't you served yourself up at my table for so many meals? Come on, what is Agamemnon?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Thy commander, Achilles. Then tell me, Patroclus, what's Achilles?"],"id":388,"m":"Your commander, Achilles. Now tell me, Patroclus, what is Achilles?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Thy lord, Thersites. Then tell me, I pray thee, what's Thersites?"],"id":389,"m":"Your lord, Thersites. Now tell me, please, what is Thersites?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Thy knower, Patroclus. Then tell me, Patroclus, what art thou?"],"id":390,"m":"The one who knows you, Patroclus. Now tell me, Patroclus, what are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Thou must tell that knowest."],"id":391,"m":"You must say, since you know."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["O, tell, tell,"],"id":392,"m":"Oh, tell us, tell us."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["I'll decline the whole question. Agamemnon commands Achilles; Achilles is my lord; I am Patroclus' knower; and Patroclus is a fool."],"id":393,"m":"I'll go through the whole question like a grammar lesson. Agamemnon commands Achilles; Achilles is my lord; I am the one who knows Patroclus; and Patroclus is a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["You rascal!"],"id":394,"m":"You rascal!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Peace, fool! I have not done."],"id":395,"m":"Quiet, fool! I haven't finished."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["He is a privileg'd man. Proceed, Thersites."],"id":396,"m":"He's allowed to say anything. Carry on, Thersites."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Agamemnon is a fool; Achilles is a fool; Thersites is a fool; and, as aforesaid, Patroclus is a fool."],"id":397,"m":"Agamemnon is a fool; Achilles is a fool; Thersites is a fool; and, as I said before, Patroclus is a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Derive this; come."],"id":398,"m":"Prove it, come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Agamemnon is a fool to offer to command Achilles; Achilles is a fool to be commanded of Agamemnon; Thersites is a fool to serve such a fool; and this Patroclus is a fool positive."],"id":399,"m":"Agamemnon is a fool to try to command Achilles; Achilles is a fool to be commanded by Agamemnon; Thersites is a fool to serve such a fool; and this Patroclus is a fool, plain and simple."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Why am I a fool?"],"id":400,"m":"Why am I a fool?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Make that demand of the Creator. It suffices me thou art. Look you, who comes here?"],"id":401,"m":"Ask the Creator that. It's enough for me that you are. Look, who's coming here?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Agamemnon, Ulysses, Nestor, Diomedes, Ajax and Calchas.","id":402},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Come, Patroclus, I'll speak with nobody. Come in with me, Thersites."],"id":403,"m":"Come on, Patroclus, I won't speak to anyone. Come in with me, Thersites."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":404},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Here is such patchery, such juggling, and such knavery. All the argument is a whore and a cuckold—a good quarrel to draw emulous factions and bleed to death upon. Now the dry serpigo on the subject, and war and lechery confound all!"],"id":405,"m":"Here's such patching up, such trickery and such villainy. The whole cause is a whore and a cuckold, a fine quarrel to set rival factions against each other and bleed to death over. May a dry skin disease fall on the cause, and war and lechery destroy everything!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":406},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Where is Achilles?"],"id":407,"m":"Where is Achilles?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Within his tent; but ill-dispos'd, my lord."],"id":408,"m":"In his tent, but unwell, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Let it be known to him that we are here.","He sate our messengers; and we lay by","Our appertainings, visiting of him.","Let him be told so; lest, perchance, he think","We dare not move the question of our place","Or know not what we are."],"id":409,"m":"Let him know that we are here. He has insulted our messengers, and we have put aside our rank to visit him. Tell him so, in case he thinks we don't dare insist on our position, or don't know who we are."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["I shall say so to him."],"id":410,"m":"I'll tell him so."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":411},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["We saw him at the opening of his tent.","He is not sick."],"id":412,"m":"We saw him at the entrance of his tent. He isn't sick."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Yes, lion-sick, sick of proud heart. You may call it melancholy, if you will favour the man; but, by my head, 'tis pride. But why, why? Let him show us a cause. A word, my lord."],"id":413,"m":"Yes, lion-sick, sick with a proud heart. You can call it melancholy if you want to be kind to the man, but, by my head, it's pride. But why, why? Let him show us a reason. A word, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Takes Agamemnon aside.","id":414},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["What moves Ajax thus to bay at him?"],"id":415,"m":"What makes Ajax bark at him like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Achilles hath inveigled his fool from him."],"id":416,"m":"Achilles has lured his fool away from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Who, Thersites?"],"id":417,"m":"Who, Thersites?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["He."],"id":418,"m":"Him."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Then will Ajax lack matter, if he have lost his argument."],"id":419,"m":"Then Ajax will run short of material, if he has lost his subject."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["No; you see he is his argument that has his argument, Achilles."],"id":420,"m":"No; you see, his subject now is the man who has his subject: Achilles."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["All the better; their fraction is more our wish than their faction. But it was a strong composure a fool could disunite!"],"id":421,"m":"So much the better. We want them split more than we want them united. But it was a strong alliance if a fool could break it up!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["The amity that wisdom knits not, folly may easily untie."],"id":422,"m":"A friendship that wisdom doesn't bind together, folly can easily untie."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Patroclus.","id":423},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","cont":1,"lines":["Here comes Patroclus."],"id":424,"m":"Here comes Patroclus."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["No Achilles with him."],"id":425,"m":"Without Achilles."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["The elephant hath joints, but none for courtesy; his legs are legs for necessity, not for flexure."],"id":426,"m":"The elephant has joints, but none for courtesy. His legs are legs for necessity, not for bending."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Achilles bids me say he is much sorry","If any thing more than your sport and pleasure","Did move your greatness and this noble state","To call upon him; he hopes it is no other","But for your health and your digestion sake,","An after-dinner's breath."],"id":427,"m":"Achilles tells me to say he is very sorry if anything more than your amusement and pleasure moved your greatness and this noble company to call on him. He hopes it is nothing more than an after-dinner stroll for the sake of your health and your digestion."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Hear you, Patroclus.","We are too well acquainted with these answers;","But his evasion, wing'd thus swift with scorn,","Cannot outfly our apprehensions.","Much attribute he hath, and much the reason","Why we ascribe it to him. Yet all his virtues,","Not virtuously on his own part beheld,","Do in our eyes begin to lose their gloss;","Yea, like fair fruit in an unwholesome dish,","Are like to rot untasted. Go and tell him","We come to speak with him; and you shall not sin","If you do say we think him over-proud","And under-honest, in self-assumption greater","Than in the note of judgement; and worthier than himself","Here tend the savage strangeness he puts on,","Disguise the holy strength of their command,","And underwrite in an observing kind","His humorous predominance; yea, watch","His course and time, his ebbs and flows, as if","The passage and whole stream of this commencement","Rode on his tide. Go tell him this, and add","That if he overhold his price so much","We'll none of him, but let him, like an engine","Not portable, lie under this report:","Bring action hither; this cannot go to war.","A stirring dwarf we do allowance give","Before a sleeping giant. Tell him so."],"id":428,"m":"Listen, Patroclus. We know these answers too well, but his evasion, though it flies so fast on the wings of scorn, cannot outfly our understanding. He has many fine qualities, and many reasons why we credit him with them. Yet all his virtues, since he does not display them virtuously, are beginning to lose their shine in our eyes; yes, like good fruit on a dirty dish, they are likely to rot untasted. Go and tell him we have come to speak with him. And it won't be a sin if you tell him we think him too proud and not honest enough, greater in his own opinion than in the judgement of sensible people, and that better men than he are here waiting on the savage aloofness he puts on, hiding the sacred strength of their authority and humbly putting up with his moody dominance; yes, watching his moods and timing, his ebbs and flows, as if the whole course and current of this campaign rode on his tide. Go and tell him this, and add that if he overprices himself so much, we'll have nothing to do with him. We'll let him lie, like a siege engine too heavy to move, under this verdict: \"Bring the fighting here; this cannot go to war.\" We value a busy dwarf above a sleeping giant. Tell him so."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["I shall, and bring his answer presently."],"id":429,"m":"I will, and bring his answer straight away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":430},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["In second voice we'll not be satisfied;","We come to speak with him. Ulysses, enter you."],"id":431,"m":"We won't be satisfied with a go-between. We have come to speak with him. Ulysses, you go in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Ulysses.","id":432},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["What is he more than another?"],"id":433,"m":"What is he more than anyone else?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["No more than what he thinks he is."],"id":434,"m":"No more than what he thinks he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Is he so much? Do you not think he thinks himself a better man than I am?"],"id":435,"m":"Is he as much as that? Don't you think he thinks he's a better man than me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["No question."],"id":436,"m":"No doubt about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Will you subscribe his thought and say he is?"],"id":437,"m":"Will you agree with his opinion and say he is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["No, noble Ajax; you are as strong, as valiant, as wise, no less noble, much more gentle, and altogether more tractable."],"id":438,"m":"No, noble Ajax. You are as strong, as brave, as wise, no less noble, much more courteous, and altogether easier to deal with."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Why should a man be proud? How doth pride grow? I know not what pride is."],"id":439,"m":"Why should a man be proud? How does pride grow? I don't know what pride is."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Your mind is the clearer, Ajax, and your virtues the fairer. He that is proud eats up himself. Pride is his own glass, his own trumpet, his own chronicle; and whatever praises itself but in the deed devours the deed in the praise."],"id":440,"m":"Your mind is all the clearer for it, Ajax, and your virtues all the finer. A proud man eats himself up. Pride is its own mirror, its own trumpet, its own history book; and whatever praises itself except by its actions destroys the action in the praising."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Ulysses.","id":441},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["I do hate a proud man as I do hate the engend'ring of toads."],"id":442,"m":"I hate a proud man as much as I hate toads breeding."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["[Aside] And yet he loves himself: is't not strange?"],"id":443,"m":"(Aside) And yet he loves himself. Isn't that strange?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Achilles will not to the field tomorrow."],"id":444,"m":"Achilles won't go to the battlefield tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["What's his excuse?"],"id":445,"m":"What's his excuse?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["He doth rely on none;","But carries on the stream of his dispose,","Without observance or respect of any,","In will peculiar and in self-admission."],"id":446,"m":"He doesn't bother with one. He just follows the stream of his own mood, without paying attention or respect to anyone, with a will of his own and only his own approval."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Why will he not, upon our fair request,","Untent his person and share th'air with us?"],"id":447,"m":"Why won't he, when we ask him politely, come out of his tent and share the air with us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Things small as nothing, for request's sake only,","He makes important; possess'd he is with greatness,","And speaks not to himself but with a pride","That quarrels at self-breath. Imagin'd worth","Holds in his blood such swol'n and hot discourse","That 'twixt his mental and his active parts","Kingdom'd Achilles in commotion rages,","And batters down himself. What should I say?","He is so plaguy proud that the death tokens of it","Cry 'No recovery.'"],"id":448,"m":"He makes things as small as nothing important, just because he is asked. He is possessed with his own greatness, and he can't even talk to himself without a pride that quarrels with its own breath. His imagined worth stirs up such a swollen, hot argument in his blood that Achilles, a kingdom in himself, rages in civil war between his mind and his actions and batters himself down. What can I say? He is so plaguily proud that the fatal spots of the disease cry \"No recovery.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Let Ajax go to him.","Dear lord, go you and greet him in his tent.","'Tis said he holds you well; and will be led","At your request a little from himself."],"id":449,"m":"Let Ajax go to him. Dear lord, go and greet him in his tent. They say he thinks well of you, and he will let himself be drawn a little out of himself if you ask him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["O Agamemnon, let it not be so!","We'll consecrate the steps that Ajax makes","When they go from Achilles. Shall the proud lord","That bastes his arrogance with his own seam","And never suffers matter of the world","Enter his thoughts, save such as doth revolve","And ruminate himself—shall he be worshipp'd","Of that we hold an idol more than he?","No, this thrice worthy and right valiant lord","Shall not so stale his palm, nobly acquir'd,","Nor, by my will, assubjugate his merit,","As amply titled as Achilles is,","By going to Achilles.","That were to enlard his fat-already pride,","And add more coals to Cancer when he burns","With entertaining great Hyperion.","This lord go to him! Jupiter forbid,","And say in thunder 'Achilles go to him.'"],"id":450,"m":"Oh Agamemnon, don't let that happen! We will bless every step Ajax takes away from Achilles. Shall that proud lord, who bastes his arrogance in his own fat, and never lets any worldly matter enter his thoughts except what concerns himself, be worshipped by the man we idolise more than him? No, this three-times-worthy and truly brave lord must not cheapen his nobly won glory, nor, if I have anything to do with it, make his merit, which is as amply honoured as Achilles', subject to Achilles by going to him. That would be adding lard to his already fat pride, and adding more coal to the sign of Cancer when it burns with the summer sun. This lord go to him! Jupiter forbid, and say in thunder, \"Achilles, go to him.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["[Aside] O, this is well! He rubs the vein of him."],"id":451,"m":"(Aside) Oh, this is good! He's rubbing him the right way."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["[Aside] And how his silence drinks up this applause!"],"id":452,"m":"(Aside) And how his silence soaks up this praise!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["If I go to him, with my armed fist I'll pash him o'er the face."],"id":453,"m":"If I go to him, I'll smash him in the face with my armoured fist."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["O, no, you shall not go."],"id":454,"m":"Oh no, you shan't go."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["An a' be proud with me I'll pheeze his pride.","Let me go to him."],"id":455,"m":"If he's proud with me, I'll fix his pride. Let me go to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Not for the worth that hangs upon our quarrel."],"id":456,"m":"Not for all that depends on our quarrel."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["A paltry, insolent fellow!"],"id":457,"m":"A paltry, insolent fellow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["[Aside] How he describes himself!"],"id":458,"m":"(Aside) How he describes himself!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Can he not be sociable?"],"id":459,"m":"Can't he be sociable?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["[Aside] The raven chides blackness."],"id":460,"m":"(Aside) The raven criticising blackness."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["I'll let his humours blood."],"id":461,"m":"I'll bleed him to cure his moods."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["[Aside] He will be the physician that should be the patient."],"id":462,"m":"(Aside) He'll be the doctor when he should be the patient."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["And all men were o' my mind—"],"id":463,"m":"If all men thought like me..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["[Aside] Wit would be out of fashion."],"id":464,"m":"(Aside) Wit would be out of fashion."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["A' should not bear it so, a' should eat's words first.","Shall pride carry it?"],"id":465,"m":"He shouldn't get away with it like this; he should eat his words first. Shall pride win the day?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["[Aside] And 'twould, you'd carry half."],"id":466,"m":"(Aside) If it did, you'd have half the winnings."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["[Aside] A' would have ten shares."],"id":467,"m":"(Aside) He'd have ten shares."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["I will knead him, I'll make him supple."],"id":468,"m":"I'll knead him, I'll make him soft."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["[Aside] He's not yet through warm. Force him with praises; pour in, pour in; his ambition is dry."],"id":469,"m":"(Aside) He's not warmed through yet. Stuff him with praise; pour it in, pour it in; his ambition is dry."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["[To Agamemnon] My lord, you feed too much on this dislike."],"id":470,"m":"(To Agamemnon) My lord, you dwell too much on this resentment."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Our noble general, do not do so."],"id":471,"m":"Our noble general, don't do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["You must prepare to fight without Achilles."],"id":472,"m":"You must prepare to fight without Achilles."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Why 'tis this naming of him does him harm.","Here is a man—but 'tis before his face;","I will be silent."],"id":473,"m":"Why, it's this talk about him that does the harm. Here is a man... but he's standing right here; I'll say nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Wherefore should you so?","He is not emulous, as Achilles is."],"id":474,"m":"Why should you? He isn't jealous of praise, the way Achilles is."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Know the whole world, he is as valiant."],"id":475,"m":"Let the whole world know, he is as brave."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["A whoreson dog, that shall palter with us thus!","Would he were a Trojan!"],"id":476,"m":"The bastard dog, to mess us around like this! I wish he were a Trojan!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["What a vice were it in Ajax now—"],"id":477,"m":"What a fault it would be in Ajax now..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["If he were proud."],"id":478,"m":"If he were proud."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Or covetous of praise."],"id":479,"m":"Or greedy for praise."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Ay, or surly borne."],"id":480,"m":"Yes, or bad-tempered."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Or strange, or self-affected."],"id":481,"m":"Or aloof, or in love with himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Thank the heavens, lord, thou art of sweet composure","Praise him that gat thee, she that gave thee suck;","Fam'd be thy tutor, and thy parts of nature","Thrice fam'd beyond, beyond all erudition;","But he that disciplin'd thine arms to fight—","Let Mars divide eternity in twain","And give him half; and, for thy vigour,","Bull-bearing Milo his addition yield","To sinewy Ajax. I will not praise thy wisdom,","Which, like a bourn, a pale, a shore, confines","Thy spacious and dilated parts. Here's Nestor,","Instructed by the antiquary times—","He must, he is, he cannot but be wise;","But pardon, father Nestor, were your days","As green as Ajax' and your brain so temper'd,","You should not have the eminence of him,","But be as Ajax."],"id":482,"m":"Thank heaven, lord, you have a sweet nature. Praise your father, and your mother who nursed you. Let your teacher be famous, and your natural gifts three times more famous, beyond all learning. But as for the man who trained your arms to fight, let Mars cut eternity in two and give him half. And as for your strength, let Milo, who carried a bull, give up his title to muscular Ajax. I won't praise your wisdom, which, like a boundary, a fence, a shore, contains your wide-ranging talents. Here's Nestor, taught by ancient times. He must be wise, he is, he can't help being wise. But excuse me, father Nestor, if your days were as young as Ajax's and your brain as well balanced, you wouldn't be superior to him; you would be just like Ajax."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Shall I call you father?"],"id":483,"m":"Shall I call you father?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Ay, my good son."],"id":484,"m":"Yes, my good son."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Be rul'd by him, Lord Ajax."],"id":485,"m":"Take his advice, Lord Ajax."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["There is no tarrying here; the hart Achilles","Keeps thicket. Please it our great general","To call together all his state of war;","Fresh kings are come to Troy. Tomorrow","We must with all our main of power stand fast;","And here's a lord—come knights from east to west","And cull their flower, Ajax shall cope the best."],"id":486,"m":"There's no point waiting here. The stag Achilles is hiding in the thicket. May it please our great general to call his whole war council together. New kings have come to help Troy. Tomorrow we must stand firm with all our strength. And here's a lord: let knights come from east to west and pick their best, Ajax will match the best of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Go we to council. Let Achilles sleep.","Light boats sail swift, though greater hulks draw deep."],"id":487,"m":"Let's go to the council. Let Achilles sleep. Light boats sail fast, though bigger ships sit deeper in the water."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":488},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Troy. Priam's palace.","id":489},{"t":"sd","text":"Music sounds within. Enter Pandarus and a Servant.","id":490},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Friend, you—pray you, a word. Do you not follow the young Lord Paris?"],"id":491,"m":"Friend, you there, please, a word. Don't you follow the young Lord Paris?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Ay, sir, when he goes before me."],"id":492,"m":"Yes, sir, when he walks in front of me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["You depend upon him, I mean?"],"id":493,"m":"I mean, you depend on him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Sir, I do depend upon the Lord."],"id":494,"m":"Sir, I depend on the Lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["You depend upon a notable gentleman; I must needs praise him."],"id":495,"m":"You depend on a remarkable gentleman. I must praise him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["The Lord be praised!"],"id":496,"m":"The Lord be praised!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["You know me, do you not?"],"id":497,"m":"You know me, don't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Faith, sir, superficially."],"id":498,"m":"Well, sir, superficially."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Friend, know me better: I am the Lord Pandarus."],"id":499,"m":"Friend, get to know me better. I am the Lord Pandarus."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["I hope I shall know your honour better."],"id":500,"m":"I hope I'll know your honour better (that you'll become better)."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I do desire it."],"id":501,"m":"I'd like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["You are in the state of grace?"],"id":502,"m":"Are you in a state of grace?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Grace? Not so, friend; honour and lordship are my titles. What music is this?"],"id":503,"m":"Grace? No, friend; \"honour\" and \"lordship\" are my titles. What music is this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["I do but partly know, sir; it is music in parts."],"id":504,"m":"I only partly know, sir. It is music in parts."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Know you the musicians?"],"id":505,"m":"Do you know the musicians?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Wholly, sir."],"id":506,"m":"Wholly, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Who play they to?"],"id":507,"m":"Who are they playing to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["To the hearers, sir."],"id":508,"m":"To the listeners, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["At whose pleasure, friend?"],"id":509,"m":"At whose pleasure, friend?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["At mine, sir, and theirs that love music."],"id":510,"m":"At mine, sir, and at the pleasure of those who love music."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Command, I mean, friend."],"id":511,"m":"I mean at whose command, friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Who shall I command, sir?"],"id":512,"m":"Who should I command, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Friend, we understand not one another: I am too courtly, and thou art too cunning. At whose request do these men play?"],"id":513,"m":"Friend, we don't understand each other. I am too polite and you are too clever. At whose request are these men playing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["That's to't, indeed, sir. Marry, sir, at the request of Paris my lord, who is there in person; with him the mortal Venus, the heart-blood of beauty, love's invisible soul—"],"id":514,"m":"Now you've got to the point, sir. Well, sir, at the request of my lord Paris, who is there in person, and with him the mortal Venus, the heart's blood of beauty, love's invisible soul..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Who, my cousin, Cressida?"],"id":515,"m":"Who, my niece Cressida?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["No, sir, Helen. Could not you find out that by her attributes?"],"id":516,"m":"No, sir, Helen. Couldn't you tell that from the description?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["It should seem, fellow, that thou hast not seen the Lady Cressida. I come to speak with Paris from the Prince Troilus; I will make a complimental assault upon him, for my business seethes."],"id":517,"m":"It seems, fellow, that you have never seen the Lady Cressida. I have come to speak to Paris on behalf of Prince Troilus. I'll launch a polite attack on him, because my business is boiling."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Sodden business! There's a stew'd phrase indeed!"],"id":518,"m":"Boiled business! That's a stewed phrase indeed! (Stews were brothels.)"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Paris and Helen, attended.","id":519},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Fair be to you, my lord, and to all this fair company! Fair desires, in all fair measure, fairly guide them—especially to you, fair queen! Fair thoughts be your fair pillow."],"id":520,"m":"Fairness be with you, my lord, and with all this fair company! May fair desires, in all fair measure, guide them fairly, especially you, fair queen! May fair thoughts be your fair pillow."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["Dear lord, you are full of fair words."],"id":521,"m":"Dear lord, you are full of fair words."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["You speak your fair pleasure, sweet queen. Fair prince, here is good broken music."],"id":522,"m":"You're just saying what pleases you, sweet queen. Fair prince, here is some fine music in parts."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["You have broke it, cousin; and by my life, you shall make it whole again; you shall piece it out with a piece of your performance."],"id":523,"m":"You have broken it, cousin, and by my life you'll make it whole again; you'll patch it up with a piece of your own performance."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["He is full of harmony."],"id":524,"m":"He's full of harmony."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Truly, lady, no."],"id":525,"m":"Truly, lady, no."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["O, sir—"],"id":526,"m":"Oh, sir..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Rude, in sooth; in good sooth, very rude."],"id":527,"m":"Rough, honestly; in all honesty, very rough."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["Well said, my lord. Well, you say so in fits."],"id":528,"m":"Well said, my lord. Well, you say so in bursts (in musical sections)."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I have business to my lord, dear queen. My lord, will you vouchsafe me a word?"],"id":529,"m":"I have business with my lord, dear queen. My lord, will you grant me a word?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["Nay, this shall not hedge us out. We'll hear you sing, certainly—"],"id":530,"m":"No, this won't shut us out. We'll hear you sing, certainly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Well sweet queen, you are pleasant with me. But, marry, thus, my lord: my dear lord and most esteemed friend, your brother Troilus—"],"id":531,"m":"Well, sweet queen, you're teasing me. But, well, it's this, my lord: my dear lord and most respected friend, your brother Troilus..."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["My Lord Pandarus, honey-sweet lord—"],"id":532,"m":"My Lord Pandarus, honey-sweet lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Go to, sweet queen, go to—commends himself most affectionately to you—"],"id":533,"m":"Come on, sweet queen, come on... sends his most affectionate greetings to you..."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["You shall not bob us out of our melody. If you do, our melancholy upon your head!"],"id":534,"m":"You won't cheat us out of our song. If you do, our sadness be on your head!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Sweet queen, sweet queen; that's a sweet queen, i' faith."],"id":535,"m":"Sweet queen, sweet queen; that's a sweet queen, honestly."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["And to make a sweet lady sad is a sour offence."],"id":536,"m":"And to make a sweet lady sad is a sour offence."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Nay, that shall not serve your turn; that shall it not, in truth, la. Nay, I care not for such words; no, no.—And, my lord, he desires you that, if the King call for him at supper, you will make his excuse."],"id":537,"m":"No, that won't work; it won't, really, no. No, I don't care for such words, no, no. And, my lord, he asks that if the King calls for him at supper, you'll make his excuses."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["My Lord Pandarus!"],"id":538,"m":"My Lord Pandarus!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["What says my sweet queen, my very very sweet queen?"],"id":539,"m":"What does my sweet queen say, my very, very sweet queen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["What exploit's in hand? Where sups he tonight?"],"id":540,"m":"What's he up to? Where is he having supper tonight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["Nay, but, my lord—"],"id":541,"m":"No, but, my lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["What says my sweet queen?—My cousin will fall out with you."],"id":542,"m":"What does my sweet queen say? My niece will fall out with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["You must not know where he sups."],"id":543,"m":"You mustn't know where he's having supper."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["I'll lay my life, with my disposer Cressida."],"id":544,"m":"I'll bet my life it's with my disposer Cressida."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["No, no, no such matter; you are wide. Come, your disposer is sick."],"id":545,"m":"No, no, nothing of the kind; you're way off. Come on, your disposer is sick."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["Well, I'll make's excuse."],"id":546,"m":"Well, I'll make his excuses."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Ay, good my lord. Why should you say Cressida?","No, your poor disposer's sick."],"id":547,"m":"Yes, my good lord. Why should you say Cressida? No, your poor disposer is sick."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["I spy."],"id":548,"m":"I see what's going on."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["You spy! What do you spy?—Come, give me an instrument. Now, sweet queen."],"id":549,"m":"You see! What do you see? Come, give me an instrument. Now, sweet queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["Why, this is kindly done."],"id":550,"m":"Why, this is kind of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["My niece is horribly in love with a thing you have, sweet queen."],"id":551,"m":"My niece is horribly in love with something you have, sweet queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["She shall have it, my lord, if it be not my Lord Paris."],"id":552,"m":"She can have it, my lord, as long as it isn't my Lord Paris."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["He? No, she'll none of him; they two are twain."],"id":553,"m":"Him? No, she wants nothing to do with him. Those two are two separate people."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["Falling in, after falling out, may make them three."],"id":554,"m":"Making up after falling out might make them three (a baby)."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Come, come. I'll hear no more of this; I'll sing you a song now."],"id":555,"m":"Come, come, I won't hear any more of this. I'll sing you a song now."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["Ay, ay, prithee now. By my troth, sweet lord, thou hast a fine forehead."],"id":556,"m":"Yes, yes, please do. Honestly, sweet lord, you have a fine forehead."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Ay, you may, you may."],"id":557,"m":"Yes, go on, go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["Let thy song be love. This love will undo us all. O Cupid, Cupid, Cupid!"],"id":558,"m":"Let your song be about love. This love will ruin us all. Oh Cupid, Cupid, Cupid!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Love! Ay, that it shall, i' faith."],"id":559,"m":"Love! Yes, it shall be, truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["Ay, good now, love, love, nothing but love."],"id":560,"m":"Yes, please, love, love, nothing but love."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["In good troth, it begins so."],"id":561,"m":"Honestly, that's how it begins."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sings.","id":562},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["~Love, love, nothing but love, still love, still more!","~For, oh, love's bow","~Shoots buck and doe;","~The shaft confounds","~Not that it wounds,","~But tickles still the sore.","~These lovers cry, O ho, they die!","~Yet that which seems the wound to kill","~Doth turn O ho! to ha! ha! he!","~So dying love lives still.","~O ho! a while, but ha! ha! ha!","~O ho! groans out for ha! ha! ha!—hey ho!"],"id":563,"m":"Love, love, nothing but love, always more love! Oh, love's bow shoots both buck and doe. The arrow doesn't kill by wounding, but keeps tickling the sore. These lovers cry, \"Oh ho, we're dying!\" Yet what seems to be the fatal wound turns \"Oh ho!\" into \"Ha ha he!\" So dying love lives on. \"Oh ho!\" for a while, then \"Ha ha ha!\" \"Oh ho!\" groans into \"Ha ha ha!\" Heigh ho!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["In love, i' faith, to the very tip of the nose."],"id":564,"m":"In love, truly, to the very tip of his nose."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["He eats nothing but doves, love; and that breeds hot blood, and hot blood begets hot thoughts, and hot thoughts beget hot deeds, and hot deeds is love."],"id":565,"m":"He eats nothing but doves, my love, and that breeds hot blood, and hot blood breeds hot thoughts, and hot thoughts breed hot deeds, and hot deeds are love."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Is this the generation of love: hot blood, hot thoughts, and hot deeds? Why, they are vipers. Is love a generation of vipers? Sweet lord, who's a-field today?"],"id":566,"m":"Is this how love is bred: hot blood, hot thoughts and hot deeds? Why, they are vipers. Is love a brood of vipers? Sweet lord, who's out fighting today?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["Hector, Deiphobus, Helenus, Antenor, and all the gallantry of Troy. I would fain have arm'd today, but my Nell would not have it so. How chance my brother Troilus went not?"],"id":567,"m":"Hector, Deiphobus, Helenus, Antenor, and all the fine knights of Troy. I wanted to arm myself today, but my Nell wouldn't let me. How is it my brother Troilus didn't go?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["He hangs the lip at something. You know all, Lord Pandarus."],"id":568,"m":"He's sulking about something. You know everything, Lord Pandarus."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Not I, honey-sweet queen. I long to hear how they spend today. You'll remember your brother's excuse?"],"id":569,"m":"Not I, honey-sweet queen. I'm longing to hear how they get on today. You'll remember your brother's excuse?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["To a hair."],"id":570,"m":"To the last hair."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Farewell, sweet queen."],"id":571,"m":"Goodbye, sweet queen."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["Commend me to your niece."],"id":572,"m":"Give my regards to your niece."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I will, sweet queen."],"id":573,"m":"I will, sweet queen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit. Sound a retreat.","id":574},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["They're come from the field. Let us to Priam's hall","To greet the warriors. Sweet Helen, I must woo you","To help unarm our Hector. His stubborn buckles,","With these your white enchanting fingers touch'd,","Shall more obey than to the edge of steel","Or force of Greekish sinews; you shall do more","Than all the island kings—disarm great Hector."],"id":575,"m":"They've come back from the battlefield. Let's go to Priam's hall to greet the warriors. Sweet Helen, I must ask you to help take off our Hector's armour. His stubborn buckles, touched by these white, enchanting fingers of yours, will give way more readily than to the edge of steel or the force of Greek muscle. You will do more than all the island kings: disarm great Hector."},{"t":"sp","who":"HELEN","lines":["'Twill make us proud to be his servant, Paris;","Yea, what he shall receive of us in duty","Gives us more palm in beauty than we have,","Yea, overshines ourself."],"id":576,"m":"It will make me proud to be his servant, Paris. Yes, the duty he receives from me will give me more glory than my beauty does; yes, it will outshine me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["Sweet, above thought I love thee."],"id":577,"m":"Sweet, I love you more than thought can say."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":578},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Troy. Pandarus' orchard.","id":579},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pandarus and Troilus' Boy, meeting.","id":580},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["How now! Where's thy master? At my cousin Cressida's?"],"id":581,"m":"Hello! Where's your master? At my niece Cressida's?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOY","lines":["No, sir; he stays for you to conduct him thither."],"id":582,"m":"No, sir; he's waiting for you to take him there."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Troilus.","id":583},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["O, here he comes. How now, how now?"],"id":584,"m":"Oh, here he comes. Well, well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Sirrah, walk off."],"id":585,"m":"Boy, go away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Boy.","id":586},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Have you seen my cousin?"],"id":587,"m":"Have you seen my niece?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["No, Pandarus. I stalk about her door","Like a strange soul upon the Stygian banks","Staying for waftage. O, be thou my Charon,","And give me swift transportance to these fields","Where I may wallow in the lily beds","Propos'd for the deserver! O gentle Pandar,","from Cupid's shoulder pluck his painted wings,","and fly with me to Cressid!"],"id":588,"m":"No, Pandarus. I prowl about her door like a newly dead soul on the banks of the Styx waiting to be ferried across. Oh, be my Charon, and give me quick passage to those fields where I can roll in the lily beds promised to those who deserve them! Oh gentle Pandarus, pluck the painted wings from Cupid's shoulders and fly with me to Cressida!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Walk here i' th' orchard, I'll bring her straight."],"id":589,"m":"Walk here in the orchard. I'll bring her straight away."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":590},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["I am giddy; expectation whirls me round.","Th'imaginary relish is so sweet","That it enchants my sense; what will it be","When that the wat'ry palate tastes indeed","Love's thrice-repured nectar? Death, I fear me;","Sounding destruction; or some joy too fine,","Too subtle-potent, tun'd too sharp in sweetness,","For the capacity of my ruder powers.","I fear it much; and I do fear besides","That I shall lose distinction in my joys;","As doth a battle, when they charge on heaps","The enemy flying."],"id":591,"m":"I'm dizzy; expectation spins me round. The imagined taste is so sweet that it enchants my senses. What will it be like when my watering mouth really tastes love's three-times-purified nectar? Death, I'm afraid; a swooning destruction; or some joy too fine, too subtly powerful, tuned too sharply sweet for my coarser senses to take in. I'm very afraid of it. And I'm also afraid that I'll lose all sense of distinction in my joys, like an army charging in a confused mass when the enemy is fleeing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Pandarus.","id":592},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["She's making her ready, she'll come straight; you must be witty now. She does so blush, and fetches her wind so short, as if she were fray'd with a sprite. I'll fetch her. It is the prettiest villain; she fetches her breath as short as a new-ta'en sparrow."],"id":593,"m":"She's getting herself ready; she'll come straight away. You must be clever now. She's blushing so much, and catching her breath so short, as if she'd been frightened by a ghost. I'll fetch her. She's the prettiest little rascal; she catches her breath as short as a newly caught sparrow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":594},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Even such a passion doth embrace my bosom.","My heart beats thicker than a feverous pulse,","And all my powers do their bestowing lose,","Like vassalage at unawares encount'ring","The eye of majesty."],"id":595,"m":"Just such a passion fills my chest. My heart beats faster than a fevered pulse, and all my faculties lose their function, like subjects suddenly meeting the eye of their king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Pandarus with Cressida.","id":596},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Come, come, what need you blush? Shame's a baby. Here she is now; swear the oaths now to her that you have sworn to me.—What, are you gone again? You must be watch'd ere you be made tame, must you? Come your ways, come your ways; and you draw backward, we'll put you i' th' fills. Why do you not speak to her? Come, draw this curtain and let's see your picture. Alas the day, how loath you are to offend daylight! And 'twere dark, you'd close sooner. So, so; rub on, and kiss the mistress. How now, a kiss in fee-farm! Build there, carpenter; the air is sweet. Nay, you shall fight your hearts out ere I part you. The falcon as the tercel, for all the ducks i' th' river. Go to, go to."],"id":597,"m":"Come, come, why do you need to blush? Shyness is for babies. Here she is now. Swear to her now the oaths you've sworn to me. What, are you running off again? You need to be kept awake before you're tamed, do you, like a hawk? Come along, come along. If you pull back, we'll put you in the shafts like a horse. Why don't you speak to her? Come, draw back this curtain and let's see your picture. (He lifts her veil.) Alas, how unwilling you are to offend the daylight! If it were dark, you'd come together sooner. So, so; keep going and kiss the target. Well, a kiss with a lease for life! Build there, carpenter, the air is sweet. No, you can fight till your hearts give out before I separate you. The female falcon matches the male, I'll bet all the ducks in the river. Go on, go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["You have bereft me of all words, lady."],"id":598,"m":"You have taken away all my words, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Words pay no debts, give her deeds; but she'll bereave you o' th' deeds too, if she call your activity in question. What, billing again? Here's 'In witness whereof the parties interchangeably.' Come in, come in; I'll go get a fire."],"id":599,"m":"Words don't pay debts; give her deeds. But she'll take away your deeds too, if she puts your energy to the test. What, kissing again? Here's \"In witness of which, the parties have exchanged signatures.\" Come in, come in. I'll go and light a fire."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":600},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Will you walk in, my lord?"],"id":601,"m":"Will you come in, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O Cressid, how often have I wish'd me thus!"],"id":602,"m":"Oh Cressida, how often I have wished to be like this!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Wish'd, my lord! The gods grant—O my lord!"],"id":603,"m":"Wished, my lord! May the gods grant... oh, my lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["What should they grant? What makes this pretty abruption? What too curious dreg espies my sweet lady in the fountain of our love?"],"id":604,"m":"What should they grant? Why did you break off so suddenly? What tiny speck of dirt has my sweet lady spotted in the fountain of our love?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["More dregs than water, if my fears have eyes."],"id":605,"m":"More dirt than water, if my fears can see."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Fears make devils of cherubins; they never see truly."],"id":606,"m":"Fears turn angels into devils. They never see truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Blind fear, that seeing reason leads, finds safer footing than blind reason stumbling without fear. To fear the worst oft cures the worse."],"id":607,"m":"Blind fear, led by clear-sighted reason, walks more safely than blind reason stumbling about without fear. Fearing the worst often cures the worst."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O, let my lady apprehend no fear! In all Cupid's pageant there is presented no monster."],"id":608,"m":"Oh, let my lady not be afraid of anything! In all of Cupid's pageant there's no monster."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Nor nothing monstrous neither?"],"id":609,"m":"And nothing monstrous either?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Nothing, but our undertakings when we vow to weep seas, live in fire, eat rocks, tame tigers; thinking it harder for our mistress to devise imposition enough than for us to undergo any difficulty imposed. This is the monstruosity in love, lady, that the will is infinite, and the execution confin'd; that the desire is boundless, and the act a slave to limit."],"id":610,"m":"Nothing, except our promises, when we vow to weep oceans, live in fire, eat rocks, tame tigers, thinking it harder for our lady to think up enough tasks than for us to perform any hard task she sets. This is what is monstrous in love, lady: that the will is infinite and the performance limited; that the desire is boundless and the act a slave to its limits."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["They say all lovers swear more performance than they are able, and yet reserve an ability that they never perform; vowing more than the perfection of ten, and discharging less than the tenth part of one. They that have the voice of lions and the act of hares, are they not monsters?"],"id":611,"m":"They say all lovers swear to perform more than they can, and yet keep back an ability they never use, vowing more than ten men could do perfectly and doing less than a tenth of what one man could. Those who have the voices of lions and the actions of hares, aren't they monsters?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Are there such? Such are not we. Praise us as we are tasted, allow us as we prove; our head shall go bare till merit crown it. No perfection in reversion shall have a praise in present. We will not name desert before his birth; and, being born, his addition shall be humble. Few words to fair faith: Troilus shall be such to Cressid as what envy can say worst shall be a mock for his truth; and what truth can speak truest not truer than Troilus."],"id":612,"m":"Are there such men? We are not like that. Praise us as you find us when you've tried us; value us as we prove ourselves. We will go bareheaded until merit crowns us. No perfection still to come will get praise now. We will not name what we deserve before it is born, and once it is born, its title will be modest. A few words to true faith: Troilus will be such to Cressida that the worst that envy can say will only be a joke against his loyalty, and the truest thing truth can say will be no truer than Troilus."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Will you walk in, my lord?"],"id":613,"m":"Will you come in, my lord?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Pandarus.","id":614},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["What, blushing still? Have you not done talking yet?"],"id":615,"m":"What, still blushing? Haven't you finished talking yet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Well, uncle, what folly I commit, I dedicate to you."],"id":616,"m":"Well, uncle, whatever foolishness I commit, I dedicate to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["I thank you for that; if my lord get a boy of you, you'll give him me. Be true to my lord; if he flinch, chide me for it."],"id":617,"m":"Thank you for that. If my lord gets a boy by you, you'll give him to me. Be faithful to my lord. If he backs off, blame me for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["You know now your hostages: your uncle's word and my firm faith."],"id":618,"m":"Now you know your guarantees: your uncle's word and my firm faith."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Nay, I'll give my word for her too: our kindred, though they be long ere they are wooed, they are constant being won; they are burs, I can tell you; they'll stick where they are thrown."],"id":619,"m":"No, I'll give my word for her too. Our family, though they take a long time to be won over, are faithful once they're won. They're burrs, I can tell you; they stick where they're thrown."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Boldness comes to me now and brings me heart.","Prince Troilus, I have lov'd you night and day","For many weary months."],"id":620,"m":"Now boldness comes to me and gives me courage. Prince Troilus, I have loved you night and day for many weary months."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Why was my Cressid then so hard to win?"],"id":621,"m":"Then why was my Cressida so hard to win?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Hard to seem won; but I was won, my lord,","With the first glance that ever—pardon me.","If I confess much, you will play the tyrant.","I love you now; but till now not so much","But I might master it. In faith, I lie;","My thoughts were like unbridled children, grown","Too headstrong for their mother. See, we fools!","Why have I blabb'd? Who shall be true to us,","When we are so unsecret to ourselves?","But, though I lov'd you well, I woo'd you not;","And yet, good faith, I wish'd myself a man,","Or that we women had men's privilege","Of speaking first. Sweet, bid me hold my tongue,","For in this rapture I shall surely speak","The thing I shall repent. See, see, your silence,","Cunning in dumbness, from my weakness draws","My very soul of counsel. Stop my mouth."],"id":622,"m":"Hard to seem won. But I was won, my lord, with the first look that ever... forgive me. If I confess too much, you'll play the tyrant. I love you now, but until now not so much that I couldn't control it. No, honestly, I'm lying. My thoughts were like unruly children, grown too headstrong for their mother. See what fools we are! Why have I blurted it out? Who will keep our secrets when we can't keep them ourselves? But though I loved you well, I didn't woo you. And yet, honestly, I wished I were a man, or that we women had men's right to speak first. Sweet, tell me to hold my tongue, because in this rapture I'm sure to say something I'll regret. Look, look, your silence, cunning in saying nothing, draws the secrets out of my very soul in my weakness. Stop my mouth."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["And shall, albeit sweet music issues thence."],"id":623,"m":"And I will, even though sweet music comes from it. (He kisses her.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Pretty, i' faith."],"id":624,"m":"Pretty, truly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["My lord, I do beseech you, pardon me;","'Twas not my purpose thus to beg a kiss.","I am asham'd. O heavens! what have I done?","For this time will I take my leave, my lord."],"id":625,"m":"My lord, I beg you, forgive me. I didn't mean to beg for a kiss like that. I'm ashamed. Oh heavens! What have I done? I'll say goodbye for now, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Your leave, sweet Cressid!"],"id":626,"m":"Goodbye, sweet Cressida!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Leave! And you take leave till tomorrow morning—"],"id":627,"m":"Goodbye! If you say goodbye before tomorrow morning..."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Pray you, content you."],"id":628,"m":"Please, be quiet."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["What offends you, lady?"],"id":629,"m":"What upsets you, lady?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Sir, mine own company."],"id":630,"m":"Sir, my own company."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["You cannot shun yourself."],"id":631,"m":"You can't escape yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Let me go and try.","I have a kind of self resides with you;","But an unkind self, that itself will leave","To be another's fool. I would be gone.","Where is my wit? I know not what I speak."],"id":632,"m":"Let me go and try. I have a kind of self that lives with you, but an unkind self, that will leave itself to be another's fool. I want to go. Where are my wits? I don't know what I'm saying."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Well know they what they speak that speak so wisely."],"id":633,"m":"People who speak so wisely know very well what they're saying."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Perchance, my lord, I show more craft than love;","And fell so roundly to a large confession","To angle for your thoughts; but you are wise—","Or else you love not; for to be wise and love","Exceeds man's might; that dwells with gods above."],"id":634,"m":"Perhaps, my lord, I'm showing more cunning than love, and launched so readily into a full confession to fish for your thoughts. But you are wise, or else you're not in love, because to be wise and in love at once is beyond man's power; that belongs to the gods above."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O that I thought it could be in a woman—","As, if it can, I will presume in you—","To feed for aye her lamp and flames of love;","To keep her constancy in plight and youth,","Outliving beauty's outward, with a mind","That doth renew swifter than blood decays!","Or that persuasion could but thus convince me","That my integrity and truth to you","Might be affronted with the match and weight","Of such a winnowed purity in love.","How were I then uplifted! But, alas,","I am as true as truth's simplicity,","And simpler than the infancy of truth."],"id":635,"m":"Oh, if only I thought a woman could (and if one can, I'll believe it of you) keep the lamp and flames of her love burning for ever, keep her faith as fresh and young as when she pledged it, outlasting her outward beauty, with a mind that renews itself faster than the body decays! Or if only I could be convinced that my loyalty and faith to you would be met with an equal measure and weight of such purified love. How lifted up I would be then! But alas, I am as true as plain truth itself, and simpler than truth in its infancy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["In that I'll war with you."],"id":636,"m":"I'll fight you on that."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O virtuous fight,","When right with right wars who shall be most right!","True swains in love shall in the world to come","Approve their truth by Troilus, when their rhymes,","Full of protest, of oath, and big compare,","Want similes, truth tir'd with iteration—","As true as steel, as plantage to the moon,","As sun to day, as turtle to her mate,","As iron to adamant, as earth to th' centre—","Yet, after all comparisons of truth,","As truth's authentic author to be cited,","'As true as Troilus' shall crown up the verse","And sanctify the numbers."],"id":637,"m":"Oh virtuous battle, when right fights right over who will be most right! In times to come, faithful lovers will prove their faithfulness by Troilus. When their poems, full of promises, oaths and grand comparisons, run out of similes, and truth is worn out with repeating \"as true as steel, as plants to the moon, as sun to day, as turtle dove to her mate, as iron to a magnet, as earth to the centre\", then, after all these comparisons of truth, \"as true as Troilus\" will crown the verse and make the poem holy, quoted as the original authority on truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Prophet may you be!","If I be false, or swerve a hair from truth,","When time is old and hath forgot itself,","When waterdrops have worn the stones of Troy,","And blind oblivion swallow'd cities up,","And mighty states characterless are grated","To dusty nothing—yet let memory","From false to false, among false maids in love,","Upbraid my falsehood when th' have said 'As false","As air, as water, wind, or sandy earth,","As fox to lamb, or wolf to heifer's calf,","Pard to the hind, or stepdame to her son'—","Yea, let them say, to stick the heart of falsehood,","'As false as Cressid.'"],"id":638,"m":"May you be a prophet! If I am false, or stray a hair's breadth from truth, when time is old and has forgotten itself, when drops of water have worn away the stones of Troy, and blind oblivion has swallowed up cities, and mighty states have been ground down without trace to dusty nothing, then let memory, from one false lover to the next, among unfaithful girls in love, condemn my falseness. When they have said \"as false as air, as water, wind or sandy ground, as fox to lamb or wolf to calf, leopard to deer or stepmother to stepson\", yes, let them say, to pierce falseness to the heart, \"as false as Cressida.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Go to, a bargain made; seal it, seal it; I'll be the witness. Here I hold your hand; here my cousin's. If ever you prove false one to another, since I have taken such pains to bring you together, let all pitiful goers-between be call'd to the world's end after my name—call them all Pandars; let all constant men be Troiluses, all false women Cressids, and all brokers between Pandars. Say 'Amen.'"],"id":639,"m":"Go on, it's a deal. Seal it, seal it; I'll be the witness. Here I hold your hand, and here my niece's. If you ever prove false to each other, after all the trouble I've taken to bring you together, let all pitiful go-betweens be called by my name until the end of the world: call them all Pandars. Let all faithful men be Troiluses, all false women Cressidas, and all go-betweens Pandars. Say \"Amen.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Amen."],"id":640,"m":"Amen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Amen."],"id":641,"m":"Amen."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Amen. Whereupon I will show you a chamber and a bed; which bed, because it shall not speak of your pretty encounters, press it to death. Away!"],"id":642,"m":"Amen. And so I'll show you to a room with a bed, and so that the bed won't tell about your pretty encounters, press it to death. Away with you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Troilus and Cressida.","id":643},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","cont":1,"lines":["And Cupid grant all tongue-tied maidens here,","Bed, chamber, pander, to provide this gear!"],"id":644,"m":"And may Cupid give all the tongue-tied girls here a bed, a room and a go-between to provide this stuff!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":645},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"The Greek camp.","id":646},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter Agamemnon, Ulysses, Diomedes, Nestor, Ajax, Menelaus and Calchas.","id":647},{"t":"sp","who":"CALCHAS","lines":["Now, Princes, for the service I have done,","Th'advantage of the time prompts me aloud","To call for recompense. Appear it to your mind","That, through the sight I bear in things to come,","I have abandon'd Troy, left my possession,","Incurr'd a traitor's name, expos'd myself","From certain and possess'd conveniences","To doubtful fortunes, sequest'ring from me all","That time, acquaintance, custom, and condition,","Made tame and most familiar to my nature;","And here, to do you service, am become","As new into the world, strange, unacquainted—","I do beseech you, as in way of taste,","To give me now a little benefit","Out of those many regist'red in promise,","Which you say live to come in my behalf."],"id":648,"m":"Now, Princes, for the service I have done you, the opportunity of the moment prompts me to ask out loud for my reward. Remember that, because of my power to see the future, I have abandoned Troy, left my property, earned the name of traitor, and exposed myself, leaving safe and secure comforts for an uncertain fortune. I have cut myself off from everything that time, friends, habit and position had made comfortable and familiar to me, and here, to serve you, I have become like a newborn in the world, a stranger who knows no one. I beg you, as a kind of first taste, to give me now a small reward out of the many you have promised, which you say are still to come for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["What wouldst thou of us, Trojan? Make demand."],"id":649,"m":"What do you want from us, Trojan? Ask."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALCHAS","lines":["You have a Trojan prisoner call'd Antenor,","Yesterday took; Troy holds him very dear.","Oft have you—often have you thanks therefore—","Desir'd my Cressid in right great exchange,","Whom Troy hath still denied; but this Antenor,","I know, is such a wrest in their affairs","That their negotiations all must slack","Wanting his manage; and they will almost","Give us a prince of blood, a son of Priam,","In change of him. Let him be sent, great Princes,","And he shall buy my daughter; and her presence","Shall quite strike off all service I have done","In most accepted pain."],"id":650,"m":"You have a Trojan prisoner called Antenor, captured yesterday. Troy values him very highly. You have often (and I thank you for it) asked for my Cressida in exchange for important prisoners, and Troy has always refused. But I know this Antenor is so essential to their affairs that all their negotiations will grind to a halt without his management, and they would almost give us a royal prince, a son of Priam, in exchange for him. Let him be sent, great Princes, and he will buy my daughter back. Her presence will completely pay off all the service I have done, so willingly and so painfully."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Let Diomedes bear him,","And bring us Cressid hither. Calchas shall have","What he requests of us. Good Diomed,","Furnish you fairly for this interchange;","Withal, bring word if Hector will tomorrow","Be answer'd in his challenge. Ajax is ready."],"id":651,"m":"Let Diomedes take him and bring Cressida here. Calchas shall have what he asks of us. Good Diomedes, equip yourself properly for this exchange. And bring back word whether Hector's challenge will be answered tomorrow. Ajax is ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["This shall I undertake; and 'tis a burden","Which I am proud to bear."],"id":652,"m":"I'll do this, and it's a burden I'm proud to carry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Diomedes and Calchas.","id":653},{"t":"sd","text":"Achilles and Patroclus stand in their tent.","id":654},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Achilles stands i' th'entrance of his tent.","Please it our general pass strangely by him,","As if he were forgot; and, Princes all,","Lay negligent and loose regard upon him.","I will come last. 'Tis like he'll question me","Why such unplausive eyes are bent, why turn'd on him.","If so, I have derision med'cinable","To use between your strangeness and his pride,","Which his own will shall have desire to drink.","It may do good. Pride hath no other glass","To show itself but pride; for supple knees","Feed arrogance and are the proud man's fees."],"id":655,"m":"Achilles is standing at the entrance of his tent. Please, general, walk past him coldly, as if he were forgotten. And, all you Princes, give him only careless, offhand looks. I will come last. He will probably ask me why such disapproving looks are turned on him. If so, I have some healing scorn to apply between your coldness and his pride, which his own will will want to drink. It may do some good. Pride has no mirror to see itself in except other people's pride, because bending knees feed arrogance and are the proud man's wages."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["We'll execute your purpose, and put on","A form of strangeness as we pass along.","So do each lord; and either greet him not,","Or else disdainfully, which shall shake him more","Than if not look'd on. I will lead the way."],"id":656,"m":"We'll carry out your plan and put on a show of coldness as we go past. Let each lord do the same, and either not greet him or greet him scornfully, which will shake him more than not being looked at. I'll lead the way."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["What comes the general to speak with me?","You know my mind. I'll fight no more 'gainst Troy."],"id":657,"m":"Is the general coming to speak to me? You know my mind. I won't fight against Troy any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["What says Achilles? Would he aught with us?"],"id":658,"m":"What does Achilles say? Does he want anything from us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Would you, my lord, aught with the general?"],"id":659,"m":"Do you want anything from the general, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["No."],"id":660,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Nothing, my lord."],"id":661,"m":"Nothing, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["The better."],"id":662,"m":"So much the better."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Agamemnon and Nestor.","id":663},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Good day, good day."],"id":664,"m":"Good day, good day."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["How do you? How do you?"],"id":665,"m":"How are you? How are you?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":666},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["What, does the cuckold scorn me?"],"id":667,"m":"What, does the cuckold scorn me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["How now, Patroclus?"],"id":668,"m":"Hello, Patroclus."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Good morrow, Ajax."],"id":669,"m":"Good morning, Ajax."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Ha?"],"id":670,"m":"What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Good morrow."],"id":671,"m":"Good morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Ay, and good next day too."],"id":672,"m":"Yes, and good next day too."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":673},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["What mean these fellows? Know they not Achilles?"],"id":674,"m":"What do these fellows mean? Don't they know Achilles?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["They pass by strangely. They were us'd to bend,","To send their smiles before them to Achilles,","To come as humbly as they us'd to creep","To holy altars."],"id":675,"m":"They pass by coldly. They used to bow, to send their smiles ahead of them to Achilles, to come as humbly as they used to creep up to holy altars."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["What, am I poor of late?","'Tis certain, greatness, once fall'n out with fortune,","Must fall out with men too. What the declin'd is,","He shall as soon read in the eyes of others","As feel in his own fall; for men, like butterflies,","Show not their mealy wings but to the summer;","And not a man for being simply man","Hath any honour, but honour for those honours","That are without him, as place, riches, and favour,","Prizes of accident, as oft as merit;","Which when they fall, as being slippery standers,","The love that lean'd on them as slippery too,","Doth one pluck down another, and together","Die in the fall. But 'tis not so with me:","Fortune and I are friends; I do enjoy","At ample point all that I did possess","Save these men's looks; who do, methinks, find out","Something not worth in me such rich beholding","As they have often given. Here is Ulysses.","I'll interrupt his reading.","How now, Ulysses!"],"id":676,"m":"What, have I become poor lately? It's certain that greatness, once it falls out with fortune, must fall out with men too. A fallen man will read his fall in other people's eyes as soon as he feels it himself. For men, like butterflies, only show their powdery wings in summer. And no man is honoured simply for being a man, but for honours that are outside him, like position, wealth and favour, which are prizes of chance as often as of merit. When those fall, being slippery supports, the love that leaned on them, just as slippery, falls too; one pulls the other down and they die together in the fall. But that isn't how it is with me. Fortune and I are friends. I still enjoy in full everything I had, except these men's looks. They seem, I think, to find something in me not worth the rich attention they used to give. Here is Ulysses. I'll interrupt his reading. Hello, Ulysses!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Now, great Thetis' son!"],"id":677,"m":"Hello, great son of Thetis!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["What are you reading?"],"id":678,"m":"What are you reading?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["A strange fellow here","Writes me that man—how dearly ever parted,","How much in having, or without or in—","Cannot make boast to have that which he hath,","Nor feels not what he owes, but by reflection;","As when his virtues shining upon others","Heat them, and they retort that heat again","To the first giver."],"id":679,"m":"A strange writer here tells me that a man, however richly gifted, however much he has inside or outside himself, cannot boast of having what he has, or feel what he owns, except by reflection, as when his virtues shine on others and warm them, and they send that warmth back to the one who first gave it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["This is not strange, Ulysses.","The beauty that is borne here in the face","The bearer knows not, but commends itself","To others' eyes; nor doth the eye itself—","That most pure spirit of sense—behold itself,","Not going from itself; but eye to eye opposed","Salutes each other with each other's form;","For speculation turns not to itself","Till it hath travell'd, and is mirror'd there","Where it may see itself. This is not strange at all."],"id":680,"m":"That isn't strange, Ulysses. The beauty a person carries in his face is not known to him, but shows itself to other people's eyes. Nor does the eye itself, that purest of the senses, see itself, since it cannot go out of itself. But when two eyes face each other, each greets the other with its own image, because sight does not turn back on itself until it has travelled out and been mirrored where it can see itself. This isn't strange at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["I do not strain at the position—","It is familiar—but at the author's drift;","Who, in his circumstance, expressly proves","That no man is the lord of anything,","Though in and of him there be much consisting,","Till he communicate his parts to others;","Nor doth he of himself know them for aught","Till he behold them formed in the applause","Where th'are extended; who, like an arch, reverb'rate","The voice again; or, like a gate of steel","Fronting the sun, receives and renders back","His figure and his heat. I was much rapt in this;","And apprehended here immediately","Th'unknown Ajax. Heavens, what a man is there!","A very horse that has he knows not what!","Nature, what things there are","Most abject in regard and dear in use!","What things again most dear in the esteem","And poor in worth! Now shall we see tomorrow—","An act that very chance doth throw upon him—","Ajax renown'd. O heavens, what some men do,","While some men leave to do!","How some men creep in skittish Fortune's hall,","Whiles others play the idiots in her eyes!","How one man eats into another's pride,","While pride is fasting in his wantonness!","To see these Grecian lords!—why, even already","They clap the lubber Ajax on the shoulder,","As if his foot were on brave Hector's breast,","And great Troy shrieking."],"id":681,"m":"I don't have trouble with the idea, which is familiar, but with where the author is going with it. He argues in detail that no man is master of anything, however much there is in him and of him, until he shares his qualities with others. Nor does he know them to be worth anything himself until he sees them shaped in the applause they receive. That applause, like an arch, echoes the voice back, or, like a steel gate facing the sun, receives and reflects back its image and its heat. I was very absorbed in this, and immediately thought of the unrecognised Ajax. Heavens, what a man he is! A real horse, who has he doesn't know what! Nature, what things there are that are thought worthless and are precious in use! And other things highly valued and poor in worth! Now tomorrow we shall see, through an act that pure chance throws on him, Ajax made famous. Oh heavens, what some men do while other men leave things undone! How some men creep into fickle Fortune's hall while others play the idiot in front of her! How one man feeds on another's glory while the proud man starves in his idleness! To see these Greek lords! Why, already they're slapping that lump Ajax on the shoulder, as if his foot were on brave Hector's chest and great Troy were shrieking."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["I do believe it; for they pass'd by me","As misers do by beggars, neither gave to me","Good word nor look. What, are my deeds forgot?"],"id":682,"m":"I believe it, because they passed me like misers passing beggars; they gave me neither a kind word nor a look. What, are my deeds forgotten?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Time hath, my lord, a wallet at his back,","Wherein he puts alms for oblivion,","A great-siz'd monster of ingratitudes.","Those scraps are good deeds past, which are devour'd","As fast as they are made, forgot as soon","As done. Perseverance, dear my lord,","Keeps honour bright. To have done is to hang","Quite out of fashion, like a rusty mail","In monumental mock'ry. Take the instant way;","For honour travels in a strait so narrow—","Where one but goes abreast. Keep then the path,","For emulation hath a thousand sons","That one by one pursue; if you give way,","Or hedge aside from the direct forthright,","Like to an ent'red tide they all rush by","And leave you hindmost;","Or, like a gallant horse fall'n in first rank,","Lie there for pavement to the abject rear,","O'er-run and trampled on. Then what they do in present,","Though less than yours in past, must o'ertop yours;","For Time is like a fashionable host,","That slightly shakes his parting guest by th'hand;","And with his arms out-stretch'd, as he would fly,","Grasps in the comer. The welcome ever smiles,","And farewell goes out sighing. O, let not virtue seek","Remuneration for the thing it was;","For beauty, wit,","High birth, vigour of bone, desert in service,","Love, friendship, charity, are subjects all","To envious and calumniating Time.","One touch of nature makes the whole world kin—","That all with one consent praise new-born gauds,","Though they are made and moulded of things past,","And give to dust that is a little gilt","More laud than gilt o'er-dusted.","The present eye praises the present object.","Then marvel not, thou great and complete man,","That all the Greeks begin to worship Ajax,","Since things in motion sooner catch the eye","Than what stirs not. The cry went once on thee,","And still it might, and yet it may again,","If thou wouldst not entomb thyself alive","And case thy reputation in thy tent,","Whose glorious deeds but in these fields of late","Made emulous missions 'mongst the gods themselves,","And drave great Mars to faction."],"id":683,"m":"Time, my lord, has a bag on his back in which he puts alms for oblivion, a huge monster of ingratitude. Those scraps are past good deeds, which are devoured as soon as they are done, forgotten as soon as they are finished. Perseverance, my dear lord, keeps honour bright. To have done something is to hang completely out of fashion, like rusty armour on a monument, a mockery. Take the immediate path, because honour travels down a passage so narrow that only one can walk in it at a time. So keep to the path, because rivalry has a thousand sons who follow one after another. If you give way, or step aside from the straight road, they all rush past like a tide coming in and leave you last. Or, like a fine horse that has fallen in the front rank, you lie there as a pavement for the miserable rear, run over and trampled on. Then what they do now, though less than what you did in the past, must top yours. For Time is like a fashionable host who barely shakes his departing guest's hand, and grasps the new arrival with outstretched arms as if he wanted to fly. Welcome always smiles, and farewell goes out sighing. Oh, don't let virtue look for a reward for what it was. Beauty, wit, high birth, strength, merit in service, love, friendship, charity, are all subject to envious, slandering Time. One trait of human nature makes the whole world alike: everyone agrees in praising new-made trinkets, even though they are made and moulded out of old things, and they give more praise to dust with a little gold on it than to gold covered in dust. The eye of the present praises what is present. So don't be surprised, you great and perfect man, that all the Greeks are beginning to worship Ajax, since things that move catch the eye sooner than things that are still. The cheering was once for you, and still could be, and may be again, if you would not bury yourself alive and shut your reputation up in your tent. Your glorious deeds on these very fields only recently made the gods themselves compete and drove great Mars to take sides."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Of this my privacy","I have strong reasons."],"id":684,"m":"I have strong reasons for keeping to myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["But 'gainst your privacy","The reasons are more potent and heroical.","'Tis known, Achilles, that you are in love","With one of Priam's daughters."],"id":685,"m":"But the reasons against your keeping to yourself are stronger and more heroic. It is known, Achilles, that you are in love with one of Priam's daughters."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Ha! known!"],"id":686,"m":"What! Known!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Is that a wonder?","The providence that's in a watchful state","Knows almost every grain of Plutus' gold;","Finds bottom in th'uncomprehensive deeps;","Keeps place with thought, and almost, like the gods,","Do thoughts unveil in their dumb cradles.","There is a mystery—with whom relation","Durst never meddle—in the soul of state,","Which hath an operation more divine","Than breath or pen can give expressure to.","All the commerce that you have had with Troy","As perfectly is ours as yours, my lord;","And better would it fit Achilles much","To throw down Hector than Polyxena.","But it must grieve young Pyrrhus now at home,","When fame shall in our island sound her trump,","And all the Greekish girls shall tripping sing","'Great Hector's sister did Achilles win;","But our great Ajax bravely beat down him.'","Farewell, my lord. I as your lover speak.","The fool slides o'er the ice that you should break."],"id":687,"m":"Is that a surprise? The foresight of a watchful state knows almost every grain of Plutus' gold, finds the bottom of unfathomable depths, keeps pace with thought, and, almost like the gods, uncovers thoughts while they are still unspoken in their cradles. There is a mystery in the soul of the state, which no report has ever dared to meddle with, whose workings are more divine than speech or writing can express. All the dealings you have had with Troy are as well known to us as to you, my lord. And it would suit Achilles much better to throw down Hector than Polyxena. But it must grieve young Pyrrhus, your son at home, when fame blows her trumpet in our island and all the Greek girls sing as they dance, \"Great Hector's sister was won by Achilles, but our great Ajax bravely beat Hector down.\" Goodbye, my lord. I speak as one who loves you. The fool is sliding over the ice that you should break."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":688},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["To this effect, Achilles, have I mov'd you.","A woman impudent and mannish grown","Is not more loath'd than an effeminate man","In time of action. I stand condemn'd for this;","They think my little stomach to the war","And your great love to me restrains you thus.","Sweet, rouse yourself; and the weak wanton Cupid","Shall from your neck unloose his amorous fold,","And, like a dew-drop from the lion's mane,","Be shook to air."],"id":689,"m":"This is what I have urged you to do, Achilles. A shameless, mannish woman is not more hated than an effeminate man in time of war. I am blamed for this. They think my small appetite for war and your great love for me keep you back like this. Sweet, wake yourself up, and weak, playful Cupid will loosen his loving grip on your neck and be shaken off into the air like a dewdrop from a lion's mane."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Shall Ajax fight with Hector?"],"id":690,"m":"Is Ajax going to fight Hector?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Ay, and perhaps receive much honour by him."],"id":691,"m":"Yes, and perhaps win a lot of honour from it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["I see my reputation is at stake;","My fame is shrewdly gor'd."],"id":692,"m":"I see my reputation is at stake. My fame has been badly wounded."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["O, then, beware:","Those wounds heal ill that men do give themselves;","Omission to do what is necessary","Seals a commission to a blank of danger;","And danger, like an ague, subtly taints","Even then when they sit idly in the sun."],"id":693,"m":"Oh, then be careful. The wounds men give themselves heal badly. Failing to do what is necessary signs a blank warrant for danger, and danger, like a fever, infects quietly, even while men sit idle in the sun."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Go call Thersites hither, sweet Patroclus.","I'll send the fool to Ajax, and desire him","T'invite the Trojan lords, after the combat,","To see us here unarm'd. I have a woman's longing,","An appetite that I am sick withal,","To see great Hector in his weeds of peace;","To talk with him, and to behold his visage,","Even to my full of view."],"id":694,"m":"Go and call Thersites here, sweet Patroclus. I'll send the fool to Ajax and ask him to invite the Trojan lords, after the fight, to come and see us here unarmed. I have a woman's craving, an appetite that makes me sick, to see great Hector in his peacetime clothes, to talk with him and look at his face until I've had my fill."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Thersites.","id":695},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","cont":1,"lines":["A labour sav'd!"],"id":696,"m":"That saves some trouble!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["A wonder!"],"id":697,"m":"A wonder!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["What?"],"id":698,"m":"What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Ajax goes up and down the field asking for himself."],"id":699,"m":"Ajax is going up and down the field asking for himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["How so?"],"id":700,"m":"How so?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["He must fight singly tomorrow with Hector, and is so prophetically proud of an heroical cudgelling that he raves in saying nothing."],"id":701,"m":"He has to fight Hector in single combat tomorrow, and he's so proud in advance of a heroic beating that he raves without saying a word."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["How can that be?"],"id":702,"m":"How can that be?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Why, a' stalks up and down like a peacock—a stride and a stand; ruminates like an hostess that hath no arithmetic but her brain to set down her reckoning, bites his lip with a politic regard, as who should say 'There were wit in this head, and 'twould out'; and so there is; but it lies as coldly in him as fire in a flint, which will not show without knocking. The man's undone for ever; for if Hector break not his neck i' th' combat, he'll break't himself in vainglory. He knows not me. I said 'Good morrow, Ajax'; and he replies 'Thanks, Agamemnon.' What think you of this man that takes me for the general? He's grown a very land fish, languageless, a monster. A plague of opinion! A man may wear it on both sides, like leather jerkin."],"id":703,"m":"Why, he struts up and down like a peacock, a stride and then a pause. He broods like a landlady with no arithmetic except her head to work out the bill. He bites his lip with a statesman-like look, as if to say, \"There's wisdom in this head, and it will come out.\" And so there is, but it lies as cold in him as fire in a flint, which won't show unless it's struck. The man is ruined for ever, because if Hector doesn't break his neck in the fight, he'll break it himself with vanity. He doesn't know me. I said \"Good morning, Ajax,\" and he replies \"Thank you, Agamemnon.\" What do you think of a man who takes me for the general? He's become a real fish out of water, speechless, a monster. A plague on reputation! A man can wear it either way out, like a leather jacket."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Thou must be my ambassador to him, Thersites."],"id":704,"m":"You must be my ambassador to him, Thersites."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Who, I? Why, he'll answer nobody; he professes not answering. Speaking is for beggars: he wears his tongue in's arms. I will put on his presence. Let Patroclus make his demands to me, you shall see the pageant of Ajax."],"id":705,"m":"Who, me? Why, he won't answer anyone. He makes a profession of not answering. Speaking is for beggars; he wears his tongue in his arms. I'll put on his manner. Let Patroclus make his requests to me, and you'll see the Ajax show."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["To him, Patroclus. Tell him I humbly desire the valiant Ajax to invite the most valorous Hector to come unarm'd to my tent; and to procure safe conduct for his person of the magnanimous and most illustrious six-or-seven-times-honour'd Captain General of the Grecian army, Agamemnon. Do this."],"id":706,"m":"Go on, Patroclus. Tell him I humbly ask the brave Ajax to invite the most valiant Hector to come unarmed to my tent, and to get a guarantee of safe passage for him from the generous and most illustrious, six or seven times honoured Commander-in-Chief of the Greek army, Agamemnon. Go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Jove bless great Ajax!"],"id":707,"m":"Jove bless great Ajax!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Hum!"],"id":708,"m":"Hmm!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["I come from the worthy Achilles—"],"id":709,"m":"I come from the worthy Achilles..."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Ha!"],"id":710,"m":"Ha!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Who most humbly desires you to invite Hector to his tent—"],"id":711,"m":"...who most humbly asks you to invite Hector to his tent..."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Hum!"],"id":712,"m":"Hmm!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["And to procure safe conduct from Agamemnon."],"id":713,"m":"...and to get a guarantee of safe passage from Agamemnon."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Agamemnon?"],"id":714,"m":"Agamemnon?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Ay, my lord."],"id":715,"m":"Yes, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Ha!"],"id":716,"m":"Ha!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["What you say to't?"],"id":717,"m":"What do you say to that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["God buy you, with all my heart."],"id":718,"m":"God be with you, with all my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Your answer, sir."],"id":719,"m":"Your answer, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["If tomorrow be a fair day, by eleven of the clock it will go one way or other. Howsoever, he shall pay for me ere he has me."],"id":720,"m":"If tomorrow is a fine day, by eleven o'clock it will go one way or the other. Either way, he'll pay for me before he gets me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Your answer, sir."],"id":721,"m":"Your answer, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Fare ye well, with all my heart."],"id":722,"m":"Goodbye, with all my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Why, but he is not in this tune, is he?"],"id":723,"m":"Why, he isn't in this mood, is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["No, but out of tune thus. What music will be in him when Hector has knock'd out his brains, I know not; but, I am sure, none; unless the fiddler Apollo get his sinews to make catlings on."],"id":724,"m":"No, but out of tune like this. What music there will be in him when Hector has knocked his brains out, I don't know. None, I'm sure, unless the fiddler Apollo takes his sinews to make strings."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Come, thou shalt bear a letter to him straight."],"id":725,"m":"Come on, you'll take a letter to him straight away."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Let me bear another to his horse; for that's the more capable creature."],"id":726,"m":"Let me take another to his horse, because that's the more intelligent creature."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["My mind is troubled, like a fountain stirr'd;","And I myself see not the bottom of it."],"id":727,"m":"My mind is troubled, like a fountain that has been stirred up, and I can't see to the bottom of it myself."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Achilles and Patroclus.","id":728},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Would the fountain of your mind were clear again, that I might water an ass at it. I had rather be a tick in a sheep than such a valiant ignorance."],"id":729,"m":"I wish the fountain of your mind were clear again, so I could water a donkey at it. I'd rather be a tick on a sheep than such a brave ignoramus."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":730},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Troy. A street.","id":731},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, at one side, Aeneas and servant with a torch; at another Paris, Deiphobus, Antenor, Diomedes the Grecian, and others, with torches.","id":732},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["See, ho! Who is that there?"],"id":733,"m":"Look, there! Who's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DEIPHOBUS","lines":["It is the Lord Aeneas."],"id":734,"m":"It's Lord Aeneas."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Is the Prince there in person?","Had I so good occasion to lie long","As you, Prince Paris, nothing but heavenly business","Should rob my bed-mate of my company."],"id":735,"m":"Is the Prince here in person? If I had as good a reason to stay in bed late as you, Prince Paris, nothing but the business of heaven would rob my bedfellow of my company."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["That's my mind too. Good morrow, Lord Aeneas."],"id":736,"m":"I think so too. Good morning, Lord Aeneas."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["A valiant Greek, Aeneas—take his hand:","Witness the process of your speech, wherein","You told how Diomed, a whole week by days,","Did haunt you in the field."],"id":737,"m":"A brave Greek, Aeneas. Take his hand. You yourself said so, when you told how Diomedes chased you on the battlefield every day for a whole week."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Health to you, valiant sir,","During all question of the gentle truce;","But when I meet you arm'd, as black defiance","As heart can think or courage execute."],"id":738,"m":"Good health to you, brave sir, while this gentle truce lasts. But when I meet you armed, I wish you the blackest defiance a heart can think of or courage can carry out."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["The one and other Diomed embraces.","Our bloods are now in calm; and so long health!","But when contention and occasion meet,","By Jove, I'll play the hunter for thy life","With all my force, pursuit, and policy."],"id":739,"m":"Diomedes welcomes both. Our blood is calm now, so long life to you! But when the quarrel and the chance meet, by Jove, I'll hunt for your life with all my strength, pursuit and cunning."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["And thou shalt hunt a lion that will fly","With his face backward. In humane gentleness,","Welcome to Troy! Now, by Anchises' life,","Welcome indeed! By Venus' hand I swear","No man alive can love in such a sort","The thing he means to kill, more excellently."],"id":740,"m":"And you'll be hunting a lion that runs away with its face turned back towards you. With human courtesy, welcome to Troy! Now, by my father Anchises' life, welcome indeed! I swear by Venus' hand, no man alive can love so well the thing he means to kill."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["We sympathise. Jove let Aeneas live,","If to my sword his fate be not the glory,","A thousand complete courses of the sun!","But in mine emulous honour let him die","With every joint a wound, and that tomorrow!"],"id":741,"m":"We feel the same. May Jove let Aeneas live a thousand full years, if his death is not to bring glory to my sword! But for my competing honour, let him die with a wound in every joint, and tomorrow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["We know each other well."],"id":742,"m":"We know each other well."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["We do; and long to know each other worse."],"id":743,"m":"We do, and long to know each other worse."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["This is the most despiteful gentle greeting","The noblest hateful love, that e'er I heard of.","What business, lord, so early?"],"id":744,"m":"This is the most spiteful friendly greeting, the noblest hate-filled love, I ever heard of. What business are you on so early, lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["I was sent for to the King; but why, I know not."],"id":745,"m":"I was sent for by the King, but I don't know why."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["His purpose meets you: 'twas to bring this Greek","To Calchas' house, and there to render him,","For the enfreed Antenor, the fair Cressid.","Let's have your company; or, if you please,","Haste there before us. I constantly believe—","Or rather call my thought a certain knowledge—","My brother Troilus lodges there tonight.","Rouse him and give him note of our approach,","With the whole quality wherefore; I fear","We shall be much unwelcome."],"id":746,"m":"His purpose is coming to meet you: it was for you to bring this Greek to Calchas' house, and there hand over to him the fair Cressida in exchange for the freed Antenor. Come with us, or, if you prefer, hurry there ahead of us. I firmly believe (or rather, I'd call it certain knowledge) that my brother Troilus is staying there tonight. Wake him and tell him we're coming, and the whole reason why. I'm afraid we'll be very unwelcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["That I assure you:","Troilus had rather Troy were borne to Greece","Than Cressid borne from Troy."],"id":747,"m":"That I can promise you. Troilus would rather Troy were carried off to Greece than Cressida carried away from Troy."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["There is no help;","The bitter disposition of the time","Will have it so. On, lord; we'll follow you."],"id":748,"m":"It can't be helped. The bitter needs of the time make it so. Go on, lord; we'll follow you."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Good morrow, all."],"id":749,"m":"Good morning, everyone."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit with servant.","id":750},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["And tell me, noble Diomed, faith, tell me true,","Even in the soul of sound good-fellowship,","Who in your thoughts deserves fair Helen best,","Myself, or Menelaus?"],"id":751,"m":"And tell me, noble Diomedes, honestly, tell me truly, in the spirit of real friendship: who, in your opinion, deserves fair Helen more, me or Menelaus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Both alike:","He merits well to have her that doth seek her,","Not making any scruple of her soilure,","With such a hell of pain and world of charge;","And you as well to keep her that defend her,","Not palating the taste of her dishonour,","With such a costly loss of wealth and friends.","He like a puling cuckold would drink up","The lees and dregs of a flat tamed piece;","You, like a lecher, out of whorish loins","Are pleas'd to breed out your inheritors.","Both merits pois'd, each weighs nor less nor more,","But he as he, the heavier for a whore."],"id":752,"m":"Both equally. He deserves to have her, since he seeks her back without caring about her pollution, at the cost of such hellish pain and a world of expense; and you deserve to keep her, since you defend her without tasting her dishonour, at the cost of such a loss of wealth and friends. He, like a whining cuckold, would drink up the stale dregs of a flat, used-up barrel. You, like a lecher, are happy to breed your heirs from a whore's body. Weigh both claims, and neither is heavier or lighter; but each of you is weighed down by a whore."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["You are too bitter to your country-woman."],"id":753,"m":"You are too harsh to your countrywoman."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["She's bitter to her country. Hear me, Paris:","For every false drop in her bawdy veins","A Grecian's life hath sunk; for every scruple","Of her contaminated carrion weight","A Trojan hath been slain. Since she could speak,","She hath not given so many good words breath","As for her Greeks and Trojans suff'red death."],"id":754,"m":"She has been harsh to her country. Listen, Paris: for every false drop in her lustful veins, a Greek's life has gone down; for every tiny ounce of her corrupted carcass, a Trojan has been killed. Since she could first speak, she hasn't spoken as many good words as Greeks and Trojans have died for her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["Fair Diomed, you do as chapmen do,","Dispraise the thing that you desire to buy;","But we in silence hold this virtue well,","We'll not commend what we intend to sell.","Here lies our way."],"id":755,"m":"Fair Diomedes, you do what dealers do, running down the thing you want to buy. But we keep quiet about this virtue: we won't praise what we don't intend to sell. This is our way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":756},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Troy. The court of Pandarus' house.","id":757},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Troilus and Cressida.","id":758},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Dear, trouble not yourself; the morn is cold."],"id":759,"m":"Dear, don't trouble yourself; the morning is cold."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Then, sweet my lord, I'll call mine uncle down;","He shall unbolt the gates."],"id":760,"m":"Then, my sweet lord, I'll call my uncle down. He'll unbolt the gates."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Trouble him not;","To bed, to bed! Sleep kill those pretty eyes,","And give as soft attachment to thy senses","As infants empty of all thought!"],"id":761,"m":"Don't trouble him. To bed, to bed! May sleep close those pretty eyes and hold your senses as softly as it holds babies who have no thoughts!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Good morrow, then."],"id":762,"m":"Good morning, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["I prithee now, to bed."],"id":763,"m":"Please, go to bed now."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Are you aweary of me?"],"id":764,"m":"Are you tired of me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O Cressida! but that the busy day,","Wak'd by the lark, hath rous'd the ribald crows,","And dreaming night will hide our joys no longer,","I would not from thee."],"id":765,"m":"Oh Cressida! If the busy day, woken by the lark, hadn't roused the noisy crows, and if dreaming night would hide our joys any longer, I wouldn't leave you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Night hath been too brief."],"id":766,"m":"The night has been too short."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Beshrew the witch! with venomous wights she stays","As tediously as hell, but flies the grasps of love","With wings more momentary-swift than thought.","You will catch cold, and curse me."],"id":767,"m":"Curse the witch! With evil people she stays as long and wearily as hell, but she flies from the arms of love on wings faster than thought. You'll catch cold and curse me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Prithee tarry.","You men will never tarry.","O foolish Cressid! I might have still held off,","And then you would have tarried. Hark! there's one up."],"id":768,"m":"Please stay. You men never stay. Oh foolish Cressida! I could have kept holding off, and then you would have stayed. Listen! Someone's up."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["[Within] What's all the doors open here?"],"id":769,"m":"(Offstage) Why are all the doors open here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["It is your uncle."],"id":770,"m":"It's your uncle."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pandarus.","id":771},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["A pestilence on him! Now will he be mocking.","I shall have such a life!"],"id":772,"m":"A plague on him! Now he'll make fun of us. I'll never hear the end of it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["How now, how now! How go maidenheads?","Here, you maid! Where's my cousin Cressid?"],"id":773,"m":"Well, well! How are maidenheads doing? Here, you maid! Where's my niece Cressida?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Go hang yourself, you naughty mocking uncle.","You bring me to do, and then you flout me too."],"id":774,"m":"Go hang yourself, you naughty, mocking uncle. You lead me into it, and then you make fun of me too."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["To do what? to do what? Let her say what.","What have I brought you to do?"],"id":775,"m":"Into what? Into what? Let her say what. What have I led you into?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Come, come, beshrew your heart! You'll ne'er be good, nor suffer others."],"id":776,"m":"Come on, curse you! You'll never be good yourself, or let others be."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Ha, ha! Alas, poor wretch! Ah, poor capocchia! Hast not slept tonight? Would he not, a naughty man, let it sleep? A bugbear take him!"],"id":777,"m":"Ha ha! Alas, poor thing! Ah, poor little fool! Haven't you slept tonight? Wouldn't he let it sleep, the naughty man? May a bogeyman take him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Did not I tell you? Would he were knock'd i' th' head!"],"id":778,"m":"Didn't I tell you? I wish he'd be hit on the head!"},{"t":"sd","text":"One knocks.","id":779},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","cont":1,"lines":["Who's that at door? Good uncle, go and see.","My lord, come you again into my chamber.","You smile and mock me, as if I meant naughtily."],"id":780,"m":"Who's that at the door? Good uncle, go and see. My lord, come back into my room. You're smiling and teasing me, as if I meant something naughty."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Ha! ha!"],"id":781,"m":"Ha, ha!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Come, you are deceiv'd, I think of no such thing."],"id":782,"m":"Come on, you're wrong; I'm thinking of no such thing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Knock.","id":783},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","cont":1,"lines":["How earnestly they knock! Pray you come in:","I would not for half Troy have you seen here."],"id":784,"m":"How urgently they're knocking! Please come in. I wouldn't have you seen here for half of Troy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Troilus and Cressida.","id":785},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Who's there? What's the matter? Will you beat down the door? How now? What's the matter?"],"id":786,"m":"Who's there? What's the matter? Are you going to knock the door down? Well? What's the matter?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aeneas.","id":787},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Good morrow, lord, good morrow."],"id":788,"m":"Good morning, lord, good morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Who's there? My lord Aeneas? By my troth,","I knew you not. What news with you so early?"],"id":789,"m":"Who's there? My lord Aeneas? Honestly, I didn't recognise you. What news do you have so early?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Is not Prince Troilus here?"],"id":790,"m":"Isn't Prince Troilus here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Here! What should he do here?"],"id":791,"m":"Here! What would he be doing here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Come, he is here, my lord; do not deny him.","It doth import him much to speak with me."],"id":792,"m":"Come on, he's here, my lord; don't deny it. It's very important for him to speak with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Is he here, say you? It's more than I know, I'll be sworn. For my own part, I came in late. What should he do here?"],"id":793,"m":"Is he here, you say? That's more than I know, I swear. For my part, I came in late. What would he be doing here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Who, nay then! Come, come, you'll do him wrong ere you are ware; you'll be so true to him to be false to him. Do not you know of him, but yet go fetch him hither; go."],"id":794,"m":"Oh, come on now! Come, come, you'll do him harm before you know it; you'll be so loyal to him that you'll betray him. Pretend you don't know where he is, but go and fetch him here anyway. Go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Troilus.","id":795},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["How now! What's the matter?"],"id":796,"m":"What's this? What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["My lord, I scarce have leisure to salute you,","My matter is so rash. There is at hand","Paris your brother, and Deiphobus,","The Grecian Diomed, and our Antenor","Deliver'd to us; and for him forthwith,","Ere the first sacrifice, within this hour,","We must give up to Diomedes' hand","The Lady Cressida."],"id":797,"m":"My lord, I hardly have time to greet you, my business is so urgent. Your brother Paris is close by, with Deiphobus, the Greek Diomedes and our Antenor, who has been handed back to us. In exchange for him, at once, before the first sacrifice of the day, within this hour, we must hand over the Lady Cressida to Diomedes."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Is it so concluded?"],"id":798,"m":"Has this been decided?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["By Priam and the general state of Troy.","They are at hand, and ready to effect it."],"id":799,"m":"By Priam and the council of Troy. They are close by and ready to carry it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["How my achievements mock me!","I will go meet them; and, my Lord Aeneas,","We met by chance; you did not find me here."],"id":800,"m":"How my successes mock me! I'll go and meet them. And, my Lord Aeneas, we met by chance; you didn't find me here."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Good, good, my lord, the secrets of neighbour Pandar","Have not more gift in taciturnity."],"id":801,"m":"Good, good, my lord. The secrets of nature don't keep silent better than I will."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Troilus and Aeneas.","id":802},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Is't possible? No sooner got but lost? The devil take Antenor! The young prince will go mad. A plague upon Antenor! I would they had broke's neck."],"id":803,"m":"Is it possible? No sooner won than lost? The devil take Antenor! The young prince will go mad. A plague on Antenor! I wish they'd broken his neck."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Cressida.","id":804},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["How now! What's the matter? Who was here?"],"id":805,"m":"What's happening? What's the matter? Who was here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Ah, ah!"],"id":806,"m":"Ah, ah!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Why sigh you so profoundly? Where's my lord? Gone? Tell me, sweet uncle, what's the matter?"],"id":807,"m":"Why are you sighing so deeply? Where's my lord? Gone? Tell me, sweet uncle, what's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Would I were as deep under the earth as I am above!"],"id":808,"m":"I wish I were as deep under the earth as I am above it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["O the gods! What's the matter?"],"id":809,"m":"Oh gods! What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Pray thee get thee in. Would thou hadst ne'er been born! I knew thou wouldst be his death! O, poor gentleman! A plague upon Antenor!"],"id":810,"m":"Please, go inside. I wish you'd never been born! I knew you'd be the death of him! Oh, poor gentleman! A plague on Antenor!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Good uncle, I beseech you, on my knees I beseech you, what's the matter?"],"id":811,"m":"Good uncle, I beg you, on my knees I beg you, what's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Thou must be gone, wench, thou must be gone; thou art chang'd for Antenor; thou must to thy father, and be gone from Troilus. 'Twill be his death; 'twill be his bane; he cannot bear it."],"id":812,"m":"You must go, girl, you must go. You've been exchanged for Antenor. You must go to your father and leave Troilus. It will be the death of him; it will destroy him; he can't bear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["O you immortal gods! I will not go."],"id":813,"m":"Oh you immortal gods! I won't go."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Thou must."],"id":814,"m":"You must."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["I will not, uncle. I have forgot my father;","I know no touch of consanguinity,","No kin, no love, no blood, no soul so near me","As the sweet Troilus. O you gods divine,","Make Cressid's name the very crown of falsehood,","If ever she leave Troilus! Time, force, and death,","Do to this body what extremes you can,","But the strong base and building of my love","Is as the very centre of the earth,","Drawing all things to it. I'll go in and weep—"],"id":815,"m":"I won't, uncle. I have forgotten my father. I feel no tie of blood, no family, no love, no blood, no soul as close to me as sweet Troilus. Oh you divine gods, make Cressida's name the very crown of falseness if she ever leaves Troilus! Time, force and death, do the worst you can to this body, but the strong foundation and building of my love is like the very centre of the earth, drawing everything towards it. I'll go in and weep..."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Do, do."],"id":816,"m":"Do, do."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Tear my bright hair, and scratch my praised cheeks,","Crack my clear voice with sobs and break my heart,","With sounding 'Troilus.' I will not go from Troy."],"id":817,"m":"...tear my bright hair, scratch my much-praised cheeks, crack my clear voice with sobs and break my heart crying \"Troilus.\" I will not leave Troy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":818},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Troy. A street before Pandarus' house.","id":819},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Paris, Troilus, Aeneas, Deiphobus, Antenor and Diomedes.","id":820},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["It is great morning; and the hour prefix'd","For her delivery to this valiant Greek","Comes fast upon. Good my brother Troilus,","Tell you the lady what she is to do","And haste her to the purpose."],"id":821,"m":"It is broad daylight, and the hour fixed for handing her over to this brave Greek is coming fast. My good brother Troilus, tell the lady what she must do and hurry her along."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Walk into her house.","I'll bring her to the Grecian presently;","And to his hand when I deliver her,","Think it an altar, and thy brother Troilus","A priest, there off'ring to it his own heart."],"id":822,"m":"Walk to her house. I'll bring her to the Greek at once. And when I hand her over to him, think of his hand as an altar, and your brother Troilus as a priest, offering his own heart on it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":823},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["I know what 'tis to love,","And would, as I shall pity, I could help!","Please you walk in, my lords?"],"id":824,"m":"I know what it is to love, and I wish I could help as much as I pity him! Will you walk in, my lords?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":825},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"Troy. Pandarus' house.","id":826},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pandarus and Cressida.","id":827},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Be moderate, be moderate."],"id":828,"m":"Calm down, calm down."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Why tell you me of moderation?","The grief is fine, full, perfect, that I taste,","And violenteth in a sense as strong","As that which causeth it. How can I moderate it?","If I could temporize with my affections","Or brew it to a weak and colder palate,","The like allayment could I give my grief.","My love admits no qualifying dross;","No more my grief, in such a precious loss."],"id":829,"m":"Why do you talk to me about calming down? The grief I taste is pure, full and complete, and it rages as strongly as the thing that causes it. How can I calm it? If I could compromise with my feelings, or water them down to a weaker, colder taste, I could calm my grief in the same way. My love won't allow any mixing with impurities, and nor will my grief, at such a precious loss."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Troilus.","id":830},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Here, here, here he comes. Ah, sweet ducks!"],"id":831,"m":"Here, here, here he comes. Ah, sweet ducks!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["[Embracing him] O Troilus! Troilus!"],"id":832,"m":"(Embracing him) Oh Troilus! Troilus!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["What a pair of spectacles is here! Let me embrace too. 'O heart,' as the goodly saying is,—","~O heart, heavy heart,","~Why sigh'st thou without breaking?","where he answers again","~Because thou canst not ease thy smart","~By friendship nor by speaking.","There was never a truer rhyme. Let us cast away nothing, for we may live to have need of such a verse. We see it, we see it. How now, lambs!"],"id":833,"m":"What a pair of spectacles this is! Let me embrace too. \"Oh heart,\" as the good old saying goes: \"Oh heart, heavy heart, why do you sigh without breaking?\" And then it answers: \"Because you cannot ease your pain by friendship or by speaking.\" There was never a truer rhyme. Let's not throw anything away, because we may live to need a verse like that. We see it, we see it. Well, lambs!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Cressid, I love thee in so strain'd a purity","That the bless'd gods, as angry with my fancy,","More bright in zeal than the devotion which","Cold lips blow to their deities, take thee from me."],"id":834,"m":"Cressida, I love you with such a refined purity that the blessed gods, as if angry with my love, which burns brighter than the devotion cold lips breathe to their gods, are taking you from me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Have the gods envy?"],"id":835,"m":"Are the gods jealous?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Ay, ay, ay, ay; 'tis too plain a case."],"id":836,"m":"Yes, yes, yes, yes; it's all too plain."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["And is it true that I must go from Troy?"],"id":837,"m":"And is it true that I must leave Troy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["A hateful truth."],"id":838,"m":"A hateful truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["What! and from Troilus too?"],"id":839,"m":"What! And leave Troilus too?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["From Troy and Troilus."],"id":840,"m":"Troy and Troilus."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Is't possible?"],"id":841,"m":"Is it possible?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["And suddenly; where injury of chance","Puts back leave-taking, justles roughly by","All time of pause, rudely beguiles our lips","Of all rejoindure, forcibly prevents","Our lock'd embrasures, strangles our dear vows","Even in the birth of our own labouring breath.","We two, that with so many thousand sighs","Did buy each other, must poorly sell ourselves","With the rude brevity and discharge of one.","Injurious time now with a robber's haste","Crams his rich thiev'ry up, he knows not how.","As many farewells as be stars in heaven,","With distinct breath and consign'd kisses to them,","He fumbles up into a loose adieu,","And scants us with a single famish'd kiss,","Distasted with the salt of broken tears."],"id":842,"m":"And immediately, so that the injury of chance puts off our farewells, roughly shoves aside any pause, rudely cheats our lips of any reply, forcibly stops our close embraces, and strangles our dear vows in the very birth of our struggling breath. We two, who bought each other with so many thousand sighs, must sell ourselves cheaply with the rough shortness of a single one. Cruel time, with a robber's hurry, now crams his rich loot together any old how. As many farewells as there are stars in the sky, each with its own breath and the kisses that seal it, he bundles into one careless goodbye, and stints us to a single starved kiss, spoiled by the salt of broken-off tears."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["[Within] My lord, is the lady ready?"],"id":843,"m":"(Offstage) My lord, is the lady ready?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Hark! you are call'd. Some say the Genius","Cries so to him that instantly must die.","Bid them have patience; she shall come anon."],"id":844,"m":"Listen! You are being called. Some say the guardian spirit calls like this to a man who is about to die. Tell them to be patient; she'll come soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Where are my tears? Rain, to lay this wind, or my heart will be blown up by my throat!"],"id":845,"m":"Where are my tears? Rain, to calm this wind of sighs, or my heart will be blown up through my throat!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":846},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["I must then to the Grecians?"],"id":847,"m":"So I must go to the Greeks?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["No remedy."],"id":848,"m":"There's no help for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["A woeful Cressid 'mongst the merry Greeks!","When shall we see again?"],"id":849,"m":"A grieving Cressida among the merry Greeks! When shall we see each other again?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Hear me, my love. Be thou but true of heart."],"id":850,"m":"Listen to me, my love. Only be faithful in your heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["I true? How now! What wicked deem is this?"],"id":851,"m":"Me faithful? What! What wicked thought is this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Nay, we must use expostulation kindly,","For it is parting from us.","I speak not 'Be thou true' as fearing thee,","For I will throw my glove to Death himself","That there's no maculation in thy heart;","But 'Be thou true' say I to fashion in","My sequent protestation: be thou true,","And I will see thee."],"id":852,"m":"No, we must talk gently, because it is leaving us. I don't say \"Be faithful\" because I fear you, for I would throw down my glove to Death himself to defend that there is no stain in your heart. I say \"Be faithful\" to lead into the promise that follows: be faithful, and I will come and see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["O! you shall be expos'd, my lord, to dangers","As infinite as imminent! But I'll be true."],"id":853,"m":"Oh, you'll be exposed to dangers, my lord, as endless as they are close! But I'll be faithful."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["And I'll grow friend with danger. Wear this sleeve."],"id":854,"m":"And I'll make friends with danger. Wear this sleeve."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["And you this glove. When shall I see you?"],"id":855,"m":"And you wear this glove. When shall I see you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["I will corrupt the Grecian sentinels","To give thee nightly visitation.","But yet be true."],"id":856,"m":"I will bribe the Greek sentries so that I can visit you every night. But still, be faithful."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["O heavens! 'Be true' again!"],"id":857,"m":"Oh heavens! \"Be faithful\" again!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Hear why I speak it, love.","The Grecian youths are full of quality;","They're loving, well compos'd, with gifts of nature,","Flowing and swelling o'er with arts and exercise.","How novelty may move, and parts with person,","Alas, a kind of godly jealousy,","Which, I beseech you, call a virtuous sin,","Makes me afear'd."],"id":858,"m":"Hear why I say it, love. The Greek young men are very accomplished. They are loving, well made, with natural gifts, overflowing with skills and training. How novelty, and talents together with good looks, may move you... alas, a kind of holy jealousy, which I beg you to call a virtuous sin, makes me afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["O heavens! you love me not!"],"id":859,"m":"Oh heavens! You don't love me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Die I a villain then!","In this I do not call your faith in question","So mainly as my merit. I cannot sing,","Nor heel the high lavolt, nor sweeten talk,","Nor play at subtle games; fair virtues all,","To which the Grecians are most prompt and pregnant;","But I can tell that in each grace of these","There lurks a still and dumb-discoursive devil","That tempts most cunningly. But be not tempted."],"id":860,"m":"Then let me die a villain! In this I don't question your faithfulness as much as my own merit. I can't sing, or dance the high lavolta, or make sweet conversation, or play clever games. They are all fine accomplishments, which the Greeks are very quick and skilled at. But I can tell you that in each of these graces there lurks a quiet devil that speaks without words and tempts most cunningly. But don't be tempted."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Do you think I will?"],"id":861,"m":"Do you think I will?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["No.","But something may be done that we will not;","And sometimes we are devils to ourselves,","When we will tempt the frailty of our powers,","Presuming on their changeful potency."],"id":862,"m":"No. But sometimes we do things we don't intend to do, and sometimes we are devils to ourselves, when we test the frailty of our powers, relying too much on their changeable strength."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["[Within] Nay, good my lord!"],"id":863,"m":"(Offstage) Come on, my good lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Come, kiss; and let us part."],"id":864,"m":"Come, kiss, and let's part."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["[Within] Brother Troilus!"],"id":865,"m":"(Offstage) Brother Troilus!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Good brother, come you hither;","And bring Aeneas and the Grecian with you."],"id":866,"m":"Good brother, come here, and bring Aeneas and the Greek with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["My lord, will you be true?"],"id":867,"m":"My lord, will you be faithful?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Who, I? Alas, it is my vice, my fault!","Whiles others fish with craft for great opinion,","I with great truth catch mere simplicity;","Whilst some with cunning gild their copper crowns,","With truth and plainness I do wear mine bare.","Fear not my truth: the moral of my wit","Is plain and true; there's all the reach of it."],"id":868,"m":"Who, me? Alas, it is my vice, my fault! While others fish cleverly for a great reputation, I catch only a name for simplicity with my great truthfulness. While some cunningly gild their copper crowns, I wear mine bare, with truth and plainness. Don't fear my faithfulness: the whole meaning of my mind is plain and true; that's as far as it goes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aeneas, Paris, Antenor, Deiphobus and Diomedes.","id":869},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","cont":1,"lines":["Welcome, Sir Diomed! Here is the lady","Which for Antenor we deliver you;","At the port, lord, I'll give her to thy hand,","And by the way possess thee what she is.","Entreat her fair; and, by my soul, fair Greek,","If e'er thou stand at mercy of my sword,","Name Cressid, and thy life shall be as safe","As Priam is in Ilion."],"id":870,"m":"Welcome, Sir Diomedes! Here is the lady we are handing over to you in exchange for Antenor. At the city gate, lord, I'll give her into your hand, and on the way I'll tell you what she is. Treat her well. And, by my soul, fair Greek, if you are ever at the mercy of my sword, say \"Cressida,\" and your life will be as safe as Priam is in Troy."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Fair Lady Cressid,","So please you, save the thanks this prince expects.","The lustre in your eye, heaven in your cheek,","Pleads your fair usage; and to Diomed","You shall be mistress, and command him wholly."],"id":871,"m":"Fair Lady Cressida, if you please, don't bother with the thanks this prince expects. The shine in your eyes and the heaven in your cheeks plead for your good treatment, and you shall be mistress to Diomedes and command him completely."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Grecian, thou dost not use me courteously","To shame the zeal of my petition to thee","In praising her. I tell thee, lord of Greece,","She is as far high-soaring o'er thy praises","As thou unworthy to be call'd her servant.","I charge thee use her well, even for my charge;","For, by the dreadful Pluto, if thou dost not,","Though the great bulk Achilles be thy guard,","I'll cut thy throat."],"id":872,"m":"Greek, you don't treat me courteously, shaming the sincerity of my request by praising her. I tell you, lord of Greece, she soars as far above your praises as you are unworthy to be called her servant. I order you to treat her well, simply because I order it. For, by dreadful Pluto, if you don't, even if great bulky Achilles is your bodyguard, I'll cut your throat."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["O, be not mov'd, Prince Troilus.","Let me be privileg'd by my place and message","To be a speaker free: when I am hence","I'll answer to my lust. And know you, lord,","I'll nothing do on charge: to her own worth","She shall be priz'd. But that you say 'Be't so,'","I speak it in my spirit and honour, 'No.'"],"id":873,"m":"Oh, don't get upset, Prince Troilus. Let my position as an envoy give me the right to speak freely. When I am gone from here, I'll do as I please. And know this, lord: I'll do nothing because I am ordered to. She will be valued for her own worth. But when you say \"Do it,\" I say, on my spirit and honour, \"No.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Come, to the port. I'll tell thee, Diomed,","This brave shall oft make thee to hide thy head.","Lady, give me your hand; and, as we walk,","To our own selves bend we our needful talk."],"id":874,"m":"Come, to the gate. I tell you, Diomedes, this boast will often make you hide your head. Lady, give me your hand, and as we walk, let's say what we need to say to each other."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Troilus, Cressida and Diomedes.","id":875},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound trumpet.","id":876},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["Hark! Hector's trumpet."],"id":877,"m":"Listen! Hector's trumpet."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["How have we spent this morning!","The Prince must think me tardy and remiss,","That swore to ride before him to the field."],"id":878,"m":"How we have wasted this morning! The Prince must think me late and careless, when I swore to ride ahead of him to the battlefield."},{"t":"sp","who":"PARIS","lines":["'Tis Troilus' fault. Come, come to field with him."],"id":879,"m":"It's Troilus' fault. Come, come to the field with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DEIPHOBUS","lines":["Let us make ready straight."],"id":880,"m":"Let's get ready at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Yea, with a bridegroom's fresh alacrity","Let us address to tend on Hector's heels.","The glory of our Troy doth this day lie","On his fair worth and single chivalry."],"id":881,"m":"Yes, with a bridegroom's eager readiness, let's prepare to follow at Hector's heels. The glory of our Troy depends today on his fine worth and his single knighthood."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":882},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"The Grecian camp. Lists set out.","id":883},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ajax, armed; Agamemnon, Achilles, Patroclus, Menelaus, Ulysses, Nestor and others.","id":884},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Here art thou in appointment fresh and fair,","Anticipating time with starting courage.","Give with thy trumpet a loud note to Troy,","Thou dreadful Ajax, that the appalled air","May pierce the head of the great combatant,","And hale him hither."],"id":885,"m":"Here you are, fresh and splendid in your armour, ahead of time in your eager courage. Sound a loud note to Troy with your trumpet, dreadful Ajax, so that the terrified air may pierce the ears of the great fighter and drag him here."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Thou, trumpet, there's my purse.","Now crack thy lungs and split thy brazen pipe;","Blow, villain, till thy sphered bias cheek","Out-swell the colic of puff'd Aquilon.","Come, stretch thy chest, and let thy eyes spout blood:","Thou blowest for Hector."],"id":886,"m":"You, trumpeter, there's my purse. Now crack your lungs and split your brass pipe. Blow, you rascal, until your round, puffed-out cheek swells bigger than the bloated north wind. Come on, stretch your chest and let your eyes spout blood. You're blowing for Hector."},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpet sounds.","id":887},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["No trumpet answers."],"id":888,"m":"No trumpet answers."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["'Tis but early days."],"id":889,"m":"It's still early."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Is not yond Diomed, with Calchas' daughter?"],"id":890,"m":"Isn't that Diomedes over there, with Calchas' daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["'Tis he, I ken the manner of his gait:","He rises on the toe. That spirit of his","In aspiration lifts him from the earth."],"id":891,"m":"It's him; I recognise the way he walks. He rises on his toes. That spirit of his lifts him off the ground with ambition."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Diomedes and Cressida.","id":892},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Is this the Lady Cressid?"],"id":893,"m":"Is this the Lady Cressida?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Even she."],"id":894,"m":"It is."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Most dearly welcome to the Greeks, sweet lady."],"id":895,"m":"A dear welcome to the Greeks, sweet lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Our general doth salute you with a kiss."],"id":896,"m":"Our general greets you with a kiss."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Yet is the kindness but particular;","'Twere better she were kiss'd in general."],"id":897,"m":"But that kindness is only from one person. It would be better if she were kissed by everyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["And very courtly counsel: I'll begin.","So much for Nestor."],"id":898,"m":"And very courtly advice. I'll begin. (He kisses her.) So much for Nestor."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["I'll take that winter from your lips, fair lady.","Achilles bids you welcome."],"id":899,"m":"I'll take that winter off your lips, fair lady. Achilles bids you welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["I had good argument for kissing once."],"id":900,"m":"I had a good reason for kissing once."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["But that's no argument for kissing now;","For thus popp'd Paris in his hardiment,","And parted thus you and your argument."],"id":901,"m":"But that's no reason for kissing now, because this is how Paris boldly popped in and separated you from your reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["O deadly gall, and theme of all our scorns!","For which we lose our heads to gild his horns."],"id":902,"m":"Oh deadly bitterness, and the subject of all our scorn! We lose our heads to gild his cuckold's horns."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["The first was Menelaus' kiss; this, mine:","Patroclus kisses you."],"id":903,"m":"The first kiss was Menelaus'; this one is mine. Patroclus kisses you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["O, this is trim!"],"id":904,"m":"Oh, this is fine!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Paris and I kiss evermore for him."],"id":905,"m":"Paris and I always do his kissing for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["I'll have my kiss, sir. Lady, by your leave."],"id":906,"m":"I'll have my kiss, sir. Lady, with your permission."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["In kissing, do you render or receive?"],"id":907,"m":"In kissing, do you give or receive?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Both take and give."],"id":908,"m":"Both take and give."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["I'll make my match to live,","The kiss you take is better than you give;","Therefore no kiss."],"id":909,"m":"I'll bet my life the kiss you take is better than the one you give. So no kiss."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["I'll give you boot; I'll give you three for one."],"id":910,"m":"I'll give you something extra; I'll give you three for one."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["You are an odd man; give even or give none."],"id":911,"m":"You're an odd man; give even or give none."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["An odd man, lady! Every man is odd."],"id":912,"m":"An odd man, lady! Every man is odd (single, unique)."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["No, Paris is not; for you know 'tis true","That you are odd, and he is even with you."],"id":913,"m":"No, Paris isn't, because you know it's true that you are odd (alone), and he has got even with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["You fillip me o' th'head."],"id":914,"m":"You flick me on the head (on my horns)."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["No, I'll be sworn."],"id":915,"m":"No, I swear I don't."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["It were no match, your nail against his horn.","May I, sweet lady, beg a kiss of you?"],"id":916,"m":"It would be an unequal match, your fingernail against his horn. May I, sweet lady, beg a kiss from you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["You may."],"id":917,"m":"You may."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["I do desire it."],"id":918,"m":"I want one."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Why, beg then."],"id":919,"m":"Why, then beg."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Why then, for Venus' sake give me a kiss","When Helen is a maid again, and his."],"id":920,"m":"Why then, for Venus' sake, give me a kiss when Helen is a virgin again and his."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["I am your debtor; claim it when 'tis due."],"id":921,"m":"I owe it to you. Claim it when it's due."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Never's my day, and then a kiss of you."],"id":922,"m":"My day will never come, and then I'll have a kiss from you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Lady, a word. I'll bring you to your father."],"id":923,"m":"Lady, a word. I'll take you to your father."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit with Cressida.","id":924},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["A woman of quick sense."],"id":925,"m":"A woman with a quick wit."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Fie, fie upon her!","There's language in her eye, her cheek, her lip,","Nay, her foot speaks; her wanton spirits look out","At every joint and motive of her body.","O! these encounterers so glib of tongue","That give a coasting welcome ere it comes,","And wide unclasp the tables of their thoughts","To every tickling reader! Set them down","For sluttish spoils of opportunity,","And daughters of the game."],"id":926,"m":"Shame, shame on her! There's language in her eyes, her cheeks, her lips; even her foot speaks. Her lustful spirit looks out from every joint and movement of her body. Oh, these flirts with such smooth tongues, who give a welcome before a man has even approached, and open wide the notebooks of their thoughts to every reader who tickles them! Mark them down as easy spoils for any opportunist, and daughters of the game."},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpet within.","id":927},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["The Trojans' trumpet."],"id":928,"m":"The Trojans' trumpet."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Yonder comes the troop."],"id":929,"m":"Here comes the company."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hector, armed; Aeneas, Troilus, Paris, Deiphobus and other Trojans, with attendants.","id":930},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Hail, all you state of Greece! What shall be done","To him that victory commands? Or do you purpose","A victor shall be known? Will you the knights","Shall to the edge of all extremity","Pursue each other, or shall be divided","By any voice or order of the field?","Hector bade ask."],"id":931,"m":"Greetings, all you lords of Greece! What is to be done for the one who wins? Or do you intend that a winner should be declared? Do you want the knights to fight each other to the very end, or should they be separated by some command or rule of the contest? Hector asked me to ask."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Which way would Hector have it?"],"id":932,"m":"How does Hector want it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["He cares not; he'll obey conditions."],"id":933,"m":"He doesn't mind. He'll follow whatever terms are set."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["'Tis done like Hector."],"id":934,"m":"That's just like Hector."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["But securely done,","A little proudly, and great deal misprising","The knight oppos'd."],"id":935,"m":"But done overconfidently, a little proudly, and greatly underrating the knight he is fighting."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["If not Achilles, sir,","What is your name?"],"id":936,"m":"If you aren't Achilles, sir, what is your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["If not Achilles, nothing."],"id":937,"m":"If not Achilles, nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Therefore Achilles. But whate'er, know this:","In the extremity of great and little","Valour and pride excel themselves in Hector;","The one almost as infinite as all,","The other blank as nothing. Weigh him well,","And that which looks like pride is courtesy.","This Ajax is half made of Hector's blood;","In love whereof half Hector stays at home;","Half heart, half hand, half Hector comes to seek","This blended knight, half Trojan and half Greek."],"id":938,"m":"Achilles, then. But whoever you are, know this: courage and pride are both at their extremes in Hector, one great and one small. His courage is almost infinite, and his pride is as empty as nothing. Weigh him carefully, and what looks like pride is courtesy. This Ajax is half made of Hector's blood. Out of love for that, half of Hector stays at home. Half a heart, half a hand, half of Hector comes to fight this mixed knight, half Trojan and half Greek."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["A maiden battle then? O! I perceive you."],"id":939,"m":"A girls' fight, then? Oh, I see what you mean."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Diomedes.","id":940},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Here is Sir Diomed. Go, gentle knight,","Stand by our Ajax. As you and Lord Aeneas","Consent upon the order of their fight,","So be it; either to the uttermost,","Or else a breath. The combatants being kin","Half stints their strife before their strokes begin.","[Ajax and Hector enter the lists]"],"id":941,"m":"Here is Sir Diomedes. Go, gentle knight, stand by our Ajax. Whatever you and Lord Aeneas agree about how they should fight, let it be so, either to the death or just a bout. Because the fighters are related, their quarrel is half-checked before their blows begin. (Ajax and Hector enter the fighting ground.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["They are oppos'd already."],"id":942,"m":"They are facing each other already."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["What Trojan is that same that looks so heavy?"],"id":943,"m":"Who is that Trojan who looks so gloomy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["The youngest son of Priam, a true knight;","Not yet mature, yet matchless; firm of word;","Speaking in deeds and deedless in his tongue;","Not soon provok'd, nor being provok'd soon calm'd;","His heart and hand both open and both free;","For what he has he gives, what thinks he shows,","Yet gives he not till judgement guide his bounty,","Nor dignifies an impure thought with breath;","Manly as Hector, but more dangerous;","For Hector in his blaze of wrath subscribes","To tender objects, but he in heat of action","Is more vindicative than jealous love.","They call him Troilus, and on him erect","A second hope as fairly built as Hector.","Thus says Aeneas, one that knows the youth","Even to his inches, and, with private soul,","Did in great Ilion thus translate him to me."],"id":944,"m":"Priam's youngest son, a true knight. Not yet fully grown, yet unmatched; firm to his word; he speaks in deeds and not with his tongue. He is not quickly provoked, nor quickly calmed once provoked. His heart and hand are both open and generous, for he gives what he has and shows what he thinks; yet he doesn't give until judgement guides his generosity, nor does he honour an unworthy thought by speaking it. He is as manly as Hector, but more dangerous, because Hector, in the heat of his anger, spares those who are helpless, while he, in the heat of the fight, is more vengeful than jealous love. They call him Troilus, and build on him a second hope as fine as Hector. So says Aeneas, who knows the young man down to the last inch, and who described him to me like this, in confidence, in great Troy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Hector and Ajax fight.","id":945},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["They are in action."],"id":946,"m":"They're fighting."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Now, Ajax, hold thine own!"],"id":947,"m":"Now, Ajax, hold your own!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Hector, thou sleep'st; awake thee!"],"id":948,"m":"Hector, you're asleep; wake up!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["His blows are well dispos'd. There, Ajax!"],"id":949,"m":"His blows are well placed. There, Ajax!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets cease.","id":950},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["You must no more."],"id":951,"m":"You must stop now."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Princes, enough, so please you."],"id":952,"m":"Princes, that's enough, if you please."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["I am not warm yet; let us fight again."],"id":953,"m":"I'm not warmed up yet. Let's fight again."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["As Hector pleases."],"id":954,"m":"As Hector wishes."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Why, then will I no more.","Thou art, great lord, my father's sister's son,","A cousin-german to great Priam's seed;","The obligation of our blood forbids","A gory emulation 'twixt us twain:","Were thy commixtion Greek and Trojan so","That thou could'st say 'This hand is Grecian all,","And this is Trojan; the sinews of this leg","All Greek, and this all Troy; my mother's blood","Runs on the dexter cheek, and this sinister","Bounds in my father's; by Jove multipotent,","Thou shouldst not bear from me a Greekish member","Wherein my sword had not impressure made","Of our rank feud; but the just gods gainsay","That any drop thou borrow'dst from thy mother,","My sacred aunt, should by my mortal sword","Be drained! Let me embrace thee, Ajax.","By him that thunders, thou hast lusty arms;","Hector would have them fall upon him thus.","Cousin, all honour to thee!"],"id":955,"m":"Why, then I'll fight no more. Great lord, you are my father's sister's son, a first cousin to great Priam's children. The bond of our blood forbids a bloody contest between us two. If your mixture of Greek and Trojan were such that you could say, \"This hand is all Greek, and this is Trojan; the muscles of this leg are all Greek and this all Trojan; my mother's blood runs in my right cheek and this left one throbs with my father's,\" then, by all-powerful Jove, you would not carry away from me a single Greek limb without my sword printing on it the mark of our bitter feud. But the just gods forbid that any drop of blood you got from your mother, my sacred aunt, should be drained by my deadly sword! Let me embrace you, Ajax. By the god of thunder, you have strong arms. This is how Hector would like them to fall on him. Cousin, all honour to you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["I thank thee, Hector.","Thou art too gentle and too free a man.","I came to kill thee, cousin, and bear hence","A great addition earned in thy death."],"id":956,"m":"Thank you, Hector. You are too gentle and too generous a man. I came to kill you, cousin, and to carry away the great title of having won it by your death."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Not Neoptolemus so mirable,","On whose bright crest Fame with her loud'st Oyes","Cries 'This is he!' could promise to himself","A thought of added honour torn from Hector."],"id":957,"m":"Not even Neoptolemus, so admirable, on whose bright helmet Fame cries out with her loudest \"Oyez!\", \"This is the man!\", could expect to win a scrap of extra honour from Hector."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["There is expectance here from both the sides","What further you will do."],"id":958,"m":"Both sides are waiting to see what you will do next."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["We'll answer it:","The issue is embracement. Ajax, farewell."],"id":959,"m":"We'll give them their answer: the result is an embrace. Ajax, goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["If I might in entreaties find success,","As seld' I have the chance, I would desire","My famous cousin to our Grecian tents."],"id":960,"m":"If I could succeed in asking (and I seldom get the chance), I would invite my famous cousin to our Greek tents."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["'Tis Agamemnon's wish; and great Achilles","Doth long to see unarm'd the valiant Hector."],"id":961,"m":"It is Agamemnon's wish, and great Achilles longs to see brave Hector without his armour."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Aeneas, call my brother Troilus to me,","And signify this loving interview","To the expecters of our Trojan part;","Desire them home. Give me thy hand, my cousin;","I will go eat with thee, and see your knights.","[Agamemnon and the rest of the Greeks come forward]"],"id":962,"m":"Aeneas, call my brother Troilus to me, and report this friendly meeting to those waiting on the Trojan side. Tell them to go home. Give me your hand, my cousin. I'll go and eat with you and see your knights. (Agamemnon and the other Greeks come forward.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Great Agamemnon comes to meet us here."],"id":963,"m":"Great Agamemnon is coming to meet us here."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["The worthiest of them tell me name by name;","But for Achilles, my own searching eyes","Shall find him by his large and portly size."],"id":964,"m":"Tell me the names of the worthiest of them, one by one. But as for Achilles, my own searching eyes will find him by his large and stately size."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Worthy all arms! as welcome as to one","That would be rid of such an enemy.","But that's no welcome. Understand more clear,","What's past and what's to come is strew'd with husks","And formless ruin of oblivion;","But in this extant moment, faith and troth,","Strain'd purely from all hollow bias-drawing,","Bids thee with most divine integrity,","From heart of very heart, great Hector, welcome."],"id":965,"m":"Worthy in arms! As welcome as you can be to someone who would like to be rid of such an enemy. But that's no welcome. Let me put it more clearly: the past and the future are strewn with husks and the shapeless ruins of oblivion. But in this present moment, faith and truth, purified of all false and crooked motives, bid you, with the most divine sincerity, from the very heart of my heart, great Hector, welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["I thank thee, most imperious Agamemnon."],"id":966,"m":"Thank you, most powerful Agamemnon."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["[To Troilus] My well-fam'd lord of Troy, no less to you."],"id":967,"m":"(To Troilus) My famous lord of Troy, the same welcome to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["Let me confirm my princely brother's greeting.","You brace of warlike brothers, welcome hither."],"id":968,"m":"Let me confirm my royal brother's greeting. You pair of warlike brothers, welcome here."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Who must we answer?"],"id":969,"m":"Who must we answer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["The noble Menelaus."],"id":970,"m":"The noble Menelaus."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["O you, my lord? By Mars his gauntlet, thanks!","Mock not that I affect the untraded oath;","Your quondam wife swears still by Venus' glove.","She's well, but bade me not commend her to you."],"id":971,"m":"Oh, you, my lord? By Mars's glove, thank you! Don't laugh that I use an unusual oath; your former wife still swears by Venus' glove. She's well, but she didn't ask me to give you her regards."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["Name her not now, sir; she's a deadly theme."],"id":972,"m":"Don't mention her now, sir. She's a deadly subject."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["O, pardon; I offend."],"id":973,"m":"Oh, forgive me. I'm being offensive."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["I have, thou gallant Trojan, seen thee oft,","Labouring for destiny, make cruel way","Through ranks of Greekish youth; and I have seen thee,","As hot as Perseus, spur thy Phrygian steed,","Despising many forfeits and subduements,","When thou hast hung thy advanced sword i' th'air,","Not letting it decline on the declined;","That I have said to some my standers-by","'Lo, Jupiter is yonder, dealing life!'","And I have seen thee pause and take thy breath,","When that a ring of Greeks have shrap'd thee in,","Like an Olympian wrestling. This have I seen;","But this thy countenance, still lock'd in steel,","I never saw till now. I knew thy grandsire,","And once fought with him. He was a soldier good,","But, by great Mars, the captain of us all,","Never like thee. O, let an old man embrace thee;","And, worthy warrior, welcome to our tents."],"id":974,"m":"I have often seen you, you gallant Trojan, working for fate, cutting a cruel path through the ranks of young Greeks. And I have seen you, as hot as Perseus, spur on your Trojan horse, scorning many who had surrendered and were at your mercy, when you held your raised sword in the air and would not let it fall on the fallen, so that I said to some who stood by me, \"Look, there's Jupiter, giving out life!\" And I have seen you pause and take a breath when a ring of Greeks had hemmed you in, like an Olympic wrestler. This I have seen. But this face of yours, always locked in steel, I never saw until now. I knew your grandfather, and once fought with him. He was a good soldier, but, by great Mars, the leader of us all, never like you. Oh, let an old man embrace you, and, worthy warrior, welcome to our tents."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["'Tis the old Nestor."],"id":975,"m":"It's old Nestor."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Let me embrace thee, good old chronicle,","That hast so long walk'd hand in hand with time.","Most reverend Nestor, I am glad to clasp thee."],"id":976,"m":"Let me embrace you, good old history book, who has walked so long hand in hand with time. Most reverend Nestor, I am glad to hold you."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["I would my arms could match thee in contention","As they contend with thee in courtesy."],"id":977,"m":"I wish my arms could match you in fighting as they compete with you in courtesy."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["I would they could."],"id":978,"m":"I wish they could."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Ha!","By this white beard, I'd fight with thee tomorrow.","Well, welcome, welcome! I have seen the time."],"id":979,"m":"Ha! By this white beard, I'd fight you tomorrow. Well, welcome, welcome! I have seen the day."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["I wonder now how yonder city stands,","When we have here her base and pillar by us."],"id":980,"m":"I wonder how that city over there is still standing, when we have its foundation and pillar here with us."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["I know your favour, Lord Ulysses, well.","Ah, sir, there's many a Greek and Trojan dead,","Since first I saw yourself and Diomed","In Ilion on your Greekish embassy."],"id":981,"m":"I know your face well, Lord Ulysses. Ah, sir, many Greeks and Trojans have died since I first saw you and Diomedes in Troy on your Greek embassy."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Sir, I foretold you then what would ensue.","My prophecy is but half his journey yet;","For yonder walls, that pertly front your town,","Yon towers, whose wanton tops do buss the clouds,","Must kiss their own feet."],"id":982,"m":"Sir, I told you then what would follow. My prophecy has only gone half its journey yet, because those walls that stand so boldly in front of your town, those towers whose proud tops kiss the clouds, must kiss their own feet."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["I must not believe you.","There they stand yet; and modestly I think","The fall of every Phrygian stone will cost","A drop of Grecian blood. The end crowns all;","And that old common arbitrator, Time,","Will one day end it."],"id":983,"m":"I mustn't believe you. There they still stand, and I modestly think that the fall of every Trojan stone will cost a drop of Greek blood. The end crowns everything, and that old common judge, Time, will end it one day."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["So to him we leave it.","Most gentle and most valiant Hector, welcome.","After the General, I beseech you next","To feast with me and see me at my tent."],"id":984,"m":"Then we'll leave it to him. Most gentle and most brave Hector, welcome. After the General, I beg you to feast with me next and visit me at my tent."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["I shall forestall thee, Lord Ulysses, thou!","Now, Hector, I have fed mine eyes on thee;","I have with exact view perus'd thee, Hector,","And quoted joint by joint."],"id":985,"m":"I'll get in before you, Lord Ulysses, you! Now, Hector, I have fed my eyes on you. I have studied you carefully, Hector, and noted you joint by joint."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Is this Achilles?"],"id":986,"m":"Is this Achilles?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["I am Achilles."],"id":987,"m":"I am Achilles."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Stand fair, I pray thee; let me look on thee."],"id":988,"m":"Stand still, please; let me look at you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Behold thy fill."],"id":989,"m":"Look all you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Nay, I have done already."],"id":990,"m":"No, I've finished already."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Thou art too brief. I will the second time,","As I would buy thee, view thee limb by limb."],"id":991,"m":"You're too quick. I'll look at you a second time, limb by limb, as if I were going to buy you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["O, like a book of sport thou'lt read me o'er;","But there's more in me than thou understand'st.","Why dost thou so oppress me with thine eye?"],"id":992,"m":"Oh, you'll read me through like a book about hunting. But there's more in me than you understand. Why do you weigh me down so with your stare?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Tell me, you heavens, in which part of his body","Shall I destroy him? Whether there, or there, or there?","That I may give the local wound a name,","And make distinct the very breach whereout","Hector's great spirit flew. Answer me, heavens."],"id":993,"m":"Tell me, you heavens, in which part of his body shall I destroy him? There, or there, or there? So that I can give the wound a name and mark out the very gap through which Hector's great spirit flew out. Answer me, heavens."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["It would discredit the blest gods, proud man,","To answer such a question. Stand again.","Think'st thou to catch my life so pleasantly","As to prenominate in nice conjecture","Where thou wilt hit me dead?"],"id":994,"m":"It would disgrace the blessed gods, proud man, to answer such a question. Stand still again. Do you think you can take my life so easily that you can name in advance, with precise guesswork, where you will strike me dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["I tell thee yea."],"id":995,"m":"I tell you yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Wert thou an oracle to tell me so,","I'd not believe thee. Henceforth guard thee well;","For I'll not kill thee there, nor there, nor there;","But, by the forge that stithied Mars his helm,","I'll kill thee everywhere, yea, o'er and o'er.","You wisest Grecians, pardon me this brag.","His insolence draws folly from my lips;","But I'll endeavour deeds to match these words,","Or may I never—"],"id":996,"m":"Even if you were an oracle telling me so, I wouldn't believe you. From now on, guard yourself well, because I won't kill you there, or there, or there; but, by the forge that hammered out Mars's helmet, I'll kill you everywhere, yes, over and over. You wisest Greeks, forgive me this boast. His insolence draws foolishness from my lips. But I'll try to make my deeds match these words, or may I never..."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Do not chafe thee, cousin;","And you, Achilles, let these threats alone","Till accident or purpose bring you to't.","You may have every day enough of Hector,","If you have stomach. The general state, I fear,","Can scarce entreat you to be odd with him."],"id":997,"m":"Don't get angry, cousin. And you, Achilles, leave these threats alone until chance or intention brings you to it. You can have enough of Hector every day, if you have the appetite. I'm afraid the whole Greek council can hardly persuade you to fight with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["I pray you let us see you in the field;","We have had pelting wars since you refus'd","The Grecians' cause."],"id":998,"m":"Please, let us see you on the battlefield. We've had petty wars since you refused to fight for the Greek cause."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Dost thou entreat me, Hector?","Tomorrow do I meet thee, fell as death;","Tonight all friends."],"id":999,"m":"Are you begging me, Hector? Tomorrow I'll meet you, as deadly as death. Tonight, all friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Thy hand upon that match."],"id":1000,"m":"Your hand on that bargain."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["First, all you peers of Greece, go to my tent;","There in the full convive we; afterwards,","As Hector's leisure and your bounties shall","Concur together, severally entreat him.","Beat loud the tambourines, let the trumpets blow,","That this great soldier may his welcome know."],"id":1001,"m":"First, all you lords of Greece, come to my tent. There we'll all feast together. Afterwards, as Hector's time and your generosity allow, invite him one by one. Beat the drums loud, let the trumpets blow, so that this great soldier knows he is welcome."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Troilus and Ulysses.","id":1002},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["My Lord Ulysses, tell me, I beseech you,","In what place of the field doth Calchas keep?"],"id":1003,"m":"My Lord Ulysses, tell me, please, where in the camp does Calchas stay?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["At Menelaus' tent, most princely Troilus.","There Diomed doth feast with him tonight,","Who neither looks upon the heaven nor earth,","But gives all gaze and bent of amorous view","On the fair Cressid."],"id":1004,"m":"At Menelaus' tent, most princely Troilus. Diomedes is feasting with him there tonight, and he looks neither at heaven nor earth, but turns all his gaze and loving attention on the fair Cressida."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Shall I, sweet lord, be bound to you so much,","After we part from Agamemnon's tent,","To bring me thither?"],"id":1005,"m":"Sweet lord, will you do me the great favour, after we leave Agamemnon's tent, of taking me there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["You shall command me, sir.","As gentle tell me of what honour was","This Cressida in Troy? Had she no lover there","That wails her absence?"],"id":1006,"m":"You can command me, sir. In return, kindly tell me what reputation this Cressida had in Troy. Did she have no lover there who weeps at her absence?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O, sir, to such as boasting show their scars","A mock is due. Will you walk on, my lord?","She was belov'd, she lov'd; she is, and doth;","But still sweet love is food for fortune's tooth."],"id":1007,"m":"Oh, sir, those who show off their scars deserve to be mocked. Will you walk on, my lord? She was loved, she loved; she is, and she does. But sweet love is always food for fortune's teeth."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1008},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"The Grecian camp. Before the tent of Achilles.","id":1009},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Achilles and Patroclus.","id":1010},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["I'll heat his blood with Greekish wine tonight,","Which with my scimitar I'll cool tomorrow.","Patroclus, let us feast him to the height."],"id":1011,"m":"I'll heat his blood with Greek wine tonight, and cool it tomorrow with my curved sword. Patroclus, let's feast him to the full."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Here comes Thersites."],"id":1012,"m":"Here comes Thersites."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Thersites.","id":1013},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["How now, thou core of envy!","Thou crusty batch of nature, what's the news?"],"id":1014,"m":"Well, you rotten core of envy! You burnt crust of nature, what's the news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Why, thou picture of what thou seemest, and idol of idiot worshippers, here's a letter for thee."],"id":1015,"m":"Why, you image of what you seem to be, and idol of idiot worshippers, here's a letter for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["From whence, fragment?"],"id":1016,"m":"Where from, you scrap?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Why, thou full dish of fool, from Troy."],"id":1017,"m":"Why, you full dish of fool, from Troy."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Who keeps the tent now?"],"id":1018,"m":"Who's in the tent now? (Who's keeping to it?)"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["The surgeon's box or the patient's wound."],"id":1019,"m":"The surgeon's case or the patient's wound. (Thersites puns on \"tent\", a probe for wounds.)"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Well said, adversity! And what needs these tricks?"],"id":1020,"m":"Well said, you pain in the neck! And what's the point of these tricks?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Prithee, be silent, boy; I profit not by thy talk; thou art said to be Achilles' male varlet."],"id":1021,"m":"Please be quiet, boy. I get nothing from your talk. You're said to be Achilles' male servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Male varlet, you rogue! What's that?"],"id":1022,"m":"Male servant, you rogue! What's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Why, his masculine whore. Now, the rotten diseases of the south, the guts-griping ruptures, catarrhs, loads o' gravel in the back, lethargies, cold palsies, raw eyes, dirt-rotten livers, wheezing lungs, bladders full of imposthume, sciaticas, lime-kilns i' th' palm, incurable bone-ache, and the rivelled fee-simple of the tetter, take and take again such preposterous discoveries!"],"id":1023,"m":"Why, his masculine whore. Now may the rotten diseases of the south, gut-wrenching hernias, colds, loads of kidney stones, sleeping sickness, paralysis, sore eyes, livers rotten with dirt, wheezing lungs, bladders full of abscesses, sciatica, burning palms, incurable bone-ache and permanent wrinkled skin disease take, and take again, such unnatural freaks!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Why, thou damnable box of envy, thou, what meanest thou to curse thus?"],"id":1024,"m":"Why, you damnable box of envy, you, what do you mean by cursing like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Do I curse thee?"],"id":1025,"m":"Am I cursing you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Why, no, you ruinous butt; you whoreson indistinguishable cur, no."],"id":1026,"m":"Why, no, you ruined barrel; you shapeless bastard dog, no."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["No! Why art thou, then, exasperate, thou idle immaterial skein of sleave silk, thou green sarcenet flap for a sore eye, thou tassel of a prodigal's purse, thou? Ah, how the poor world is pestered with such water-flies, diminutives of nature!"],"id":1027,"m":"No! Then why are you so annoyed, you useless, flimsy tangle of silk thread, you green silk patch for a sore eye, you tassel on a spendthrift's purse, you? Ah, how the poor world is pestered with such mayflies, nature's tiny creatures!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PATROCLUS","lines":["Out, gall!"],"id":1028,"m":"Get out, you bile!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Finch egg!"],"id":1029,"m":"Finch's egg!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["My sweet Patroclus, I am thwarted quite","From my great purpose in tomorrow's battle.","Here is a letter from Queen Hecuba,","A token from her daughter, my fair love,","Both taxing me and gaging me to keep","An oath that I have sworn. I will not break it.","Fall Greeks; fail fame; honour or go or stay;","My major vow lies here, this I'll obey.","Come, come, Thersites, help to trim my tent;","This night in banqueting must all be spent.","Away, Patroclus!"],"id":1030,"m":"My sweet Patroclus, I am completely prevented from carrying out my great plan in tomorrow's battle. Here is a letter from Queen Hecuba, and a token from her daughter, my fair love, both urging me and binding me to keep an oath I have sworn. I will not break it. Let the Greeks fall, let fame fail, let honour come or go: my greater vow is here, and this I will obey. Come, come, Thersites, help to get my tent ready. This whole night must be spent banqueting. Away, Patroclus!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit with Patroclus.","id":1031},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["With too much blood and too little brain these two may run mad; but, if with too much brain and too little blood they do, I'll be a curer of madmen. Here's Agamemnon, an honest fellow enough, and one that loves quails, but he has not so much brain as ear-wax; and the goodly transformation of Jupiter there, his brother, the bull, the primitive statue and oblique memorial of cuckolds, a thrifty shoeing-horn in a chain at his brother's leg, to what form but that he is, should wit larded with malice, and malice forced with wit, turn him to? To an ass, were nothing: he is both ass and ox. To an ox, were nothing: he is both ox and ass. To be a dog, a mule, a cat, a fitchook, a toad, a lizard, an owl, a puttock, or a herring without a roe, I would not care; but to be Menelaus, I would conspire against destiny. Ask me not what I would be, if I were not Thersites; for I care not to be the louse of a lazar, so I were not Menelaus. Hey-day! sprites and fires!"],"id":1032,"m":"With too much blood and too little brain, these two may go mad; but if they go mad with too much brain and too little blood, I'll become a doctor for madmen. Here's Agamemnon, an honest enough fellow, and one who loves quails (loose women), but he has less brain than earwax. And as for that fine transformation of Jupiter there, his brother the bull (Menelaus), the original image and crooked monument of cuckolds, a cheap shoehorn on a chain at his brother's leg: what shape could wit stuffed with malice, and malice stuffed with wit, turn him into, other than what he is? Turning him into a donkey would be nothing: he's both donkey and ox. Turning him into an ox would be nothing: he's both ox and donkey. I wouldn't mind being a dog, a mule, a cat, a polecat, a toad, a lizard, an owl, a kite or a herring without roe; but if I were Menelaus, I'd plot against fate itself. Don't ask me what I'd be if I weren't Thersites, because I wouldn't mind being the louse on a leper, as long as I weren't Menelaus. Hey there! Spirits and will-o'-the-wisps!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hector, Troilus, Ajax, Agamemnon, Ulysses, Nestor, Menelaus and Diomedes with lights.","id":1033},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["We go wrong, we go wrong."],"id":1034,"m":"We're going the wrong way, the wrong way."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["No, yonder 'tis;","There, where we see the lights."],"id":1035,"m":"No, it's over there, where we see the lights."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["I trouble you."],"id":1036,"m":"I'm troubling you."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["No, not a whit."],"id":1037,"m":"No, not a bit."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Here comes himself to guide you."],"id":1038,"m":"Here he comes himself to show you the way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Achilles.","id":1039},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Welcome, brave Hector; welcome, Princes all."],"id":1040,"m":"Welcome, brave Hector; welcome, all you Princes."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["So now, fair Prince of Troy, I bid good night;","Ajax commands the guard to tend on you."],"id":1041,"m":"So now, fair Prince of Troy, I'll say good night. Ajax will order the guard to attend you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Thanks, and good night to the Greeks' general."],"id":1042,"m":"Thank you, and good night to the Greeks' general."},{"t":"sp","who":"MENELAUS","lines":["Good night, my lord."],"id":1043,"m":"Good night, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Good night, sweet Lord Menelaus."],"id":1044,"m":"Good night, sweet Lord Menelaus."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Sweet draught! 'Sweet' quoth a'!","Sweet sink, sweet sewer!"],"id":1045,"m":"Sweet toilet! \"Sweet,\" he says! Sweet drain, sweet sewer!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Good night and welcome, both at once, to those","That go or tarry."],"id":1046,"m":"Good night and welcome, both at once, to those who are going or staying."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Good night."],"id":1047,"m":"Good night."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Agamemnon and Menelaus.","id":1048},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Old Nestor tarries; and you too, Diomed,","Keep Hector company an hour or two."],"id":1049,"m":"Old Nestor is staying; and you too, Diomedes, keep Hector company for an hour or two."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["I cannot, lord; I have important business,","The tide whereof is now. Good night, great Hector."],"id":1050,"m":"I can't, lord. I have important business, and now's the time for it. Good night, great Hector."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Give me your hand."],"id":1051,"m":"Give me your hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["[Aside to Troilus] Follow his torch; he goes to","Calchas' tent; I'll keep you company."],"id":1052,"m":"(Aside to Troilus) Follow his torch. He's going to Calchas' tent. I'll keep you company."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Sweet sir, you honour me."],"id":1053,"m":"Sweet sir, you honour me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["And so, good night."],"id":1054,"m":"And so, good night."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Diomedes, Ulysses and Troilus following.","id":1055},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Come, come, enter my tent."],"id":1056,"m":"Come, come, come into my tent."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Thersites.","id":1057},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["That same Diomed's a false-hearted rogue, a most unjust knave; I will no more trust him when he leers than I will a serpent when he hisses. He will spend his mouth and promise, like Brabbler the hound; but when he performs, astronomers foretell it: it is prodigious, there will come some change; the sun borrows of the moon when Diomed keeps his word. I will rather leave to see Hector than not to dog him. They say he keeps a Trojan drab, and uses the traitor Calchas' tent. I'll after. Nothing but lechery! All incontinent varlets!"],"id":1058,"m":"That Diomedes is a false-hearted rogue, a thoroughly dishonest villain. I'll trust him no more when he leers than I trust a snake when it hisses. He'll open his mouth and promise like a yapping hound, but when he actually does something, astronomers predict it: it's a portent, some great change is coming. The sun borrows light from the moon when Diomedes keeps his word. I'd rather miss seeing Hector than miss following him. They say he keeps a Trojan whore and uses the traitor Calchas' tent. I'll go after him. Nothing but lechery! All lustful rascals!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1059},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"The Grecian camp. Before Calchas' tent.","id":1060},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Diomedes.","id":1061},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["What, are you up here, ho! Speak."],"id":1062,"m":"Hey, is anyone up in here? Speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALCHAS","lines":["[Within] Who calls?"],"id":1063,"m":"(Inside) Who's calling?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Diomed. Calchas, I think. Where's your daughter?"],"id":1064,"m":"Diomedes. Calchas, I think? Where's your daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CALCHAS","lines":["[Within] She comes to you."],"id":1065,"m":"(Inside) She's coming to you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Troilus and Ulysses, at a distance; after them Thersites.","id":1066},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Stand where the torch may not discover us."],"id":1067,"m":"Stand where the torch won't show us."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cressida.","id":1068},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Cressid comes forth to him."],"id":1069,"m":"Cressida is coming out to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["How now, my charge!"],"id":1070,"m":"Well, my charge!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Now, my sweet guardian! Hark, a word with you."],"id":1071,"m":"Now, my sweet guardian! Listen, a word with you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Whispers.","id":1072},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Yea, so familiar?"],"id":1073,"m":"What, so familiar?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["She will sing any man at first sight."],"id":1074,"m":"She'll sing to any man at first sight (sight-read any man)."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["And any man may sing her, if he can take her cliff; she's noted."],"id":1075,"m":"And any man can sing her, if he can find her clef. She's been marked out (as loose)."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Will you remember?"],"id":1076,"m":"Will you remember?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Remember! Yes."],"id":1077,"m":"Remember! Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Nay, but do, then;","And let your mind be coupled with your words."],"id":1078,"m":"No, but do it, then, and let your mind match your words."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["What should she remember?"],"id":1079,"m":"What should she remember?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["List!"],"id":1080,"m":"Listen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Sweet honey Greek, tempt me no more to folly."],"id":1081,"m":"Sweet honey Greek, don't tempt me into foolishness any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Roguery!"],"id":1082,"m":"Villainy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Nay, then—"],"id":1083,"m":"No, then..."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["I'll tell you what—"],"id":1084,"m":"I'll tell you what..."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Fo, fo! come, tell a pin; you are a forsworn."],"id":1085,"m":"Pooh, pooh! Come on, that's nothing; you're breaking your word."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["In faith, I cannot. What would you have me do?"],"id":1086,"m":"Honestly, I can't. What do you want me to do?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["A juggling trick, to be secretly open."],"id":1087,"m":"A conjuring trick: to be secretly open (for him)."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["What did you swear you would bestow on me?"],"id":1088,"m":"What did you swear you would give me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["I prithee, do not hold me to mine oath;","Bid me do anything but that, sweet Greek."],"id":1089,"m":"Please, don't hold me to my oath. Ask me to do anything but that, sweet Greek."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Good night."],"id":1090,"m":"Good night."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Hold, patience!"],"id":1091,"m":"Hold on, patience!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["How now, Trojan!"],"id":1092,"m":"What's the matter, Trojan!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Diomed!"],"id":1093,"m":"Diomedes!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["No, no, good night; I'll be your fool no more."],"id":1094,"m":"No, no, good night. I won't be your fool any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Thy better must."],"id":1095,"m":"Your better is (Troilus himself)."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Hark! a word in your ear."],"id":1096,"m":"Listen! A word in your ear."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O plague and madness!"],"id":1097,"m":"Oh plague and madness!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["You are moved, Prince; let us depart, I pray,","Lest your displeasure should enlarge itself","To wrathful terms. This place is dangerous;","The time right deadly; I beseech you, go."],"id":1098,"m":"You are upset, Prince. Please, let's leave, in case your anger breaks out into furious words. This place is dangerous, and the time is deadly. I beg you, go."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Behold, I pray you."],"id":1099,"m":"Look, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Nay, good my lord, go off;","You flow to great distraction; come, my lord."],"id":1100,"m":"No, my good lord, come away. You're heading for great distress. Come, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["I pray thee stay."],"id":1101,"m":"Please, stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["You have not patience; come."],"id":1102,"m":"You have no patience. Come."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["I pray you, stay; by hell and all hell's torments,","I will not speak a word."],"id":1103,"m":"Please, stay. By hell and all hell's torments, I won't say a word."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["And so, good night."],"id":1104,"m":"And so, good night."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Nay, but you part in anger."],"id":1105,"m":"No, but you're leaving angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Doth that grieve thee? O withered truth!"],"id":1106,"m":"Does that upset you? Oh withered truth!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["How now, my lord?"],"id":1107,"m":"What's the matter, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["By Jove, I will be patient."],"id":1108,"m":"By Jove, I'll be patient."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Guardian! Why, Greek!"],"id":1109,"m":"Guardian! Why, Greek!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Fo, fo! adieu! you palter."],"id":1110,"m":"Pooh, pooh! Goodbye. You're playing games with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["In faith, I do not. Come hither once again."],"id":1111,"m":"Honestly, I'm not. Come here once more."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["You shake, my lord, at something; will you go?","You will break out."],"id":1112,"m":"You're shaking at something, my lord. Will you come away? You'll lose control."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["She strokes his cheek."],"id":1113,"m":"She's stroking his cheek."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Come, come."],"id":1114,"m":"Come, come."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Nay, stay; by Jove, I will not speak a word:","There is between my will and all offences","A guard of patience. Stay a little while."],"id":1115,"m":"No, stay. By Jove, I won't say a word. There is a guard of patience between my will and any violence. Stay a little while."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["How the devil Luxury, with his fat rump and potato finger, tickles these together! Fry, lechery, fry!"],"id":1116,"m":"How the devil Lust, with his fat backside and potato finger, tickles these two together! Fry, lechery, fry!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["But will you, then?"],"id":1117,"m":"But will you, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["In faith, I will, la; never trust me else."],"id":1118,"m":"Honestly, I will, I promise. Never trust me otherwise."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Give me some token for the surety of it."],"id":1119,"m":"Give me some token as a guarantee."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["I'll fetch you one."],"id":1120,"m":"I'll fetch you one."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1121},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["You have sworn patience."],"id":1122,"m":"You have sworn to be patient."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Fear me not, my lord;","I will not be myself, nor have cognition","Of what I feel. I am all patience."],"id":1123,"m":"Don't worry about me, my lord. I won't be myself, or know what I feel. I am all patience."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Cressida.","id":1124},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Now the pledge; now, now, now!"],"id":1125,"m":"Now the pledge; now, now, now!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Here, Diomed, keep this sleeve."],"id":1126,"m":"Here, Diomedes, keep this sleeve."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O beauty! where is thy faith?"],"id":1127,"m":"Oh beauty! Where is your faithfulness?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["My lord!"],"id":1128,"m":"My lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["I will be patient; outwardly I will."],"id":1129,"m":"I will be patient; outwardly I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["You look upon that sleeve; behold it well.","He lov'd me—O false wench!—Give't me again."],"id":1130,"m":"You're looking at that sleeve; look at it well. He loved me... oh false girl! Give it back to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Whose was't?"],"id":1131,"m":"Whose was it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["It is no matter, now I have't again.","I will not meet with you tomorrow night.","I prithee, Diomed, visit me no more."],"id":1132,"m":"It doesn't matter, now that I have it back. I won't meet you tomorrow night. Please, Diomedes, don't visit me any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Now she sharpens. Well said, whetstone."],"id":1133,"m":"Now she's sharpening his appetite. Well done, whetstone."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["I shall have it."],"id":1134,"m":"I will have it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["What, this?"],"id":1135,"m":"What, this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Ay, that."],"id":1136,"m":"Yes, that."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["O all you gods! O pretty, pretty pledge!","Thy master now lies thinking on his bed","Of thee and me, and sighs, and takes my glove,","And gives memorial dainty kisses to it,","As I kiss thee. Nay, do not snatch it from me;","He that takes that doth take my heart withal."],"id":1137,"m":"Oh all you gods! Oh pretty, pretty pledge! Your master is lying on his bed now thinking of you and me, and sighing, and taking my glove and giving it delicate kisses in memory of me, as I kiss you. No, don't snatch it from me. Whoever takes that takes my heart along with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["I had your heart before; this follows it."],"id":1138,"m":"I had your heart already; this follows it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["I did swear patience."],"id":1139,"m":"I did swear to be patient."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["You shall not have it, Diomed; faith, you shall not;","I'll give you something else."],"id":1140,"m":"You shan't have it, Diomedes; honestly, you shan't. I'll give you something else."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["I will have this. Whose was it?"],"id":1141,"m":"I will have this. Whose was it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["It is no matter."],"id":1142,"m":"It doesn't matter."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Come, tell me whose it was."],"id":1143,"m":"Come on, tell me whose it was."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["'Twas one's that lov'd me better than you will.","But, now you have it, take it."],"id":1144,"m":"It belonged to someone who loved me better than you will. But now that you have it, take it."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Whose was it?"],"id":1145,"m":"Whose was it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["By all Diana's waiting women yond,","And by herself, I will not tell you whose."],"id":1146,"m":"By all Diana's attendants up there (the stars), and by Diana herself, I won't tell you whose."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Tomorrow will I wear it on my helm,","And grieve his spirit that dares not challenge it."],"id":1147,"m":"Tomorrow I'll wear it on my helmet and torment the spirit of the man who doesn't dare to challenge it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Wert thou the devil and wor'st it on thy horn,","It should be challeng'd."],"id":1148,"m":"Even if you were the devil and wore it on your horn, it would be challenged."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Well, well, 'tis done, 'tis past; and yet it is not;","I will not keep my word."],"id":1149,"m":"Well, well, it's done, it's over. And yet it isn't. I won't keep my word."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Why, then farewell;","Thou never shalt mock Diomed again."],"id":1150,"m":"Why, then, goodbye. You'll never make a fool of Diomedes again."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["You shall not go. One cannot speak a word","But it straight starts you."],"id":1151,"m":"You mustn't go. I can't say a word without it setting you off at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["I do not like this fooling."],"id":1152,"m":"I don't like this fooling about."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Nor I, by Pluto; but that that likes not you","Pleases me best."],"id":1153,"m":"Nor do I, by Pluto; but whatever you don't like pleases me most."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["What, shall I come? The hour?"],"id":1154,"m":"What, shall I come? At what time?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Ay, come; O Jove! Do come. I shall be plagu'd."],"id":1155,"m":"Yes, come. Oh Jove! Do come. I'll be tormented for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Farewell till then."],"id":1156,"m":"Goodbye until then."},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","lines":["Good night. I prithee come."],"id":1157,"m":"Good night. Please come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Diomedes.","id":1158},{"t":"sp","who":"CRESSIDA","cont":1,"lines":["Troilus, farewell! One eye yet looks on thee;","But with my heart the other eye doth see.","Ah, poor our sex! this fault in us I find,","The error of our eye directs our mind.","What error leads must err; O, then conclude,","Minds sway'd by eyes are full of turpitude."],"id":1159,"m":"Troilus, goodbye! One eye still looks at you, but the other sees with my heart. Ah, our poor sex! I find this fault in us: the error of our eyes directs our minds. What error leads must go astray. Oh then, conclude: minds ruled by eyes are full of wickedness."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1160},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["A proof of strength she could not publish more,","Unless she said 'My mind is now turn'd whore.'"],"id":1161,"m":"She couldn't have proclaimed it more strongly, unless she said, \"My mind has now turned whore.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["All's done, my lord."],"id":1162,"m":"It's all over, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["It is."],"id":1163,"m":"It is."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Why stay we, then?"],"id":1164,"m":"Then why are we staying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["To make a recordation to my soul","Of every syllable that here was spoke.","But if I tell how these two did co-act,","Shall I not lie in publishing a truth?","Sith yet there is a credence in my heart,","An esperance so obstinately strong,","That doth invert th'attest of eyes and ears;","As if those organs had deceptious functions","Created only to calumniate.","Was Cressid here?"],"id":1165,"m":"To make a record in my soul of every syllable that was spoken here. But if I tell how these two acted together, won't I be lying even while I tell the truth? Because there is still a belief in my heart, a hope so stubbornly strong that it overturns the evidence of my eyes and ears, as if those organs had deceptive functions created only to slander. Was Cressida here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["I cannot conjure, Trojan."],"id":1166,"m":"I can't conjure up spirits, Trojan."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["She was not, sure."],"id":1167,"m":"Surely she wasn't."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Most sure she was."],"id":1168,"m":"She certainly was."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Why, my negation hath no taste of madness."],"id":1169,"m":"Why, my denial has no taste of madness."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["Nor mine, my lord. Cressid was here but now."],"id":1170,"m":"Nor has mine, my lord. Cressida was here just now."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Let it not be believ'd for womanhood.","Think, we had mothers; do not give advantage","To stubborn critics, apt, without a theme,","For depravation, to square the general sex","By Cressid's rule. Rather think this not Cressid."],"id":1171,"m":"For the sake of womanhood, don't let it be believed. Remember, we had mothers. Don't give an opening to harsh critics, who are ready to run women down even without a reason, to measure the whole sex by Cressida's standard. Rather, think this was not Cressida."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["What hath she done, Prince, that can soil our mothers?"],"id":1172,"m":"What has she done, Prince, that could stain our mothers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Nothing at all, unless that this were she."],"id":1173,"m":"Nothing at all, unless this really was her."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Will he swagger himself out on's own eyes?"],"id":1174,"m":"Is he going to bluster himself out of believing his own eyes?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["This she? No; this is Diomed's Cressida.","If beauty have a soul, this is not she;","If souls guide vows, if vows be sanctimonies,","If sanctimony be the god's delight,","If there be rule in unity itself,","This was not she. O madness of discourse,","That cause sets up with and against itself!","Bi-fold authority! where reason can revolt","Without perdition, and loss assume all reason","Without revolt: this is, and is not, Cressid.","Within my soul there doth conduce a fight","Of this strange nature, that a thing inseparate","Divides more wider than the sky and earth;","And yet the spacious breadth of this division","Admits no orifice for a point as subtle","As Ariachne's broken woof to enter.","Instance, O instance! strong as Pluto's gates:","Cressid is mine, tied with the bonds of heaven.","Instance, O instance! strong as heaven itself:","The bonds of heaven are slipp'd, dissolv'd, and loos'd;","And with another knot, five-finger-tied,","The fractions of her faith, orts of her love,","The fragments, scraps, the bits, and greasy relics","Of her o'er-eaten faith, are given to Diomed."],"id":1175,"m":"This her? No, this is Diomedes' Cressida. If beauty has a soul, this is not her. If souls guide vows, if vows are sacred, if sacredness is the gods' delight, if there is any rule in oneness itself, this was not her. Oh madness of reasoning, which argues both for and against itself! Double authority, where reason can rebel without being lost, and loss can take on all the appearance of reason without rebelling: this is, and is not, Cressida. Inside my soul a fight of such a strange kind is going on that a thing that cannot be divided splits wider apart than the sky and the earth, and yet the vast breadth of this division leaves no opening wide enough for a point as fine as Arachne's broken thread to enter. Proof, oh proof, as strong as the gates of hell: Cressida is mine, tied with the bonds of heaven. Proof, oh proof, as strong as heaven itself: the bonds of heaven have slipped, dissolved and come loose, and with another knot, tied by five fingers, the scraps of her faith, the leftovers of her love, the fragments, crumbs, bits and greasy remains of her over-eaten faith are given to Diomedes."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["May worthy Troilus be half attach'd","With that which here his passion doth express?"],"id":1176,"m":"Can worthy Troilus feel even half of what his passion is expressing here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Ay, Greek; and that shall be divulged well","In characters as red as Mars his heart","Inflam'd with Venus. Never did young man fancy","With so eternal and so fix'd a soul.","Hark, Greek: as much as I do Cressid love,","So much by weight hate I her Diomed.","That sleeve is mine that he'll bear on his helm;","Were it a casque compos'd by Vulcan's skill","My sword should bite it. Not the dreadful spout","Which shipmen do the hurricano call,","Constring'd in mass by the almighty sun,","Shall dizzy with more clamour Neptune's ear","In his descent than shall my prompted sword","Falling on Diomed."],"id":1177,"m":"Yes, Greek, and that will be made known in letters as red as Mars's heart when Venus set it on fire. Never did a young man love with so eternal and so fixed a soul. Listen, Greek: as much as I love Cressida, I hate her Diomedes by the same weight. That sleeve he'll wear on his helmet is mine. Even if it were a helmet made by Vulcan's skill, my sword would bite it. The dreadful waterspout that sailors call a hurricane, drawn up into a mass by the almighty sun, will not deafen Neptune's ear with more noise as it falls than my eager sword will make falling on Diomedes."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["He'll tickle it for his concupy."],"id":1178,"m":"He'll give him a good tickling for his lust."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O Cressid! O false Cressid! false, false, false!","Let all untruths stand by thy stained name,","And they'll seem glorious."],"id":1179,"m":"Oh Cressida! Oh false Cressida! False, false, false! Let all lies stand beside your stained name, and they will seem glorious."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["O, contain yourself;","Your passion draws ears hither."],"id":1180,"m":"Oh, control yourself. Your passion is drawing people's ears here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aeneas.","id":1181},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["I have been seeking you this hour, my lord.","Hector, by this, is arming him in Troy;","Ajax, your guard, stays to conduct you home."],"id":1182,"m":"I've been looking for you for an hour, my lord. By now Hector is arming himself in Troy. Ajax, your escort, is waiting to take you home."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Have with you, Prince. My courteous lord, adieu.","Fairwell, revolted fair! and, Diomed,","Stand fast, and wear a castle on thy head."],"id":1183,"m":"I'm with you, Prince. My courteous lord, goodbye. Farewell, unfaithful beauty! And, Diomedes, stand firm, and wear a castle on your head."},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["I'll bring you to the gates."],"id":1184,"m":"I'll take you to the gates."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Accept distracted thanks."],"id":1185,"m":"Accept the thanks of a distracted man."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Troilus, Aeneas and Ulysses.","id":1186},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Would I could meet that rogue Diomed! I would croak like a raven; I would bode, I would bode. Patroclus will give me anything for the intelligence of this whore; the parrot will not do more for an almond than he for a commodious drab. Lechery, lechery! Still wars and lechery! Nothing else holds fashion. A burning devil take them!"],"id":1187,"m":"I wish I could meet that rogue Diomedes! I'd croak like a raven; I'd foretell disaster, I'd foretell it. Patroclus will give me anything for news of this whore. A parrot wouldn't do more for an almond than he would for an available woman. Lechery, lechery! Always wars and lechery! Nothing else stays in fashion. May a burning devil take them!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1188},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Troy. Before Priam's palace.","id":1189},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hector and Andromache.","id":1190},{"t":"sp","who":"ANDROMACHE","lines":["When was my lord so much ungently temper'd","To stop his ears against admonishment?","Unarm, unarm, and do not fight today."],"id":1191,"m":"When was my lord ever so harsh in his mood as to block his ears against advice? Take off your armour, take it off, and don't fight today."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["You train me to offend you; get you in.","By all the everlasting gods, I'll go."],"id":1192,"m":"You're provoking me to be rude to you. Go inside. By all the everlasting gods, I will go."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANDROMACHE","lines":["My dreams will, sure, prove ominous to the day."],"id":1193,"m":"My dreams will surely prove to be warnings about today."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["No more, I say."],"id":1194,"m":"No more, I say."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cassandra.","id":1195},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["Where is my brother Hector?"],"id":1196,"m":"Where is my brother Hector?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANDROMACHE","lines":["Here, sister, arm'd, and bloody in intent.","Consort with me in loud and dear petition,","Pursue we him on knees; for I have dreamt","Of bloody turbulence, and this whole night","Hath nothing been but shapes and forms of slaughter."],"id":1197,"m":"Here, sister, armed and set on bloodshed. Join me in loud and heartfelt pleading. Let's follow him on our knees, because I have dreamt of bloody chaos, and this whole night has been nothing but shapes and images of slaughter."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["O, 'tis true!"],"id":1198,"m":"Oh, it's true!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Ho! bid my trumpet sound."],"id":1199,"m":"Hey! Tell my trumpeter to sound."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["No notes of sally, for the heavens, sweet brother!"],"id":1200,"m":"No signal to attack, for heaven's sake, sweet brother!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Be gone, I say. The gods have heard me swear."],"id":1201,"m":"Go away, I say. The gods have heard me swear."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["The gods are deaf to hot and peevish vows;","They are polluted off'rings, more abhorr'd","Than spotted livers in the sacrifice."],"id":1202,"m":"The gods are deaf to hot-headed, stubborn vows. They are polluted offerings, more hateful than diseased livers in a sacrifice."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANDROMACHE","lines":["O, be persuaded! Do not count it holy","To hurt by being just. It is as lawful,","For we would give much, to use violent thefts","And rob in the behalf of charity."],"id":1203,"m":"Oh, be persuaded! Don't think it holy to do harm by keeping your word. You might as well say that because we want to give a lot to charity, it is lawful to steal violently and rob on charity's behalf."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["It is the purpose that makes strong the vow;","But vows to every purpose must not hold.","Unarm, sweet Hector."],"id":1204,"m":"It is the purpose that makes a vow strong, but vows made for any purpose at all must not be kept. Take off your armour, sweet Hector."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Hold you still, I say.","Mine honour keeps the weather of my fate.","Life every man holds dear; but the dear man","Holds honour far more precious dear than life."],"id":1205,"m":"Keep quiet, I say. My honour holds the upper hand over my fate. Every man values life, but the worthy man values honour far more dearly than life."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Troilus.","id":1206},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","cont":1,"lines":["How now, young man! Mean'st thou to fight today?"],"id":1207,"m":"Well, young man! Do you mean to fight today?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANDROMACHE","lines":["Cassandra, call my father to persuade."],"id":1208,"m":"Cassandra, call my father to persuade him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Cassandra.","id":1209},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["No, faith, young Troilus; doff thy harness, youth;","I am today i' th'vein of chivalry.","Let grow thy sinews till their knots be strong,","And tempt not yet the brushes of the war.","Unarm thee, go; and doubt thou not, brave boy,","I'll stand today for thee and me and Troy."],"id":1210,"m":"No, honestly, young Troilus, take off your armour, youth. I am in the mood for knightly deeds today. Let your muscles grow until they are strong and knotted, and don't risk the clashes of war yet. Take off your armour, go; and don't doubt it, brave boy, I'll stand up today for you and me and Troy."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Brother, you have a vice of mercy in you,","Which better fits a lion than a man."],"id":1211,"m":"Brother, you have a weakness for mercy, which suits a lion better than a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["What vice is that? Good Troilus, chide me for it."],"id":1212,"m":"What weakness is that? Good Troilus, scold me for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["When many times the captive Grecian falls,","Even in the fan and wind of your fair sword,","You bid them rise and live."],"id":1213,"m":"Many times, when a captured Greek falls just from the breeze and wind of your fine sword, you tell him to get up and live."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["O, 'tis fair play!"],"id":1214,"m":"Oh, that's fair play!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Fool's play, by heaven, Hector."],"id":1215,"m":"Fool's play, by heaven, Hector."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["How now? how now?"],"id":1216,"m":"What? What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["For th' love of all the gods,","Let's leave the hermit Pity with our mother;","And when we have our armours buckled on,","The venom'd vengeance ride upon our swords,","Spur them to ruthful work, rein them from ruth!"],"id":1217,"m":"For the love of all the gods, let's leave pity, that hermit, at home with our mother. And when we have buckled on our armour, let poisonous vengeance ride on our swords; spur them on to pitiful work and rein them back from pity!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Fie, savage, fie!"],"id":1218,"m":"Shame, savage, shame!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Hector, then 'tis wars."],"id":1219,"m":"Hector, that's what war is."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Troilus, I would not have you fight today."],"id":1220,"m":"Troilus, I don't want you to fight today."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Who should withhold me?","Not fate, obedience, nor the hand of Mars","Beckoning with fiery truncheon my retire;","Not Priamus and Hecuba on knees,","Their eyes o'er-galled with recourse of tears;","Nor you, my brother, with your true sword drawn,","Oppos'd to hinder me, should stop my way,","But by my ruin."],"id":1221,"m":"Who could hold me back? Not fate, not obedience, not the hand of Mars signalling my retreat with his fiery staff; not Priam and Hecuba on their knees, their eyes sore from streaming tears; nor you, my brother, with your true sword drawn against me to stop me, could block my way, except by killing me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Cassandra with Priam.","id":1222},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["Lay hold upon him, Priam, hold him fast;","He is thy crutch; now if thou lose thy stay,","Thou on him leaning, and all Troy on thee,","Fall all together."],"id":1223,"m":"Take hold of him, Priam, hold him tight. He is your crutch. Now if you lose your support, you who lean on him, and all Troy that leans on you, will all fall together."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRIAM","lines":["Come, Hector, come, go back.","Thy wife hath dreamt; thy mother hath had visions;","Cassandra doth foresee; and I myself","Am like a prophet suddenly enrapt","To tell thee that this day is ominous.","Therefore, come back."],"id":1224,"m":"Come, Hector, come, go back. Your wife has dreamt, your mother has had visions, Cassandra foresees it, and I myself am like a prophet suddenly seized to tell you that this day is ill-omened. So come back."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Aeneas is a-field;","And I do stand engag'd to many Greeks,","Even in the faith of valour, to appear","This morning to them."],"id":1225,"m":"Aeneas is on the battlefield, and I am pledged to many Greeks, on my honour as a soldier, to meet them this morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRIAM","lines":["Ay, but thou shalt not go."],"id":1226,"m":"Yes, but you shall not go."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["I must not break my faith.","You know me dutiful; therefore, dear sir,","Let me not shame respect; but give me leave","To take that course by your consent and voice","Which you do here forbid me, royal Priam."],"id":1227,"m":"I must not break my word. You know I am dutiful, so, dear sir, don't make me shame my respect for you; but give me permission, with your consent and approval, to do what you are forbidding me here, royal Priam."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["O Priam, yield not to him!"],"id":1228,"m":"Oh Priam, don't give in to him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANDROMACHE","lines":["Do not, dear father."],"id":1229,"m":"Don't, dear father."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Andromache, I am offended with you.","Upon the love you bear me, get you in."],"id":1230,"m":"Andromache, I am angry with you. By the love you have for me, go inside."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Andromache.","id":1231},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["This foolish, dreaming, superstitious girl","Makes all these bodements."],"id":1232,"m":"This foolish, dreaming, superstitious girl makes up all these omens."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["O, farewell, dear Hector!","Look how thou diest. Look how thy eye turns pale.","Look how thy wounds do bleed at many vents.","Hark how Troy roars; how Hecuba cries out;","How poor Andromache shrills her dolours forth;","Behold distraction, frenzy, and amazement,","Like witless antics, one another meet,","And all cry, 'Hector! Hector's dead! O Hector!'"],"id":1233,"m":"Oh, farewell, dear Hector! Look how you die! Look how your eyes grow pale! Look how your wounds bleed from many openings! Listen how Troy roars, how Hecuba cries out, how poor Andromache shrieks out her grief! See madness, frenzy and horror meet each other like crazed clowns, and all cry, \"Hector! Hector's dead! Oh Hector!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Away, away!"],"id":1234,"m":"Away, away!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSANDRA","lines":["Farewell! yet, soft! Hector, I take my leave.","Thou dost thyself and all our Troy deceive."],"id":1235,"m":"Farewell! Yet wait! Hector, I say goodbye. You are deceiving yourself and all our Troy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1236},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["You are amaz'd, my liege, at her exclaim.","Go in, and cheer the town; we'll forth, and fight,","Do deeds worth praise and tell you them at night."],"id":1237,"m":"You are shaken by her outcry, my lord. Go in and cheer up the town. We'll go out and fight, do deeds worth praising and tell you about them tonight."},{"t":"sp","who":"PRIAM","lines":["Farewell. The gods with safety stand about thee!"],"id":1238,"m":"Farewell. May the gods stand around you and keep you safe!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt severally Priam and Hector. Alarums.","id":1239},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["They are at it, hark! Proud Diomed, believe,","I come to lose my arm or win my sleeve."],"id":1240,"m":"They're at it, listen! Proud Diomedes, believe me, I'm coming to lose my arm or win back my sleeve."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pandarus.","id":1241},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Do you hear, my lord? Do you hear?"],"id":1242,"m":"Do you hear, my lord? Do you hear?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["What now?"],"id":1243,"m":"What now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["Here's a letter come from yond poor girl."],"id":1244,"m":"Here's a letter from that poor girl over there."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Let me read."],"id":1245,"m":"Let me read it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["A whoreson tisick, a whoreson rascally tisick, so troubles me, and the foolish fortune of this girl, and what one thing, what another, that I shall leave you one o' these days; and I have a rheum in mine eyes too, and such an ache in my bones that unless a man were curs'd I cannot tell what to think on't. What says she there?"],"id":1246,"m":"A damned cough, a damned rascally cough troubles me so much, and this girl's foolish luck, and one thing and another, that I'll leave you one of these days. And I have a watering in my eyes too, and such an ache in my bones that, unless a man had been cursed, I don't know what to make of it. What does she say there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Words, words, mere words, no matter from the heart;","Th'effect doth operate another way."],"id":1247,"m":"Words, words, mere words, nothing from the heart. The deeds work another way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Tearing the letter.","id":1248},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","cont":1,"lines":["Go, wind, to wind, there turn and change together.","My love with words and errors still she feeds,","But edifies another with her deeds."],"id":1249,"m":"Go, wind, to the wind, and there turn and change together. She still feeds my love with words and lies, but builds up another man with her deeds."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt severally.","id":1250},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"The plain between Troy and the Grecian camp.","id":1251},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums. Excursions. Enter Thersites.","id":1252},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Now they are clapper-clawing one another; I'll go look on. That dissembling abominable varlet, Diomed, has got that same scurvy doting foolish young knave's sleeve of Troy there in his helm. I would fain see them meet, that that same young Trojan ass that loves the whore there might send that Greekish whoremasterly villain with the sleeve back to the dissembling luxurious drab of a sleeve-less errand. O' the other side, the policy of those crafty swearing rascals that stale old mouse-eaten dry cheese, Nestor, and that same dog-fox, Ulysses, is not prov'd worth a blackberry. They set me up, in policy, that mongrel cur, Ajax, against that dog of as bad a kind, Achilles; and now is the cur, Ajax prouder than the cur Achilles, and will not arm today; whereupon the Grecians begin to proclaim barbarism, and policy grows into an ill opinion."],"id":1253,"m":"Now they're clawing at each other; I'll go and watch. That lying, disgusting rascal Diomedes has got that scabby, doting, foolish young Trojan rogue's sleeve on his helmet there. I'd love to see them meet, so that the young Trojan donkey who loves the whore there could send that Greek whoremongering villain with the sleeve back to the lying, lustful tart on a sleeveless (pointless) errand. On the other side, the cunning of those crafty, lying rascals, that stale, old, mouse-eaten dry cheese Nestor and that dog-fox Ulysses, has turned out not to be worth a blackberry. They set up that mongrel dog Ajax, as a strategy, against that dog of just as bad a breed, Achilles. And now the dog Ajax is prouder than the dog Achilles, and won't arm himself today. So the Greeks are starting to declare themselves barbarians, and strategy is getting a bad name."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Diomedes, Troilus following.","id":1254},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","cont":1,"lines":["Soft! here comes sleeve, and t'other."],"id":1255,"m":"Wait! Here comes the sleeve, and the other one."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Fly not; for shouldst thou take the river Styx, I would swim after."],"id":1256,"m":"Don't run. Even if you took to the river Styx, I would swim after you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Thou dost miscall retire.","I do not fly; but advantageous care","Withdrew me from the odds of multitude.","Have at thee!"],"id":1257,"m":"You give my retreat the wrong name. I'm not running away; I withdrew carefully, for my own advantage, from being outnumbered. Here I come!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["Hold thy whore, Grecian; now for thy whore,","Trojan! now the sleeve, now the sleeve!"],"id":1258,"m":"Hold on to your whore, Greek! Now fight for your whore, Trojan! Now the sleeve, now the sleeve!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Troilus and Diomedes fighting.","id":1259},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hector.","id":1260},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["What art thou, Greek? Art thou for Hector's match?","Art thou of blood and honour?"],"id":1261,"m":"Who are you, Greek? Are you a match for Hector? Are you of noble blood and honour?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["No, no I am a rascal; a scurvy railing knave; a very filthy rogue."],"id":1262,"m":"No, no, I'm a rascal, a scabby, ranting rogue, a really filthy villain."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["I do believe thee. Live."],"id":1263,"m":"I believe you. Live."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1264},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["God-a-mercy, that thou wilt believe me; but a plague break thy neck for frighting me! What's become of the wenching rogues? I think they have swallowed one another. I would laugh at that miracle. Yet, in a sort, lechery eats itself. I'll seek them."],"id":1265,"m":"Thank God you believe me. But a plague break your neck for frightening me! What's become of the whoring rogues? I think they've swallowed each other. I'd laugh at that miracle. Still, in a way, lechery eats itself. I'll look for them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1266},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"Another part of the plain.","id":1267},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Diomedes and a Servant.","id":1268},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Go, go, my servant, take thou Troilus' horse;","Present the fair steed to my lady Cressid.","Fellow, commend my service to her beauty;","Tell her I have chastis'd the amorous Trojan,","And am her knight by proof."],"id":1269,"m":"Go, go, my servant, take Troilus' horse. Present the fine horse to my lady Cressida. Fellow, give my service to her beauty. Tell her I have punished the lovesick Trojan, and I am her knight, as I have proved."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["I go, my lord."],"id":1270,"m":"I'm going, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1271},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Agamemnon.","id":1272},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Renew, renew! The fierce Polydamas","Hath beat down Menon; bastard Margarelon","Hath Doreus prisoner,","And stands colossus-wise, waving his beam,","Upon the pashed corses of the kings","Epistrophus and Cedius. Polixenes is slain;","Amphimacus and Thoas deadly hurt;","Patroclus ta'en, or slain; and Palamedes","Sore hurt and bruis'd. The dreadful Sagittary","Appals our numbers. Haste we, Diomed,","To reinforcement, or we perish all."],"id":1273,"m":"Renew the attack, renew it! The fierce Polydamas has beaten down Menon. The bastard Margarelon has taken Doreus prisoner and stands like a colossus, waving his heavy spear over the battered corpses of the kings Epistrophus and Cedius. Polixenes is killed; Amphimachus and Thoas are fatally wounded; Patroclus is captured or killed; and Palamedes is badly wounded and bruised. The dreadful centaur archer terrifies our troops. Let's hurry, Diomedes, to bring reinforcements, or we'll all die."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Nestor.","id":1274},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Go, bear Patroclus' body to Achilles,","And bid the snail-pac'd Ajax arm for shame.","There is a thousand Hectors in the field;","Now here he fights on Galathe his horse,","And there lacks work; anon he's there afoot,","And there they fly or die, like scaled sculls","Before the belching whale; then is he yonder,","And there the strawy Greeks, ripe for his edge,","Fall down before him like the mower's swath.","Here, there, and everywhere, he leaves and takes;","Dexterity so obeying appetite","That what he will he does, and does so much","That proof is call'd impossibility."],"id":1275,"m":"Go, take Patroclus' body to Achilles, and tell the snail-paced Ajax to arm himself for shame. There are a thousand Hectors on the battlefield. Now here he fights on his horse Galathe, and soon there's no one left to fight. Next he's over there on foot, and there they run or die, like shoals of scaly fish before a spouting whale. Then he's over there, and there the Greeks fall down before him like straw, ripe for his blade, like grass before the mower. Here, there and everywhere he leaves and takes, his skill so obedient to his desire that whatever he wants to do he does, and does so much that the evidence seems impossible."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ulysses.","id":1276},{"t":"sp","who":"ULYSSES","lines":["O, courage, courage, courage, Princes! Great Achilles","Is arming, weeping, cursing, vowing vengeance.","Patroclus' wounds have rous'd his drowsy blood,","Together with his mangled Myrmidons,","That noseless, handless, hack'd and chipp'd, come to him,","Crying on Hector. Ajax hath lost a friend","And foams at mouth, and he is arm'd and at it,","Roaring for Troilus; who hath done today","Mad and fantastic execution,","Engaging and redeeming of himself","With such a careless force and forceless care","As if that lust, in very spite of cunning,","Bade him win all."],"id":1277,"m":"Oh, courage, courage, courage, Princes! Great Achilles is arming, weeping, cursing, swearing vengeance. Patroclus' wounds have roused his drowsy blood, together with his mangled Myrmidons, who come to him noseless, handless, hacked and chipped, crying out against Hector. Ajax has lost a friend and is foaming at the mouth; he is armed and in the fight, roaring for Troilus, who has done mad and incredible slaughter today, throwing himself into danger and getting himself out again with such careless strength and effortless care, as if lust for battle, in spite of all skill, told him to win everything."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ajax.","id":1278},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Troilus! thou coward Troilus!"],"id":1279,"m":"Troilus! You coward Troilus!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1280},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Ay, there, there."],"id":1281,"m":"Yes, there, there."},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["So, so, we draw together."],"id":1282,"m":"So, so, we're fighting together."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1283},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Achilles.","id":1284},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Where is this Hector?","Come, come, thou boy-queller, show thy face;","Know what it is to meet Achilles angry.","Hector! where's Hector? I will none but Hector."],"id":1285,"m":"Where is this Hector? Come, come, you boy-killer, show your face. Find out what it is to meet Achilles angry. Hector! Where's Hector? I want no one but Hector."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1286},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"VI","loc":"Another part of the plain.","id":1287},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ajax.","id":1288},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Troilus, thou coward Troilus, show thy head."],"id":1289,"m":"Troilus, you coward Troilus, show your head."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Diomedes.","id":1290},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Troilus, I say! Where's Troilus?"],"id":1291,"m":"Troilus, I say! Where's Troilus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["What wouldst thou?"],"id":1292,"m":"What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["I would correct him."],"id":1293,"m":"I want to punish him."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["Were I the general, thou shouldst have my office","Ere that correction. Troilus, I say! What, Troilus!"],"id":1294,"m":"If I were the general, you could have my position before you got to punish him. Troilus, I say! Hey, Troilus!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Troilus.","id":1295},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["O traitor Diomed! Turn thy false face, thou traitor,","And pay thy life thou owest me for my horse."],"id":1296,"m":"Oh traitor Diomedes! Turn your false face round, you traitor, and pay me the life you owe me for my horse."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["Ha! art thou there?"],"id":1297,"m":"Ha! Are you there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["I'll fight with him alone. Stand, Diomed."],"id":1298,"m":"I'll fight him alone. Stand back, Diomedes."},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["He is my prize. I will not look upon."],"id":1299,"m":"He's my prize. I won't just stand and watch."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Come, both, you cogging Greeks; have at you both!"],"id":1300,"m":"Come on, both of you, you cheating Greeks. I'll take you both on!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt fighting.","id":1301},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hector.","id":1302},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Yea, Troilus? O, well fought, my youngest brother!"],"id":1303,"m":"Yes, Troilus? Oh, well fought, my youngest brother!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Achilles.","id":1304},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Now do I see thee. Ha! have at thee, Hector!"],"id":1305,"m":"Now I see you. Ha! Here I come, Hector!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Pause, if thou wilt."],"id":1306,"m":"Take a rest, if you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["I do disdain thy courtesy, proud Trojan.","Be happy that my arms are out of use;","My rest and negligence befriend thee now,","But thou anon shalt hear of me again;","Till when, go seek thy fortune."],"id":1307,"m":"I scorn your courtesy, proud Trojan. Be glad that my arms are out of practice. My rest and laziness are your friends now, but you'll hear from me again soon. Until then, go and look for your luck."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1308},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Fare thee well.","I would have been much more a fresher man,","Had I expected thee."],"id":1309,"m":"Goodbye. I would have been a much fresher man if I had been expecting you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Troilus.","id":1310},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","cont":1,"lines":["How now, my brother!"],"id":1311,"m":"Well, my brother!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Ajax hath ta'en Aeneas. Shall it be?","No, by the flame of yonder glorious heaven,","He shall not carry him; I'll be ta'en too,","Or bring him off. Fate, hear me what I say:","I reck not though thou end my life today."],"id":1312,"m":"Ajax has captured Aeneas. Shall we allow it? No, by the flame of that glorious sky up there, he won't take him away. I'll be captured too, or rescue him. Fate, hear what I say: I don't care if you end my life today."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1313},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter one in armour.","id":1314},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Stand, stand, thou Greek; thou art a goodly mark.","No? wilt thou not? I like thy armour well;","I'll frush it and unlock the rivets all","But I'll be master of it. Wilt thou not, beast, abide?","Why then, fly on; I'll hunt thee for thy hide."],"id":1315,"m":"Stop, stop, you Greek; you're a fine target. No? Won't you? I like your armour. I'll smash it and break open all the rivets, but I'll be its master. Won't you stay, you beast? Why then, run on; I'll hunt you for your hide."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1316},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"VII","loc":"Another part of the plain.","id":1317},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Achilles with Myrmidons.","id":1318},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Come here about me, you my Myrmidons;","Mark what I say. Attend me where I wheel;","Strike not a stroke, but keep yourselves in breath;","And when I have the bloody Hector found,","Empale him with your weapons round about;","In fellest manner execute your arms.","Follow me, sirs, and my proceedings eye.","It is decreed Hector the great must die."],"id":1319,"m":"Gather round me here, you, my Myrmidons. Listen to what I say. Follow me wherever I turn. Don't strike a blow, but save your breath, and when I have found the bloodthirsty Hector, surround him with your weapons and use them in the most savage way. Follow me, men, and watch what I do. It is decreed that great Hector must die."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1320},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Menelaus and Paris, fighting; then Thersites.","id":1321},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["The cuckold and the cuckold-maker are at it. Now, bull! Now, dog! 'Loo, Paris, 'loo! now my double-hen'd Spartan! 'loo, Paris, 'loo! The bull has the game. 'Ware horns, ho!"],"id":1322,"m":"The cuckold and the cuckold-maker are at it. Now, bull! Now, dog! Go on, Paris, go on! Now, my Spartan with two hens (two husbands for one wife)! Go on, Paris, go on! The bull is winning. Watch out for the horns, ho!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Paris and Menelaus.","id":1323},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Margarelon.","id":1324},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARELON","lines":["Turn, slave, and fight."],"id":1325,"m":"Turn round, slave, and fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["What art thou?"],"id":1326,"m":"Who are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARELON","lines":["A bastard son of Priam's."],"id":1327,"m":"A bastard son of Priam's."},{"t":"sp","who":"THERSITES","lines":["I am a bastard too; I love bastards. I am a bastard begot, bastard instructed, bastard in mind, bastard in valour, in everything illegitimate. One bear will not bite another, and wherefore should one bastard? Take heed, the quarrel's most ominous to us: if the son of a whore fight for a whore, he tempts judgement. Farewell, bastard."],"id":1328,"m":"I'm a bastard too. I love bastards. I was born a bastard, educated a bastard, I'm a bastard in mind, a bastard in courage, illegitimate in everything. One bear won't bite another, so why should one bastard bite another? Be careful: the quarrel is very unlucky for us. If the son of a whore fights for a whore, he's tempting judgement. Goodbye, bastard."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1329},{"t":"sp","who":"MARGARELON","lines":["The devil take thee, coward!"],"id":1330,"m":"The devil take you, coward!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1331},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"VIII","loc":"Another part of the plain.","id":1332},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hector.","id":1333},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["Most putrified core so fair without,","Thy goodly armour thus hath cost thy life.","Now is my day's work done; I'll take my breath:","Rest, sword; thou hast thy fill of blood and death!"],"id":1334,"m":"You rotten core, so beautiful on the outside, your fine armour has cost you your life. Now my day's work is done. I'll get my breath back. Rest, sword; you've had your fill of blood and death!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Disarms.","id":1335},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Achilles and Myrmidons.","id":1336},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Look, Hector, how the sun begins to set,","How ugly night comes breathing at his heels;","Even with the vail and dark'ning of the sun,","To close the day up, Hector's life is done."],"id":1337,"m":"Look, Hector, how the sun begins to set, how ugly night comes breathing at its heels. Just as the sun goes down and darkens to close the day, Hector's life is over."},{"t":"sp","who":"HECTOR","lines":["I am unarm'd; forego this vantage, Greek."],"id":1338,"m":"I am unarmed. Give up this advantage, Greek."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["Strike, fellows, strike; this is the man I seek."],"id":1339,"m":"Strike, men, strike; this is the man I'm looking for."},{"t":"sd","text":"Hector falls.","id":1340},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","cont":1,"lines":["So, Ilion, fall thou next! Now, Troy, sink down;","Here lies thy heart, thy sinews, and thy bone.","On, Myrmidons, and cry you all amain","'Achilles hath the mighty Hector slain.'"],"id":1341,"m":"So, Troy, fall next! Now, Troy, sink down. Here lies your heart, your muscles and your bones. On, Myrmidons, and all shout as loud as you can, \"Achilles has killed the mighty Hector.\""},{"t":"sd","text":"A retreat sounded.","id":1342},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","cont":1,"lines":["Hark! a retire upon our Grecian part."],"id":1343,"m":"Listen! A retreat sounded on our Greek side."},{"t":"sp","who":"MYRMIDON","lines":["The Trojan trumpets sound the like, my lord."],"id":1344,"m":"The Trojan trumpets are sounding the same, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","lines":["The dragon wing of night o'erspreads the earth","And, stickler-like, the armies separates.","My half-supp'd sword, that frankly would have fed,","Pleas'd with this dainty bait, thus goes to bed."],"id":1345,"m":"The dragon wings of night spread over the earth and separate the armies, like an umpire. My half-fed sword, which would gladly have eaten more, pleased with this tasty snack, goes to bed like this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sheathes his sword.","id":1346},{"t":"sp","who":"ACHILLES","cont":1,"lines":["Come, tie his body to my horse's tail;","Along the field I will the Trojan trail."],"id":1347,"m":"Come, tie his body to my horse's tail. I'll drag the Trojan along the battlefield."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1348},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IX","loc":"Another part of the plain.","id":1349},{"t":"sd","text":"Sound retreat. Shout. Enter Agamemnon, Ajax, Menelaus, Nestor, Diomedes and the rest, marching.","id":1350},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["Hark! hark! what shout is this?"],"id":1351,"m":"Listen, listen! What's that shouting?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NESTOR","lines":["Peace, drums!"],"id":1352,"m":"Quiet, drums!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOLDIERS","lines":["[Within] Achilles! Achilles! Hector's slain. Achilles!"],"id":1353,"m":"(Offstage) Achilles! Achilles! Hector's dead. Achilles!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DIOMEDES","lines":["The bruit is, Hector's slain, and by Achilles."],"id":1354,"m":"The rumour is that Hector has been killed, and by Achilles."},{"t":"sp","who":"AJAX","lines":["If it be so, yet bragless let it be;","Great Hector was as good a man as he."],"id":1355,"m":"If it's true, let there be no boasting about it. Great Hector was as good a man as he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"AGAMEMNON","lines":["March patiently along. Let one be sent","To pray Achilles see us at our tent.","If in his death the gods have us befriended;","Great Troy is ours, and our sharp wars are ended."],"id":1356,"m":"March on steadily. Let someone be sent to ask Achilles to meet us at our tent. If the gods have done us a favour in his death, great Troy is ours and our bitter wars are over."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1357},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"X","loc":"Another part of the plain.","id":1358},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Aeneas, Paris, Antenor and Deiphobus.","id":1359},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["Stand, ho! yet are we masters of the field.","Never go home; here starve we out the night."],"id":1360,"m":"Halt, there! We are still masters of the field. Let's not go home; we'll last out the night here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Troilus.","id":1361},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Hector is slain."],"id":1362,"m":"Hector is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Hector! The gods forbid!"],"id":1363,"m":"Hector! The gods forbid!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["He's dead, and at the murderer's horse's tail,","In beastly sort, dragg'd through the shameful field.","Frown on, you heavens, effect your rage with speed.","Sit, gods, upon your thrones, and smile at Troy.","I say at once let your brief plagues be mercy,","And linger not our sure destructions on."],"id":1364,"m":"He's dead, and dragged like an animal through the shameful field, tied to his murderer's horse's tail. Frown on, you heavens, and carry out your anger quickly. Sit on your thrones, you gods, and smile at Troy. I say, let your quick plagues be merciful, and don't drag out our certain destruction."},{"t":"sp","who":"AENEAS","lines":["My lord, you do discomfort all the host."],"id":1365,"m":"My lord, you are discouraging the whole army."},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["You understand me not that tell me so.","I do not speak of flight, of fear of death,","But dare all imminence that gods and men","Address their dangers in. Hector is gone.","Who shall tell Priam so, or Hecuba?","Let him that will a screech-owl aye be call'd","Go in to Troy, and say there 'Hector's dead.'","There is a word will Priam turn to stone;","Make wells and Niobes of the maids and wives,","Cold statues of the youth; and, in a word,","Scare Troy out of itself. But, march away;","Hector is dead; there is no more to say.","Stay yet. You vile abominable tents,","Thus proudly pight upon our Phrygian plains,","Let Titan rise as early as he dare,","I'll through and through you. And, thou great-siz'd coward,","No space of earth shall sunder our two hates;","I'll haunt thee like a wicked conscience still,","That mouldeth goblins swift as frenzy's thoughts.","Strike a free march to Troy. With comfort go;","Hope of revenge shall hide our inward woe."],"id":1366,"m":"You don't understand me if you say that. I'm not talking about running away or fear of death, but I dare every danger that gods and men can threaten. Hector is gone. Who will tell Priam, or Hecuba? Let whoever is willing to be called a screech owl for ever go into Troy and say there, \"Hector's dead.\" Those words will turn Priam to stone, turn the young women and wives into wells of tears like Niobe, and the young men into cold statues; in a word, frighten Troy out of its wits. But march away. Hector is dead; there is no more to say. Wait a moment. You vile, disgusting tents, pitched so proudly on our Trojan plains, let the sun rise as early as it dares, I'll go through and through you. And you, great big coward (Achilles), no distance on earth will keep our two hatreds apart. I'll haunt you for ever like a guilty conscience, which makes up goblins as fast as a madman's thoughts. Beat a lively march back to Troy. Go with comfort: the hope of revenge will hide our inner grief."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pandarus.","id":1367},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["But hear you, hear you!"],"id":1368,"m":"But listen to me, listen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TROILUS","lines":["Hence, broker-lackey. Ignominy and shame","Pursue thy life, and live aye with thy name!"],"id":1369,"m":"Get out, you go-between, you lackey. May disgrace and shame follow you all your life, and live for ever with your name!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Pandarus.","id":1370},{"t":"sp","who":"PANDARUS","lines":["A goodly medicine for my aching bones! O world! world! Thus is the poor agent despis'd! O traitors and bawds, how earnestly are you set a-work, and how ill requited! Why should our endeavour be so lov'd, and the performance so loathed? What verse for it? What instance for it? Let me see—","~Full merrily the humble-bee doth sing","~Till he hath lost his honey and his sting;","~And being once subdu'd in armed trail,","~Sweet honey and sweet notes together fail.","Good traders in the flesh, set this in your painted cloths.","As many as be here of Pandar's hall,","Your eyes, half out, weep out at Pandar's fall;","Or, if you cannot weep, yet give some groans,","Though not for me, yet for your aching bones.","Brethren and sisters of the hold-door trade,","Some two months hence my will shall here be made.","It should be now, but that my fear is this,","Some galled goose of Winchester would hiss.","Till then I'll sweat and seek about for eases,","And at that time bequeath you my diseases."],"id":1371,"m":"A fine medicine for my aching bones! Oh world, world! This is how the poor agent is despised! Oh traitors and pimps, how eagerly you are put to work, and how badly you are paid! Why should our efforts be so wanted and the result so hated? What verse is there for it? What example? Let me see: \"The bumblebee sings very merrily until he has lost his honey and his sting; and once his weapon's tail is gone, sweet honey and sweet songs both fail together.\" Good traders in flesh, put this on your painted wall-hangings. All of you here who belong to Pandar's hall, let your eyes, half blind already, weep at Pandar's fall. Or if you can't weep, at least give a few groans, if not for me, then for your aching bones. Brothers and sisters of the door-keeping trade, in about two months I'll make my will here. It should be now, except that I'm afraid some sore prostitute from Winchester would hiss. Until then I'll sweat and look around for remedies, and then I'll leave you my diseases in my will."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1372}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Troilus","group":"The Trojans","desc":"Priam's youngest son, not yet twenty-three, a fierce fighter whom Ulysses calls a second Hector. In love with Cressida, he relies on her uncle Pandarus to win her and spends one night with her before the Trojan council trades her to the Greeks. He swears to be true and makes her promise the same, then follows Ulysses to Calchas's tent and watches her give his love token to Diomedes. Unable at first to believe his eyes, he turns his love into rage, hunts Diomedes in battle, and after Hector's death vows endless revenge on Achilles and curses Pandarus."},{"name":"Cressida","group":"The Trojans","desc":"Daughter of Calchas, the Trojan priest who has defected to the Greeks, left behind in Troy in her uncle Pandarus's care. Quick-witted and guarded, she enjoys sparring with Pandarus and believes men value women only until they win them, so she hides her love for Troilus. When she gives in, she swears that if she is false, 'as false as Cressid' will become a proverb. Exchanged for Antenor the next morning, she is kissed by the Greek commanders on arrival, and within a day accepts Diomedes, giving him Troilus's sleeve while still half regretting it."},{"name":"Pandarus","group":"The Trojans","desc":"Cressida's uncle and Troilus's go-between, a chattering old lord who loves gossip, songs and other people's love affairs. He praises Troilus to Cressida, brings the lovers together, and blesses their night with a joke about pressing the bed to death. His own name becomes a word for a pimp, as he predicts. At the end Troilus rejects him, and, sick and complaining, he speaks the epilogue to the 'traders in the flesh' in the audience, promising to leave them his diseases."},{"name":"Hector","group":"The Trojans","desc":"Priam's eldest son and Troy's greatest warrior: courteous, generous and merciful in battle. In the Trojan council he argues by reason and law that Helen should be returned, then suddenly agrees to keep her for the sake of honour. He fights his cousin Ajax but stops for kinship, visits the Greek camp, and is insulted by Achilles' boasting. On the last day he ignores the warnings of Andromache, Cassandra and Priam, spares Achilles once, and is killed unarmed by Achilles' Myrmidons after chasing a Greek for his fine armour."},{"name":"Priam","group":"The Trojans","desc":"King of Troy, father of Hector, Troilus, Paris, Helenus, Deiphobus and Cassandra. He puts the Greek offer to his sons, and on the last day begs Hector not to fight."},{"name":"Paris","group":"The Trojans","desc":"Priam's son, whose abduction of Helen started the war. He defends keeping her as a matter of honour, lazes at home with her while others fight, and helps carry out the exchange of Cressida."},{"name":"Helen","group":"The Trojans","desc":"Wife of Menelaus, carried off to Troy by Paris. The 'pearl' the war is fought over appears only once, flirting with Pandarus and asking him to sing of love. To Diomedes she is a whore for whom a life has been lost for every drop of her blood."},{"name":"Cassandra","group":"The Trojans","desc":"Priam's daughter, a prophetess no one believes. She bursts into the council crying that Troy will burn unless Helen goes, and on the last day foresees Hector's death in detail."},{"name":"Andromache","group":"The Trojans","desc":"Hector's wife. Terrified by dreams of slaughter, she begs him not to fight, and he sends her away angrily."},{"name":"Aeneas","group":"The Trojans","desc":"A Trojan commander who carries Hector's challenge to the Greek camp, trades courteous threats with Diomedes, and brings the news that Cressida must be handed over."},{"name":"Helenus, Deiphobus and Antenor","group":"The Trojans","desc":"Priam's sons Helenus (a priest, mocked by Troilus for preferring reason) and Deiphobus, and the statesman Antenor, the prisoner whose release is bought with Cressida."},{"name":"Margarelon","group":"The Trojans","desc":"A bastard son of Priam, who challenges Thersites in the battle and is talked out of it."},{"name":"Alexander","group":"The Trojans","desc":"Cressida's servant, who tells her about Hector's anger and gives a comic portrait of Ajax."},{"name":"Agamemnon","group":"The Greeks","desc":"The Greek commander-in-chief. He opens the Greek council by blaming the seven-year failure on Jove's tests, adopts Ulysses' plan to snub Achilles, and receives Hector courteously."},{"name":"Ulysses","group":"The Greeks","desc":"The cleverest Greek. His great speech on 'degree' blames the stalemate on the collapse of order in the camp. He plots to humble Achilles by rigging the lottery for Ajax and having the generals ignore him, lectures Achilles on Time and reputation, and leads Troilus to Calchas's tent to witness Cressida's betrayal. Thersites calls him a dog-fox, and his schemes do little in the end."},{"name":"Nestor","group":"The Greeks","desc":"The oldest Greek leader, full of long speeches and old memories. He offers to fight Hector himself for his lady's honour, and helps Ulysses with his plot."},{"name":"Achilles","group":"The Greeks","desc":"The greatest Greek warrior, who sulks in his tent with Patroclus, mocking the generals. He is secretly in love with Priam's daughter Polyxena and keeps a promise to her mother not to fight. Snubbed by the generals, he invites Hector to his tent and stares at him like a buyer. Patroclus's death drives him back to battle, where, after Hector has spared him, he has his Myrmidons kill Hector unarmed and drags the body behind his horse."},{"name":"Patroclus","group":"The Greeks","desc":"Achilles' companion, who entertains him by mimicking the generals. Thersites calls him Achilles' 'masculine whore'. He urges Achilles to fight, and his death in battle finally brings Achilles back to the field."},{"name":"Ajax","group":"The Greeks","desc":"A huge, slow-witted Greek lord, Hector's cousin through his Trojan mother. He beats Thersites, swells with pride when chosen to fight Hector, and claims to hate pride while praising himself. The duel with Hector ends in an embrace."},{"name":"Diomedes","group":"The Greeks","desc":"A blunt, cynical Greek commander. He escorts Cressida to the Greek camp, refuses Troilus's orders about how to treat her, and seduces her, taking Troilus's sleeve to wear on his helmet. He calls Helen a whore to Paris's face and sends Troilus's captured horse to Cressida."},{"name":"Menelaus","group":"The Greeks","desc":"Agamemnon's brother and Helen's husband, the cuckold whose wrong began the war. Everyone, from Troilus to Thersites, jokes about his horns."},{"name":"Thersites","group":"The Greeks","desc":"A deformed, foul-mouthed Greek who rails at everyone. He reduces the war to 'a whore and a cuckold', curses the leaders with diseases, comments on the scene at Calchas's tent, and escapes every fight by declaring himself a coward and a bastard. His voice is the play's harshest view of war and lust."},{"name":"Calchas","group":"The Greeks","desc":"A Trojan priest who has foreseen Troy's fall and defected to the Greeks. He asks the Greeks to exchange the prisoner Antenor for his daughter Cressida."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"An armed Prologue explains that the Greeks have come to sack Troy to win back Helen, and that the play begins in the middle of the war. In Troy, Troilus takes off his armour: he is too lovesick for Cressida to fight. Pandarus, her uncle, teases him about having to wait and then sulks, saying he will no longer act as go-between. Alone, Troilus calls the war foolish. Aeneas brings news that Paris has been wounded by Menelaus, and Troilus goes off to the battle after all.","key":["The Prologue's 'Beginning in the middle' warns that this is not a heroic epic but a play in the middle of a long stalemate.","Troilus's 'Helen must needs be fair, / When with your blood you daily paint her thus' questions the war's cause in the very first scene.","Troilus depends entirely on Pandarus: 'I cannot come to Cressid but by Pandar'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Troilus take off his armour?","o":["He has been wounded","He is too lovesick to fight","Hector has called a truce","He wants to visit Helen"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Pandarus compare winning Cressida to?","o":["Climbing a wall","Making a cake","Taming a hawk","Sailing to India"],"a":1},{"q":"Who has wounded Paris?","o":["Achilles","Ajax","Menelaus","Diomedes"],"a":2}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Cressida's servant Alexander tells her that Hector is angry because Ajax knocked him down in battle. Pandarus arrives and insists that Troilus is a better man than Hector, telling a long story about Helen finding a white hair on Troilus's chin. As the Trojan warriors return from the field, Pandarus names each one and praises Troilus above all. Cressida mocks everything her uncle says, but when he leaves, she admits in soliloquy that she loves Troilus and will hide it, because men value what they have not yet won.","key":["Cressida's wit keeps Pandarus at a distance: she guards herself 'at a thousand watches'.","Her soliloquy ('Women are angels, wooing') shows she loves Troilus but distrusts men's love once satisfied.","The parade of warriors is seen through Pandarus's gossip, making heroes into objects of display."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Hector angry?","o":["Paris refused to fight beside him in the field","Ajax knocked him down in battle","Andromache scolded him","Achilles insulted him"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Helen joke about on Troilus's chin?","o":["A scar","A dimple shaped like a heart","A white hair among fifty-two","A wart"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Cressida hide her love?","o":["She secretly loves Diomedes and not Troilus at all","Her father has forbidden it in a letter from the Greek camp","Men prize what they have not yet won","She thinks Troilus is a coward"],"a":2}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"In the Greek camp, Agamemnon and Nestor say the seven-year failure to take Troy is a test from Jove. Ulysses argues instead that the army has lost respect for rank ('degree'): Achilles lies in his tent mocking the generals with Patroclus, and Ajax and Thersites copy him. Aeneas arrives with Hector's challenge: he will fight any Greek who claims his lady is better than Hector's. Nestor offers to fight himself. Ulysses and Nestor see the challenge is aimed at Achilles and plan to rig a lottery so that Ajax is chosen instead, to humble Achilles' pride.","key":["Ulysses' 'degree' speech warns that without order 'appetite, an universal wolf' will 'last eat up himself'.","Patroclus's mimicry of the generals shows the Greek leaders as ridiculous before Thersites even speaks.","The plan to promote Ajax uses one proud man to cure another: 'Two curs shall tame each other'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Ulysses blame for the Greek failure?","o":["Jove's anger at the Greeks for their pride and greed","The neglect of rank and order","Lack of ships, men and food in the Greek camp","Hector's great strength in the field"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Hector's challenge offer?","o":["A truce for a month","Single combat to prove his lady is finest","An exchange of prisoners","The return of Helen"],"a":1},{"q":"Who do Ulysses and Nestor plan to send against Hector?","o":["Achilles","Diomedes","Ajax","Nestor"],"a":2}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Ajax demands that Thersites tell him what Hector's proclamation says. Thersites insults him instead, and Ajax beats him. Achilles and Patroclus come in and Thersites mocks all three, calling them oxen yoked by Ulysses and Nestor. Thersites leaves in disgust. Achilles explains the challenge and says it will be settled by lottery.","key":["Thersites' abuse ('thou mongrel beef-witted lord') turns the Greek heroes into beasts.","Ajax answers words with blows, the opposite of Ulysses' policy.","Achilles knows the lottery is meant for him: 'otherwise, / He knew his man'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Ajax beat Thersites?","o":["Thersites stole his armour from his tent and sold it to the Trojans for gold","Thersites will not tell him the proclamation and insults him","Thersites praised Hector","Thersites works for Achilles"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Thersites say Ulysses and Nestor do to Ajax and Achilles?","o":["Feed them like prize cattle before the fair","Yoke them like oxen to plough the war","Teach them poetry and good manners at court","Bribe them"],"a":1},{"q":"How will Hector's opponent be chosen?","o":["By Agamemnon's command","By lottery","By a duel","By a vote of the kings"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Priam puts the Greek offer to his sons: return Helen and all losses will be forgotten. Hector argues she is not worth the lives she costs, and Helenus backs reason. Troilus mocks Helenus and says value is what we give a thing, and that returning her would shame them. Cassandra bursts in, crying that Troy will burn unless Helen goes. Hector argues from the laws of nature and nations that Helen should go back to her husband, but then agrees to keep her for the sake of their honour, and reveals he has already sent a challenge to the Greeks.","key":["Troilus asks 'What's aught but as 'tis valued?', making worth depend on desire.","Hector's argument is sound ('these moral laws ... speak aloud / To have her back return'd') but he abandons it at once.","Cassandra's warning is ignored, as her prophecies always are."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the Greeks offer through Nestor?","o":["Peace for ever if Hector will surrender himself to the Greek generals","To forget all losses if Helen is returned","Gold for Helen, as much as Troy can hold","A marriage alliance between Greece and Troy"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Cassandra cry?","o":["Hector will die","Troy will burn unless Helen goes","Paris is a traitor","The gods love Troy"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Hector finally decide?","o":["To return Helen to Menelaus at once","To keep Helen for honour's sake","To let Priam decide","To fight Paris"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Thersites rails alone, then mocks Patroclus and Achilles, proving that all of them are fools. Agamemnon and the generals come to Achilles' tent; he refuses to see them and sends Patroclus with excuses. The generals flatter Ajax, who boasts that he hates pride while praising himself. Ulysses persuades Agamemnon not to send Ajax to Achilles, and the generals heap praise on Ajax to set him against Achilles.","key":["Thersites sums up the war: 'All the argument is a whore and a cuckold'.","Ajax says 'I do hate a proud man' in a scene that shows him as proud as Achilles.","The generals' asides show that their praise of Ajax is cynical manipulation."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Thersites say the whole war is about?","o":["Gold, land and the trade of the sea","A whore and a cuckold","Revenge for the death of Achilles' friend","The will of the gods"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Achilles respond to the generals' visit?","o":["He greets them warmly and invites them to supper in his tent","He sends excuses through Patroclus","He challenges Agamemnon to a duel before the whole army","He agrees to fight Hector at once"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Ulysses stop Ajax visiting Achilles?","o":["Ajax might be killed","It would feed Achilles' pride","Achilles is ill","Ajax is needed on guard"],"a":1}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Pandarus comes to Priam's palace to ask Paris to make Troilus's excuses if the king asks for him at supper. He fences with a witty servant, then flatters Paris and Helen, who make him sing a song about love. Paris guesses that Troilus is dining with Cressida. Helen agrees to help unarm Hector when the warriors return.","key":["Helen, the cause of the war, appears only here, idle and flirtatious: 'This love will undo us all'.","Pandarus's song turns the dying of love into sexual laughter: 'O ho! to ha! ha! he!'.","Paris, the man who started the war, has stayed at home today with Helen."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Pandarus want from Paris?","o":["To lend him money for the war and the wedding","To make Troilus's excuses at supper","A place in the army","An introduction to Helen"],"a":1},{"q":"What is Pandarus's song about?","o":["War","Love","Troy","Death of Hector"],"a":1},{"q":"What will Helen do when the warriors return?","o":["Hide from them","Help unarm Hector","Go to the Greek camp","Pray for Paris"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Troilus waits in Pandarus's orchard, giddy with expectation. Pandarus brings Cressida in and teases them. Alone, the lovers talk of fears and of the limits of love. Cressida confesses she has loved Troilus for months, regrets saying so, and tries to leave. Troilus swears he will be the model of truth, 'as true as Troilus'; Cressida swears that if she is false, 'as false as Cressid' will be the byword. Pandarus seals the bargain and leads them to bed.","key":["Troilus fears love's 'thrice-repured nectar' will be too much for his senses.","Cressida's 'If I be false ... As false as Cressid' is an oath the audience knows she will break.","Pandarus names himself: 'let all pitiful goers-between be call'd ... Pandars'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Troilus say is 'monstrous' in love?","o":["Jealousy that comes too soon after love has started","That desire is boundless but the act limited","Marriage, which kills love","Pandarus"],"a":1},{"q":"What proverb does Cressida invite if she is false?","o":["As false as Helen","As false as Cressid","As false as water","As false as a Greek"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Pandarus say all go-betweens will be called?","o":["Pandars","Cupids","Brokers","Mercuries"],"a":0}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"Calchas asks the Greeks to exchange the captured Trojan Antenor for his daughter Cressida, and Agamemnon sends Diomedes. Following Ulysses' plan, the generals walk past Achilles' tent and ignore him. Ulysses then lectures Achilles: reputation exists only in other people's eyes, Time forgets past deeds, and the Greeks now admire Ajax. He reveals that Achilles' secret love for Polyxena is known. Patroclus urges Achilles to fight. Achilles, troubled, sends Thersites to invite Hector to his tent; Thersites mimics the swollen-headed Ajax.","key":["The exchange of Cressida is arranged in a few lines of business, before the lovers have spent one night together.","Ulysses' Time speech: 'One touch of nature makes the whole world kin'.","Achilles admits 'My mind is troubled, like a fountain stirr'd'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Calchas ask for?","o":["His house and his property, which he left behind in Troy","His daughter in exchange for Antenor","A share of the spoils once Troy has fallen to the Greeks","A place on the council"],"a":1},{"q":"How do the generals treat Achilles?","o":["They beg him to fight","They pass by and ignore him","They arrest him","They praise him"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Ulysses say Achilles loves?","o":["Helen","Cressida","Polyxena","Cassandra"],"a":2}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Before dawn in Troy, Aeneas meets Paris and Diomedes, who has brought Antenor. Aeneas and Diomedes exchange courteous promises to kill each other in battle. Paris sends Aeneas ahead to warn Troilus that Cressida must be handed over. Paris asks Diomedes who deserves Helen more, him or Menelaus; Diomedes answers that both deserve a whore and that a life has been lost for every drop of her blood.","key":["Aeneas and Diomedes show chivalry as elaborate politeness wrapped round hatred.","Diomedes' 'For every false drop in her bawdy veins / A Grecian's life hath sunk' is the bluntest verdict on Helen.","Paris knows the news will hurt Troilus but says 'There is no help'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom has Diomedes brought to Troy?","o":["Calchas","Antenor","Ajax","Helen"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Diomedes say about Helen?","o":["She is the fairest woman alive in Greece or Troy","Both her husbands deserve a whore","She should be returned","She loves Paris"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Paris believe Troilus is staying?","o":["At Priam's palace, with Hector","At Calchas's house","At Hector's house","With Aeneas"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"At dawn after their night together, Troilus urges Cressida back to bed; she worries he is tired of her. Pandarus teases them crudely. Aeneas knocks and, despite Pandarus's denials, asks for Troilus, who learns that Cressida must be handed to Diomedes within the hour. Pandarus tells Cressida, who swears she will not leave Troy and will tear her hair and cry Troilus's name.","key":["Troilus's 'How my achievements mock me!' sums up the speed of the reversal.","Cressida's 'You men will never tarry' shows her fear of being left after being won.","She swears 'Make Cressid's name the very crown of falsehood, / If ever she leave Troilus'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Cressida fear when Troilus wants to leave?","o":["That he is tired of her","That Pandarus will tell","That her father will return","That Hector will be angry"],"a":0},{"q":"Who brings the news of the exchange?","o":["Paris","Aeneas","Pandarus","Diomedes"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Cressida say she will do?","o":["Go willingly","Refuse to leave Troy","Run away with Troilus","Ask Hector for help"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"In the street outside Pandarus's house, Paris reminds Troilus that the hour for handing Cressida over has come. Troilus goes to fetch her, calling himself a priest offering his own heart on an altar.","key":["Troilus's image of an altar shows him making a sacrifice of his love.","Paris pities him but does nothing to stop the exchange.","The scene is very short: the public deal moves faster than private feeling."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Troilus compare Diomedes' hand to?","o":["A cage with an open door","An altar","A grave","A ship"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Paris say he knows?","o":["What it is to love","That Cressida is false","That Troy will fall","That Diomedes is cruel"],"a":0},{"q":"Who goes in to bring Cressida?","o":["Paris","Aeneas","Troilus","Pandarus"],"a":2}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Cressida grieves; Pandarus quotes a ballad. Troilus says the gods are jealous of their love. He keeps asking her to 'be true', which offends her; he admits he fears the accomplished Greeks. They exchange tokens, his sleeve for her glove, and he promises to bribe the Greek sentries to visit her. Diomedes arrives; Troilus orders him to treat her well, and Diomedes insolently replies that he will treat her as she deserves, not as he is told.","key":["Troilus's repeated 'Be thou true' betrays his own fear of the Greek youths' 'subtle games'.","The sleeve and glove become the play's central tokens of faith.","Diomedes' 'I'll nothing do on charge' sets him up as Troilus's rival at once."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Troilus give Cressida?","o":["A ring","A sleeve","A letter","A glove"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Troilus keep saying 'be true'?","o":["He has heard gossip","He fears the charms of the Greek youths","Pandarus asked him to","He is leaving Troy"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Diomedes answer Troilus's orders?","o":["He promises to obey him in every way and guard her with his life in the Greek camp","He says he will treat her as she deserves, not on command","He refuses to take her","He challenges Troilus to a duel"],"a":1}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"In the Greek camp, Ajax waits armed for Hector. Diomedes brings in Cressida; at Ulysses' suggestion the generals take turns kissing her as she trades jokes with them, but Ulysses himself refuses and condemns her as one of the 'daughters of the game'. Hector arrives, fights Ajax briefly, and stops because they are cousins. The Greeks welcome Hector; Nestor and Ulysses praise him, Achilles studies his body and boasts where he will kill him, and they agree to fight tomorrow. Troilus asks Ulysses to take him to Calchas's tent.","key":["Cressida is passed round the generals with a kiss each: her new world treats her as a prize.","Ulysses' 'There's language in her eye, her cheek, her lip' judges her instantly.","Hector's mercy to Ajax and Achilles' 'in which part of his body / Shall I destroy him?' set up the ending."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Ulysses call Cressida?","o":["A goddess come down to earth","A daughter of the game","A true lover","A prisoner"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Hector stop fighting Ajax?","o":["He is wounded","Ajax is his cousin","Agamemnon orders it","Troilus begs him"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Achilles do when he meets Hector?","o":["Embraces him like a brother in front of both armies and the generals","Looks him over and names where he will kill him","Refuses to speak a single word to him at the feast","Challenges him at once to fight"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Achilles plans to feast Hector tonight and kill him tomorrow. Thersites brings a letter and trades insults with Patroclus. The letter, from Queen Hecuba, with a token from Polyxena, reminds Achilles of his oath, and he decides not to fight tomorrow after all. Thersites mocks Agamemnon and Menelaus. The Greek leaders bring Hector to Achilles' tent. Diomedes leaves early, and Ulysses and Troilus follow him; Thersites follows them all.","key":["Achilles drops his vow to fight Hector for a private vow to Polyxena: 'My major vow lies here'.","Thersites' catalogue of diseases shows the camp as a body rotting.","Ulysses' aside 'Follow his torch' leads Troilus to the truth."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who sends Achilles a letter?","o":["Hector","Queen Hecuba","Helen","Cressida"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Achilles decide after reading it?","o":["To fight tomorrow","Not to fight tomorrow","To leave Greece","To kill Patroclus"],"a":1},{"q":"Where is Diomedes going?","o":["To Troy","To Calchas's tent","To Agamemnon","To the ships"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Diomedes calls for Cressida at her father's tent. Hidden, Troilus and Ulysses watch, and Thersites watches them. Cressida whispers and teases, half refuses, and finally gives Diomedes the sleeve Troilus gave her, takes it back, and then lets him keep it and promises to see him. Alone, she admits her eye has turned her mind. Troilus, held back by Ulysses, cannot believe what he has seen ('This is, and is not, Cressid') and swears to kill Diomedes. Aeneas fetches him home.","key":["The scene is watched in three layers: Cressida and Diomedes, Troilus and Ulysses, and Thersites mocking all of them.","Cressida's 'The error of our eye directs our mind' is her own verdict on her change.","Troilus's 'This is, and is not, Cressid' shows his reason split by what he has seen."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Cressida give Diomedes?","o":["A ring that her mother gave her","Troilus's sleeve","A glove of white silk","A letter for her father"],"a":1},{"q":"Who watches Troilus and Ulysses?","o":["Pandarus","Calchas","Thersites","Achilles"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Troilus swear to do?","o":["Forgive Cressida","Kill Diomedes","Leave Troy","Become a priest"],"a":1}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"Andromache and Cassandra beg Hector not to fight: they have had dreams and visions of slaughter. Hector insists honour demands he keep his word. Troilus mocks Hector's habit of sparing fallen enemies. Priam begs Hector too, but he goes. Cassandra foresees his death. Pandarus brings Troilus a letter from Cressida; he tears it up: 'Words, words, mere words'.","key":["Hector's 'Life every man holds dear; but the dear man / Holds honour far more precious dear than life' is his creed.","Troilus has turned savage: 'Let's leave the hermit Pity with our mother'.","Cassandra's vision of Hector's wounds is ignored as 'superstitious'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Andromache want Hector to stay?","o":["She is ill","She has dreamt of slaughter","Priam has ordered it","Hector is wounded"],"a":1},{"q":"What fault does Troilus find in Hector?","o":["Cowardice in the field","Too much mercy","Pride in his armour","Laziness"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Troilus do with Cressida's letter?","o":["Keeps it next to his heart","Tears it up","Gives it to Hector","Reads it aloud"],"a":1}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"Thersites watches the battle, scorning Diomedes, Troilus and the failure of Ulysses' and Nestor's policy, since Ajax has now grown too proud to fight. Troilus chases Diomedes. Hector finds Thersites, who calls himself a rascal, and Hector spares him.","key":["Thersites concludes that 'policy grows into an ill opinion': Ulysses' plan has backfired.","His 'the sleeve, now the sleeve!' turns the lovers' token into a joke.","Hector spares Thersites because he is no worthy match, a sign of his chivalry."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why has Ulysses' policy failed, according to Thersites?","o":["Ajax is now prouder than Achilles and will not fight","Hector killed Ajax in the night and the Greeks are lost without him in the field","Achilles left the camp for home with all his Myrmidons in the night","Nestor died"],"a":0},{"q":"What is Diomedes wearing?","o":["Hector's armour","Troilus's sleeve on his helmet","Cressida's glove","Achilles' shield"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Hector spare Thersites?","o":["Thersites says he is a worthless rascal","Thersites surrenders his sword to him at once","Ulysses asks him to spare the wretch","Thersites is his cousin"],"a":0}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"Diomedes sends Troilus's captured horse to Cressida. Agamemnon reports Greek losses; Nestor orders Patroclus's body carried to Achilles and describes Hector as a thousand Hectors everywhere, and Ulysses brings news that Patroclus has been killed and that Achilles is arming in grief and rage. Ajax hunts Troilus. Achilles enters calling for Hector.","key":["Diomedes sends a trophy to Cressida: love has become a contest of possessions.","Nestor's 'There is a thousand Hectors in the field' shows Hector at his height.","Patroclus's death does what all of Ulysses' policy could not: it brings Achilles to fight."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Diomedes send Cressida?","o":["A letter of love and apology","Troilus's horse","Hector's sword, taken in battle","A sleeve"],"a":1},{"q":"What brings Achilles back to the battle?","o":["Ulysses' speech","Patroclus's death","Agamemnon's order","Cressida's request"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is Ajax hunting?","o":["Hector","Troilus","Paris","Aeneas"],"a":1}]},"a5s6":{"sum":"Ajax and Diomedes both want to fight Troilus, who takes them both on. Hector meets Achilles, who is out of practice, and courteously lets him rest; Achilles scorns the courtesy and leaves, promising to return. Troilus goes to rescue Aeneas. Hector chases a Greek in splendid armour, wanting it for himself.","key":["Hector's 'Pause, if thou wilt' is his last act of chivalry, and it saves his killer.","Troilus fights 'Come, both, you cogging Greeks', reckless with despair.","Hector's greed for the fine armour leads him away from his fellows."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Hector do when he meets the tired Achilles?","o":["Kills him","Lets him rest","Takes him prisoner","Runs away"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom has Ajax captured?","o":["Paris","Aeneas","Troilus","Deiphobus"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Hector chase the Greek in armour?","o":["He wants the armour","The Greek insulted him","He is Achilles","He killed Patroclus"],"a":0}]},"a5s7":{"sum":"Achilles orders his Myrmidons to surround Hector when they find him and kill him without striking until then. Thersites watches Paris and Menelaus fight, cheering on the cuckold and the cuckold-maker. Priam's bastard son Margarelon challenges Thersites, who talks his way out: one bastard should not fight another.","key":["Achilles plans an ambush, not a duel: 'Empale him with your weapons round about'.","Thersites turns the war's original quarrel, Paris and Menelaus, into a bull-baiting.","Thersites survives by refusing honour altogether."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Achilles order his Myrmidons to do?","o":["Guard the ships from the Trojan attack","Surround and kill Hector","Find Troilus and bring him alive","Rescue Patroclus"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Thersites watch fighting?","o":["Hector and Ajax","Paris and Menelaus","Troilus and Diomedes","Achilles and Hector"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Thersites escape Margarelon?","o":["He runs into a tent","He says one bastard should not fight another","He bribes him","Ajax rescues him"],"a":1}]},"a5s8":{"sum":"Hector has killed the Greek in fine armour and found him rotten inside. As the sun sets he takes off his armour to rest. Achilles arrives with his Myrmidons; Hector protests that he is unarmed, but Achilles orders his men to strike, and they kill him. Achilles orders his men to proclaim that Achilles killed Hector, and to tie the body to his horse's tail.","key":["Hector's 'Most putrified core so fair without' describes the Greek he killed, and also the war itself.","Achilles' 'Strike, fellows, strike' destroys the play's last pretence of chivalry.","Achilles claims the kill: 'Achilles hath the mighty Hector slain'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Hector find inside the fine armour?","o":["Gold and jewels","A rotten body","Patroclus alive","Nothing at all inside it"],"a":1},{"q":"Who kills Hector?","o":["Achilles alone, in a fair fight","Achilles' Myrmidons on his order","Ajax","Diomedes"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Achilles do with Hector's body?","o":["Returns it to Priam at the gates of Troy","Ties it to his horse's tail","Buries it with honour on the field","Gives it to Agamemnon as a prize"],"a":1}]},"a5s9":{"sum":"The Greek leaders hear the shout that Hector has been killed by Achilles. Ajax says Hector was as good a man as Achilles and the death should not be boasted of. Agamemnon hopes the war is now won.","key":["Ajax's tribute ('Great Hector was as good a man as he') is a rare honest note.","Agamemnon thinks of the victory: 'Great Troy is ours'.","The truth of the killing is hidden behind the shout of 'Achilles!'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Ajax say about Hector?","o":["He deserved to die for what Troy has done to Greece","He was as good a man as Achilles","He was a coward at heart","He should be dragged"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Agamemnon hope?","o":["That Hector is alive","That the war is ended","That Achilles will leave","That Troilus will surrender"],"a":1},{"q":"How do the Greeks learn of Hector's death?","o":["A letter brought from Achilles' tent","A shout from the soldiers","Achilles comes to tell them","Thersites tells them"],"a":1}]},"a5s10":{"sum":"Troilus brings the Trojans the news that Hector is dead and dragged through the field. He vows to haunt Achilles like a guilty conscience and leads the army back to Troy with hope of revenge. When Pandarus tries to speak to him, Troilus curses him with lasting shame. Left alone, Pandarus complains of his aching bones, speaks to the bawds and 'traders in the flesh' in the audience, and promises to leave them his diseases.","key":["Troilus's 'Hector is dead; there is no more to say' ends the war plot without resolution.","His dismissal of Pandarus ('Hence, broker-lackey') ends the love plot with a curse.","Pandarus's epilogue addresses the audience as fellow bawds and leaves them his diseases."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Troilus vow?","o":["To make peace with the Greeks now that Hector is dead","To haunt Achilles with revenge","To forgive Cressida and win her back","To leave Troy"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Troilus treat Pandarus?","o":["Thanks him","Curses him","Asks him to fetch Cressida","Ignores him silently"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Pandarus leave the audience?","o":["His fortune","His diseases","His songs","His blessing"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"2":"The Prologue comes on 'arm'd', a joke on the war and on the rival theatre's armed prologues. 'Beginning in the middle' (in medias res) is how classical epics start, but this play never reaches Troy's fall.","4":"A 'varlet' is a squire or servant who helps a knight arm. Troilus begins the play by taking his armour off: love has disarmed him.","7":"Pandarus's cake: grinding, bolting (sifting), leavening, kneading, baking and cooling. Each stage is a joke on the long wait before Troilus can 'eat'.","18":"Troilus's praise of Cressida's hand ('all whites are ink') is in the Petrarchan style of love poetry, where the lady is described part by part.","33":"Daphne fled from Apollo and was turned into a laurel. Troilus pictures Cressida as a pearl in India and himself as a merchant, an image of love as trade that the play will return to.","40":"Menelaus' horn: a cuckold, a man whose wife is unfaithful, was said to grow horns. Every joke about Menelaus in the play uses this image.","54":"Alexander calls Ajax Hector's nephew; in 4.5 Hector calls him his father's sister's son, a cousin. In legend Ajax's mother Hesione was Priam's sister.","58":"Briareus was a giant with a hundred hands; Argus a monster with a hundred eyes. Alexander's portrait of Ajax is a comic list of contradictions.","109":"A 'merry Greek' meant a loose, pleasure-loving person.","140":"Priam had fifty sons; Troilus's joke makes Paris the 'forked' hair because a cuckold's horns are forked: Helen's husband Menelaus is the cuckold, but Paris will be one too.","200":"Cressida's soliloquy is in rhyming couplets, like proverbs. 'Achievement is command; ungain'd, beseech': once a man has won a woman he gives orders; before that he pleads.","204":"Agamemnon's first word, 'Princes', opens a long council scene. His argument that failure is Jove's test of 'persistive constancy' sounds wise but explains nothing.","205":"Boreas is the north wind, Thetis a sea goddess (here, the sea), Perseus' horse is Pegasus. Nestor expands Agamemnon's point with a long sea image.","208":"The 'degree' speech is the most famous speech in the play. It describes a universe ordered by rank, from the planets to the family, and warns that without it 'appetite' will devour everything. The rest of the play shows exactly that happening.","211":"Patroclus's 'pageants' are imitations of the generals, like a bad actor stamping on a wooden stage ('scaffoldage'). Typhon was a giant whose roar was a byword for noise.","214":"Thetis was Achilles' mother. Nestor's joke: if brains count for nothing, Achilles' horse is worth many Achilleses.","238":"Hector's challenge is in the language of chivalric romance: a knight defending his lady's beauty. It is a medieval idea set in the ancient world.","249":"Ulysses' plan: blunt wedges split hard knots, so use the blunt Ajax to split Achilles' pride.","257":"The 'great Myrmidon' is Achilles, leader of the Myrmidons. Iris is the rainbow goddess; Achilles' crest curves more proudly than a rainbow.","263":"Thersites plays on 'run' and 'matter': a running boil would discharge pus (matter), and the general would then have some 'matter' (sense) in him.","280":"Cerberus, the three-headed dog of hell, barking at Proserpina, queen of the underworld.","303":"A pun: Ajax was pronounced like 'a jakes', a privy.","347":"Nestor's offer: return Helen and all the war's losses will be written off. This is the only peace offer in the play.","348":"Hector's 'tithe' and 'dismes' are tenths: every tenth Trojan soul lost has been as valuable as Helen.","353":"Troilus's question 'What's aught but as 'tis valued?' is the centre of the debate: is a thing valuable in itself, or only because someone desires it?","355":"'Whose price hath launch'd above a thousand ships' echoes Marlowe's Doctor Faustus on Helen: 'the face that launched a thousand ships'. Troilus uses the marriage analogy just before he loses Cressida.","364":"Cassandra was cursed to prophesy truly and never be believed. Paris is a 'firebrand' because his mother Hecuba dreamed she gave birth to a burning torch.","371":"Aristotle wrote that young men are unfit to study moral philosophy because they follow their passions. Shakespeare's Hector quotes a philosopher who lived centuries after Troy, a deliberate or careless anachronism.","377":"Thersites' 'Neapolitan bone-ache' is syphilis; a 'placket' is a slit in a petticoat, and so a woman's sex.","393":"Thersites 'declines' the question like a Latin noun, going through all the cases.","405":"The 'dry serpigo' is a creeping skin disease. Thersites' curses are almost always diseases.","426":"Elephants were thought to have no knee joints, so could not kneel: Achilles will not bend.","450":"Cancer is the summer sign of the zodiac; Hyperion is the sun. Ulysses says sending Ajax would add fuel to a fire already burning.","482":"Milo of Croton was famous for carrying a bull. Ulysses' 'praise' of Ajax is a mock-heroic blazon.","514":"The servant puns on 'depend' (rely on) and on 'the Lord' (God), and on 'grace' (God's grace, and a title).","518":"'Seethes' means boils. 'Stewed' is a joke: 'stews' were brothels.","563":"The song mixes 'O ho!' (cries of dying) with 'ha ha!' (laughter): 'die' was slang for sexual climax.","588":"The Styx was the river of the underworld; Charon the ferryman who carried souls across. Troilus imagines Cressida's bed as the Elysian fields, heaven for the 'deserver'.","597":"Pandarus uses hawking terms: a hawk was 'watched' (kept awake) to tame it; 'the falcon as the tercel' means the female as eager as the male.","599":"'In witness whereof the parties interchangeably' is legal language from the end of a contract: Pandarus treats the kiss as a deal signed.","637":"The comparisons ('As true as steel ... as iron to adamant') are lovers' clichés. Troilus imagines future poets citing him as the model of truth.","638":"Cressida's oath is a curse on herself. The audience, who know the legend from Chaucer and Henryson, already know she will be 'false'.","639":"Pandarus predicts that his name will mean go-between ('pander'), and it does: the word comes from Chaucer's Pandarus.","648":"Calchas foresaw Troy's fall and defected to the Greeks, leaving his daughter in Troy.","679":"The 'strange fellow' writes that a man knows his own qualities only when they are reflected back by others. Ulysses uses the idea to tell Achilles that his reputation depends on public opinion.","683":"Ulysses' speech on Time is a famous meditation on fame. 'One touch of nature makes the whole world kin' originally meant that all people share one weakness: they love novelty.","687":"Polyxena was Priam's daughter. In legend Achilles was killed while trying to marry her. Pyrrhus is Achilles' son.","703":"Thersites mimics Ajax's swollen pride. 'A land fish' is a monster, something out of its element.","752":"Diomedes' verdict on Helen's two men: Menelaus would drink the dregs of a used barrel; Paris would breed heirs from a whore.","801":"Aeneas promises to keep the secret as well as Pandarus would.","833":"Pandarus quotes an old ballad, 'O heart, heavy heart'.","842":"Troilus personifies 'injurious Time' as a robber cramming his loot into a bag. Time keeps turning up as the enemy of love and fame.","854":"A sleeve was a detachable part of a garment and a common love token, worn by knights on their helmets.","860":"The 'lavolt' (lavolta) was a lively dance with leaps. Troilus fears the Greeks' social polish more than their swords.","897":"'Kissed in general' puns on 'the general' (Agamemnon) and 'by everyone'.","913":"Cressida's wordplay: odd and even. Menelaus is 'odd' (single, wifeless), and Paris is 'even' with him (has paid him back).","926":"'Daughters of the game' are prostitutes. Ulysses judges Cressida on one meeting.","955":"Ajax's mother Hesione was Priam's sister, so Ajax and Hector are cousins.","957":"Neoptolemus (Pyrrhus) is Achilles' son. 'Oyes' is the herald's cry 'Oyez!'.","971":"Hector teases Menelaus about Helen, who 'swears by Venus' glove'. Menelaus calls her 'a deadly theme'.","1019":"'Tent' is also a roll of lint used to probe a wound. Thersites puns on Patroclus's question.","1023":"The 'rotten diseases of the south' were believed to come from Naples and Italy. Thersites lists every disease he can think of.","1032":"Jupiter turned into a bull to carry off Europa; Menelaus is a 'bull' because of his horns.","1075":"Musical puns: 'sing' her (seduce her), 'cliff' (clef, and also sexual), 'noted' (marked in music, and notorious).","1146":"Diana, goddess of chastity and the moon; her 'waiting women' are the stars. Cressida swears by chastity while breaking faith.","1159":"Cressida's last speech is in couplets, like her soliloquy in 1.2, and she judges herself harshly.","1175":"Troilus's 'bi-fold authority' is reason divided against itself. Ariachne is Arachne, the weaver turned into a spider. Orts are scraps of food.","1183":"'Wear a castle on thy head' means wear a strong helmet.","1253":"Thersites calls the expedition a 'sleeveless errand', a pointless one, and a pun on the sleeve.","1275":"The 'Sagittary' is a centaur archer fighting for Troy. Nestor's 'thousand Hectors' describes Hector at his most glorious.","1334":"Hector's line about the armoured Greek, 'Most putrified core so fair without', can stand as a judgement on the whole play.","1345":"A 'stickler' was an umpire who separated fighters.","1371":"'Painted cloths' were cheap wall hangings with mottoes. A 'galled goose of Winchester' is a prostitute with syphilis: the Southwark brothels were licensed by the Bishop of Winchester."},"gloss":{"varlet":"servant, squire","gear":"business, matter","bolting":"sifting flour","leavening":"letting the dough rise","blench":"flinch","suff'rance":"suffering","rive":"split","cygnet":"young swan","mends":"remedy","blackamoor":"black African","tetchy":"touchy, irritable","bark":"ship","afield":"out on the battlefield","husbandry":"good housekeeping","harness'd":"armoured","per se":"unique, by himself","additions":"titles, distinguishing qualities","churlish":"surly, rude","humours":"moods, temperaments","purblind":"half-blind","cop'd":"met, fought with","favour":"face, appearance","tapster":"barman","addle":"rotten","marvell's":"marvellous","nettle":"stinging weed","ward":"guard (fencing)","bawd":"pimp, go-between","jaundies":"jaundice, yellow sickness","tortive":"twisted","errant":"straying","protractive":"drawn-out","winnows":"separates grain from chaff","Boreas":"the north wind","Thetis":"a sea goddess; the sea","toast":"bread soaked in wine","breeze":"gadfly","degree":"rank, order","vizarded":"masked","insisture":"steady course","med'cinable":"healing","deracinate":"uproot","primogenity":"right of the first-born","oppugnancy":"conflict","imbecility":"weakness","emulation":"rivalry, envy","fusty":"stale, mouldy","gorget":"armour for the throat","spleen":"seat of laughter and anger","mapp'ry":"map-making","debonair":"gracious","galls":"spirit, bitterness","resty":"restless","vambrace":"armour for the forearm","beaver":"face guard of a helmet","perspicuous":"clear","scantling":"sample","imputation":"reputation","physic":"cure, purge","tarre":"provoke, urge on","murrain":"plague","porpentine":"porcupine","cobloaf":"lumpy loaf","asinico":"little ass","pia mater":"brain","modicums":"small amounts","clotpoles":"blockheads","brach":"bitch hound","tithe":"a tenth","dismes":"tenths","counters":"tokens used in counting","propension":"inclination","gloz'd":"commented","propend":"incline","roisting":"rousing, swaggering","caduceus":"Mercury's snake-twined staff","placket":"slit in a petticoat","lazars":"lepers","patchery":"botched work, trickery","serpigo":"skin disease","appertainings":"rights of rank","inveigled":"lured","underwrite":"submit to","pash":"smash","pheeze":"fix, settle","bourn":"boundary","antiquary":"ancient","cull":"pick out","fills":"shafts of a cart","abruption":"breaking off","reversion":"future possession","capocchia":"simpleton","unrespective sieve":"slop bucket","plantage":"plants","adamant":"magnet; hardest stone","pard":"leopard","sequest'ring":"cutting off","wrest":"key used to tune a harp","unplausive":"disapproving","derision":"scorn","parted":"gifted","reverb'rate":"echo","abject":"despised","lubber":"clumsy lout","mail":"armour","forthright":"straight path","gauds":"trinkets","Plutus":"god of wealth","Pyrrhus":"Achilles' son","ague":"fever","jerkin":"jacket","catlings":"lute strings made of gut","Anchises":"Aeneas' father","despiteful":"spiteful","chapmen":"traders","puling":"whining","lees":"dregs","scruple":"tiny weight","wights":"people","fee-farm":"lease for ever","consanguinity":"blood relationship","violenteth":"rages","temporize":"compromise","embrasures":"embraces","rejoindure":"meeting again","maculation":"stain","lavolt":"leaping dance","casque":"helmet","Oyes":"a herald's cry of 'hear ye'","Neoptolemus":"Achilles' son","quondam":"former","untraded":"unusual","chronicle":"history","pelting":"petty, paltry","stithied":"forged","core":"heart; centre of a boil","sarcenet":"fine silk","skein":"bundle of thread","imposthume":"abscess","tetter":"skin disease","fitchook":"polecat","puttock":"kite (bird of prey)","palter":"play tricks","cliff":"clef (music)","concupy":"lust","orts":"scraps of food","recordation":"record, memorial","esperance":"hope","calumniate":"slander","sanctimonies":"sacred things","Ariachne":"Arachne, the weaver turned spider","deceptious":"deceptive","tisick":"cough","rheum":"watering of the eyes","drab":"prostitute","colossus":"giant statue","Sagittary":"centaur archer","sculls":"shoals of fish","swath":"row cut by a mower","boy-queller":"boy-killer","cogging":"cheating","frush":"smash","empale":"surround","fell":"cruel","stickler":"umpire","bruit":"rumour","pight":"pitched","Niobes":"weeping mothers (Niobe wept for her children)","broker-lackey":"go-between servant","painted cloths":"cheap painted wall hangings","galled":"sore, diseased","orgulous":"proud","fraughtage":"cargo","immures":"walls","crownets":"coronets","corresponsive":"matching","firstlings":"first events","vaunt":"beginning","broils":"wars","skittish":"nervous, excitable","Tenedos":"island near Troy","Phrygia":"region of Troy","Ilium":"Priam's palace in Troy","attaint":"stain, fault","chaf'd":"angered, inflamed","wallet":"bag","Briareus":"giant with a hundred hands","Argus":"monster with a hundred eyes","Typhon":"roaring giant","Myrmidons":"Achilles' soldiers","Hyperion":"the sun god","Aquilon":"the north wind","Galathe":"Hector's horse","Daphne":"nymph loved by Apollo","Charon":"ferryman of the dead","Stygian":"of the river Styx","quails":"loose women","Pluto":"god of the underworld","Cerberus":"three-headed dog of hell","Proserpina":"queen of the underworld","Ilion":"Troy"}};
const PARTS={"2":[[0,9,"Our scene is Troy. Proud princes from the islands of Greece, their noble blood inflamed, have sent their ships to the port of Athens, loaded with the men and weapons of cruel war. Sixty-nine kings wearing royal crowns set sail from the bay of Athens towards Phrygia, sworn to sack Troy, where, behind strong walls, Helen, Menelaus' stolen queen, sleeps with the lustful Paris. That is the quarrel."],[10,18,"They land at Tenedos, and the heavy ships unload their warlike cargo there. Now the fresh Greeks, not yet bruised by fighting, pitch their splendid tents on the plains of Troy, while Priam's city, with its six gates (Dardan, Tymbria, Ilias, Chetas, Troien and Antenorides), barred with massive staples and close-fitting bolts, shuts in the sons of Troy."],[19,30,"Now anticipation, teasing the nervous spirits of both sides, Trojan and Greek, puts everything at risk. And I have come here as a prologue in armour, not because I am confident in the author's pen or the actors' voices, but dressed to suit our subject, to tell you, fair audience, that our play skips the boasts and opening skirmishes of this war and begins in the middle, going on from there to whatever can be fitted into a play. Like it or find fault; do as you please. Good or bad, it is only the luck of war."]],"18":[[0,6,"Oh Pandarus! I tell you, Pandarus, when I tell you that my hopes are drowned there, don't answer by telling me how many fathoms deep they lie. I tell you I am mad with love for Cressida. You answer, \"She's beautiful,\" and pour into the open wound of my heart her eyes, her hair, her cheek, her walk, her voice."],[7,15,"You go on and on about them. Oh, her hand, next to which every white thing looks like ink writing its own disgrace, and compared to whose soft touch a young swan's down is rough and the most delicate sense of touch is as hard as a ploughman's palm! You tell me this, and it is true, when I say I love her. But by saying it, instead of oil and soothing ointment, you lay in every wound that love has given me the knife that made it."]],"33":[[0,8,"Quiet, you ugly noises! Quiet, you rough sounds! Fools on both sides! Helen must be beautiful when you paint her red with your blood every day. I can't fight for this cause; it's too thin a subject for my sword. But Pandarus! Oh gods, how you torment me! I can't get to Cressida except through Pandarus, and he is as touchy about being persuaded to do the wooing as she is stubbornly chaste against every suitor."],[9,15,"Tell me, Apollo, for the love of your Daphne, what Cressida is, what Pandarus is, and what we are. Her bed is India; she lies there like a pearl. Let the space between our palace in Troy and where she lives be called the wild and stormy sea. I am the merchant, and this drifting Pandarus is my uncertain hope, my escort and my ship."]],"52":[[0,9,"Up to the eastern tower, which is high enough to look down over the whole valley, to watch the battle. Hector, whose patience is as steady as a virtue, was angry today. He scolded Andromache and hit his armourer, and, as if war were a matter of good housekeeping, he put on his light armour before sunrise and went out to the battlefield, where every flower wept with dew, like a prophet foreseeing what Hector's anger would do."]],"200":[[0,6,"By that same token, you are a pimp. He offers words, vows, gifts, tears, and the whole sacrifice of love on another man's behalf. But I see a thousand times more in Troilus than the mirror of Pandarus' praise can show. Still I hold back. Women are angels while they are being wooed. Once a thing is won, it is finished; the soul of joy is in the chase."],[7,14,"A woman who is loved knows nothing if she doesn't know this: men value what they haven't won more than it is worth. No woman has ever known love to be as sweet once it is gained as when desire was still pleading. So love teaches me this rule: \"Once won, men command; while unwon, they beg.\" So although my heart is full of steady love, none of it will show in my eyes."]],"204":[[0,8,"Princes, what trouble has spread this yellow sickness over your faces? The great promises that hope makes at the start of every plan on earth fall short of what was promised. Setbacks and disasters grow inside the highest undertakings, just as knots, caused by the sap meeting, infect the healthy pine and twist its grain off its straight course of growth."],[9,16,"And it is nothing new to us, princes, that we have fallen so far short of our expectations that after seven years of siege the walls of Troy still stand. Every action in the past that we have records of went crooked and wrong when it was put to the test, and did not match the aim or the imagined shape that the thought first gave it."],[17,24,"So why, princes, do you look at our efforts with embarrassed faces and call them shameful? They are really nothing but the drawn-out tests of great Jove, to find out which men have lasting endurance. The quality of that metal is not found when fortune is kind, because then the brave and the cowardly, the wise and the foolish, the learned and the ignorant, the hard and the soft, all seem alike and related."],[25,29,"But in the wind and storm of fortune's frown, the difference between them, like a broad and powerful fan, blows on everything and winnows the light chaff away, and what has real weight and substance lies there rich in virtue and pure."]],"205":[[0,6,"With due respect to your godlike throne, great Agamemnon, Nestor will apply your last words. The true test of men lies in how they stand up to bad luck. When the sea is calm, how many flimsy little boats dare to sail on her patient surface, keeping pace with much larger ships!"],[7,13,"But let the rough north wind once stir up the gentle sea, and soon you see the strong-ribbed ship cutting through mountains of water, leaping between the sea and the sky like Perseus' winged horse. Where is the cheeky little boat then, whose weak, flimsy sides a moment ago rivalled greatness? Either it has fled to harbour or it has been swallowed by Neptune like toast in wine."],[14,23,"In the same way, the appearance of courage and real courage separate in the storms of fortune. In fortune's bright sunshine the herd is more troubled by the gadfly than by the tiger, but when the splitting wind bends the knees of knotted oaks, and the flies have run for shelter, then the courageous creature, roused to anger, matches the storm's rage and answers back to scolding fortune in the same key."]],"206":[[0,4,"Agamemnon, great commander, nerve and bone of Greece, heart of our army, soul and single spirit in whom the temperaments and minds of all of us should be contained: listen to what Ulysses says."],[5,15,"Besides the applause and approval that I give to both your speeches (to you, most mighty, for your rank and power, and to you, most reverend, for your long life), speeches which Agamemnon and the hand of Greece should engrave in brass, and which venerable Nestor, silver-haired, should use to bind all Greek ears to his experienced tongue with a bond of breath as strong as the axle on which heaven turns: still, may it please you both, great and wise, to hear Ulysses speak."]],"208":[[0,9,"Troy would have fallen by now, still on its foundations, and great Hector's sword would have lost its master, if not for these reasons. The proper order of command has been neglected. Look how many Greek tents stand empty on this plain: there are just as many empty factions. When the general is not like the hive that all the foraging bees return to, what honey can be expected? When rank is disguised, the most unworthy man looks as good as anyone behind the mask."],[10,18,"The heavens themselves, the planets, and the earth at the centre keep to rank, priority and place, regularity, course, proportion, season, form, function and custom, all in order. That is why the glorious sun is enthroned in noble majesty among the other planets, and its healing eye corrects the influence of evil planets and moves without hindrance, like a king's command, to good and bad alike."],[19,25,"But when the planets wander into disorder in a bad combination, what plagues and omens, what rebellion, what raging of the sea, earthquakes, storms of wind! Terrors, upheavals and horrors twist and crack, tear and uproot the unity and wedded calm of states right off their foundations!"],[26,33,"Oh, when rank is shaken, which is the ladder to all high undertakings, the whole enterprise falls sick! How could communities, ranks in universities, guilds in cities, peaceful trade between distant shores, the rights of the eldest son, the privileges of age, crowns, sceptres and laurels keep their rightful place except through rank?"],[34,43,"Just take away rank, put that string out of tune, and listen to the discord that follows! Everything would clash in pure conflict. The seas would rise higher than the shores and turn the whole solid globe into a soggy mess. The strong would rule the weak, and the brutal son would strike his father dead. Might would be right; or rather, right and wrong, between whose endless quarrel justice lives, would lose their names, and so would justice."],[44,51,"Then everything would be reduced to power, power to will, and will to appetite. And appetite, a universal wolf, backed up by both will and power, would have to make the whole world its prey, and finally eat itself. Great Agamemnon, this chaos follows the choking of rank."],[52,62,"It is this neglect of rank that makes us go backwards when we mean to climb. The general is scorned by the man one step below him, that man by the next, and that one by the man below him. So every step, following the example of the first one that grew sick of its superior, grows into a jealous fever of pale, bloodless rivalry. It is this fever that keeps Troy standing, not her own strength. To finish a long story: Troy stands because of our weakness, not her strength."]],"211":[[0,8,"The great Achilles, whom public opinion crowns as the muscle and front line of our army, has had his ears filled with his windy fame and has become fussy about his own worth. He lies in his tent mocking our plans. Patroclus lies with him on a lazy bed all day long cracking crude jokes, and with ridiculous, clumsy gestures, which the slanderer calls imitation, he acts us out."],[9,18,"Sometimes, great Agamemnon, he puts on your supreme authority, and like a strutting actor whose talent is all in his legs, who thinks it wonderful to hear the wooden echo of his stamping on the stage, he acts your greatness in an over-strained performance that deserves pity. When he speaks, it sounds like a chime being repaired, with crooked words that would seem exaggerated even from the mouth of the roaring giant Typhon."],[19,29,"At this stale stuff, the large Achilles, lolling on his crushed bed, lets out loud applause from his deep chest, crying, \"Excellent! That's Agamemnon exactly! Now play Nestor for me. Clear your throat and stroke your beard, like him getting ready to make a speech.\" That is done, as close to the real thing as two parallel lines are to each other, as alike as Vulcan and his wife Venus. Yet god Achilles still cries, \"Excellent! That's Nestor exactly! Now play him for me, Patroclus, arming himself to answer a night attack.\""],[30,42,"And then, if you please, the weaknesses of old age become the comic scene: coughing and spitting, and shaking the rivets in and out of his neck armour with trembling fingers. At this game Sir Courage dies laughing and cries, \"Oh, enough, Patroclus, or give me ribs of steel! I'll split my sides laughing.\" And in this way all our abilities, gifts, characters, appearances, our individual and shared qualities, our achievements, plans, orders, precautions, calls to battle or speeches for truce, success or loss, what is and what is not, all become material for these two to make jokes about."]],"212":[[0,6,"And many have been infected by copying these two, whom, as Ulysses says, opinion crowns with an emperor's authority. Ajax has become self-willed and holds his head as high and proudly as big Achilles. He keeps to his tent like him, holds rebellious feasts, and criticises our conduct of the war as boldly as an oracle."],[7,11,"And he sets Thersites, a slave whose bile turns out slanders like a mint turns out coins, to compare us to dirt, to weaken and discredit our position, however badly we are surrounded by danger."]],"213":[[0,8,"They criticise our strategy and call it cowardice. They don't count wisdom as part of the war. They reject foresight and value no action except physical fighting. The quiet thinking that plans how many soldiers should strike when the moment is right, and that works out the enemy's strength through careful observation, isn't worth a finger to them. They call it bed-work, map-making, war in a study."],[9,13,"So they rate the battering ram that knocks down the wall, because of the great swing and force of its weight, above the man who made the machine, or above those who guide its use by reason and subtle intelligence."]],"228":[[0,9,"Courtiers as open and gracious, when unarmed, as bowing angels; that is their reputation in peace. But when they want to be soldiers, they have spirit, good weapons, strong limbs, true swords, and, with Jove's help, nothing is so full of courage. But quiet, Aeneas, quiet, Trojan; put your finger to your lips. Praise loses its worth if the man praised praises himself. But what an unwilling enemy praises, that is what fame spreads; that praise alone is pure and goes beyond everything."]],"238":[[0,12,"Great Agamemnon, we have here in Troy a prince called Hector, Priam's son, who has grown restless in this dull, long truce. He told me to bring a trumpeter and say this: \"Kings, princes, lords! If there is one among the best of Greece who values his honour more than his comfort, who seeks praise more than he fears danger, who knows his courage and does not know fear, who loves his lady more than in idle vows spoken to her own lips, and dares to defend her beauty and worth in other arms than hers, to him this challenge."],[13,23,"Hector, in sight of Trojans and Greeks, will prove, or do his best to prove, that he has a lady wiser, more beautiful and more faithful than any Greek ever held in his arms. Tomorrow he will call with his trumpet, halfway between your tents and the walls of Troy, to rouse a Greek who is true in love. If anyone comes, Hector will honour him. If no one comes, he will say in Troy, when he goes back, that the Greek ladies are sunburnt and not worth a splinter of a lance.\" That is all."]],"240":[[0,10,"Tell him about Nestor, a man who was grown up when Hector's grandfather was still a baby. He is old now, but if there isn't a single nobleman in our Greek army with one spark of fire to defend his love, tell him from me that I'll hide my silver beard in a gold visor, put these withered arms into armour, and when I meet him tell him that my lady was more beautiful than his grandmother, and as chaste as anyone in the world. Against his youth in full flood I'll prove this truth with my three drops of blood."]],"254":[[0,9,"Why, that would be most fitting. Who else could you put up against him who could come away from Hector with honour, if not Achilles? Although it is a friendly fight, a lot of reputation depends on this trial. Here the Trojans will taste our dearest reputation with their finest palate, and believe me, Ulysses, our standing will be strangely balanced on this petty action, because its outcome, though only a single fight, will give a sample of good or bad for the whole army."],[10,23,"And in such indexes, though they are small marks compared with the volumes that follow, you can see a baby version of the giant mass of things to come. People will assume that the man who meets Hector is our chosen one, and choice, being the agreed act of all our souls, picks by merit and boils out of us all, as it were, one man distilled from our virtues. If he fails, what encouragement will the winning side take from that to strengthen their opinion of themselves? Once they believe that, their limbs become its tools, just as swords and bows are directed by limbs."]],"257":[[0,6,"Whatever glory our Achilles wins from Hector, we would all share with him if he weren't proud. But he is already too arrogant, and it would be better to be scorched in the African sun than in the pride and bitter scorn of his eyes if he came away from Hector well. If he were beaten, then we would crush our main reputation by disgracing our best man."],[7,13,"No, let's hold a lottery, and rig it so that thick-headed Ajax draws the lot to fight Hector. Among ourselves, let's treat him as the better man, because that will be good medicine for the great leader of the Myrmidons, who basks in loud applause, and make him lower his crest, which curves more proudly than the rainbow."],[14,19,"If the dull, brainless Ajax comes off safely, we'll dress him up in praise. If he fails, we can still keep our reputation that we have better men. But hit or miss, the life of our plan comes down to this: using Ajax will pluck Achilles' feathers."]],"348":[[0,9,"Although no man fears the Greeks less than I do, as far as I personally am concerned, still, revered Priam, there is no lady with a softer heart, more ready to soak up fear, more ready to cry out \"Who knows what will happen next?\" than Hector. The danger of peace is overconfidence, careless overconfidence. But reasonable doubt is called the beacon of the wise, the probe that searches the worst of a wound to the bottom. Let Helen go."],[10,17,"Since the first sword was drawn over this question, every tenth soul among many thousands of tenths has been as precious as Helen; I mean, of our people. If we have lost so many tenths of ours to keep something that is not ours, and that would not be worth one of those tenths even if it were ours, what sense is there in the argument that refuses to give her up?"]],"351":[[0,9,"You are for dreams and sleeping, brother priest. You line your gloves with reason. Here are your reasons: you know an enemy means to harm you; you know a sword in use is dangerous; and reason runs from anything that can harm. So who is surprised when Helenus, seeing a Greek with his sword, fixes the wings of reason to his heels and flies off like Mercury scolded by Jove, or like a star knocked out of its orbit?"],[10,13,"No, if we're going to talk about reason, let's shut our gates and go to sleep. Manhood and honour would have the hearts of hares if they fed their thoughts on this stuffed reason. Reason and caution turn the liver pale and make vigour lose heart."]],"355":[[0,7,"Suppose I take a wife today, and my choice is guided by my desire; my desire set on fire by my eyes and ears, two experienced pilots steering between the dangerous shores of desire and judgement. How could I get rid of the wife I chose, even if my desire came to dislike what it had chosen? There is no way to back out of it and still stand firm in honour."],[8,15,"We don't give silks back to the merchant once we've dirtied them, and we don't throw leftover food into the slop bucket just because we're now full. It was thought right that Paris should take some revenge on the Greeks. Your breath, all in agreement, filled his sails. The seas and winds, old quarrellers, made a truce and helped him on his way."],[16,22,"He reached the ports he wanted, and in exchange for an old aunt whom the Greeks held captive, he brought back a Greek queen whose youth and freshness make Apollo look wrinkled and the morning look stale. Why do we keep her? The Greeks keep our aunt. Is she worth keeping? Why, she is a pearl whose price has launched more than a thousand ships and turned crowned kings into merchants."],[23,31,"If you agree it was wise for Paris to go (as you must, because you all cried \"Go, go\"), if you admit he brought home a worthy prize (as you must, because you all clapped your hands and cried \"Priceless!\"), why do you now criticise the result of your own wisdom, and do something Fortune never did, reduce to nothing the value of what you rated richer than sea and land?"],[32,35,"Oh, what a shameful theft, to have stolen something we are afraid to keep! We would be thieves unworthy of what we stole, if we disgraced the Greeks in their own country and are now afraid to defend it in our own."]],"367":[[0,11,"Why, brother Hector, we shouldn't judge whether an action is right only by how it turns out, nor should we lose heart because Cassandra is mad. Her brain-sick fits can't spoil the goodness of a quarrel that all our honours are committed to making honourable. For my own part, I am no more involved than any of Priam's sons, and Jove forbid that we should do anything that even the weakest temper would find offensive to fight for and defend."]],"368":[[0,4,"Otherwise the world might accuse both my actions and your advice of being frivolous. But I call the gods to witness, your full agreement gave wings to my inclination and cut off all the fears that came with such a dangerous project."],[5,12,"For what, alas, can my two arms do on their own? What defence is there in one man's courage to withstand the attack and hostility that this quarrel would stir up? Yet I swear, if I had to face the difficulties alone, and had as much power as I have will, Paris would never take back what he has done, or lose heart in following it through."]],"370":[[0,9,"Sir, I am not thinking only of the pleasures such a beauty brings with her. I want the stain of her abduction wiped away by keeping her honourably. What treason it would be to the stolen queen, what a disgrace to your great honour and what a shame to me, to hand her over now on terms forced on us so humiliatingly! Can such a degenerate idea ever find a foothold in your noble hearts?"],[10,16,"There isn't the lowest spirit on our side who lacks the heart to dare or the sword to draw when Helen is being defended, and no one so noble that his life would be wasted or his death unfamous when Helen is the cause. So I say we may well fight for her, since we know the whole wide world has no equal to her."]],"371":[[0,10,"Paris and Troilus, you have both spoken well, and you have commented on the cause and question we are discussing, but only superficially, not unlike the young men whom Aristotle thought unfit to hear moral philosophy. The reasons you give have more to do with the hot passion of disordered blood than with reaching a free judgement between right and wrong, because pleasure and revenge have ears deafer than adders to the voice of any true decision."],[11,19,"Nature demands that everything owed be given back to its owner. Now, what closer debt is there in all humanity than a wife to her husband? If this law of nature is corrupted by passion, and great men resist it out of indulgence to their own numbed wills, there is a law in every well-ordered nation to curb those raging appetites that are most disobedient and stubborn."],[20,30,"So if Helen is the wife of the king of Sparta, as everyone knows she is, these moral laws of nature and of nations loudly demand that she be returned. To keep on doing wrong does not lessen the wrong, but makes it much heavier. That is Hector's opinion, as a matter of truth. But nevertheless, my spirited brothers, I lean towards your decision to keep Helen, because it is a cause on which our honour, together and individually, depends heavily."]],"372":[[0,12,"Why, now you've touched the heart of our purpose. If it weren't glory that we cared about more than giving vent to our angry tempers, I wouldn't want a single drop more Trojan blood spent defending her. But, worthy Hector, she is a cause of honour and fame, a spur to brave and noble deeds, whose courage now may beat down our enemies and whose fame in times to come may make saints of us. For I assume brave Hector would not give up such a rich chance of promised glory as smiles on the face of this enterprise, not for all the wealth of the wide world."]],"428":[[0,9,"Listen, Patroclus. We know these answers too well, but his evasion, though it flies so fast on the wings of scorn, cannot outfly our understanding. He has many fine qualities, and many reasons why we credit him with them. Yet all his virtues, since he does not display them virtuously, are beginning to lose their shine in our eyes; yes, like good fruit on a dirty dish, they are likely to rot untasted."],[10,20,"Go and tell him we have come to speak with him. And it won't be a sin if you tell him we think him too proud and not honest enough, greater in his own opinion than in the judgement of sensible people, and that better men than he are here waiting on the savage aloofness he puts on, hiding the sacred strength of their authority and humbly putting up with his moody dominance; yes, watching his moods and timing, his ebbs and flows, as if the whole course and current of this campaign rode on his tide."],[21,26,"Go and tell him this, and add that if he overprices himself so much, we'll have nothing to do with him. We'll let him lie, like a siege engine too heavy to move, under this verdict: \"Bring the fighting here; this cannot go to war.\" We value a busy dwarf above a sleeping giant. Tell him so."]],"448":[[0,9,"He makes things as small as nothing important, just because he is asked. He is possessed with his own greatness, and he can't even talk to himself without a pride that quarrels with its own breath. His imagined worth stirs up such a swollen, hot argument in his blood that Achilles, a kingdom in himself, rages in civil war between his mind and his actions and batters himself down. What can I say? He is so plaguily proud that the fatal spots of the disease cry \"No recovery.\""]],"450":[[0,7,"Oh Agamemnon, don't let that happen! We will bless every step Ajax takes away from Achilles. Shall that proud lord, who bastes his arrogance in his own fat, and never lets any worldly matter enter his thoughts except what concerns himself, be worshipped by the man we idolise more than him?"],[8,17,"No, this three-times-worthy and truly brave lord must not cheapen his nobly won glory, nor, if I have anything to do with it, make his merit, which is as amply honoured as Achilles', subject to Achilles by going to him. That would be adding lard to his already fat pride, and adding more coal to the sign of Cancer when it burns with the summer sun. This lord go to him! Jupiter forbid, and say in thunder, \"Achilles, go to him.\""]],"482":[[0,8,"Thank heaven, lord, you have a sweet nature. Praise your father, and your mother who nursed you. Let your teacher be famous, and your natural gifts three times more famous, beyond all learning. But as for the man who trained your arms to fight, let Mars cut eternity in two and give him half. And as for your strength, let Milo, who carried a bull, give up his title to muscular Ajax."],[9,16,"I won't praise your wisdom, which, like a boundary, a fence, a shore, contains your wide-ranging talents. Here's Nestor, taught by ancient times. He must be wise, he is, he can't help being wise. But excuse me, father Nestor, if your days were as young as Ajax's and your brain as well balanced, you wouldn't be superior to him; you would be just like Ajax."]],"563":[[0,5,"Love, love, nothing but love, always more love! Oh, love's bow shoots both buck and doe. The arrow doesn't kill by wounding, but keeps tickling the sore."],[6,11,"These lovers cry, \"Oh ho, we're dying!\" Yet what seems to be the fatal wound turns \"Oh ho!\" into \"Ha ha he!\" So dying love lives on. \"Oh ho!\" for a while, then \"Ha ha ha!\" \"Oh ho!\" groans into \"Ha ha ha!\" Heigh ho!"]],"591":[[0,7,"I'm dizzy; expectation spins me round. The imagined taste is so sweet that it enchants my senses. What will it be like when my watering mouth really tastes love's three-times-purified nectar? Death, I'm afraid; a swooning destruction; or some joy too fine, too subtly powerful, tuned too sharply sweet for my coarser senses to take in."],[8,11,"I'm very afraid of it. And I'm also afraid that I'll lose all sense of distinction in my joys, like an army charging in a confused mass when the enemy is fleeing."]],"622":[[0,8,"Hard to seem won. But I was won, my lord, with the first look that ever... forgive me. If I confess too much, you'll play the tyrant. I love you now, but until now not so much that I couldn't control it. No, honestly, I'm lying. My thoughts were like unruly children, grown too headstrong for their mother. See what fools we are! Why have I blurted it out? Who will keep our secrets when we can't keep them ourselves?"],[9,16,"But though I loved you well, I didn't woo you. And yet, honestly, I wished I were a man, or that we women had men's right to speak first. Sweet, tell me to hold my tongue, because in this rapture I'm sure to say something I'll regret. Look, look, your silence, cunning in saying nothing, draws the secrets out of my very soul in my weakness. Stop my mouth."]],"635":[[0,5,"Oh, if only I thought a woman could (and if one can, I'll believe it of you) keep the lamp and flames of her love burning for ever, keep her faith as fresh and young as when she pledged it, outlasting her outward beauty, with a mind that renews itself faster than the body decays!"],[6,12,"Or if only I could be convinced that my loyalty and faith to you would be met with an equal measure and weight of such purified love. How lifted up I would be then! But alas, I am as true as plain truth itself, and simpler than truth in its infancy."]],"637":[[0,8,"Oh virtuous battle, when right fights right over who will be most right! In times to come, faithful lovers will prove their faithfulness by Troilus. When their poems, full of promises, oaths and grand comparisons, run out of similes, and truth is worn out with repeating \"as true as steel, as plants to the moon, as sun to day, as turtle dove to her mate, as iron to a magnet, as earth to the centre\","],[9,12,"then, after all these comparisons of truth, \"as true as Troilus\" will crown the verse and make the poem holy, quoted as the original authority on truth."]],"638":[[0,6,"May you be a prophet! If I am false, or stray a hair's breadth from truth, when time is old and has forgotten itself, when drops of water have worn away the stones of Troy, and blind oblivion has swallowed up cities, and mighty states have been ground down without trace to dusty nothing,"],[7,13,"then let memory, from one false lover to the next, among unfaithful girls in love, condemn my falseness. When they have said \"as false as air, as water, wind or sandy ground, as fox to lamb or wolf to calf, leopard to deer or stepmother to stepson\", yes, let them say, to pierce falseness to the heart, \"as false as Cressida.\""]],"648":[[0,11,"Now, Princes, for the service I have done you, the opportunity of the moment prompts me to ask out loud for my reward. Remember that, because of my power to see the future, I have abandoned Troy, left my property, earned the name of traitor, and exposed myself, leaving safe and secure comforts for an uncertain fortune. I have cut myself off from everything that time, friends, habit and position had made comfortable and familiar to me, and here, to serve you, I have become like a newborn in the world, a stranger who knows no one."],[12,15,"I beg you, as a kind of first taste, to give me now a small reward out of the many you have promised, which you say are still to come for me."]],"650":[[0,8,"You have a Trojan prisoner called Antenor, captured yesterday. Troy values him very highly. You have often (and I thank you for it) asked for my Cressida in exchange for important prisoners, and Troy has always refused. But I know this Antenor is so essential to their affairs that all their negotiations will grind to a halt without his management, and they would almost give us a royal prince, a son of Priam, in exchange for him."],[9,12,"Let him be sent, great Princes, and he will buy my daughter back. Her presence will completely pay off all the service I have done, so willingly and so painfully."]],"655":[[0,8,"Achilles is standing at the entrance of his tent. Please, general, walk past him coldly, as if he were forgotten. And, all you Princes, give him only careless, offhand looks. I will come last. He will probably ask me why such disapproving looks are turned on him. If so, I have some healing scorn to apply between your coldness and his pride, which his own will will want to drink."],[9,11,"It may do some good. Pride has no mirror to see itself in except other people's pride, because bending knees feed arrogance and are the proud man's wages."]],"676":[[0,9,"What, have I become poor lately? It's certain that greatness, once it falls out with fortune, must fall out with men too. A fallen man will read his fall in other people's eyes as soon as he feels it himself. For men, like butterflies, only show their powdery wings in summer. And no man is honoured simply for being a man, but for honours that are outside him, like position, wealth and favour, which are prizes of chance as often as of merit."],[10,19,"When those fall, being slippery supports, the love that leaned on them, just as slippery, falls too; one pulls the other down and they die together in the fall. But that isn't how it is with me. Fortune and I are friends. I still enjoy in full everything I had, except these men's looks. They seem, I think, to find something in me not worth the rich attention they used to give. Here is Ulysses. I'll interrupt his reading."],[20,20,"Hello, Ulysses!"]],"680":[[0,9,"That isn't strange, Ulysses. The beauty a person carries in his face is not known to him, but shows itself to other people's eyes. Nor does the eye itself, that purest of the senses, see itself, since it cannot go out of itself. But when two eyes face each other, each greets the other with its own image, because sight does not turn back on itself until it has travelled out and been mirrored where it can see itself. This isn't strange at all."]],"681":[[0,11,"I don't have trouble with the idea, which is familiar, but with where the author is going with it. He argues in detail that no man is master of anything, however much there is in him and of him, until he shares his qualities with others. Nor does he know them to be worth anything himself until he sees them shaped in the applause they receive. That applause, like an arch, echoes the voice back, or, like a steel gate facing the sun, receives and reflects back its image and its heat."],[12,21,"I was very absorbed in this, and immediately thought of the unrecognised Ajax. Heavens, what a man he is! A real horse, who has he doesn't know what! Nature, what things there are that are thought worthless and are precious in use! And other things highly valued and poor in worth! Now tomorrow we shall see, through an act that pure chance throws on him, Ajax made famous. Oh heavens, what some men do while other men leave things undone!"],[22,29,"How some men creep into fickle Fortune's hall while others play the idiot in front of her! How one man feeds on another's glory while the proud man starves in his idleness! To see these Greek lords! Why, already they're slapping that lump Ajax on the shoulder, as if his foot were on brave Hector's chest and great Troy were shrieking."]],"683":[[0,10,"Time, my lord, has a bag on his back in which he puts alms for oblivion, a huge monster of ingratitude. Those scraps are past good deeds, which are devoured as soon as they are done, forgotten as soon as they are finished. Perseverance, my dear lord, keeps honour bright. To have done something is to hang completely out of fashion, like rusty armour on a monument, a mockery. Take the immediate path, because honour travels down a passage so narrow that only one can walk in it at a time."],[11,19,"So keep to the path, because rivalry has a thousand sons who follow one after another. If you give way, or step aside from the straight road, they all rush past like a tide coming in and leave you last. Or, like a fine horse that has fallen in the front rank, you lie there as a pavement for the miserable rear, run over and trampled on. Then what they do now, though less than what you did in the past, must top yours."],[20,29,"For Time is like a fashionable host who barely shakes his departing guest's hand, and grasps the new arrival with outstretched arms as if he wanted to fly. Welcome always smiles, and farewell goes out sighing. Oh, don't let virtue look for a reward for what it was. Beauty, wit, high birth, strength, merit in service, love, friendship, charity, are all subject to envious, slandering Time."],[30,35,"One trait of human nature makes the whole world alike: everyone agrees in praising new-made trinkets, even though they are made and moulded out of old things, and they give more praise to dust with a little gold on it than to gold covered in dust. The eye of the present praises what is present."],[36,45,"So don't be surprised, you great and perfect man, that all the Greeks are beginning to worship Ajax, since things that move catch the eye sooner than things that are still. The cheering was once for you, and still could be, and may be again, if you would not bury yourself alive and shut your reputation up in your tent. Your glorious deeds on these very fields only recently made the gods themselves compete and drove great Mars to take sides."]],"687":[[0,9,"Is that a surprise? The foresight of a watchful state knows almost every grain of Plutus' gold, finds the bottom of unfathomable depths, keeps pace with thought, and, almost like the gods, uncovers thoughts while they are still unspoken in their cradles. There is a mystery in the soul of the state, which no report has ever dared to meddle with, whose workings are more divine than speech or writing can express."],[10,20,"All the dealings you have had with Troy are as well known to us as to you, my lord. And it would suit Achilles much better to throw down Hector than Polyxena. But it must grieve young Pyrrhus, your son at home, when fame blows her trumpet in our island and all the Greek girls sing as they dance, \"Great Hector's sister was won by Achilles, but our great Ajax bravely beat Hector down.\" Goodbye, my lord. I speak as one who loves you. The fool is sliding over the ice that you should break."]],"689":[[0,9,"This is what I have urged you to do, Achilles. A shameless, mannish woman is not more hated than an effeminate man in time of war. I am blamed for this. They think my small appetite for war and your great love for me keep you back like this. Sweet, wake yourself up, and weak, playful Cupid will loosen his loving grip on your neck and be shaken off into the air like a dewdrop from a lion's mane."]],"746":[[0,9,"His purpose is coming to meet you: it was for you to bring this Greek to Calchas' house, and there hand over to him the fair Cressida in exchange for the freed Antenor. Come with us, or, if you prefer, hurry there ahead of us. I firmly believe (or rather, I'd call it certain knowledge) that my brother Troilus is staying there tonight. Wake him and tell him we're coming, and the whole reason why. I'm afraid we'll be very unwelcome."]],"752":[[0,6,"Both equally. He deserves to have her, since he seeks her back without caring about her pollution, at the cost of such hellish pain and a world of expense; and you deserve to keep her, since you defend her without tasting her dishonour, at the cost of such a loss of wealth and friends."],[7,12,"He, like a whining cuckold, would drink up the stale dregs of a flat, used-up barrel. You, like a lecher, are happy to breed your heirs from a whore's body. Weigh both claims, and neither is heavier or lighter; but each of you is weighed down by a whore."]],"815":[[0,9,"I won't, uncle. I have forgotten my father. I feel no tie of blood, no family, no love, no blood, no soul as close to me as sweet Troilus. Oh you divine gods, make Cressida's name the very crown of falseness if she ever leaves Troilus! Time, force and death, do the worst you can to this body, but the strong foundation and building of my love is like the very centre of the earth, drawing everything towards it. I'll go in and weep..."]],"842":[[0,8,"And immediately, so that the injury of chance puts off our farewells, roughly shoves aside any pause, rudely cheats our lips of any reply, forcibly stops our close embraces, and strangles our dear vows in the very birth of our struggling breath. We two, who bought each other with so many thousand sighs, must sell ourselves cheaply with the rough shortness of a single one."],[9,15,"Cruel time, with a robber's hurry, now crams his rich loot together any old how. As many farewells as there are stars in the sky, each with its own breath and the kisses that seal it, he bundles into one careless goodbye, and stints us to a single starved kiss, spoiled by the salt of broken-off tears."]],"926":[[0,9,"Shame, shame on her! There's language in her eyes, her cheeks, her lips; even her foot speaks. Her lustful spirit looks out from every joint and movement of her body. Oh, these flirts with such smooth tongues, who give a welcome before a man has even approached, and open wide the notebooks of their thoughts to every reader who tickles them! Mark them down as easy spoils for any opportunist, and daughters of the game."]],"938":[[0,5,"Achilles, then. But whoever you are, know this: courage and pride are both at their extremes in Hector, one great and one small. His courage is almost infinite, and his pride is as empty as nothing. Weigh him carefully, and what looks like pride is courtesy."],[6,9,"This Ajax is half made of Hector's blood. Out of love for that, half of Hector stays at home. Half a heart, half a hand, half of Hector comes to fight this mixed knight, half Trojan and half Greek."]],"944":[[0,7,"Priam's youngest son, a true knight. Not yet fully grown, yet unmatched; firm to his word; he speaks in deeds and not with his tongue. He is not quickly provoked, nor quickly calmed once provoked. His heart and hand are both open and generous, for he gives what he has and shows what he thinks; yet he doesn't give until judgement guides his generosity, nor does he honour an unworthy thought by speaking it."],[8,16,"He is as manly as Hector, but more dangerous, because Hector, in the heat of his anger, spares those who are helpless, while he, in the heat of the fight, is more vengeful than jealous love. They call him Troilus, and build on him a second hope as fine as Hector. So says Aeneas, who knows the young man down to the last inch, and who described him to me like this, in confidence, in great Troy."]],"955":[[0,10,"Why, then I'll fight no more. Great lord, you are my father's sister's son, a first cousin to great Priam's children. The bond of our blood forbids a bloody contest between us two. If your mixture of Greek and Trojan were such that you could say, \"This hand is all Greek, and this is Trojan; the muscles of this leg are all Greek and this all Trojan; my mother's blood runs in my right cheek and this left one throbs with my father's,\""],[11,19,"then, by all-powerful Jove, you would not carry away from me a single Greek limb without my sword printing on it the mark of our bitter feud. But the just gods forbid that any drop of blood you got from your mother, my sacred aunt, should be drained by my deadly sword! Let me embrace you, Ajax. By the god of thunder, you have strong arms. This is how Hector would like them to fall on him. Cousin, all honour to you!"]],"974":[[0,8,"I have often seen you, you gallant Trojan, working for fate, cutting a cruel path through the ranks of young Greeks. And I have seen you, as hot as Perseus, spur on your Trojan horse, scorning many who had surrendered and were at your mercy, when you held your raised sword in the air and would not let it fall on the fallen, so that I said to some who stood by me, \"Look, there's Jupiter, giving out life!\""],[9,17,"And I have seen you pause and take a breath when a ring of Greeks had hemmed you in, like an Olympic wrestler. This I have seen. But this face of yours, always locked in steel, I never saw until now. I knew your grandfather, and once fought with him. He was a good soldier, but, by great Mars, the leader of us all, never like you. Oh, let an old man embrace you, and, worthy warrior, welcome to our tents."]],"1030":[[0,7,"My sweet Patroclus, I am completely prevented from carrying out my great plan in tomorrow's battle. Here is a letter from Queen Hecuba, and a token from her daughter, my fair love, both urging me and binding me to keep an oath I have sworn. I will not break it. Let the Greeks fall, let fame fail, let honour come or go: my greater vow is here, and this I will obey."],[8,10,"Come, come, Thersites, help to get my tent ready. This whole night must be spent banqueting. Away, Patroclus!"]],"1165":[[0,9,"To make a record in my soul of every syllable that was spoken here. But if I tell how these two acted together, won't I be lying even while I tell the truth? Because there is still a belief in my heart, a hope so stubbornly strong that it overturns the evidence of my eyes and ears, as if those organs had deceptive functions created only to slander. Was Cressida here?"]],"1175":[[0,9,"This her? No, this is Diomedes' Cressida. If beauty has a soul, this is not her. If souls guide vows, if vows are sacred, if sacredness is the gods' delight, if there is any rule in oneness itself, this was not her. Oh madness of reasoning, which argues both for and against itself! Double authority, where reason can rebel without being lost, and loss can take on all the appearance of reason without rebelling: this is, and is not, Cressida."],[10,15,"Inside my soul a fight of such a strange kind is going on that a thing that cannot be divided splits wider apart than the sky and the earth, and yet the vast breadth of this division leaves no opening wide enough for a point as fine as Arachne's broken thread to enter."],[16,23,"Proof, oh proof, as strong as the gates of hell: Cressida is mine, tied with the bonds of heaven. Proof, oh proof, as strong as heaven itself: the bonds of heaven have slipped, dissolved and come loose, and with another knot, tied by five fingers, the scraps of her faith, the leftovers of her love, the fragments, crumbs, bits and greasy remains of her over-eaten faith are given to Diomedes."]],"1177":[[0,6,"Yes, Greek, and that will be made known in letters as red as Mars's heart when Venus set it on fire. Never did a young man love with so eternal and so fixed a soul. Listen, Greek: as much as I love Cressida, I hate her Diomedes by the same weight. That sleeve he'll wear on his helmet is mine."],[7,13,"Even if it were a helmet made by Vulcan's skill, my sword would bite it. The dreadful waterspout that sailors call a hurricane, drawn up into a mass by the almighty sun, will not deafen Neptune's ear with more noise as it falls than my eager sword will make falling on Diomedes."]],"1273":[[0,10,"Renew the attack, renew it! The fierce Polydamas has beaten down Menon. The bastard Margarelon has taken Doreus prisoner and stands like a colossus, waving his heavy spear over the battered corpses of the kings Epistrophus and Cedius. Polixenes is killed; Amphimachus and Thoas are fatally wounded; Patroclus is captured or killed; and Palamedes is badly wounded and bruised. The dreadful centaur archer terrifies our troops. Let's hurry, Diomedes, to bring reinforcements, or we'll all die."]],"1275":[[0,8,"Go, take Patroclus' body to Achilles, and tell the snail-paced Ajax to arm himself for shame. There are a thousand Hectors on the battlefield. Now here he fights on his horse Galathe, and soon there's no one left to fight. Next he's over there on foot, and there they run or die, like shoals of scaly fish before a spouting whale. Then he's over there, and there the Greeks fall down before him like straw, ripe for his blade, like grass before the mower."],[9,12,"Here, there and everywhere he leaves and takes, his skill so obedient to his desire that whatever he wants to do he does, and does so much that the evidence seems impossible."]],"1277":[[0,6,"Oh, courage, courage, courage, Princes! Great Achilles is arming, weeping, cursing, swearing vengeance. Patroclus' wounds have roused his drowsy blood, together with his mangled Myrmidons, who come to him noseless, handless, hacked and chipped, crying out against Hector. Ajax has lost a friend and is foaming at the mouth; he is armed and in the fight, roaring for Troilus,"],[7,12,"who has done mad and incredible slaughter today, throwing himself into danger and getting himself out again with such careless strength and effortless care, as if lust for battle, in spite of all skill, told him to win everything."]],"1366":[[0,11,"You don't understand me if you say that. I'm not talking about running away or fear of death, but I dare every danger that gods and men can threaten. Hector is gone. Who will tell Priam, or Hecuba? Let whoever is willing to be called a screech owl for ever go into Troy and say there, \"Hector's dead.\" Those words will turn Priam to stone, turn the young women and wives into wells of tears like Niobe, and the young men into cold statues; in a word, frighten Troy out of its wits. But march away. Hector is dead; there is no more to say."],[12,20,"Wait a moment. You vile, disgusting tents, pitched so proudly on our Trojan plains, let the sun rise as early as it dares, I'll go through and through you. And you, great big coward (Achilles), no distance on earth will keep our two hatreds apart. I'll haunt you for ever like a guilty conscience, which makes up goblins as fast as a madman's thoughts. Beat a lively march back to Troy. Go with comfort: the hope of revenge will hide our inner grief."]],"1371":[[0,0,"A fine medicine for my aching bones! Oh world, world! This is how the poor agent is despised! Oh traitors and pimps, how eagerly you are put to work, and how badly you are paid! Why should our efforts be so wanted and the result so hated? What verse is there for it? What example? Let me see:"],[1,4,"\"The bumblebee sings very merrily until he has lost his honey and his sting; and once his weapon's tail is gone, sweet honey and sweet songs both fail together.\""],[5,9,"Good traders in flesh, put this on your painted wall-hangings. All of you here who belong to Pandar's hall, let your eyes, half blind already, weep at Pandar's fall. Or if you can't weep, at least give a few groans, if not for me, then for your aching bones."],[10,15,"Brothers and sisters of the door-keeping trade, in about two months I'll make my will here. It should be now, except that I'm afraid some sore prostitute from Winchester would hiss. Until then I'll sweat and look around for remedies, and then I'll leave you my diseases in my will."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"love":["Love and desire","Troilus's idealism, Cressida's caution, and love that turns into appetite."],"faith":["Truth and falseness","Vows, tokens and betrayal: 'as true as Troilus', 'as false as Cressid'."],"value":["Worth and value","Is anything valuable in itself, or only because someone wants it? Helen, Cressida and reputation as goods."],"war":["War and honour","Chivalry, challenges and heroism, and how the play exposes them."],"order":["Order and degree","Ulysses' vision of rank and order, and the chaos of appetite."],"pride":["Pride and policy","Achilles, Ajax and the generals' schemes to use one pride against another."],"time":["Time and reputation","Time the robber, oblivion, and fame that must be constantly renewed."],"lust":["Lechery and disease","Thersites' and Pandarus's view of the world: whores, cuckolds and the pox."]},"quotes":[{"id":"2","who":"PROLOGUE","text":"Beginning in the middle; starting thence away,","themes":["war"],"note":"The play skips the heroic start and ending of the war."},{"id":"2","who":"PROLOGUE","text":"Now good or bad, 'tis but the chance of war.","themes":["war"],"note":"The Prologue shrugs at the outcome before it begins."},{"id":"2","who":"PROLOGUE","text":"With wanton Paris sleeps—and that's the quarrel.","themes":["lust","war"],"note":"The war's cause reduced to a bed."},{"id":"4","who":"TROILUS","text":"Why should I war without the walls of Troy That find such cruel battle here within?","themes":["love","war"],"note":"Love is a battle inside Troilus."},{"id":"6","who":"TROILUS","text":"But I am weaker than a woman's tear,","themes":["love"],"note":"Troilus thinks love has unmanned him."},{"id":"7","who":"PANDARUS","text":"He that will have a cake out of the wheat must tarry the grinding.","themes":["love"],"note":"Pandarus makes courtship a recipe."},{"id":"16","who":"TROILUS","text":"But sorrow that is couch'd in seeming gladness Is like that mirth fate turns to sudden sadness.","themes":["love","time"],"note":"A rhyme that foreshadows the play's turn."},{"id":"18","who":"TROILUS","text":"In whose comparison all whites are ink","themes":["love"],"note":"Troilus idealises Cressida in poetic clichés."},{"id":"18","who":"TROILUS","text":"Thou lay'st in every gash that love hath given me The knife that made it.","themes":["love"],"note":"Pandarus's praise wounds instead of healing."},{"id":"33","who":"TROILUS","text":"Helen must needs be fair, When with your blood you daily paint her thus.","themes":["war","value"],"note":"Helen's beauty is created by the blood spent on her."},{"id":"33","who":"TROILUS","text":"It is too starv'd a subject for my sword.","themes":["war","value"],"note":"Troilus thinks the cause unworthy of fighting."},{"id":"33","who":"TROILUS","text":"I cannot come to Cressid but by Pandar;","themes":["love"],"note":"Troilus's love depends on a go-between."},{"id":"33","who":"TROILUS","text":"Her bed is India; there she lies, a pearl;","themes":["love","value"],"note":"Cressida as a precious object to be won by trade."},{"id":"40","who":"TROILUS","text":"Paris is gor'd with Menelaus' horn.","themes":["lust","war"],"note":"The cuckold joke that runs through the play."},{"id":"58","who":"ALEXANDER","text":"he is a gouty Briareus, many hands and no use, or purblind Argus, all eyes and no sight.","themes":["pride"],"note":"Alexander's mock-epic portrait of Ajax."},{"id":"176","who":"PANDARUS","text":"Paris? Paris is dirt to him;","themes":["love","value"],"note":"Pandarus sells Troilus to Cressida."},{"id":"179","who":"PANDARUS","text":"I had rather be such a man as Troilus than Agamemnon and all Greece.","themes":["value"],"note":"Pandarus's salesmanship."},{"id":"183","who":"PANDARUS","text":"Is not birth, beauty, good shape, discourse, manhood, learning, gentleness, virtue, youth, liberality, and such like, the spice and salt that season a man?","themes":["value"],"note":"A man listed like ingredients."},{"id":"186","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"Upon my back, to defend my belly; upon my wit, to defend my wiles;","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Cressida's defences, sexual and verbal."},{"id":"200","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"Women are angels, wooing: Things won are done; joy's soul lies in the doing.","themes":["love","value"],"note":"Cressida's theory of desire."},{"id":"200","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"Men prize the thing ungain'd more than it is.","themes":["love","value"],"note":"Value lies in what is not yet possessed."},{"id":"200","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"'Achievement is command; ungain'd, beseech.'","themes":["love","order"],"note":"Cressida sees love as a power game."},{"id":"204","who":"AGAMEMNON","text":"What grief hath set these jaundies o'er your cheeks?","themes":["war"],"note":"Agamemnon's sickly army."},{"id":"204","who":"AGAMEMNON","text":"But the protractive trials of great Jove To find persistive constancy in men;","themes":["war","time"],"note":"Agamemnon excuses failure as a test."},{"id":"205","who":"NESTOR","text":"In the reproof of chance Lies the true proof of men.","themes":["war"],"note":"Nestor: hard times test men."},{"id":"208","who":"ULYSSES","text":"The specialty of rule hath been neglected;","themes":["order"],"note":"Ulysses diagnoses the camp's sickness."},{"id":"208","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Take but degree away, untune that string, And hark what discord follows!","themes":["order"],"note":"The heart of the degree speech."},{"id":"208","who":"ULYSSES","text":"And appetite, an universal wolf,","themes":["order","lust"],"note":"Appetite devours everything without order."},{"id":"208","who":"ULYSSES","text":"And last eat up himself.","themes":["order"],"note":"Unchecked appetite ends in self-destruction."},{"id":"208","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Troy in our weakness stands, not in her strength.","themes":["order","war"],"note":"The Greeks defeat themselves."},{"id":"208","who":"ULYSSES","text":"The heavens themselves, the planets, and this centre, Observe degree, priority, and place,","themes":["order"],"note":"Order as cosmic law."},{"id":"211","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Having his ear full of his airy fame, Grows dainty of his worth,","themes":["pride"],"note":"Achilles spoilt by reputation."},{"id":"211","who":"ULYSSES","text":"He pageants us.","themes":["pride","order"],"note":"Patroclus mocks the generals in plays."},{"id":"211","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Sir Valour dies;","themes":["pride"],"note":"Achilles laughing himself to death."},{"id":"213","who":"ULYSSES","text":"They call this bed-work, mapp'ry, closet-war;","themes":["war","order"],"note":"Brains against brawn."},{"id":"228","who":"AENEAS","text":"The worthiness of praise distains his worth, If that the prais'd himself bring the praise forth;","themes":["pride","value"],"note":"Self-praise spoils worth."},{"id":"238","who":"AENEAS","text":"That loves his mistress more than in confession With truant vows to her own lips he loves,","themes":["love","war"],"note":"Chivalric love language in Hector's challenge."},{"id":"238","who":"AENEAS","text":"The Grecian dames are sunburnt and not worth The splinter of a lance.","themes":["war","value"],"note":"The challenge's insult."},{"id":"240","who":"NESTOR","text":"I'll prove this troth with my three drops of blood.","themes":["war"],"note":"Nestor's comic old-age chivalry."},{"id":"249","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Blunt wedges rive hard knots.","themes":["pride"],"note":"Ulysses' plan in a proverb."},{"id":"257","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Ajax employ'd plucks down Achilles' plumes.","themes":["pride"],"note":"The plan's purpose."},{"id":"258","who":"NESTOR","text":"Two curs shall tame each other: pride alone Must tarre the mastiffs on, as 'twere their bone.","themes":["pride"],"note":"Pride used against pride."},{"id":"270","who":"THERSITES","text":"thou mongrel beef-witted lord!","themes":["pride"],"note":"Thersites on Ajax."},{"id":"289","who":"THERSITES","text":"Thou hast no more brain than I have in mine elbows;","themes":["pride"],"note":"Thersites' contempt for Ajax."},{"id":"307","who":"THERSITES","text":"who wears his wit in his belly and his guts in his head","themes":["pride"],"note":"Ajax upside down."},{"id":"314","who":"THERSITES","text":"As will stop the eye of Helen's needle, for whom he comes to fight.","themes":["war","lust"],"note":"The war's cause mocked again."},{"id":"329","who":"THERSITES","text":"yoke you like draught oxen, and make you plough up the wars.","themes":["war","order"],"note":"Heroes used as beasts by the planners."},{"id":"348","who":"HECTOR","text":"The wound of peace is surety, Surety secure;","themes":["war"],"note":"Hector: overconfidence is peace's danger."},{"id":"348","who":"HECTOR","text":"Let Helen go.","themes":["war","value"],"note":"Hector's plain conclusion."},{"id":"351","who":"TROILUS","text":"Reason and respect Make livers pale and lustihood deject.","themes":["war"],"note":"Troilus scorns reason."},{"id":"352","who":"HECTOR","text":"Brother, she is not worth what she doth cost the keeping.","themes":["value","war"],"note":"Hector on Helen's value."},{"id":"353","who":"TROILUS","text":"What's aught but as 'tis valued?","themes":["value"],"note":"Troilus: worth lies in valuing."},{"id":"354","who":"HECTOR","text":"'Tis mad idolatry To make the service greater than the god,","themes":["value"],"note":"Hector: value must be in the thing itself."},{"id":"355","who":"TROILUS","text":"Why, she is a pearl Whose price hath launch'd above a thousand ships, And turn'd crown'd kings to merchants.","themes":["value","war"],"note":"Helen as a priceless commodity."},{"id":"355","who":"TROILUS","text":"O theft most base, That we have stol'n what we do fear to keep!","themes":["value","war"],"note":"Troilus shames his brothers."},{"id":"364","who":"CASSANDRA","text":"Our firebrand brother, Paris, burns us all.","themes":["war"],"note":"Cassandra's unheeded prophecy."},{"id":"364","who":"CASSANDRA","text":"Cry, cry. Troy burns, or else let Helen go.","themes":["war"],"note":"Cassandra's choice."},{"id":"369","who":"PRIAM","text":"You have the honey still, but these the gall;","themes":["war","lust"],"note":"Priam: Paris gets the pleasure, others the pain."},{"id":"371","who":"HECTOR","text":"Have ears more deaf than adders to the voice Of any true decision.","themes":["order","value"],"note":"Hector: passion ignores right judgement."},{"id":"371","who":"HECTOR","text":"What nearer debt in all humanity Than wife is to the husband?","themes":["order"],"note":"Hector on the law of nature."},{"id":"371","who":"HECTOR","text":"Yet, ne'ertheless, My spritely brethren, I propend to you In resolution to keep Helen still;","themes":["war","value"],"note":"Hector's sudden reversal."},{"id":"372","who":"TROILUS","text":"She is a theme of honour and renown, A spur to valiant and magnanimous deeds,","themes":["war","value"],"note":"Troilus makes Helen a symbol of glory."},{"id":"377","who":"THERSITES","text":"the Neapolitan bone-ache! for that, methinks, is the curse depending on those that war for a placket.","themes":["lust","war"],"note":"Thersites curses the war with syphilis."},{"id":"397","who":"THERSITES","text":"Agamemnon is a fool; Achilles is a fool; Thersites is a fool; and, as aforesaid, Patroclus is a fool.","themes":["pride","order"],"note":"Thersites' logic of folly."},{"id":"405","who":"THERSITES","text":"All the argument is a whore and a cuckold—a good quarrel to draw emulous factions and bleed to death upon.","themes":["lust","war"],"note":"The play's bleakest summary."},{"id":"405","who":"THERSITES","text":"war and lechery confound all!","themes":["lust","war"],"note":"Thersites' refrain."},{"id":"422","who":"ULYSSES","text":"The amity that wisdom knits not, folly may easily untie.","themes":["order"],"note":"Ulysses on weak alliances."},{"id":"426","who":"ULYSSES","text":"The elephant hath joints, but none for courtesy;","themes":["pride"],"note":"Achilles will not bend."},{"id":"440","who":"AGAMEMNON","text":"He that is proud eats up himself.","themes":["pride"],"note":"Agamemnon's moral, lost on Ajax."},{"id":"442","who":"AJAX","text":"I do hate a proud man as I do hate the engend'ring of toads.","themes":["pride"],"note":"Ajax's irony."},{"id":"443","who":"NESTOR","text":"And yet he loves himself: is't not strange?","themes":["pride"],"note":"Nestor's aside."},{"id":"448","who":"ULYSSES","text":"He is so plaguy proud that the death tokens of it Cry 'No recovery.'","themes":["pride"],"note":"Pride as plague."},{"id":"460","who":"ULYSSES","text":"The raven chides blackness.","themes":["pride"],"note":"The pot calling the kettle black."},{"id":"487","who":"AGAMEMNON","text":"Light boats sail swift, though greater hulks draw deep.","themes":["pride","war"],"note":"Agamemnon prefers Ajax for now."},{"id":"558","who":"HELEN","text":"This love will undo us all.","themes":["love","lust"],"note":"Helen's unconscious prophecy."},{"id":"563","who":"PANDARUS","text":"So dying love lives still.","themes":["love","lust"],"note":"The song's sexual pun on dying."},{"id":"565","who":"PARIS","text":"and hot deeds is love.","themes":["love","lust"],"note":"Paris reduces love to heat."},{"id":"566","who":"PANDARUS","text":"Is love a generation of vipers?","themes":["love","lust"],"note":"Pandarus's joke turns love poisonous."},{"id":"591","who":"TROILUS","text":"I am giddy; expectation whirls me round.","themes":["love"],"note":"Troilus before the meeting."},{"id":"591","who":"TROILUS","text":"Love's thrice-repured nectar?","themes":["love"],"note":"Troilus idealises love as divine drink."},{"id":"595","who":"TROILUS","text":"My heart beats thicker than a feverous pulse,","themes":["love"],"note":"Love as fever."},{"id":"606","who":"TROILUS","text":"Fears make devils of cherubins; they never see truly.","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Troilus dismisses fear."},{"id":"607","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"To fear the worst oft cures the worse.","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Cressida's caution."},{"id":"610","who":"TROILUS","text":"that the will is infinite, and the execution confin'd; that the desire is boundless, and the act a slave to limit.","themes":["love","lust"],"note":"Troilus on the gap between desire and action."},{"id":"611","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"They that have the voice of lions and the act of hares, are they not monsters?","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Cressida doubts lovers' vows."},{"id":"612","who":"TROILUS","text":"Praise us as we are tasted, allow us as we prove;","themes":["love","value"],"note":"Troilus: judge by results."},{"id":"612","who":"TROILUS","text":"what truth can speak truest not truer than Troilus.","themes":["faith"],"note":"Troilus's pride in his own truth."},{"id":"619","who":"PANDARUS","text":"they are burs, I can tell you; they'll stick where they are thrown.","themes":["faith"],"note":"Pandarus vouches for Cressida's constancy."},{"id":"622","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"Hard to seem won; but I was won, my lord, With the first glance that ever—pardon me.","themes":["love"],"note":"Cressida's confession."},{"id":"622","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"Why have I blabb'd? Who shall be true to us, When we are so unsecret to ourselves?","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Cressida's self-reproach."},{"id":"632","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"I have a kind of self resides with you;","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Cressida's divided self."},{"id":"634","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"for to be wise and love Exceeds man's might; that dwells with gods above.","themes":["love"],"note":"Love and wisdom cannot coexist."},{"id":"635","who":"TROILUS","text":"To feed for aye her lamp and flames of love;","themes":["love","faith","time"],"note":"Troilus wishes love could outlast time."},{"id":"635","who":"TROILUS","text":"I am as true as truth's simplicity, And simpler than the infancy of truth.","themes":["faith"],"note":"Troilus's simplicity."},{"id":"637","who":"TROILUS","text":"'As true as Troilus' shall crown up the verse And sanctify the numbers.","themes":["faith"],"note":"Troilus's prophecy of his fame."},{"id":"638","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"When time is old and hath forgot itself, When waterdrops have worn the stones of Troy,","themes":["time","faith"],"note":"Cressida imagines Troy's ruin."},{"id":"638","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"'As false as Cressid.'","themes":["faith"],"note":"The oath history fulfils."},{"id":"639","who":"PANDARUS","text":"let all constant men be Troiluses, all false women Cressids, and all brokers between Pandars.","themes":["faith","love"],"note":"Pandarus fixes the three names."},{"id":"642","who":"PANDARUS","text":"press it to death. Away!","themes":["lust"],"note":"Pandarus's bawdy blessing."},{"id":"648","who":"CALCHAS","text":"Incurr'd a traitor's name,","themes":["faith","war"],"note":"Calchas betrayed Troy."},{"id":"650","who":"CALCHAS","text":"And he shall buy my daughter;","themes":["value"],"note":"Cressida exchanged like goods."},{"id":"655","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Pride hath no other glass To show itself but pride;","themes":["pride"],"note":"Ulysses' trap for Achilles."},{"id":"676","who":"ACHILLES","text":"for men, like butterflies, Show not their mealy wings but to the summer;","themes":["pride","time"],"note":"Achilles learns fortune's fickleness."},{"id":"679","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Cannot make boast to have that which he hath, Nor feels not what he owes, but by reflection;","themes":["pride","value"],"note":"Worth exists only when reflected."},{"id":"681","who":"ULYSSES","text":"How some men creep in skittish Fortune's hall, Whiles others play the idiots in her eyes!","themes":["pride","time"],"note":"Ulysses provokes Achilles."},{"id":"683","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Time hath, my lord, a wallet at his back, Wherein he puts alms for oblivion,","themes":["time"],"note":"The Time speech begins."},{"id":"683","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Perseverance, dear my lord, Keeps honour bright.","themes":["time","war"],"note":"Honour must be renewed."},{"id":"683","who":"ULYSSES","text":"One touch of nature makes the whole world kin—","themes":["time"],"note":"Everyone loves novelty."},{"id":"683","who":"ULYSSES","text":"The present eye praises the present object.","themes":["time","value"],"note":"Reputation lasts only while seen."},{"id":"683","who":"ULYSSES","text":"For Time is like a fashionable host,","themes":["time"],"note":"Time greets newcomers and forgets old guests."},{"id":"687","who":"ULYSSES","text":"The fool slides o'er the ice that you should break.","themes":["pride"],"note":"Ulysses' parting jab."},{"id":"689","who":"PATROCLUS","text":"Shall from your neck unloose his amorous fold,","themes":["love","war"],"note":"Patroclus urges Achilles to fight."},{"id":"692","who":"ACHILLES","text":"My fame is shrewdly gor'd.","themes":["pride"],"note":"Achilles realises his danger."},{"id":"693","who":"PATROCLUS","text":"Those wounds heal ill that men do give themselves;","themes":["pride","time"],"note":"Self-inflicted wounds."},{"id":"703","who":"THERSITES","text":"A plague of opinion! A man may wear it on both sides, like leather jerkin.","themes":["pride","value"],"note":"Reputation can be turned inside out."},{"id":"727","who":"ACHILLES","text":"My mind is troubled, like a fountain stirr'd;","themes":["pride"],"note":"Achilles shaken."},{"id":"729","who":"THERSITES","text":"I had rather be a tick in a sheep than such a valiant ignorance.","themes":["pride"],"note":"Thersites on Ajax."},{"id":"740","who":"AENEAS","text":"No man alive can love in such a sort The thing he means to kill, more excellently.","themes":["war"],"note":"Love and killing mixed."},{"id":"743","who":"DIOMEDES","text":"We do; and long to know each other worse.","themes":["war"],"note":"Chivalrous hatred."},{"id":"752","who":"DIOMEDES","text":"He like a puling cuckold would drink up The lees and dregs of a flat tamed piece;","themes":["lust","value"],"note":"Diomedes on Menelaus."},{"id":"754","who":"DIOMEDES","text":"For every false drop in her bawdy veins A Grecian's life hath sunk;","themes":["war","lust","value"],"note":"The cost of Helen."},{"id":"755","who":"PARIS","text":"Dispraise the thing that you desire to buy;","themes":["value"],"note":"Paris on Diomedes' bargaining."},{"id":"767","who":"TROILUS","text":"With wings more momentary-swift than thought.","themes":["love","time"],"note":"Night flies too fast for lovers."},{"id":"768","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"You men will never tarry.","themes":["love","faith"],"note":"Cressida's fear of abandonment."},{"id":"768","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"O foolish Cressid! I might have still held off,","themes":["love"],"note":"Cressida regrets yielding."},{"id":"800","who":"TROILUS","text":"How my achievements mock me!","themes":["love","time"],"note":"Troilus loses what he has just won."},{"id":"815","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"Make Cressid's name the very crown of falsehood, If ever she leave Troilus!","themes":["faith"],"note":"Another oath she will break."},{"id":"829","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"My love admits no qualifying dross;","themes":["love"],"note":"Cressida's grief."},{"id":"834","who":"TROILUS","text":"That the bless'd gods, as angry with my fancy,","themes":["love"],"note":"Troilus blames the gods."},{"id":"842","who":"TROILUS","text":"Injurious time now with a robber's haste Crams his rich thiev'ry up, he knows not how.","themes":["time","love"],"note":"Time steals the farewell."},{"id":"842","who":"TROILUS","text":"And scants us with a single famish'd kiss,","themes":["love","time"],"note":"A hungry parting."},{"id":"852","who":"TROILUS","text":"be thou true, And I will see thee.","themes":["faith"],"note":"Troilus's refrain."},{"id":"857","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"O heavens! 'Be true' again!","themes":["faith"],"note":"Cressida resents the doubt."},{"id":"860","who":"TROILUS","text":"There lurks a still and dumb-discoursive devil That tempts most cunningly.","themes":["faith","lust"],"note":"Troilus fears the Greeks' charm."},{"id":"862","who":"TROILUS","text":"And sometimes we are devils to ourselves,","themes":["faith"],"note":"Troilus foresees weakness."},{"id":"868","who":"TROILUS","text":"I with great truth catch mere simplicity;","themes":["faith"],"note":"Troilus as the simple true man."},{"id":"873","who":"DIOMEDES","text":"I'll nothing do on charge: to her own worth She shall be priz'd.","themes":["value","war"],"note":"Diomedes defies Troilus."},{"id":"926","who":"ULYSSES","text":"There's language in her eye, her cheek, her lip,","themes":["lust","faith"],"note":"Ulysses reads Cressida's body."},{"id":"926","who":"ULYSSES","text":"And daughters of the game.","themes":["lust"],"note":"Ulysses brands her."},{"id":"938","who":"AENEAS","text":"Valour and pride excel themselves in Hector;","themes":["war","pride"],"note":"Aeneas on Hector's courtesy."},{"id":"944","who":"ULYSSES","text":"Speaking in deeds and deedless in his tongue;","themes":["war"],"note":"Ulysses' praise of Troilus."},{"id":"955","who":"HECTOR","text":"Cousin, all honour to thee!","themes":["war"],"note":"Hector stops for kinship."},{"id":"965","who":"AGAMEMNON","text":"What's past and what's to come is strew'd with husks And formless ruin of oblivion;","themes":["time","war"],"note":"Agamemnon on the present moment."},{"id":"983","who":"HECTOR","text":"The end crowns all;","themes":["time","war"],"note":"Hector trusts in time."},{"id":"993","who":"ACHILLES","text":"Tell me, you heavens, in which part of his body Shall I destroy him?","themes":["war","pride"],"note":"Achilles studies Hector like meat."},{"id":"996","who":"HECTOR","text":"I'll kill thee everywhere, yea, o'er and o'er.","themes":["war","pride"],"note":"Hector's boast."},{"id":"1007","who":"TROILUS","text":"But still sweet love is food for fortune's tooth.","themes":["love","time"],"note":"Troilus's sad proverb."},{"id":"1023","who":"THERSITES","text":"the rotten diseases of the south","themes":["lust"],"note":"Thersites' disease catalogue."},{"id":"1030","who":"ACHILLES","text":"My major vow lies here, this I'll obey.","themes":["love","war"],"note":"Achilles chooses Polyxena's vow."},{"id":"1058","who":"THERSITES","text":"Nothing but lechery! All incontinent varlets!","themes":["lust"],"note":"Thersites' verdict."},{"id":"1075","who":"THERSITES","text":"she's noted.","themes":["lust","faith"],"note":"Thersites on Cressida."},{"id":"1116","who":"THERSITES","text":"How the devil Luxury, with his fat rump and potato finger, tickles these together! Fry, lechery, fry!","themes":["lust"],"note":"Thersites commentates."},{"id":"1127","who":"TROILUS","text":"O beauty! where is thy faith?","themes":["faith"],"note":"Troilus sees the sleeve given."},{"id":"1137","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"He that takes that doth take my heart withal.","themes":["faith","love"],"note":"Cressida half resists."},{"id":"1144","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"'Twas one's that lov'd me better than you will.","themes":["faith","love"],"note":"Cressida still remembers Troilus."},{"id":"1159","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"Troilus, farewell! One eye yet looks on thee; But with my heart the other eye doth see.","themes":["faith","love"],"note":"Cressida's divided sight."},{"id":"1159","who":"CRESSIDA","text":"The error of our eye directs our mind.","themes":["faith","love"],"note":"Cressida's own judgement."},{"id":"1161","who":"THERSITES","text":"Unless she said 'My mind is now turn'd whore.'","themes":["lust","faith"],"note":"Thersites' brutal summary."},{"id":"1165","who":"TROILUS","text":"Shall I not lie in publishing a truth?","themes":["faith"],"note":"Troilus cannot accept what he saw."},{"id":"1171","who":"TROILUS","text":"Think, we had mothers;","themes":["faith"],"note":"Troilus defends all women."},{"id":"1175","who":"TROILUS","text":"This she? No; this is Diomed's Cressida.","themes":["faith","love"],"note":"Troilus splits Cressida in two."},{"id":"1175","who":"TROILUS","text":"this is, and is not, Cressid.","themes":["faith"],"note":"Reason against itself."},{"id":"1175","who":"TROILUS","text":"The fractions of her faith, orts of her love,","themes":["faith","love","lust"],"note":"Love turned to leftovers."},{"id":"1179","who":"TROILUS","text":"O Cressid! O false Cressid! false, false, false!","themes":["faith"],"note":"Troilus's cry."},{"id":"1205","who":"HECTOR","text":"Life every man holds dear; but the dear man Holds honour far more precious dear than life.","themes":["war"],"note":"Hector's creed."},{"id":"1217","who":"TROILUS","text":"Let's leave the hermit Pity with our mother;","themes":["war"],"note":"Troilus turns savage."},{"id":"1233","who":"CASSANDRA","text":"Look how thou diest. Look how thy eye turns pale.","themes":["war"],"note":"Cassandra's vision."},{"id":"1240","who":"TROILUS","text":"I come to lose my arm or win my sleeve.","themes":["love","war"],"note":"Troilus's private war."},{"id":"1247","who":"TROILUS","text":"Words, words, mere words, no matter from the heart;","themes":["faith","love"],"note":"Troilus tears Cressida's letter."},{"id":"1253","who":"THERSITES","text":"policy grows into an ill opinion.","themes":["order","war"],"note":"Thersites on Ulysses' failed plan."},{"id":"1265","who":"THERSITES","text":"Yet, in a sort, lechery eats itself.","themes":["lust","order"],"note":"Thersites echoes the 'universal wolf'."},{"id":"1275","who":"NESTOR","text":"There is a thousand Hectors in the field;","themes":["war"],"note":"Hector at his greatest."},{"id":"1307","who":"ACHILLES","text":"I do disdain thy courtesy, proud Trojan.","themes":["war","pride"],"note":"Achilles refuses Hector's mercy."},{"id":"1328","who":"THERSITES","text":"if the son of a whore fight for a whore, he tempts judgement.","themes":["war","lust"],"note":"Thersites escapes the fight."},{"id":"1334","who":"HECTOR","text":"Most putrified core so fair without,","themes":["war","value"],"note":"Fine armour, rotten body."},{"id":"1338","who":"HECTOR","text":"I am unarm'd; forego this vantage, Greek.","themes":["war"],"note":"Hector appeals to fair play."},{"id":"1339","who":"ACHILLES","text":"Strike, fellows, strike; this is the man I seek.","themes":["war","pride"],"note":"Achilles' murder."},{"id":"1355","who":"AJAX","text":"Great Hector was as good a man as he.","themes":["war"],"note":"Ajax's honest tribute."},{"id":"1366","who":"TROILUS","text":"Hector is dead; there is no more to say.","themes":["war"],"note":"Troilus's grief."},{"id":"1366","who":"TROILUS","text":"I'll haunt thee like a wicked conscience still,","themes":["war"],"note":"Troilus's vow against Achilles."},{"id":"1369","who":"TROILUS","text":"Ignominy and shame Pursue thy life, and live aye with thy name!","themes":["faith","love"],"note":"Troilus curses Pandarus."},{"id":"1371","who":"PANDARUS","text":"Good traders in the flesh, set this in your painted cloths.","themes":["lust","value"],"note":"Pandarus addresses the audience."},{"id":"1371","who":"PANDARUS","text":"And at that time bequeath you my diseases.","themes":["lust"],"note":"The play's last words."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Seven years into the Trojan War, the Greeks are camped outside Troy, still trying to win back Helen, the wife of the Greek king Menelaus, whom the Trojan prince Paris carried off. In Troy, Priam's youngest son Troilus is in love with Cressida, daughter of the priest Calchas, who has defected to the Greeks. Her uncle Pandarus brings them together for one night, and they swear to be faithful: 'as true as Troilus', 'as false as Cressid'.","In the Greek camp, Ulysses blames the deadlock on the collapse of order: the great warrior Achilles sulks in his tent mocking the generals. When Hector challenges the Greeks to single combat, Ulysses rigs a lottery so that the slow-witted Ajax is chosen, to humble Achilles. In Troy, Hector argues that Helen should be returned, then agrees to keep her for the sake of honour. Calchas asks the Greeks to exchange a Trojan prisoner for Cressida, and the morning after the lovers' night she is handed over to the Greek Diomedes.","Hector and Ajax fight and embrace as cousins; Achilles promises to kill Hector the next day. That night Ulysses leads Troilus to Calchas's tent, where he watches Cressida give his love token to Diomedes. In the battle that follows, Troilus hunts Diomedes, Patroclus is killed, and Achilles, after being spared once by Hector, has his Myrmidons kill Hector while he is unarmed and drags the body behind his horse. Troilus vows revenge and curses Pandarus, who closes the play by leaving his diseases to the audience."],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The History of Troilus and Cressida (Quarto, 1609); The Tragedie of Troylus and Cressida (First Folio, 1623)"],["Written","About 1601 to 1602; printed in a Quarto in 1609"],["Genre","Tragedy (in the First Folio, where it stands unpaginated between the Histories and the Tragedies); often called a 'problem play' or satire"],["Setting","Troy and the Greek camp outside it, in the seventh year of the Trojan War"],["Protagonists","Troilus and Cressida; Hector in the war plot"],["Antagonists","Diomedes (in love); Achilles (in war); time and appetite"],["Inciting incident","Pandarus brings Troilus and Cressida together; Hector's challenge reaches the Greeks (1.3)"],["Turning point","Calchas asks for Cressida in exchange for Antenor (3.3), and she is handed over the morning after the lovers' night (4.2 to 4.4)"],["Climax","Troilus watches Cressida give his sleeve to Diomedes (5.2); Achilles' Myrmidons kill the unarmed Hector (5.8)"],["Time span","About four days, from the challenge to Hector's death"],["Major conflicts","Troy against Greece; love against war; Achilles against the generals; truth against falseness"],["Themes","Love and desire, truth and falseness, worth and value, war and honour, order and degree, pride and policy, time and reputation, lechery and disease"]],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"The armed Prologue sets the scene in the middle of the war","id":2},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Troilus is too lovesick for Cressida to fight","id":4},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Pandarus praises Troilus as the warriors pass","id":176},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Cressida admits in soliloquy that she loves Troilus","id":200},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Ulysses' speech on degree","id":208},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Aeneas delivers Hector's challenge","id":238},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Ulysses plans to rig the lottery for Ajax","id":257},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Ajax beats Thersites","id":268},{"s":"a2s2","t":"The Trojans debate whether to return Helen","id":353},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Cassandra prophesies Troy's burning","id":364},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Hector agrees to keep Helen","id":371},{"s":"a2s3","t":"The generals flatter Ajax against Achilles","id":482},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Pandarus sings of love to Paris and Helen","id":563},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Troilus and Cressida swear to be true","id":638},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Calchas asks for Cressida in exchange for Antenor","id":650},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Ulysses' speech on Time","id":683},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Diomedes calls Helen a whore","id":754},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Aeneas brings news of the exchange","id":797},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Cressida swears she will not leave Troy","id":815},{"s":"a4s4","t":"The lovers exchange sleeve and glove","id":854},{"s":"a4s4","t":"Troilus hands Cressida to Diomedes","id":870},{"s":"a4s5","t":"The Greek generals kiss Cressida","id":898},{"s":"a4s5","t":"Hector and Ajax fight and embrace","id":955},{"s":"a4s5","t":"Achilles looks Hector over and threatens him","id":993},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Achilles reads Hecuba's letter and decides not to fight","id":1030},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Cressida gives Diomedes the sleeve","id":1126},{"s":"a5s2","t":"Troilus: 'This is, and is not, Cressid'","id":1175},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Hector ignores the warnings and goes to battle","id":1205},{"s":"a5s8","t":"Achilles' Myrmidons kill the unarmed Hector","id":1339},{"s":"a5s10","t":"Troilus curses Pandarus, who speaks the epilogue","id":1369}],"irony":{"176":"Pandarus praises Troilus as 'admirable'; Cressida already loves him and is teasing.","200":"Cressida's theory that men stop valuing what they win will be tested on her, not on Troilus.","208":"Ulysses' praise of order comes from the most cunning manipulator in the camp.","442":"Ajax claims to hate pride while swelling with it.","558":"Helen jokes that 'This love will undo us all': her love is in fact destroying Troy.","612":"Troilus promises to be judged on performance just before the lovers' first night.","619":"Pandarus says his family are burs who stick where they are thrown; Cressida will stick to the next man.","637":"Troilus imagines future poets citing him as truth itself; they will, but only as the betrayed lover.","638":"Cressida swears that if she is false her name will be a byword; the audience knows it already is.","650":"Calchas arranges the exchange as a fatherly favour, without asking his daughter.","800":"Troilus's 'How my achievements mock me!' comes the morning after his greatest success.","815":"Cressida swears never to leave Troilus within minutes of learning she must.","862":"Troilus's warning that 'we are devils to ourselves' describes exactly what Cressida will be.","870":"Troilus asks Diomedes to treat Cressida well, handing her to the man who will seduce her.","983":"Hector trusts that Time will end the war well; it will end his life within a day.","1030":"Achilles keeps a vow to Polyxena, then breaks every rule of chivalry to kill her brother.","1205":"Hector's honour drives him to battle, where he dies through Achilles' dishonour.","1334":"Hector condemns the Greek who was 'rotten' inside fine armour; he has just killed him for that armour."},"devices":{"trade":"Trade, buying and selling","food":"Food, taste and appetite","disease":"Disease and sickness","war":"Battle, weapons and wounds","music":"Music and order","time":"Time and oblivion"},"dev":{"7":[["food","Courtship as baking a cake.","He that will have a cake out of the wheat must tarry the grinding."]],"18":[["war","Praise as a knife in a wound.","The knife that made it."]],"33":[["trade","Cressida as a pearl, Troilus as a merchant.","Her bed is India; there she lies, a pearl;"],["war","Helen's beauty painted with blood.","When with your blood you daily paint her thus."]],"204":[["disease","The army's sickness.","What grief hath set these jaundies o'er your cheeks?"]],"208":[["music","Order as a tuned string.","Take but degree away, untune that string,"],["food","Appetite as a wolf that eats itself.","And appetite, an universal wolf,"],["disease","Rivalry as a fever.","grows to an envious fever"]],"355":[["trade","Helen as a pearl that launched ships and made kings merchants.","Whose price hath launch'd above a thousand ships,"],["trade","Soiled silks returned to the merchant.","We turn not back the silks upon the merchant"]],"377":[["disease","War cursed with syphilis.","the Neapolitan bone-ache!"]],"448":[["disease","Pride as plague.","He is so plaguy proud that the death tokens of it"]],"563":[["music","Love's song mixes dying and laughter.","Doth turn O ho! to ha! ha! he!"]],"591":[["food","Love as nectar to be tasted.","Love's thrice-repured nectar?"]],"610":[["food","Lovers vow to eat rocks.","eat rocks, tame tigers;"]],"650":[["trade","Cressida bought with a prisoner.","And he shall buy my daughter;"]],"683":[["time","Time's bag of alms for oblivion.","Time hath, my lord, a wallet at his back,"],["time","Time as a fashionable host.","For Time is like a fashionable host,"]],"752":[["food","Helen as the dregs of a barrel.","The lees and dregs of a flat tamed piece;"]],"755":[["trade","Diomedes as a buyer running down goods.","Dispraise the thing that you desire to buy;"]],"842":[["time","Time as a robber.","Injurious time now with a robber's haste"],["food","A famished kiss.","And scants us with a single famish'd kiss,"]],"993":[["war","Achilles chooses where to wound Hector.","Shall I destroy him? Whether there, or there, or there?"]],"1023":[["disease","A catalogue of diseases.","the guts-griping ruptures,"]],"1075":[["music","Cressida as a piece of music to be sung.","And any man may sing her, if he can take her cliff;"]],"1116":[["food","Lechery frying.","Fry, lechery, fry!"]],"1175":[["food","Cressida's love as leftovers.","The fragments, scraps, the bits, and greasy relics"]],"1265":[["food","Lechery eating itself.","lechery eats itself."]],"1334":[["disease","A rotten body inside fine armour.","Most putrified core so fair without,"]],"1345":[["food","Achilles' sword half-fed.","My half-supp'd sword, that frankly would have fed,"]],"1371":[["disease","Pandarus's diseases left to the audience.","And at that time bequeath you my diseases."]]},"stage":{"palace":"Before Priam's palace in Troy","room":"Inside Priam's palace","street":"A street in Troy","house":"Pandarus's house","orchard":"Pandarus's orchard","camp":"The Greek camp","tent":"Before a tent in the Greek camp","lists":"The lists set out for the combat","field":"The plain between Troy and the Greek camp"},"stagerules":[["before priam","palace"],["priam's palace","room"],["orchard","orchard"],["street","street"],["pandarus' house","house"],["lists","lists"],["tent","tent"],["camp","camp"],["plain","field"]],"place":{"a1s1":"Troy, before Priam's palace","a1s2":"A street in Troy","a1s3":"The Greek camp, before Agamemnon's tent","a2s1":"The Greek camp","a2s2":"Priam's palace","a2s3":"Before Achilles' tent","a3s1":"Priam's palace","a3s2":"Pandarus's orchard","a3s3":"The Greek camp, before Achilles' tent","a4s1":"A street in Troy, before dawn","a4s2":"The court of Pandarus's house","a4s3":"A street before Pandarus's house","a4s4":"Pandarus's house","a4s5":"The Greek camp, the lists","a5s1":"Before Achilles' tent, at night","a5s2":"Before Calchas's tent","a5s3":"Troy, before Priam's palace","a5s4":"The battlefield","a5s5":"The battlefield","a5s6":"The battlefield","a5s7":"The battlefield","a5s8":"The battlefield, at sunset","a5s9":"The battlefield","a5s10":"The battlefield, at nightfall"},"arc":{"Troilus's faith":[8,8,8,8,9,9,9,10,10,10,10,10,9,9,8,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,1,0],"Cressida's faith":[5,6,6,6,6,6,6,9,9,9,10,10,9,6,5,2,2,2,1,1,1,1,1,1]},"arcnote":"Troilus's faith in Cressida starts high and climbs to its peak in the vows of 3.2 and the night that follows. It barely dips when he keeps telling her to 'be true' in 4.4, but it collapses in one scene, 5.2, when he watches her give his sleeve to Diomedes, and it never recovers: he tears up her letter and turns to revenge. Cressida's faith is hidden at first behind her wit, rises to its height when she swears to be true and refuses to leave Troy, then drops the moment she is passed from kiss to kiss in the Greek camp. By 5.2 she has given in to Diomedes, though her last speech shows one eye still on Troilus.","iago":[["a1s1","Troilus confesses he is lovesick for Cressida and depends on Pandarus to win her."],["a1s2","Cressida admits in soliloquy that she loves Troilus but will hold him off."],["a3s2","Pandarus brings them together; they swear 'as true as Troilus' and 'as false as Cressid' and go to bed."],["a3s3","Calchas asks the Greeks to exchange the prisoner Antenor for his daughter; Agamemnon sends Diomedes."],["a4s1","Diomedes arrives in Troy with Antenor; Paris sends Aeneas to warn Troilus."],["a4s2","The morning after, Aeneas brings the news; Cressida swears she will never leave Troilus."],["a4s3","Troilus goes to hand her over, calling himself a priest offering his heart."],["a4s4","The lovers exchange sleeve and glove; Troilus begs her to be true; Diomedes refuses to take his orders."],["a4s5","The Greek generals kiss Cressida on arrival; Ulysses calls her one of the 'daughters of the game'. Troilus asks to be taken to her."],["a5s1","Diomedes leaves Achilles' tent early; Ulysses and Troilus follow his torch."],["a5s2","Cressida gives Diomedes Troilus's sleeve and agrees to see him; Troilus watches and swears to kill Diomedes."],["a5s3","Troilus tears up Cressida's letter: 'Words, words, mere words'."],["a5s4","Diomedes wears the sleeve in battle; Troilus chases him."],["a5s5","Diomedes sends Troilus's captured horse to Cressida as a trophy."],["a5s6","Troilus fights Diomedes and Ajax together, calling Diomedes a traitor."],["a5s10","Troilus curses Pandarus, the go-between, with lasting shame."]],"rel":{"1":[["TROILUS","CRESSIDA","love"],["PANDARUS","TROILUS","trust"],["CRESSIDA","TROILUS","love"],["ULYSSES","ACHILLES","deceive"],["ACHILLES","AGAMEMNON","hate"],["PATROCLUS","ACHILLES","love"],["HECTOR","AJAX","hate"]],"2":[["THERSITES","AJAX","hate"],["AJAX","THERSITES","hate"],["TROILUS","HECTOR","trust"],["HECTOR","PARIS","trust"],["CASSANDRA","PARIS","hate"],["ULYSSES","AJAX","deceive"],["ACHILLES","PATROCLUS","love"]],"3":[["TROILUS","CRESSIDA","love"],["CRESSIDA","TROILUS","love"],["PANDARUS","CRESSIDA","trust"],["PARIS","HELEN","love"],["CALCHAS","CRESSIDA","trust"],["ULYSSES","ACHILLES","deceive"],["ACHILLES","HECTOR","hate"]],"4":[["TROILUS","CRESSIDA","love"],["TROILUS","DIOMEDES","hate"],["DIOMEDES","CRESSIDA","love"],["HECTOR","AJAX","love"],["ACHILLES","HECTOR","hate"],["ULYSSES","CRESSIDA","hate"],["AENEAS","DIOMEDES","hate"]],"5":[["CRESSIDA","DIOMEDES","love"],["CRESSIDA","TROILUS","deceive"],["TROILUS","DIOMEDES","hate"],["ACHILLES","HECTOR","hate"],["ANDROMACHE","HECTOR","love"],["TROILUS","PANDARUS","hate"],["THERSITES","DIOMEDES","hate"]]},"context":[["Date","Probably written around 1601 to 1602, between Hamlet and Othello. It was entered for printing in 1603 and published in a Quarto in 1609, whose preface claims it was 'never staled with the stage'."],["The First Folio","The editors of the 1623 Folio seem to have had trouble placing it. It was printed late, squeezed in between the Histories and the Tragedies, mostly without page numbers and missing from the contents list. Its Folio title calls it a tragedy."],["Sources","The love story comes from Chaucer's Troilus and Criseyde and Robert Henryson's Testament of Cresseid, where Cressida ends as a leper. The war plot draws on Homer's Iliad (in George Chapman's translation, published from 1598), Caxton's Recuyell of the Histories of Troy and John Lydgate's Troy Book."],["The legend","By 1600, Troilus, Cressida and Pandarus were proverbial: a faithful lover, a false woman and a go-between. The play's oaths ('as true as Troilus', 'as false as Cressid', 'all brokers between Pandars') show the characters stepping into their own legends."],["Shakespeare's changes","Unlike the Iliad, Hector is killed by a gang of Myrmidons while unarmed, not in single combat. Unlike Chaucer, the lovers have one night, not three years. Achilles is lazy and vain, Ajax a fool, and Thersites given a large role."],["Satire","The play belongs to a fashion for satire around 1600. Thersites' railing and the debunking of heroes recall the satirical comedies of Ben Jonson and John Marston, with whom Shakespeare's company was in a 'war of the theatres'. The armed Prologue may be a joke at Jonson's armed prologue in Poetaster."],["Problem play","Critics since the 1890s have grouped it with All's Well and Measure for Measure as a 'problem play': hard to classify, morally uncomfortable, without a satisfying ending."],["Order and degree","Ulysses' 'degree' speech echoes Elizabethan sermons on obedience and the idea of a great chain of being. In the play it is used as a political tool, and the order it praises is nowhere to be seen."],["The Earl of Essex","Some critics link the sulking Achilles to the Earl of Essex, a famous general who fell from favour and rebelled in 1601. Chapman dedicated his Homer to Essex as an 'Achilles'."],["Audience","The Quarto preface and the bawdy epilogue suggest the play may have been written for a learned audience, perhaps at the Inns of Court, the London law schools."],["Stage history","Rarely performed until the twentieth century. After the World Wars, directors found its disillusioned view of war modern, and it is now often staged."],["Chivalry","Hector's challenge, the sleeve and the lists come from medieval romance, not ancient Greece. The play mixes Homer's world with the language of knights and ladies, and shows both falling short."]],"critics":{"faith":[["Joyce Carol Oates, 'The Ambiguity of Troilus and Cressida' (1966)","Paraphrased: Oates argued that the play shows people trapped in a world where values can't be fixed, so that Cressida's change is less a moral failure than a sign of a world without stable truth."],["Gayle Greene, 'Shakespeare's Cressida: A Kind of Self' (1980)","Paraphrased: Greene argued that Cressida becomes what each man wants her to be, because her society gives a woman no self of her own outside men's valuation."]],"value":[["Bernard Shaw, preface (1898)","Paraphrased: Shaw praised Cressida as Shakespeare's first real woman, an unromantic study of a clever, practical girl."],["Frank Kermode, 'Shakespeare's Language' (2000)","Paraphrased: Kermode traced the play's obsession with value, opinion and price, especially in the Trojan council, and its difficult, Latinate vocabulary."]],"war":[["Jan Kott, 'Shakespeare Our Contemporary' (1964)","Paraphrased: Kott read the play as a bitter modern view of war, where heroism is a sham and Thersites tells the truth."],["Kenneth Muir, introduction to the Oxford edition (1982)","Paraphrased: Muir argued that the play debunks the heroic myth without simply being cynical, since Hector and Troilus still hold real values."]],"order":[["E. M. W. Tillyard, 'The Elizabethan World Picture' (1943)","Paraphrased: Tillyard took Ulysses' degree speech as a summary of Elizabethan belief in cosmic order."],["Terry Eagleton, 'William Shakespeare' (1986)","Paraphrased: Eagleton argued that the play exposes order and value as products of exchange and desire, not fixed realities."]],"lust":[["G. Wilson Knight, 'The Wheel of Fire' (1930)","Paraphrased: Knight saw the play as a contest between Trojan intuition and idealism and Greek reason and cynicism, both corrupted by lust and disease."],["Caroline Spurgeon, 'Shakespeare's Imagery' (1935)","Paraphrased: Spurgeon showed how images of food, taste and disease run through the play, linking love to appetite."]],"time":[["L. C. Knights, 'Troilus and Cressida Again' (1951)","Paraphrased: Knights argued the play is about the way time and appetite undermine every value its characters try to hold."],["A. P. Rossiter, 'Angel with Horns' (1961)","Paraphrased: Rossiter called it a tragicomedy of disillusion, in which time mocks every human aim."]]},"symbols":{"Value":"\\bvalu\\w*|\\bworth\\w*|\\bprice\\w*","True":"\\btrue\\b|\\btruth\\w*","Time":"\\btime\\b","Honour":"\\bhonour\\w*","Lechery":"\\blecher\\w*|\\bwhore\\w*|\\bluxury\\b","Pride":"\\bproud\\b|\\bpride\\b"},"symbols2":[["The sleeve","Troilus's love token, which Cressida gives to Diomedes and he wears on his helmet in battle: faith turned into a trophy."],["Helen","The 'pearl' the war is fought over, who appears only once, idle and flirting: worth created by the price paid for her."],["The armour","The 'goodly armour' Hector chases and wins, finding a 'putrified core' inside: glory with nothing inside it."],["Achilles' tent","Where the greatest warrior sulks: pride and idleness at the centre of the Greek camp."],["Troilus's horse","Captured by Diomedes and sent to Cressida: love reduced to spoils of war."],["The lists","The arena for the chivalric duel of Hector and Ajax, which ends in an embrace, not a victory."]],"solo":{"33":1,"200":1,"377":1,"591":1,"1032":1,"1058":1,"1159":1,"1253":1,"1371":1},"why":["Sets the play's anti-heroic tone: no beginning, no glorious end.","Treats the whole war as a gamble with no meaning.","Reduces the cause of the war to adultery from the very first speech.","Shows love and war competing for Troilus from his first lines.","Shows Troilus measuring love against manhood.","Introduces Pandarus's earthy, practical view of love.","A couplet that foreshadows how quickly happiness will turn.","Shows Troilus's love built from poetic convention.","Shows how talk of Cressida makes Troilus suffer.","Questions the value of Helen in the play's first scene.","Troilus calls the war's cause unworthy, before he defends it in 2.2.","Establishes Pandarus as essential to the love plot.","Introduces the play's language of trade and treasure in love.","Begins the running joke about Menelaus as a cuckold.","A comic portrait that prepares us for Ajax as a mass of contradictions.","Shows Pandarus marketing Troilus to Cressida.","Pandarus's enthusiasm turns praise into salesmanship.","Treats a man's qualities like a recipe of ingredients.","Shows Cressida's wit as self-defence in a world of men.","Cressida's key statement of how she thinks desire works.","Explains why Cressida delays: value falls once a thing is won.","Shows Cressida sees love as a contest of power.","Shows the Greek army as sick and dispirited.","Agamemnon's grand excuse that hides real failure.","Nestor's sea image glorifies hardship.","Ulysses' diagnosis: the problem is disorder, not fate.","The most quoted lines: order is music, its loss is discord.","Appetite becomes the play's image of self-destroying desire.","Predicts the ending, where appetite, lust and violence consume all.","A paradox that sums up the stalemate.","Links political order to the order of the heavens.","Shows how fame has spoiled Achilles.","Shows the generals mocked as actors in a cheap play.","Achilles 'dies' laughing: heroism turned to farce.","Shows the conflict between thinkers and fighters among the Greeks.","Aeneas warns against self-praise, a theme Ajax will ignore.","Hector's challenge uses the language of chivalric love.","The challenge's insult makes women the stake in men's fights.","Comic old-age bravado that shows chivalry as a game.","Ulysses' plan in a single proverb.","States the aim of the Ajax plan.","Shows the generals using pride as a weapon.","A typical Thersites insult: heroes as beasts.","Thersites mocks Ajax's lack of brains.","Thersites turns Ajax upside down.","Thersites links Ajax's stupidity to the war's cause, Helen.","Shows the heroes as beasts of burden for the planners.","Hector's wise view that doubt is safer than certainty.","Hector's plain advice, which he will soon abandon.","Troilus attacks reason as cowardice.","Hector's argument from cost.","The play's central question about value.","Hector's answer: value must be real, not imagined.","Turns Helen into a commodity and echoes Marlowe's Faustus.","Troilus's honour argument shames his brothers into keeping Helen.","Cassandra blames Paris for Troy's coming destruction.","Cassandra's stark choice, ignored by all.","Priam's sharp comment on Paris's selfishness.","Hector condemns passion as deaf to justice.","Hector's argument from the law of nature.","Hector's reversal, which seals Troy's fate.","Troilus turns Helen into a symbol of glory.","Thersites sees the war as a sexual disease.","Thersites' syllogism reduces everyone to fools.","The play's most cynical summary of the war.","Thersites' refrain that sums up the play's two plots.","Ulysses on fragile alliances.","Shows Achilles' pride as an inability to bend.","Agamemnon's moral about pride, ignored by all.","Ajax condemns pride while showing it.","Points out Ajax's self-love.","Pride as a fatal disease.","Ulysses comments on Ajax's hypocrisy.","Agamemnon's image explains the choice of Ajax.","Helen's unconscious prophecy of Troy's ruin.","The song turns dying into sexual climax.","Paris reduces love to heat and lust.","Pandarus's joke makes love poisonous.","Troilus's excitement before meeting Cressida.","Troilus idealises love as divine.","Love as a fever, sensual and dangerous.","Troilus dismisses fear as distortion.","Cressida's cautious realism.","Troilus on the gap between desire and performance.","Cressida doubts men's vows.","Troilus's promise to be judged by results.","Troilus's pride in his own constancy.","Pandarus's guarantee, which proves false.","Cressida's frank confession of love.","Cressida's self-reproach for revealing her feelings.","Cressida feels divided between herself and Troilus.","Cressida's view that love and wisdom cannot coexist.","Troilus doubts a woman's love can last.","Troilus defines himself by simple truth.","Troilus's prophecy, which the legend fulfils.","Cressida imagines Troy's ruin, as the play's future.","Cressida's oath, which history fulfils.","Fixes the three names for all time.","Pandarus's coarse blessing on the lovers.","Calchas reminds us he is a traitor, as his daughter will be.","Cressida bought and sold like goods.","Ulysses' plan: use pride to cure pride.","Achilles learns the fickleness of men.","Reputation depends on reflection by others.","Ulysses' jab at Achilles' idleness.","The famous opening of the Time speech.","Honour must be renewed by action.","The famous line about human love of novelty.","Reputation lasts only while seen.","Time as an inconstant host.","Ulysses' parting insult to Achilles.","Patroclus urges Achilles to shake off love.","Achilles feels his reputation threatened.","Patroclus warns against self-inflicted harm.","Thersites mocks reputation as a reversible jacket.","Achilles admits his confusion.","Thersites' contempt for Ajax.","Love and killing joined in chivalry.","Mock-courtesy hiding real hatred.","Diomedes' cynical view of Menelaus.","Diomedes counts Helen's cost in lives.","Paris accuses Diomedes of bargaining.","Night flies too fast for lovers.","Cressida's fear of being left.","Cressida regrets yielding too soon.","Troilus loses what he has just won.","Cressida's oath, soon broken.","Cressida's grief seems pure and total.","Troilus blames jealous gods for the parting.","Time as a robber.","A hungry, stinted parting.","Troilus's repeated plea betrays his fear.","Cressida resents Troilus's doubt.","Troilus fears temptation hidden in social graces.","Troilus foresees human weakness.","Troilus as the simple, honest man.","Diomedes defies Troilus and hints at his intentions.","Ulysses reads Cressida's body.","Ulysses' harsh verdict on Cressida.","Aeneas presents Hector as modest.","Ulysses praises Troilus's modesty.","Hector chooses kinship over combat.","Agamemnon sees only the present moment.","Hector's trust that time will decide.","Achilles studies Hector like a butcher.","Hector's boast, which he regrets.","Troilus's sad proverb about love and fortune.","Thersites' disease catalogue.","Achilles chooses a private vow over the war.","Thersites' verdict on the whole camp.","Thersites on Cressida's reputation.","Thersites' coarse commentary on the lovers.","Troilus watches the sleeve given away.","Cressida half resists giving the sleeve.","Cressida still remembers Troilus.","Cressida's divided sight.","Cressida's own judgement of her change.","Thersites' brutal summary.","Troilus cannot believe his eyes.","Troilus defends all women against Cressida's example.","Troilus splits Cressida in two.","Reason divided against itself.","Love reduced to leftovers.","Troilus's cry of betrayal.","Hector's creed of honour above life.","Troilus turns savage.","Cassandra's vision of Hector's death.","Troilus's private war over the sleeve.","Troilus dismisses Cressida's words.","Thersites on the failure of Ulysses' plan.","Thersites echoes the 'universal wolf'.","Hector at the height of his power.","Achilles refuses Hector's mercy.","Thersites refuses to fight for a whore.","Fine armour, rotten body: an image of the whole war.","Hector appeals to fair play, and is refused.","Achilles' cowardly murder of Hector.","Ajax's honest tribute to Hector.","Troilus's grief has no words left.","Troilus's vow of revenge.","Troilus's final curse on Pandarus.","Pandarus addresses the audience as fellow bawds.","The play's last words leave us with disease."],"insults":[{"id":"270","f":"thou mongrel beef-witted lord!","who":"Thersites","n":"Thersites calls Ajax a mixed-breed with beef for brains."},{"id":"271","f":"thou unsalted leaven","who":"Ajax","n":"Ajax calls Thersites tasteless, sour dough."},{"id":"277","f":"porpentine","who":"Ajax","n":"Ajax calls Thersites a porcupine for his prickly tongue."},{"id":"283","f":"Cobloaf!","who":"Ajax","n":"A lumpy loaf: Thersites' deformed body."},{"id":"288","f":"Thou stool for a witch!","who":"Ajax","n":"A seat for a witch, a degrading object."},{"id":"289","f":"thou sodden-witted lord!","who":"Thersites","n":"Thersites says Ajax's brain is waterlogged."},{"id":"294","f":"Mars his idiot!","who":"Thersites","n":"Ajax as the war god's fool."},{"id":"335","f":"Achilles' brach","who":"Thersites","n":"Patroclus as Achilles' bitch hound, suggesting their relationship."},{"id":"380","f":"thou art a fair corse","who":"Thersites","n":"Thersites wishes Patroclus dead and diseased."},{"id":"393","f":"Patroclus is a fool.","who":"Thersites","n":"Thersites' logic ends in an insult."},{"id":"405","f":"All the argument is a whore and a cuckold","who":"Thersites","n":"Thersites insults the whole war."},{"id":"460","f":"The raven chides blackness.","who":"Ulysses","n":"Ulysses calls Ajax a hypocrite in an aside."},{"id":"476","f":"A whoreson dog","who":"Ajax","n":"Ajax on Achilles."},{"id":"752","f":"He like a puling cuckold","who":"Diomedes","n":"Diomedes insults Menelaus and Paris equally."},{"id":"754","f":"her bawdy veins","who":"Diomedes","n":"Diomedes calls Helen a whore to her lover's face."},{"id":"1014","f":"Thou crusty batch of nature","who":"Achilles","n":"Achilles calls Thersites a burnt loaf."},{"id":"1015","f":"idol of idiot worshippers","who":"Thersites","n":"Thersites says only fools admire Achilles."},{"id":"1023","f":"his masculine whore","who":"Thersites","n":"Thersites' sneer at Patroclus."},{"id":"1026","f":"you ruinous butt","who":"Patroclus","n":"Patroclus calls Thersites a broken barrel."},{"id":"1027","f":"thou green sarcenet flap for a sore eye","who":"Thersites","n":"Patroclus as a flimsy silk eye-patch."},{"id":"1029","f":"Finch egg!","who":"Thersites","n":"Something small and worthless."},{"id":"1032","f":"he has not so much brain as ear-wax","who":"Thersites","n":"Thersites on Agamemnon."},{"id":"1369","f":"Hence, broker-lackey.","who":"Troilus","n":"Troilus dismisses Pandarus as a pimp's servant."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"18","f":"I tell thee, Pandarus,","n":"Troilus uses intimate 'thee' to his friend and go-between."},{"id":"24","f":"art thou angry, Pandarus? What! with me?","n":"Troilus's 'thou' pleads friendship."},{"id":"604","f":"What should they grant?","n":"Troilus speaks to Cressida with gentle formality before their night."},{"id":"622","f":"Hard to seem won; but I was won, my lord,","n":"Cressida keeps the respectful 'you' and 'my lord' with Troilus."},{"id":"850","f":"Be thou but true of heart.","n":"Troilus switches to intimate 'thou' when he pleads."},{"id":"1071","f":"Now, my sweet guardian! Hark, a word with you.","n":"Cressida's playful formality with Diomedes."},{"id":"1150","f":"Thou never shalt mock Diomed again.","n":"Diomedes shifts to 'thou' to threaten her."},{"id":"1307","f":"I do disdain thy courtesy, proud Trojan.","n":"Achilles' contemptuous 'thy' to Hector."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Troilus will win Cressida with Pandarus's help","Pandarus sulks, but goes on working for Troilus"],"a1s2":["Cressida will accept Troilus at once","She hides her love to keep its value"],"a1s3":["Achilles will answer Hector's challenge","Ulysses rigs a lottery for Ajax"],"a2s1":["Ajax and Achilles will quarrel over the challenge","The lottery is announced; Thersites storms off"],"a2s2":["The Trojans will return Helen","Hector argues for it, then agrees to keep her"],"a2s3":["Achilles will meet the generals","He refuses, and the generals flatter Ajax"],"a3s1":["Paris will discover the lovers' secret","He guesses, but agrees to cover for Troilus"],"a3s2":["The lovers will be parted at once","They swear their vows and go to bed"],"a3s3":["Achilles will agree to fight Hector","He is shaken, but only invites Hector to his tent"],"a4s1":["Troilus will refuse the exchange","Paris sends warning; the exchange goes ahead"],"a4s2":["Cressida will refuse to go","She swears she won't, but she must"],"a4s3":["Troilus will hide Cressida","He fetches her for the Greeks himself"],"a4s4":["Troilus will challenge Diomedes now","They exchange tokens, and Troilus hands her over"],"a4s5":["Hector will kill Ajax","They stop as cousins and embrace"],"a5s1":["Achilles will fight Hector tomorrow","A letter from Hecuba stops him"],"a5s2":["Cressida will stay faithful","She gives Diomedes Troilus's sleeve"],"a5s3":["Hector will listen to the warnings","He goes to battle anyway"],"a5s4":["Troilus will kill Diomedes","They fight and are separated"],"a5s5":["Achilles will stay in his tent","Patroclus's death brings him out"],"a5s6":["Achilles will kill Hector in a fair fight","Hector spares him; Achilles leaves"],"a5s7":["Thersites will be killed","He talks his way out"],"a5s8":["Hector will escape","Achilles' men kill him unarmed"],"a5s9":["The Greeks will storm Troy","They hear of Hector's death and halt for the night"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Troilus and Diomedes","pts":["Troilus idealises love; Diomedes bargains for it.","Troilus says he cannot sing or dance; Diomedes needs neither.","Troilus begs Cressida to be true; Diomedes threatens to leave.","Both wear Cressida's tokens: a glove and a sleeve."],"ids":["612","873","1094","1147"]},{"t":"Hector and Achilles","pts":["Hector fights by chivalric rules; Achilles ignores them.","Hector spares Achilles; Achilles kills Hector unarmed.","Both break vows: Hector his reasoned view on Helen, Achilles his vow to Polyxena.","Each is a greatest warrior admired by his side."],"ids":["955","993","1306","1339"]},{"t":"Ulysses and Thersites","pts":["Both see the camp as sick and disordered.","Ulysses speaks in grand verse; Thersites in prose curses.","Ulysses believes policy can fix things; Thersites sees policy fail.","Both watch the scene at Calchas's tent."],"ids":["208","405","1098","1253"]},{"t":"Cressida and Helen","pts":["Both are women exchanged between Greeks and Trojans.","Helen is valued by the war; Cressida is traded by it.","Both are judged as whores by Greek men.","Neither has a say in where she goes."],"ids":["355","650","754","926"]},{"t":"Troilus and Hector","pts":["In the council, Troilus argues for honour and Hector for reason.","Troilus is 'more dangerous' than Hector in battle.","Troilus scorns Hector's mercy: 'Fool's play'.","Hector dies; Troilus lives on to revenge."],"ids":["353","944","1215","1366"]}],"analysis":{"TROILUS":["Troilus is a romantic idealist. He opens the play disarmed by love, comparing Cressida to a pearl in India and himself to a merchant, and dreads that the real thing will be too much for his senses. He believes passionately in his own truth: 'I am as true as truth's simplicity'. In the council he argues that Helen's value is whatever honour gives her: 'What's aught but as 'tis valued?'","His idealism is also his weakness. He makes Cressida into an image, and when she changes he cannot at first believe his eyes: 'This is, and is not, Cressid'. He repeats 'be true' so often that Cressida takes offence, as if part of him expects betrayal.","After 5.2 the lover becomes a killer. He mocks Hector's mercy, tears Cressida's letter, chases Diomedes for his sleeve, and after Hector's death vows to haunt Achilles 'like a wicked conscience'. His final act is to curse Pandarus. Ulysses' early praise ('more dangerous' than Hector) proves true.","Key quotes: 'Her bed is India; there she lies, a pearl'; 'What's aught but as 'tis valued?'; 'As true as Troilus'; 'This is, and is not, Cressid'; 'Words, words, mere words'."],"CRESSIDA":["Cressida is witty, guarded and realistic. She spars with Pandarus and refuses to show her feelings, explaining in soliloquy that 'Men prize the thing ungain'd more than it is'. She knows that in her world a woman's value depends on what men think of her.","When she yields she is honest and frightened at once: 'Why have I blabb'd?' She swears a terrible oath, that if she is false her name will mean falseness. She is then traded for a prisoner without being asked, the morning after her first night with Troilus.","In the Greek camp she is kissed by the generals and has no protector but Diomedes. She gives him Troilus's sleeve, tries to take it back, and admits her own weakness: 'The error of our eye directs our mind'. Critics disagree whether she is a wanton, a survivor or a victim; the play gives her no last word.","Key quotes: 'Women are angels, wooing'; 'Achievement is command; ungain'd, beseech'; 'As false as Cressid'; 'He that takes that doth take my heart withal'; 'Minds sway'd by eyes are full of turpitude'."],"PANDARUS":["Pandarus is a comic go-between who enjoys other people's love affairs. He sells Troilus to Cressida like a market trader and makes bawdy jokes about the lovers' night.","His name becomes a word for pimp, as he predicts in 3.2. By the end he is old, sick and rejected: Troilus curses him, and in the epilogue he speaks directly to the 'traders in the flesh' in the audience.","Key quotes: 'He that will have a cake out of the wheat must tarry the grinding'; 'all brokers between Pandars'; 'Good traders in the flesh'."],"ULYSSES":["Ulysses is the play's great orator and schemer. His degree speech describes a perfectly ordered universe; his Time speech warns Achilles that reputation must be constantly renewed. Both speeches are brilliant, and both are instruments of policy.","His plot to raise Ajax and humble Achilles succeeds only in making Ajax proud too. It is Patroclus's death, not policy, that brings Achilles to fight. He leads Troilus to the truth about Cressida and judges her harshly as a 'daughter of the game'.","Key quotes: 'Take but degree away, untune that string'; 'appetite, an universal wolf'; 'One touch of nature makes the whole world kin'."],"HECTOR":["Hector is the noblest figure in the play: courteous, merciful and brave. In the council he argues clearly that Helen should be returned, then abandons his argument for honour's sake.","He stops fighting Ajax because they are cousins, spares Achilles, and sets out to battle despite every warning. His desire for a fine suit of armour leads him away alone, and he is killed unarmed by Achilles' Myrmidons. His death shows a world where chivalry has no place.","Key quotes: 'Let Helen go'; 'Life every man holds dear; but the dear man / Holds honour far more precious dear than life'; 'Most putrified core so fair without'."],"ACHILLES":["Achilles is the Greeks' greatest warrior and their biggest problem. He sulks in his tent, laughing at Patroclus's imitations of the generals, and is secretly in love with Polyxena.","Ulysses' snub unsettles him, and he admits his mind is 'troubled, like a fountain stirr'd'. He stares at Hector like a buyer, boasts where he will kill him, and finally has his men kill him unarmed. He then claims the glory. He is the play's sharpest attack on the heroic ideal.","Key quotes: 'My mind is troubled, like a fountain stirr'd'; 'in which part of his body / Shall I destroy him?'; 'Strike, fellows, strike'."],"THERSITES":["Thersites is a deformed, railing commentator who sees nothing in the war but lust and folly. 'All the argument is a whore and a cuckold.' He curses everyone with diseases and calls himself a bastard and a rascal to avoid fighting.","He often says what the action shows: Ulysses' policy fails, lechery eats itself, the heroes are fools. Some readers treat him as the play's truth-teller; others as a voice of pure spite. Either way, he leaves no hero standing.","Key quotes: 'war and lechery confound all!'; 'Lechery, lechery! Still wars and lechery!'; 'policy grows into an ill opinion'."]},"motifs":[["Buying and selling","Love and war are described as trade: Cressida is a pearl, Helen launches ships, Diomedes bargains, Pandarus is a broker.",[12,53,100,121,181]],["Appetite and food","Love is a cake, nectar, a famished kiss; Ulysses' wolf eats itself; Cressida's love is leftovers.",[5,27,79,130,162]],["Disease","The camp is sick with jaundice and fever; Thersites curses everyone with plague and syphilis; Pandarus ends with his diseases.",[22,62,71,147,182]],["Truth and oaths","The lovers swear oaths that the legend already knows will be broken.",[93,94,96,126,163]],["Time","Time is a robber, a fashionable host, a beggar with a sack of oblivion.",[105,109,129,142,143]],["Looking and watching","Characters are always watching others: the parade, the lists, Calchas's tent.",[15,137,144,155,156]]],"bigq":[["Is Cressida a villain or a victim?",["She swears to be true and betrays Troilus within a day","She is traded without consent and left without protection","She half resists and regrets her change","Ulysses and Thersites judge her before she does anything"],[19,96,126,153,156]],["Is the play a comedy, a tragedy or a satire?",["Its Folio title calls it a tragedy, and Hector dies","No lover dies and the ending is unresolved","Thersites and Pandarus make it bitter satire","The Folio printers placed it between Histories and Tragedies"],[0,1,178,182]],["Is value real or only in the eye of the beholder?",["Troilus: 'What's aught but as 'tis valued?'","Hector: value must be in the thing itself","Achilles learns his value depends on others' eyes","Cressida: men prize what they have not won"],[20,51,52,103,108]],["Is Thersites right?",["The war is fought over a whore and a cuckold","The heroes behave like fools and beasts","He mocks everyone, including himself","His view ignores Hector's real nobility"],[63,64,65,169,177]],["Does Ulysses' degree speech describe the play's world?",["The camp is clearly disordered","Ulysses uses the speech as a tool of policy","Appetite does devour everything","Policy fails, and the order he praises never returns"],[25,26,27,28,169]],["Who is to blame for Hector's death?",["Achilles, who kills him unarmed","Hector, who ignores warnings and chases armour","Troilus, who scorns mercy","The Trojans, who chose to keep Helen"],[60,164,165,174,176]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present love in Troilus and Cressida?",[3,12,19,79,94,153,162]],["How does Shakespeare present Cressida?",[18,19,88,96,137,155,156]],["How does Shakespeare present war and heroism?",[0,9,64,144,164,174,176]],["How does Shakespeare explore the idea of value?",[20,49,51,53,100,103,121]],["How important is Thersites to the play?",[42,63,64,149,157,169,170]],["How does Shakespeare present time and reputation?",[105,106,107,108,109,142,143]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (imagery, soliloquy, aside, irony, structure, prose and verse) and its effect","Linked to context (Chaucer, Homer, satire, the Folio) where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of Cressida and Thersites","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[19,"Cressida's soliloquy shows that she understands love as a market in which value falls once a thing is won.","'Things won are done' turns love into a transaction that ends at the moment of success.","The rhyming couplets make her sound like a proverb, as if this is common knowledge.","Her later fate, traded for Antenor, proves her view of women's value grimly right.","Some readers see her as calculating; others as realistic in a world where men decide women's worth."],[26,"Ulysses' degree speech presents order as the music of the universe.","'Untune that string' makes disorder a discord, a sound everyone recognises as wrong.","The speech moves from heaven to family, showing order at every level.","Yet it is spoken by the play's chief schemer as a political tool.","The rest of the play, where appetite destroys everyone, makes the speech a prophecy as much as a lesson."],[51,"Troilus's question makes value depend on desire, not on the thing itself.","'Valued' is a word from trade: worth is what someone will pay.","He argues to keep Helen and so continue the war.","Within two acts, Cressida's 'value' will be decided by others, and Troilus will learn the cost.","Hector's reply, that value must be real, is right, but he gives in anyway."],[161,"Troilus's 'This is, and is not, Cressid' shows his mind split by what he sees.","The paradox expresses a reason that argues against itself.","He imagines two Cressidas, his and Diomedes', to protect his ideal.","The scene is watched by Ulysses and Thersites, so his private agony is public.","His idealism cannot survive, and he turns to violence."],[174,"Hector's line about the Greek in fine armour sums up the play's view of war.","'Putrified core' suggests something rotten inside a beautiful shell.","Hector chased the man for his armour, a greedy act that leaves him alone.","Minutes later he is killed unarmed, the heroic myth exposed.","The image can stand for Helen, for Cressida, and for the war itself."],[64,"Thersites reduces the Trojan War to 'a whore and a cuckold'.","The crude nouns strip Helen and Menelaus of all romance.","He speaks in prose, a sign of his low status and his realism.","The action often seems to prove him right, as lust and pride destroy the heroes.","But his view leaves no room for Hector's nobility or Troilus's grief."]],"lang":{"un":[["4","Call here my varlet; I'll unarm again.","Call my servant; I'll take my armour off again."],["353","What's aught but as 'tis valued?","What is anything worth except what we value it at?"],["842","Injurious time now with a robber's haste Crams his rich thiev'ry up, he knows not how.","Cruel time hurriedly stuffs his loot into a bag any old how."],["1247","Words, words, mere words, no matter from the heart;","Just words, nothing from the heart."]],"pun":[["40","Paris is gor'd with Menelaus' horn.","horn","pun","A weapon, and a cuckold's horn."],["156","Will he give you the nod?","nod","pun","A nod of greeting, and 'noddy', a fool."],["303","for whosomever you take him to be, he is Ajax.","Ajax","pun","Ajax and 'a jakes', a toilet."],["518","Sodden business! There's a stew'd phrase indeed!","stew'd","pun","Boiled, and 'the stews', brothels."],["913","That you are odd, and he is even with you.","even","pun","An even number, and getting even."],["1075","And any man may sing her, if he can take her cliff; she's noted.","noted","pun","Set to music, and notorious."]],"st":[["208","Take but degree away, untune that string,",[["degree","Rank and order."],["untune","Disorder as discord."]]],["638","'As false as Cressid.'",[["false","The word she makes her own."],["Cressid","Her name becomes the proverb."]]],["1175","this is, and is not, Cressid.",[["is","Belief."],["not","Seeing."]]]]},"dir":[["200","Women are angels, wooing:","How should Cressida speak her soliloquy?",[["Coolly, as a theory","She has thought this through."],["Wistfully","She wishes she could trust love."],["Defiantly to the audience","She challenges men watching."]]],["208","Take but degree away, untune that string,","How should Ulysses deliver the degree speech?",[["As a sincere warning","He believes in order."],["As a performance for the generals","He is manipulating them."],["Building to fury","He is genuinely alarmed."]]],["898","So much for Nestor.","How should the kissing scene be played?",[["As courtly comedy","The Greeks are gallant."],["As menacing","Cressida is surrounded and helpless."],["With Cressida enjoying it","She flirts back to survive."]]],["1126","Here, Diomed, keep this sleeve.","How should Cressida give the sleeve?",[["Reluctantly","She is forced by her situation."],["Lightly","She has already moved on."],["Then snatching it back","She is torn."]]],["1339","Strike, fellows, strike; this is the man I seek.","How should Hector's death be staged?",[["Brutally and quickly","The ambush is murder."],["Slowly, as a ritual","The end of chivalry."],["With Achilles barely involved","He lets his men do it."]]],["1371","Good traders in the flesh, set this in your painted cloths.","How should Pandarus speak the epilogue?",[["To the audience as fellow bawds","The play turns on us."],["Feebly, as a dying man","Disease has caught up with him."],["Comically","A last bitter joke."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["4","1179"],["4","1369"],["4","637","1179"],["637","638","639"],["1338","1341","1355"],["397","405","329"],["637","1179","1247"],["211","213","422"],["214","655","694"],["642","1369"],["352","405","1339"],["238","938","1240"]],"cr":{"faith":[["353","637","1179"],["632","1137","1144"]],"value":[["200","622","634"],["352","353","354"]],"war":[["397","405","1339"],["348","354","1338"]],"order":[["211","213"],["353","755","211"]],"lust":[["352","563","566"],["565","566","1116"]],"time":[["683","800","1007"],["683","800","1247"]]}}};
